ase translate this Japanese text into French."}} {:id "vocab-87ec2157d1" :track :vocab :level "N1" :display "藁" :reading "わら" :romaji "wara" :meanings ["straw"] :meaning "straw" :example {:ja "彼はどんな藁をもつかむだろう。" :furigana "{彼|かれ}はどんな{藁|わら}をもつかむだろう。" :en "He'll clutch at any straw."}} {:id "vocab-cfd02fb96e" :track :vocab :level "N1" :display "割り当て" :reading "わりあて" :romaji "wariate" :meanings ["allotment" "assignment" "allocation"] :meaning "allotment" :example {:ja "「割り当て」という概念を考察することから始めよう。" :furigana "「{割|わ}り{当|あ}て」という{概念|がいねん}を{考察|こうさつ}することから{始|はじ}めよう。" :en "We will begin by considering the concept of \"quota\"."}} {:id "vocab-fd65caed2e" :track :vocab :level "N1" :display "割り込む" :reading "わりこむ" :romaji "warikomu" :meanings ["to cut in" "to thrust oneself into" "to wedge oneself in"] :meaning "to cut in" :example {:ja "人の話に割り込むのは失礼だぞ。" :furigana "{人|ひと}の{話|はなし}に{割|わ}り{込|こ}むのは{失礼|しつれい}だぞ。" :en "It is rude to interrupt others."}} {:id "vocab-0bc587dbfe" :track :vocab :level "N1" :display "悪者" :reading "わるもの" :romaji "warumono" :meanings ["bad fellow" "rascal" "ruffian"] :meaning "bad fellow" :example {:ja "いない者がいつも悪者になる。" :furigana "いない{者|もの}がいつも{悪者|わるもの}になる。" :en "The absent are always in the wrong."}}])

(def grammar [{:id "grammar-n5-001" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A が いちばん～" :reading "A ga ichiban～" :meaning "Express the superlative degree; 'the most', 'the best', 'the least'." :short "Express the superlative degree; 'the most', 'the best', 'the least'." :long "The A が いちばん～ grammar point is used to express a superlative degree or the highest rank. It can be translated as 'the most', 'the best', or 'the least' in English. The formation is used with various adjectives and verbs, and can also be used in comparison with other items or people." :formation "Noun + が + いちばん + Adjective/Verb" :examples [{:ja "この中で、寿司が一番好きです。" :en "Of all these, I like sushi the most."} {:ja "彼女はクラスで一番速く泳げます。" :en "She can swim the fastest in the class."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-002" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A。けれども、～B。" :reading "A. Keredomo,~ B." :meaning "Used to express a contrast between two statements. It can be translated as 'but' or 'however'." :short "Used to express a contrast between two statements. It can be translated as 'but' or 'however'." :long "The grammar point A。けれども、～B。 is used to express a contrast or contradiction between statement A and statement B. It can be translated as 'but' or 'however' in English. けれども is a conjunction that links two clauses to show there is a conflicting or opposing idea between them." :formation "Statement A + けれども、+ Statement B" :examples [{:ja "今日は晴れている。けれども、寒いです。" :en "It is sunny today. However, it is cold."} {:ja "彼女はうるさい。けれども、面白いです。" :en "She is noisy. But, she is interesting."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-003" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A。しかし、～B。" :reading "A. Shikashi, ~B." :meaning "Expresses contrast or contradiction between two clauses; 'A, but B', 'A, however B'." :short "Expresses contrast or contradiction between two clauses; 'A, but B', 'A, however B'." :long "The grammar point A。しかし、～B。 is used to show contrast or contradiction between two clauses. It can be translated as 'A, but B' or 'A, however B' in English. しかし is placed between two sentences to indicate a contrast between them." :formation "Sentence A + しかし + Sentence B." :examples [{:ja "今日は忙しい。しかし、時間があるなら、コーヒーを飲もう。" :en "I'm busy today. However, if I have time, let's have coffee."} {:ja "その映画は面白かった。しかし、長すぎました。" :en "The movie was interesting. But, it was too long."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-004" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A。じゃ、～B。" :reading "A. Ja, ~B." :meaning "Indicates a decision or suggestion; 'well then', 'in that case', 'so'." :short "Indicates a decision or suggestion; 'well then', 'in that case', 'so'." :long "The A。じゃ、～B。grammar point is used to express a decision, suggestion, or course of action based on a given situation or information. It can be translated as 'well then', 'in that case', or 'so' in English. This grammar works with any form of sentence, whether it's a verb, noun, い-adjective, or な-adjective in both parts (A and B)." :formation "A: Sentence, B: Sentence (A and B can be any sentence type)" :examples [{:ja "映画は時間がない。じゃ、また今度行こう。" :en "There's no time for a movie. Well then, let's go another time."} {:ja "お腹がすいた。じゃ、レストランに行こう。" :en "I'm hungry. In that case, let's go to a restaurant."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-005" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A。それじゃ、～B。" :reading "A. Soreja,~B." :meaning "Connect two sentences; 'A, so/in that case, B'" :short "Connect two sentences; 'A, so/in that case, B'" :long "The A。それじゃ、～B。grammar point is used to connect two sentences or ideas. It translates to 'A, so/in that case, B' in English. This expression is used when the first sentence (A) creates a situation or context and the second sentence (B) indicates the consequence or response to that situation." :formation "Sentence A + 。それじゃ、 + Sentence B" :examples [{:ja "今日は休みです。それじゃ、映画を観に行きましょう。" :en "Today is a day off. In that case, let's go watch a movie."} {:ja "電気が切れた。それじゃ、ろうそくを使いましょう。" :en "The electricity went out. In that case, let's use candles."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-006" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A。それでは、～B。" :reading "A. Soredewa,~B." :meaning "Express a change in situation; 'then', 'in that case', 'so'." :short "Express a change in situation; 'then', 'in that case', 'so'." :long "The A。それでは、～B。grammar point is used to express a change in situation or transition from one action to another. It can be translated as 'then', 'in that case', or 'so' in English. The formation consists of two sentences or clauses, with それでは connecting them and reflecting a change based on the previous statement (A)." :formation "A (Statement) + 。それでは、 + B (Follow-up action or statement)" :examples [{:ja "今日は休みだ。それでは、映画を見に行こう。" :en "Today is a day off. In that case, let's go see a movie."} {:ja "このパンはもう古いです。それでは、捨てましょう。" :en "This bread is already old. Then, let's throw it away."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-007" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A。 では、～B。" :reading "A. Dewa, ~B" :meaning "Indicates a contrast or comparison between A and B; 'As for A, ~B'." :short "Indicates a contrast or comparison between A and B; 'As for A, ~B'." :long "The A では、～B grammar point is used to indicate a contrast or comparison between A and B. It is used to express that 'As for A, ~ B' or 'Regarding A, ~ B'. This grammar structure is often used for contrasting or comparing different subjects/topics." :formation "Noun A + では、 + Subject + Predicate B" :examples [{:ja "果物では、リンゴが一番好きです。" :en "As for fruits, apples are my favorite."} {:ja "スポーツでは、サッカーが一番人気です。" :en "As for sports, soccer is the most popular."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-008" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A。でも、～B。" :reading "A. Demo, ~B" :meaning "Used to express contrast between two statements; 'A, but B', 'A, however B'." :short "Used to express contrast between two statements; 'A, but B', 'A, however B'." :long "The A。でも、～B。grammar point is used to express contrast or an unexpected continuation from statement A to statement B. It can be translated as 'A, but B' or 'A, however B' in English. The formation simply places でも at the beginning of the second sentence to show contrast with the first sentence." :formation "Clause A + でも + Clause B" :examples [{:ja "昨日は寒かった。でも、今日は暖かいです。" :en "Yesterday was cold. But today it's warm."} {:ja "彼は忙しい。でも、友達と遊ぶ時間がある。" :en "He is busy. However, he still has time to hang out with friends."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-009" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Aと Bと どちら～" :reading "A to B to dochira~" :meaning "Used to ask which of two alternatives is preferred or to compare two things" :short "Used to ask which of two alternatives is preferred or to compare two things" :long "The grammar pattern Aと Bと どちら～ is used to compare two alternatives A and B, and ask which one is preferred, better, or more suitable. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, or nouns." :formation "Aと Bと どちら + Verb/Adjective/Noun" :examples [{:ja "りんごとみかんとどちらが好きですか。" :en "Which do you like, apples or oranges?"} {:ja "電車とバスとどちらで行くべきですか。" :en "Should I go by train or bus?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-010" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "AとBと どっち〜" :reading "A to B to docchi〜" :meaning "Asking which one; 'which', 'A or B'." :short "Asking which one; 'which', 'A or B'." :long "The  AとBと どっち〜 grammar point is used to ask which one to choose between two options, A and B. It can be translated as 'which', 'A or B' in English. This is often used in casual speech and can be shortened to  AかBか どっち〜." :formation "AとBと どっち + Verb, AかBか どっち + Verb" :examples [{:ja "コーヒーと紅茶とどっちが好き？" :en "Which do you like, coffee or tea?"} {:ja "映画と音楽とどっちを楽しむ？" :en "Which do you enjoy, movies or music?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-011" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A は B が〜" :reading "A wa B ga〜" :meaning "Indicates that there is something unique or different about A and describes what is special about it." :short "Indicates that there is something unique or different about A and describes what is special about it." :long "The A は B が〜 grammar point is used to emphasize the uniqueness or difference of 'A' by describing a characteristic or feature 'B' that is specific to 'A'. This structure is used to convey that there is something notable or special about 'A' when compared to others." :formation "A は (Topic) + B が (Characteristic/Feature) + Description/Action" :examples [{:ja "彼女は髪が長いです。" :en "What's special about her is that she has long hair."} {:ja "このレストランは雰囲気が良いです。" :en "What's great about this restaurant is the atmosphere."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-012" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A は B より～" :reading "A wa B yori～" :meaning "Express a comparison between A and B; 'A is more/less than B'." :short "Express a comparison between A and B; 'A is more/less than B'." :long "The A は B より grammar point is used to express a comparison between two items or subjects (A and B). It can be translated as 'A is more/less than B' in English. This structure is followed by an adjective that describes the comparison." :formation "A は B より + Adjective" :examples [{:ja "このジュースはあのジュースより甘いです。" :en "This juice is sweeter than that juice."} {:ja "夏は冬より暑いです。" :en "Summer is hotter than winter."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-013" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "A より B のほうが～" :reading "A yori B no hou ga ～" :meaning "Used to compare two items; 'B is more ～ than A'." :short "Used to compare two items; 'B is more ～ than A'." :long "The grammar point A より B のほうが～ is used to compare two items or situations and indicate that one is more (adjective) than the other. It can be translated as 'B is more ～ than A' in English. It is used with both い-adjectives and な-adjectives." :formation "A + より + B + のほうが + Adjective" :examples [{:ja "ピザよりハンバーガーのほうが美味しい。" :en "Hamburgers are more delicious than pizza."} {:ja "夏より冬のほうが寒い。" :en "Winter is colder than summer."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-014" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "い-Adjective く + Verb" :reading "i-Adjective + ku + Verb" :meaning "Used to connect an い-adjective to a verb, modifying the verb (adverbial usage)." :short "Used to connect an い-adjective to a verb, modifying the verb (adverbial usage)." :long "The い-Adjective + く + Verb grammar point transforms an い-adjective into its adverbial form, describing how the verb is performed. In English, this often corresponds to '-ly' adverbs (e.g., 'quickly', 'slowly')." :formation "い-Adjective (remove い) + く + Verb" :examples [{:ja "彼は速く走りました。" :en "He ran quickly."} {:ja "この料理は美味しく食べました。" :en "I enjoyed eating this dish."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-015" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "い-Adjective く します" :reading "i-Adjective ku shimasu" :meaning "Use an い-adjective to express 'to make' or 'to become'." :short "Use an い-adjective to express 'to make' or 'to become'." :long "By adding くします to an い-adjective, you express making something (or causing something to become) the quality of that adjective. In English, this often corresponds to phrases like 'make something more ADJECTIVE.'" :formation "い-Adjective (remove い) + くします" :examples [{:ja "部屋を明るくします。" :en "I will make the room brighter."} {:ja "友達の気分を楽しくします。" :en "I will make my friend’s mood more pleasant."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-016" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "い-Adjective: Negative Polite Form" :reading "" :meaning "Change an い-adjective into a polite negative form with ～くないです (or ～くありません)." :short "Change an い-adjective into a polite negative form with ～くないです (or ～くありません)." :long "To make an い-adjective negative and polite, remove the final い and add くないです. An alternative more formal style is くありません. For example, 美味しい → 美味しくないです / 美味しくありません。" :formation "い-Adjective (remove い) + くないです" :examples [{:ja "このピザは美味しくないです。" :en "This pizza is not delicious."} {:ja "この部屋は広くないです。" :en "This room is not spacious."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-017" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "い-Adjective て" :reading "i-Adjective + te~" :meaning "Used to connect two or more い-adjectives to describe something." :short "Used to connect two or more い-adjectives to describe something." :long "The い-Adjective て form is used to connect two or more い-adjectives when listing attributes of something or someone. To form it, replace the final い with くて. For example, かわいい → かわいくて, おいしい → おいしくて." :formation "い-Adjective (remove い) + くて + い-Adjective" :examples [{:ja "このリンゴは甘くて美味しいです。" :en "This apple is sweet and delicious."} {:ja "彼女は優しくてかわいいです。" :en "She is kind and cute."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-018" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "な-Adjective に + Verb" :reading "" :meaning "Turn a な-adjective into an adverb by adding に." :short "Turn a な-adjective into an adverb by adding に." :long "To make a な-adjective modify a verb, remove the な and add に. This form describes how an action is performed. For instance, 静か（な） → 静かに, 親切（な） → 親切に." :formation "な-Adjective (remove な) + に + Verb" :examples [{:ja "彼は静かに話す。" :en "He speaks quietly."} {:ja "部屋をきれいに掃除した。" :en "I cleaned the room neatly."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-019" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "な-Adjective で～" :reading "na-Adjective de~" :meaning "Usage of で to connect or add information with a な-adjective." :short "Usage of で to connect or add information with a な-adjective." :long "Use な-adjective + で to connect it to another quality, action, or description. It can combine a な-adjective with another adjective, verb, or clause." :formation "な-Adjective + で" :examples [{:ja "彼は綺麗で優しい女性が好きです。" :en "He likes women who are beautiful and kind."} {:ja "この料理は健康的で、元気な気分になります。" :en "This dish is healthy and makes you feel energetic."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-020" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "な-Adjective に します" :reading "na-Adjective ni shimasu" :meaning "Expressing a decision or preference with a な-adjective." :short "Expressing a decision or preference with a な-adjective." :long "The な-adjective に します pattern is used to express a decision or choice based on a な-adjective. It often translates as 'I’ll make it ...,' 'I will choose ...,' or 'I’ve decided on ...'." :formation "な-Adjective + に + します" :examples [{:ja "静かにします。" :en "I will be quiet."} {:ja "部屋をきれいにします。" :en "I will make the room clean."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-021" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "な-Adjective に なります" :reading "na-Adjective ni narimasu" :meaning "Express a change or transformation into a state described by the な-adjective." :short "Express a change or transformation into a state described by the な-adjective." :long "The な-Adjective + に + なります grammar point is used to express a change or transformation into the state described by the な-adjective. It translates to 'become' or 'turn into' along with the meaning of the な-adjective." :formation "な-Adjective (remove な) + に + なります" :examples [{:ja "この部屋は静かになります。" :en "This room becomes quiet."} {:ja "彼女はきれいになります。" :en "She becomes beautiful."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-022" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun か Noun か～" :reading "Noun ka Noun ka～" :meaning "Expresses choice or alternatives; 'either', 'or'." :short "Expresses choice or alternatives; 'either', 'or'." :long "The Noun か Noun か～ grammar point is used to present two or more alternatives for a choice. It can be translated to 'either', 'or' in English. It is formed by placing the particle か in between two nouns or noun phrases." :formation "Noun1 + か + Noun2 + か～" :examples [{:ja "今日、映画を見るか、散歩するかどちらにしますか？" :en "Will we watch a movie or go for a walk today?"} {:ja "コーヒーか紅茶か、一つだけ選んでください。" :en "Please choose only one, either coffee or tea."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-023" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun が できます" :reading "Noun ga dekimasu" :meaning "Express the ability to do something." :short "Express the ability to do something." :long "The Noun が できます grammar point is used to express the ability to do something. In this grammar pattern, the noun is the activity that the speaker or subject can do. It translates as 'can do' or 'able to do' in English." :formation "Noun + が + できます" :examples [{:ja "英語ができます。" :en "I can speak English."} {:ja "料理ができます。" :en "I can cook."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-024" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun がほしいです" :reading "〜ga hoshii desu" :meaning "Express a desire or wanting something; 'I want', 'I would like'." :short "Express a desire or wanting something; 'I want', 'I would like'." :long "The がほしいです grammar point is used to express a desire or the act of wanting something in polite form. It can be translated as 'I want' or 'I would like' in English. It is used with a noun and the particle が." :formation "Noun + が + ほしいです" :examples [{:ja "新しい車がほしいです。" :en "I want a new car."} {:ja "コーヒーがほしいです。" :en "I would like some coffee."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-025" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun から Noun まで" :reading "Noun kara Noun made" :meaning "Expresses a range; 'from...to', 'between...and'." :short "Expresses a range; 'from...to', 'between...and'." :long "The から...まで grammar point is used to express a range between two nouns, indicating the starting point and ending point of an action, event, or period of time. It can be translated as 'from...to' or 'between...and' in English." :formation "Noun1 + から + Noun2 + まで" :examples [{:ja "朝9時から午後5時まで働きます。" :en "I work from 9 AM to 5 PM."} {:ja "東京から大阪まで電車で行く。" :en "I will go by train from Tokyo to Osaka."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-026" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun くらい～" :reading "Noun kurai～" :meaning "Express the extent or degree of something; 'about', 'approximately', 'at least'." :short "Express the extent or degree of something; 'about', 'approximately', 'at least'." :long "The くらい grammar point is used with a noun to express the extent or degree of something, meaning 'about', 'approximately', or 'at least' in English. It helps to provide a rough estimate or minimum standard to the noun it is attached to." :formation "Noun + くらい" :examples [{:ja "この本は三百ページくらいあります。" :en "This book has about 300 pages."} {:ja "彼の家まで歩いて20分くらいかかります。" :en "It takes about 20 minutes to walk to his house."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-027" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun ぐらい～" :reading "Noun gurai～" :meaning "Express an approximate amount or degree; 'about', 'approximately'." :short "Express an approximate amount or degree; 'about', 'approximately'." :long "The ぐらい grammar point is used with nouns to express an approximate amount or degree. It is often translated as 'about', 'approximately', or 'around' in English. This can be used for time, quantity, or other measurements." :formation "Noun + ぐらい" :examples [{:ja "一日中勉強したので、１０時間ぐらい勉強しました。" :en "I studied all day, so I studied for about 10 hours."} {:ja "この本は１００ページぐらいある。" :en "This book has about 100 pages."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-028" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun ごろ～" :reading "Noun + goro～" :meaning "Expresses 'around' or 'about' a certain time or age." :short "Expresses 'around' or 'about' a certain time or age." :long "The ごろ grammar point is used to indicate 'around' or 'about' a certain time or age. It is commonly used with time-related expressions and age-related expressions to give an approximate time or age." :formation "Noun + ごろ" :examples [{:ja "昼ごろに電話してください。" :en "Please call me around noon."} {:ja "彼は10歳ごろに日本に来ました。" :en "He came to Japan around the age of 10."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-029" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun だけ〜" :reading "〜dake" :meaning "Indicates 'only', 'just', or 'merely'." :short "Indicates 'only', 'just', or 'merely'." :long "The Noun だけ grammar point is used to emphasize the idea of 'only', 'just', or 'merely' in a sentence. It can be attached to nouns, verbs, い-adjectives, and な-adjectives to show this emphasis. This grammar point is commonly used in both spoken and written Japanese." :formation "Noun + だけ, Verb-casual + だけ, い-Adjective + だけ, な-Adjective-な + だけ" :examples [{:ja "コーヒーだけ飲んで、帰りました。" :en "I only drank coffee and went home."} {:ja "この本は二十ページだけ読んだ。" :en "I only read twenty pages of this book."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-030" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Nounで～" :reading "Noun de ~" :meaning "Indicate a place or means for an action or situation; 'at, in, on', 'using', or 'by means of'." :short "Indicate a place or means for an action or situation; 'at, in, on', 'using', or 'by means of'." :long "The で grammar point is used to indicate a location, method, or tool for doing an action or for being in a situation. It can be translated as 'at, in, on', 'using', or 'by means of' in English. Nounで is formed by adding で after a noun." :formation "Noun + で" :examples [{:ja "図書館で勉強します。" :en "I study at the library."} {:ja "バスで学校に行きます。" :en "I go to school by bus."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-031" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun と～" :reading "Noun to～" :meaning "Express 'with', 'and' or 'together with' when connecting nouns." :short "Express 'with', 'and' or 'together with' when connecting nouns." :long "The Noun と grammar point is used to express a connection between two or more nouns, indicating 'with', 'and', or 'together with' in English. It functions as a particle that is used to simply list multiple items or express companionship." :formation "Noun1 + と + Noun2" :examples [{:ja "リンゴとバナナを買いました。" :en "I bought apples and bananas."} {:ja "友達と映画を見に行きます。" :en "I will go to watch a movie with my friend."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-032" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun に～" :reading "Noun ni～" :meaning "Express a specific direction, target or purpose; 'to', 'toward', 'for'." :short "Express a specific direction, target or purpose; 'to', 'toward', 'for'." :long "The 'Noun に' grammar point is used to express a specific direction, target, or purpose in relation to a noun. It can be translated as 'to', 'toward', or 'for' in English. The に particle is added directly after the noun which indicates direction, target or purpose." :formation "Noun + に" :examples [{:ja "彼女にプレゼントをあげました。" :en "I gave a present to her."} {:ja "日本に行きます。" :en "I am going to Japan."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-033" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun に 帰ります" :reading "Noun ni kaerimasu" :meaning "Express going back or returning to a place: 'to return to', 'to go back to'." :short "Express going back or returning to a place: 'to return to', 'to go back to'." :long "The grammar point 'Noun に 帰ります' is used to express physically going back or returning to a specific place. It can be translated as 'to go back to' or 'to return to' in English. The particle に indicates the destination of the return." :formation "Noun + に + 帰ります" :examples [{:ja "夏休みが終わったら、学校に帰ります。" :en "When summer vacation ends, I'll go back to school."} {:ja "買い物を終えたら、家に帰ります。" :en "After finishing shopping, I will go back home."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-034" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun に します" :reading "Noun ni shimasu" :meaning "Express a choice or decision; 'choose', 'decide on'." :short "Express a choice or decision; 'choose', 'decide on'." :long "The に します grammar point is used to express making a choice or decision. It can be translated as 'choose' or 'decide on' in English. It is often used in situations where someone has to make a selection or decide on something, such as ordering food at a restaurant or deciding on a vacation destination." :formation "Noun + に します" :examples [{:ja "今日の昼ご飯は寿司にします。" :en "I'll choose sushi for today's lunch."} {:ja "この靴を買うか迷っていますが、赤い色の方にします。" :en "I was hesitating about which shoes to buy, but I'll go with the red ones."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-035" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun に なります" :reading "Noun ni narimasu" :meaning "Expresses change or becoming something; 'to become', 'to turn into', 'to be'." :short "Expresses change or becoming something; 'to become', 'to turn into', 'to be'." :long "The grammar point Noun に なります is used to express a change or transformation into something else, or to indicate that something 'becomes', 'turns into', or simply 'is' something. It is commonly used with nouns." :formation "Noun + に + なります" :examples [{:ja "彼は医者になります。" :en "He will become a doctor."} {:ja "この茶色の葉が赤になります。" :en "These brown leaves will turn red."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-036" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun に 来ます" :reading "Noun ni kimasu" :meaning "Used to express coming to a place." :short "Used to express coming to a place." :long "The grammar point Noun に 来ます is used to indicate that someone or something comes to a specific place. The noun before に indicates the destination or place, while the verb 来ます expresses the action of coming. (Similarly, 'Noun に 行きます' means 'go to [Noun],' and 'Noun に 帰ります' means 'return to [Noun].')" :formation "Noun + に + 来ます (kimasu)" :examples [{:ja "友達が私の家に来ます。" :en "My friend will come to my house."} {:ja "今晩、父が駅に迎えに来ます。" :en "Tonight, my father will come to pick me up at the station."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-037" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun に 行きます" :reading "Noun ni ikimasu" :meaning "Used to express going to a location." :short "Used to express going to a location." :long "The Noun に 行きます grammar point is used to express going to a specific location. This structure requires a noun (indicating the destination) followed by に and the verb 行きます, which means 'to go'." :formation "Noun + に + 行きます" :examples [{:ja "学校に行きます。" :en "I will go to school."} {:ja "明日、スーパーに行きます。" :en "I will go to the supermarket tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-038" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun の あとで" :reading "Noun no atode" :meaning "Expresses the order of events; 'after', 'later than'." :short "Expresses the order of events; 'after', 'later than'." :long "The の あとで grammar point is used to express the order of events, specifying that an action occurs after or later than the noun mentioned. It translates to 'after' or 'later than' in English. This structure is simply formed by attaching の あとで to the noun." :formation "Noun + の + あとで" :examples [{:ja "朝ご飯のあとで、歯を磨きます。" :en "After breakfast, I brush my teeth."} {:ja "映画のあとで、レストランで夕食を食べました。" :en "After the movie, we ate dinner at a restaurant."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-039" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun の 前に" :reading "Noun no mae ni" :meaning "Used to describe a location or position 'in front of' a noun." :short "Used to describe a location or position 'in front of' a noun." :long "The grammar point Noun の 前に is used to describe a location or position 'in front of' a noun. It is formed by adding の 前に after the noun it refers to. (Note that 'Noun の 前に' can also mean 'before [Noun]' in time contexts, but these examples focus on physical location.)" :formation "Noun + の + 前に" :examples [{:ja "駅の前にコンビニがあります。" :en "There is a convenience store in front of the station."} {:ja "家の前に車を止めてください。" :en "Please park the car in front of the house."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-040" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun は～" :reading "Noun wa〜" :meaning "Used to mark the topic of a sentence; 'as for', 'speaking of'." :short "Used to mark the topic of a sentence; 'as for', 'speaking of'." :long "The は (wa) particle is used to indicate the topic of a sentence. It is usually translated as 'as for', 'speaking of', or it simply marks what the sentence is about. This is different from が (ga), which marks the subject of a verb." :formation "Noun + は" :examples [{:ja "私は日本の料理が好きです。" :en "As for me, I like Japanese cuisine."} {:ja "彼は英語が上手です。" :en "As for him, he is good at English."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-041" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun も〜" :reading "Noun mo~" :meaning "Indicates that the action or state is the same for multiple items or people; 'also', 'as well'." :short "Indicates that the action or state is the same for multiple items or people; 'also', 'as well'." :long "The も grammar point is used after a noun to indicate that the action or state is the same for multiple items or people. It means 'also', 'as well', or 'too' in English. も often replaces は or が in the sentence to show that the same statement applies to this additional noun." :formation "Noun + も" :examples [{:ja "私も日本語を勉強しています。" :en "I am also studying Japanese."} {:ja "彼もケーキが好きです。" :en "He also likes cake."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-042" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun や Noun など～" :reading "Noun ya Noun nado" :meaning "Lists items or examples but not exhaustively; 'such as', 'like', 'and so on'." :short "Lists items or examples but not exhaustively; 'such as', 'like', 'and so on'." :long "The や...など grammar point is used to list a few items or examples that belong to a similar category or context, but it is not exhaustive. It can be translated as 'such as', 'like', or 'and so on' in English. This grammar pattern is used with nouns." :formation "Noun1 + や + Noun2 + など" :examples [{:ja "彼は野菜や果物などを食べるのが好きです。" :en "He likes to eat things like vegetables and fruits."} {:ja "日本の都市や東京、大阪などに行きたいです。" :en "I want to go to Japanese cities such as Tokyo, Osaka, and so on."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-043" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun を〜" :reading "Noun wo〜" :meaning "Indicates the direct object of a verb." :short "Indicates the direct object of a verb." :long "The を (wo) particle is used to mark the direct object of a verb in a sentence. It shows the noun that the action (verb) is performed on. The particle を is usually pronounced as 'o' in spoken Japanese." :formation "Noun + を + Verb" :examples [{:ja "私は本を読みます。" :en "I read a book."} {:ja "彼はケーキを食べました。" :en "He ate a cake."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-044" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb た あとで" :reading "ta ato de" :meaning "Indicates an action that takes place after another action." :short "Indicates an action that takes place after another action." :long "The たあとで grammar point is used to indicate that one action takes place after another action is completed. It means 'after' or 'after doing' in English. The formation is created with the た form (past tense) of a verb, followed by あとで (ato de)." :formation "Verb-た form + あとで" :examples [{:ja "宿題をしたあとで、テレビを見ました。" :en "After doing my homework, I watched TV."} {:ja "朝ごはんを食べたあとで、歯を磨きます。" :en "After eating breakfast, I brush my teeth."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-045" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb たいです" :reading "taidesu" :meaning "Express desire or want to do something; 'I want to', 'I would like to'." :short "Express desire or want to do something; 'I want to', 'I would like to'." :long "The たいです grammar point is used to express the speaker's desire or want to perform a certain action. It can be translated as 'I want to' or 'I would like to' in English. The formation differs depending on whether the verb is a る-verb or an う-verb." :formation "る-Verb: Remove る and add たいです, う-Verb: Replace the final verb character with the ～い form and add たいです, Exception: する becomes したいです" :examples [{:ja "私は寿司を食べたいです。" :en "I want to eat sushi."} {:ja "彼は日本に行きたいです。" :en "He wants to go to Japan."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-046" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb て～" :reading "Verb + te～" :meaning "A verb conjugation that connects actions or states." :short "A verb conjugation that connects actions or states." :long "The ～て form of a verb is used to connect actions or states in a sequence, implying that the first action is completed before the second one starts. The ～て form is frequently used in casual speech, and it can also be used in polite speech when combined with certain auxiliary verbs like ください (kudasai) or いる (iru)." :formation "Verb (Group 1) - Replace last syllable with 〜て, Verb (Group 2) - Replace 'る' with 〜て, Verb (Group 3) - する becomes して, くる becomes きて" :examples [{:ja "コーヒーを飲んで、出かけます。" :en "After drinking coffee, I will go out."} {:ja "買い物をして、家に帰りましょう。" :en "Let's go shopping and then go home."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-047" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb て います" :reading "Verb te imasu" :meaning "Express ongoing action or state; 'is doing', 'is doing (continuous)', 'has been doing'." :short "Express ongoing action or state; 'is doing', 'is doing (continuous)', 'has been doing'." :long "The て います grammar point is used to express an action that is ongoing, continuous, or has been happening. It is formed by conjugating the verb in its て-form and adding います. It can be translated as 'is doing', 'is doing (continuous)', or 'has been doing' in English." :formation "Verb て-form + います" :examples [{:ja "彼女は今ピアノを弾いています。" :en "She is playing the piano now."} {:ja "私たちは日本語を勉強しています。" :en "We are studying Japanese."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-048" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb てから～" :reading "〜te kara" :meaning "Express a sequence of actions or events; 'after', 'since', 'once'." :short "Express a sequence of actions or events; 'after', 'since', 'once'." :long "The ～てから grammar point is used to indicate the sequence of actions or events, meaning that one action or event occurs after another. It can be translated as 'after', 'since', or 'once' in English. The て-form indicates the first action and から shows the sequence." :formation "Verb-て形 + から" :examples [{:ja "宿題をしてから、テレビを見ました。" :en "I watched TV after doing my homework."} {:ja "食べてから、歯を磨きましょう。" :en "Let's brush our teeth after eating."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-049" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb て ください" :reading "Verb-te kudasai" :meaning "A request or command; 'please do'." :short "A request or command; 'please do'." :long "The て ください grammar point is used to make a request or command someone to do something politely. The verb is conjugated into its て-form and followed by ください, which means 'please'. This structure is used when asking someone to do something with a polite and respectful tone." :formation "Verb-て form + ください" :examples [{:ja "ドアを開けてください。" :en "Please open the door."} {:ja "電話番号を教えてください。" :en "Please tell me your phone number."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-050" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb ないで ください" :reading "〜naide kudasai" :meaning "Used to make a polite request for someone not to do something; 'please don't'." :short "Used to make a polite request for someone not to do something; 'please don't'." :long "The grammar point Verb ないで ください is used to make a polite request for someone not to do a certain action. In English, it can be translated as 'please don't'. To form this expression, take the verb in its negative form (ない-form) and add で ください." :formation "Verb-ない-form + で ください" :examples [{:ja "タバコを吸わないでください。" :en "Please don't smoke."} {:ja "ここで走らないでください。" :en "Please don't run here."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-051" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Noun に 戻ります" :reading "Noun ni modorimasu" :meaning "Expresses the action of returning to a previous state, place, or condition." :short "Expresses the action of returning to a previous state, place, or condition." :long "The Noun に 戻ります grammar point is used to express going back or reverting to a previous state, place, or condition. 戻る (modoru) literally means 'to go back' or 'to revert'. This is often used after a change has occurred, indicating that something is being undone or returned to its original form." :formation "Noun + に + 戻ります" :examples [{:ja "冷蔵庫に入れると、溶けたアイスクリームは固体に戻ります。" :en "When put in the refrigerator, the melted ice cream returns to its solid state."} {:ja "休暇が終わったら、仕事に戻ります。" :en "When the vacation ends, I will return to work."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-052" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb に 来ます" :reading "Verb ni kimasu" :meaning "Expresses the purpose of coming to a place; 'come to (do something)'." :short "Expresses the purpose of coming to a place; 'come to (do something)'." :long "The grammar point Verb に きます is used to express the purpose of coming to a place to perform an action. It can be translated as 'come to (do something)' in English. To form this expression, use the verb's ます-stem (i.e., remove ます) followed by に きます." :formation "Verb (ます-stem) + に + 来ます" :examples [{:ja "友達に会いに来ました。" :en "I came to meet my friend."} {:ja "映画を見に来ました。" :en "I came to watch a movie."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-053" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb に 行きます" :reading "Verb ni ikimasu" :meaning "To go (somewhere) in order to do an action." :short "To go (somewhere) in order to do an action." :long "The grammar point Verb に 行きます is used when expressing that someone is going to a certain place with the purpose of doing a specific action. Form it by taking the verb's ます-stem (for suru-verbs, remove する and add し) and then adding に 行きます." :formation "Verb (ます-stem) + に + 行きます" :examples [{:ja "公園に遊びに行きます。" :en "I will go to the park to play."} {:ja "図書館に勉強しに行きます。" :en "I will go to the library to study."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-054" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb ましょう" :reading "mashou" :meaning "Making a suggestion or invitation; 'let's' or 'shall we'." :short "Making a suggestion or invitation; 'let's' or 'shall we'." :long "The ましょう grammar point is used to suggest or invite someone to do something in a polite and friendly way. It can be translated as 'let's' or 'shall we' in English. This grammar point is formed by attaching ましょう to the stem of the verb (i.e. remove ます)." :formation "Verb (ます-stem) + ましょう" :examples [{:ja "一緒に映画を見ましょう。" :en "Let's watch a movie together."} {:ja "散歩しましょう。" :en "Let's take a walk."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-055" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb ましょうか。" :reading "〜mashou ka." :meaning "Used to make a polite suggestion or offer help; 'shall we', 'should we', 'do you want me to'." :short "Used to make a polite suggestion or offer help; 'shall we', 'should we', 'do you want me to'." :long "The ましょうか grammar point is used to make a polite suggestion, propose an action, or offer help. It can be translated as 'shall we', 'should we', or 'do you want me to' in English. This formation is used with verbs in their ます-form, replacing ます with ましょう and adding か." :formation "Verb (ます-stem) + ましょうか" :examples [{:ja "一緒に映画を見に行きましょうか。" :en "Shall we go see a movie together?"} {:ja "コーヒーをもう一杯飲みましょうか。" :en "Should we have another cup of coffee?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-056" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb ませんか。" :reading "Verb-masenka" :meaning "An invitation or suggestion; 'won't you', 'why don't you', 'shall we'." :short "An invitation or suggestion; 'won't you', 'why don't you', 'shall we'." :long "The ませんか grammar point is used for making invitations or suggestions in a polite manner. The verb is conjugated to its ます form and replaced with ませんか. This can be translated as 'won't you', 'why don't you', or 'shall we' in English." :formation "Verb (ます-form) + ませんか" :examples [{:ja "一緒に映画を見ませんか。" :en "Won't you watch a movie together with me?"} {:ja "明日のパーティーに参加しませんか。" :en "Why don't you join tomorrow's party?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-057" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb ること～" :reading "〜ru koto" :meaning "Express an action or event as a noun; 'the act of', 'doing'." :short "Express an action or event as a noun; 'the act of', 'doing'." :long "The ること grammar point is used to nominalize or turn a verb into a noun, which represents the action or event itself as a concept. It can be translated as 'the act of' or simply 'doing' in English. This formation is used with verbs in the dictionary (ru-)form." :formation "Verb (dictionary form) + こと" :examples [{:ja "泳ぐことが大好きです。" :en "I love swimming."} {:ja "日本語を勉強することは楽しいです。" :en "Studying Japanese is fun."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-058" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb る こと が できます" :reading "ru koto ga dekimasu" :meaning "Express the potential form, or the ability to do something." :short "Express the potential form, or the ability to do something." :long "The 〜ることができます grammar point is used to express that someone is capable of doing something or that something is possible. It is essentially the potential form of the verb. The formation is made by changing the verb to a noun phrase with こと and then adding ができます." :formation "Verb (dictionary form) + ことができます" :examples [{:ja "この問題を解くことができます。" :en "I can solve this problem."} {:ja "彼は英語を話すことができます。" :en "He can speak English."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-059" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb る こと ができる" :reading "ru koto ga dekiru" :meaning "Expresses the potential form; 'can', 'be able to', 'is possible'." :short "Expresses the potential form; 'can', 'be able to', 'is possible'." :long "The る こと ができる grammar point is used to express the potential form of a verb, meaning the ability or possibility to do something. It can be translated as 'can', 'be able to', or 'is possible' in English. This grammar is often used with verbs ending in る, but can also apply to other verbs once they are in the dictionary form." :formation "Verb (dictionary form) + ことができる" :examples [{:ja "この問題を解くことができる？" :en "Can you solve this problem?"} {:ja "彼は泳ぐことができます。" :en "He can swim."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-060" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb る の～" :reading "Verb + ru + no~" :meaning "Used to express the purpose or reason in casual speech, or nominalize the verb with の." :short "Used to express the purpose or reason in casual speech, or nominalize the verb with の." :long "The る の～ grammar point is a casual spoken pattern that can nominalize a verb or explain a purpose/reason by adding の. It's often seen in everyday conversation, and can be used for nuance such as giving an explanation. It's similar to ること, but is more colloquial or explanatory in tone." :formation "Verb (dictionary form) + の (casual)" :examples [{:ja "買い物をするために、銀行でお金をおろすの。" :en "I'm withdrawing money at the bank so I can go shopping."} {:ja "友達にプレゼントをあげるので、ケーキを焼くの。" :en "I'm baking a cake because I'm giving my friend a present."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-061" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "Verb る 前に" :reading "ru mae ni" :meaning "Used to express 'before' in a sense of an action must be done before another action or event." :short "Used to express 'before' in a sense of an action must be done before another action or event." :long "The る前に (ru mae ni) grammar point is used to indicate that an action (in the dictionary form) should be completed before another action occurs or before a specific event happens." :formation "Verb (dictionary form) + 前に" :examples [{:ja "出かける前に、部屋を掃除してください。" :en "Please clean the room before leaving."} {:ja "寝る前に、歯を磨きます。" :en "I brush my teeth before going to bed."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-062" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "あまり～ありません" :reading "amari ~ arimasen" :meaning "Express 'not very', 'not much', or 'not really' when describing a negative state or situation." :short "Express 'not very', 'not much', or 'not really' when describing a negative state or situation." :long "The grammar point あまり～ありません is used to describe a negative state or condition and indicate that it's not very, not much, or not really. It is commonly used with verbs in the negative form and can be used with い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "あまり + Verb-ないform + ありません / あまり + い-Adjective-く ありません / あまり + な-Adjective・Noun じゃありません" :examples [{:ja "彼はあまり疲れていません。" :en "He is not really tired."} {:ja "彼女はあまり早くないですよ。" :en "She is not very fast."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-063" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "あまり～ないです" :reading "amari ~ nai desu" :meaning "Express 'not really' or 'not very'; used to soften a negative statement." :short "Express 'not really' or 'not very'; used to soften a negative statement." :long "The あまり～ないです grammar point is used to express that something is 'not really' or 'not very' to a certain extent. It is often used to soften a negative statement. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, and な-adjectives in their negative forms." :formation "あまり + Verb-ないform + です / あまり + い-Adjective-くない + です / あまり + な-Adjective-じゃない + です" :examples [{:ja "あまり食べないです。" :en "I don't really eat much."} {:ja "この映画はあまり面白くないです。" :en "This movie is not really interesting."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-064" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～あります" :reading "〜arimasu" :meaning "Indicate possession or existence of inanimate objects; 'there is', 'there are'." :short "Indicate possession or existence of inanimate objects; 'there is', 'there are'." :long "The あります grammar point is used to indicate the possession or existence of inanimate objects. It can be translated as 'there is' or 'there are' in English. It is different from います, which is used for people and most living creatures. あります is commonly used with particle が to mark the object." :formation "Object + が + あります" :examples [{:ja "教室に机があります。" :en "There is a desk in the classroom."} {:ja "この公園にたくさんの花があります。" :en "There are many flowers in this park."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-065" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～いかがですか。" :reading "〜ikaga desu ka." :meaning "A polite way to ask someone's thoughts, opinions, or preferences; 'how about', 'how is'." :short "A polite way to ask someone's thoughts, opinions, or preferences; 'how about', 'how is'." :long "The ～いかがですか grammar point is used to politely ask someone's thoughts, opinions, or preferences. It can be translated as 'how about' or 'how is' in English. The formation is quite straightforward when used with verbs or nouns." :formation "Noun + は + いかがですか / (Verb-て form + は) いかがですか" :examples [{:ja "お茶はいかがですか？" :en "How about some tea?"} {:ja "このドレスはいかがですか？" :en "How about this dress?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-066" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "いくつ～" :reading "ikutsu~" :meaning "Used to express 'how many' or ask for the number of something." :short "Used to express 'how many' or ask for the number of something." :long "The grammar point いくつ is used when asking or expressing 'how many' of something there are, or to indicate an unspecified number. In English, it can be translated as 'how many' or 'several'." :formation "いくつ + Noun (optional)" :examples [{:ja "リンゴはいくつありますか？" :en "How many apples are there?"} {:ja "いくつかの問題を解決しなければなりません。" :en "I have to solve several problems."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-067" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "いつか～" :reading "itsuka～" :meaning "Indicates an unspecified point in time; 'someday', 'one day', 'at some point'." :short "Indicates an unspecified point in time; 'someday', 'one day', 'at some point'." :long "The grammar point いつか is used to indicate an unspecified point in time, meaning 'someday', 'one day', or 'at some point' in the future or the past. It can be used in sentences to express hopes, dreams, or possibilities." :formation "いつか + sentence" :examples [{:ja "いつか日本に行きたいです。" :en "I want to go to Japan someday."} {:ja "彼はいつか有名な作家になるでしょう。" :en "He will become a famous writer someday."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-068" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "いつでも～" :reading "itsudemo～" :meaning "Expresses 'anytime', 'always', or 'whenever'." :short "Expresses 'anytime', 'always', or 'whenever'." :long "The いつでも grammar point is used to express that something occurs, can occur, or is allowed to occur anytime, always, or whenever. It emphasizes the lack of restrictions related to time for a certain action or event." :formation "いつでも + (Verb)" :examples [{:ja "いつでも遊びに来てください。" :en "Please come to play anytime."} {:ja "彼女はいつでも笑顔です。" :en "She is always smiling."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-069" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "いつも～" :reading "itsumo～" :meaning "Expressing 'always' or 'usually' in a sentence." :short "Expressing 'always' or 'usually' in a sentence." :long "The grammar point いつも (itsumo) is used to express the meaning of 'always' or 'usually' when describing a habitual action or situation. It is often used with the present tense or past tense to show that something is done on a regular basis or has been happening consistently." :formation "いつも + Verb / Adjective / Noun" :examples [{:ja "彼はいつも遅刻します。" :en "He is always late."} {:ja "私はいつも優しい犬が好きです。" :en "I always like gentle dogs."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-070" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～て います" :reading "～te imasu" :meaning "Expresses the present continuous or ongoing action; 'to be doing', 'currently doing'." :short "Expresses the present continuous or ongoing action; 'to be doing', 'currently doing'." :long "The ～て います grammar pattern is used to express that an action is currently happening or ongoing. It can be translated as 'to be doing' or 'currently doing' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is a る-verb, う-verb, or an irregular verb, but all use the verb’s て-form + います." :formation "Verb て-form + います" :examples [{:ja "今、音楽を聞いています。" :en "I am listening to music right now."} {:ja "父は新聞を読んでいます。" :en "My father is reading a newspaper."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-071" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～が" :reading "〜ga" :meaning "Particle indicating the subject of a sentence or expressing 'but'." :short "Particle indicating the subject of a sentence or expressing 'but'." :long "The ～が grammar point is a particle that is used to indicate the subject of a sentence or to express a contrast similar to 'but' in English. It has various functions depending on the context." :formation "Noun + が / い-Adjective + が / な-Adjective + が / Verb + が" :examples [{:ja "私が学生です。" :en "I am a student."} {:ja "このりんごが美味しい。" :en "This apple is delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-072" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～が、～" :reading "〜ga, 〜" :meaning "Express a contrast between two sentences; 'but', 'however'." :short "Express a contrast between two sentences; 'but', 'however'." :long "The ～が、～ grammar point is used to express a contrast or contradiction between two sentences or clauses. It can be translated as 'but' or 'however' in English. This grammar point is slightly more formal than けど and is used to show a contrast in the situation." :formation "Sentence 1 + が、+ Sentence 2" :examples [{:ja "デザートは美味しいが、カロリーが高い。" :en "The dessert is delicious, but it is high in calories."} {:ja "彼は頭がいいが、少し怠け者だ。" :en "He is smart, but a bit lazy."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-073" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～から、～" :reading "〜kara、～" :meaning "Expresses reason or cause; 'because', 'since', 'so'." :short "Expresses reason or cause; 'because', 'since', 'so'." :long "The ～から grammar point is used to indicate the reason or cause for something. It can be translated as 'because', 'since', or 'so' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + から / い-Adjective + から / な-Adjective + だから / Noun + だから" :examples [{:ja "明日は試験があるから、今晩勉強しなければなりません。" :en "Because I have an exam tomorrow, I must study tonight."} {:ja "この部屋は狭いから、もっと大きい部屋を探しています。" :en "Since this room is small, I am looking for a bigger one."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-074" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～からです" :reading "〜kara desu" :meaning "Express the reason or cause; 'because', 'since', 'due to'." :short "Express the reason or cause; 'because', 'since', 'due to'." :long "The ～からです grammar point is used to state the reason or cause of something, often as a short concluding statement. It can be translated as 'because', 'since', or 'due to' in English. The formation can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, or nouns, but be careful with な-adjectives and nouns: typically, it's [Noun/な-adjective] + だからです." :formation "Verb-casual + からです / い-Adjective + からです / な-Adjective + だからです / Noun + だからです" :examples [{:ja "寒いからです。コートを着ています。" :en "It’s because it’s cold. That’s why I’m wearing a coat."} {:ja "この店は安いからです。よく来ます。" :en "It’s because this store is cheap. That’s why I come often."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-075" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～から もらいます" :reading "〜kara moraimasu" :meaning "To receive something or get someone to do something." :short "To receive something or get someone to do something." :long "The ～からもらいます grammar point is used to express receiving something from someone or getting someone to do something for you. In English, it can be translated as 'to receive from' or 'to get someone to do something'." :formation "Noun + から + (Verb-て form) + もらいます" :examples [{:ja "友達からプレゼントをもらいました。" :en "I received a present from my friend."} {:ja "先生に宿題を渡してもらいました。" :en "I had the teacher hand me the homework."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-076" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～が 私に くれます" :reading "〜ga watashi ni kuremasu" :meaning "Used to express that someone gives a gift or favor to 'me'; 'someone gives me'." :short "Used to express that someone gives a gift or favor to 'me'; 'someone gives me'." :long "The ～が私にくれます grammar point is used to express that someone gives 'me' something or does something for me. It can be translated as 'someone gives me' or 'someone does something for me' in English. Typically, the giver is marked by が, and the receiver is 私に (to me)." :formation "Person + が + (Thing or Action) + 私に + くれます" :examples [{:ja "彼がプレゼントを私にくれました。" :en "He gave me a present."} {:ja "友達が宿題を手伝って私にくれました。" :en "My friend helped me with my homework."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-077" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～けど、～" :reading "〜kedo、～" :meaning "Used to connect two clauses; 'but', 'however', 'although'" :short "Used to connect two clauses; 'but', 'however', 'although'" :long "The ～けど grammar point is used to connect two clauses, often to show a contrast or to introduce a different point of view. It can be translated as 'but', 'however', or 'although' in English. It's more casual than けれど or が." :formation "Verb-casual + けど / い-adjective + けど / な-adjective + だけど / Noun + だけど" :examples [{:ja "授業に出たけど、まだ宿題をしないといけない。" :en "I attended the class, but I still have to do my homework."} {:ja "昨日寒かったけど、今日は暖かいです。" :en "Yesterday was cold, but today is warm."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-078" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～けれど、～" :reading "〜keredo、～" :meaning "Expresses a contrast or contradiction; 'but', 'however', 'even though'." :short "Expresses a contrast or contradiction; 'but', 'however', 'even though'." :long "The ～けれど grammar point is used to express a contrast or contradiction between two statements. It can be translated as 'but', 'however', or 'even though' in English. This grammar point is slightly more formal than けど." :formation "Verb-casual + けれど / い-Adjective + けれど / な-Adjective + だけれど / Noun + だけれど" :examples [{:ja "今日は寒いけれど、散歩に行きます。" :en "Even though it's cold today, I'll go for a walk."} {:ja "彼は親切けれど、少し怖いです。" :en "He is kind, but a little scary."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-079" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "こちら～" :reading "kochira～" :meaning "Refers to a person, place or thing; 'this', 'this one', 'this way', 'here'." :short "Refers to a person, place or thing; 'this', 'this one', 'this way', 'here'." :long "The こちら grammar point is used to refer to a person, place, or thing that is closer to the speaker, or to show politeness when referring to someone. It can mean 'this', 'this one', 'this way', or 'here'." :formation "こちら / こちら + の + Person/Thing" :examples [{:ja "こちらは田中さんです。" :en "This is Mr./Ms. Tanaka."} {:ja "こちらのレストランは美味しいです。" :en "This restaurant is delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-080" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "さっき～" :reading "sakki～" :meaning "Refers to a short time ago or just now." :short "Refers to a short time ago or just now." :long "The word さっき (sakki) is an adverb used to express that something happened or was done a short time ago or just now. It can be combined with verbs or other phrases to convey that meaning." :formation "さっき + verb / さっき + phrase" :examples [{:ja "さっき電話がありました。" :en "There was a phone call just now."} {:ja "さっき部屋を掃除しました。" :en "I cleaned the room a short while ago."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-081" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "すぐに～" :reading "sugu ni～" :meaning "Immediately or right away; used to express promptness or quickness." :short "Immediately or right away; used to express promptness or quickness." :long "The すぐに～ grammar point is used to express an action happening immediately or right away. It often indicates promptness or quickness and can be used with both verbs and adjectives." :formation "すぐに + Verb/Adjective" :examples [{:ja "友達が来たら、すぐに電話をかけてください。" :en "When my friend arrives, please call me right away."} {:ja "そのニュースを聞いたら、すぐに会社に行きました。" :en "Upon hearing that news, I went to the company immediately."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-082" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "ぜんぜん～" :reading "zenzen～" :meaning "Used to express 'not at all' or 'completely' in negative sentences." :short "Used to express 'not at all' or 'completely' in negative sentences." :long "The ぜんぜん grammar point is used to emphasize a negative statement and can be translated as 'not at all' or 'completely (in negative sense)'. It is commonly used with negative verbs or negative forms of adjectives, placed before the negative part." :formation "ぜんぜん + [Negative verb/adjective]" :examples [{:ja "彼はぜんぜん話さない。" :en "He doesn't talk at all."} {:ja "この部屋はぜんぜん暗くない。" :en "This room is not dark at all."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-083" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "そして、～" :reading "soshite、～" :meaning "Used to connect two sentences; 'and then', 'and'." :short "Used to connect two sentences; 'and then', 'and'." :long "The そして grammar point is used to connect two sentences or ideas, expressing a sequence of events or actions. It is commonly translated to 'and then' or simply 'and' in English." :formation "Sentence 1 + そして + Sentence 2" :examples [{:ja "朝起きて、そして宿題をしました。" :en "I woke up in the morning and then did my homework."} {:ja "コーヒーを飲んで、そして新聞を読みました。" :en "I drank coffee and then read the newspaper."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-084" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "そちら～" :reading "sochira～" :meaning "Indicates a direction or person in moderate distance; 'that way', 'that person'." :short "Indicates a direction or person in moderate distance; 'that way', 'that person'." :long "The そちら grammar point is used to refer to a direction or a person that is moderate distance from both the speaker and listener, often closer to the listener. It can be translated as 'that way', 'that person', or 'that side'. This is distinct from あちら, used for things far from both speaker and listener." :formation "そちら [alone] / そちら + の + Noun" :examples [{:ja "そちらのレストランはどうですか？" :en "How is that restaurant over there?"} {:ja "そちらの方、お名前を教えていただけますか？" :en "Could you tell me the name of that person over there?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-085" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "それから、～" :reading "sorekara、～" :meaning "Meaning 'after that', 'and then' or 'then', indicating sequence of events." :short "Meaning 'after that', 'and then' or 'then', indicating sequence of events." :long "The それから grammar point is used to describe the sequence of events or actions, indicating that one action or event takes place after another. It can be translated as 'after that', 'and then', or 'then' in English. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, and nouns but should be placed between two clauses." :formation "Clause 1 + それから + Clause 2" :examples [{:ja "コーヒーを飲んで、それから家を出ました。" :en "I drank coffee, and then I left the house."} {:ja "買い物をして、それから映画を見に行きました。" :en "I went shopping, and then I went to watch a movie."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-086" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "だいたい〜" :reading "daitai〜" :meaning "Expresses 'roughly', 'approximately', or 'almost'." :short "Expresses 'roughly', 'approximately', or 'almost'." :long "The だいたい〜 (daitai〜) grammar point is used to express a rough estimate or generalization, meaning 'roughly', 'approximately', or 'almost' in English. It can be used before numbers, amounts, or percentages to show that the value is not exact." :formation "だいたい + Number/Amount/Percentage" :examples [{:ja "だいたい１００人がパーティーに参加しました。" :en "Approximately 100 people attended the party."} {:ja "彼女はだいたい２時間でその仕事を終えます。" :en "She finishes that work in roughly two hours."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-087" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "たいてい～" :reading "taitei～" :meaning "Used for expressing 'usually', 'mostly', or 'generally'." :short "Used for expressing 'usually', 'mostly', or 'generally'." :long "The たいてい grammar point is used to describe a situation that occurs frequently or under normal circumstances. It can be translated as 'usually', 'mostly', or 'generally' in English. It is often used with verbs, but can also be used with adjectives and nouns." :formation "たいてい + Verb/い-Adjective/な-Adjective/Noun" :examples [{:ja "たいてい朝８時に起きます。" :en "I usually wake up at 8 o'clock in the morning."} {:ja "彼はたいてい遅刻します。" :en "He is usually late."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-088" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "だから、～" :reading "dakara、～" :meaning "Expresses a reason or cause; 'because', 'so', 'therefore'." :short "Expresses a reason or cause; 'because', 'so', 'therefore'." :long "The だから grammar point is used to connect two sentences and express a reason or cause for the second sentence. It is typically used with nouns or な-adjectives, and can be translated as 'because', 'so', or 'therefore' in English. For verbs/い-adjectives, the form is usually ～から (not だから)." :formation "Noun + だから / な-Adjective + だから" :examples [{:ja "雨だから、傘を持って行きましょう。" :en "Because it's raining, let's bring an umbrella."} {:ja "このケーキは有名だから、みんなが食べたいです。" :en "Because this cake is famous, everyone wants to eat it."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-089" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～たり、～たり します" :reading "〜tari, 〜tari shimasu" :meaning "Used to list multiple actions or states; 'do things like', 'and'." :short "Used to list multiple actions or states; 'do things like', 'and'." :long "The ～たり、～たり します grammar point is used to list multiple actions or states without fully enumerating them. It can imply that there are more items in the list and can be translated as 'do things like', 'and', 'among other things', or 'and so on' in English. It is formed by attaching たり to the casual past tense of the verb, followed by します." :formation "Verb-casual-past + たり + Verb-casual-past + たり + します" :examples [{:ja "休日には、映画を見たり、友達と遊んだりします。" :en "On holidays, I do things like watch movies and hang out with friends."} {:ja "彼は歌ったり、踊ったり、ピアノを弾いたりします。" :en "He sings, dances, plays the piano, among other things."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-090" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "だれ～" :reading "dare～" :meaning "Asking 'who' in a sentence, used to inquire about someone's identity or to clarify who is being referred to." :short "Asking 'who' in a sentence, used to inquire about someone's identity or to clarify who is being referred to." :long "The だれ〜 grammar point is used to ask about the identity of someone in a sentence. It can be translated as 'who' or 'whom' in English. It is often used with the particle が, は, or を, but can also be combined with other particles depending on the context." :formation "だれ + appropriate particle (が, は or を)" :examples [{:ja "だれがその本を買いましたか。" :en "Who bought that book?"} {:ja "だれを待っていますか。" :en "Whom are you waiting for?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-091" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "だれか〜" :reading "dareka〜" :meaning "Indicates 'someone' or something done or possessed by 'someone'." :short "Indicates 'someone' or something done or possessed by 'someone'." :long "The grammar point だれか〜 is used to suggest that something is done by, possessed by, or related to 'someone', usually when the actual person is unidentified or not important. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, or nouns." :formation "だれか + Verb, だれか + Adjective, だれか + Noun" :examples [{:ja "だれかがドアをノックしました。" :en "Someone knocked on the door."} {:ja "だれかが私の傘を盗みました。" :en "Someone stole my umbrella."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-092" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "だれでも～" :reading "dare demo～" :meaning "Used to express 'anyone' or 'everyone'; 'no matter who'." :short "Used to express 'anyone' or 'everyone'; 'no matter who'." :long "The grammar point だれでも is used to emphasize that something applies to everyone or anyone, regardless of who they are. It can be translated as 'anyone', 'everyone', or 'no matter who' in English. It is used in sentences to show that a certain situation, characteristic, or condition applies to all people." :formation "だれでも + Verb/Adjective/Noun" :examples [{:ja "だれでもこのゲームができます。" :en "Anyone can play this game."} {:ja "だれでも疲れることがあります。" :en "Everyone gets tired sometimes."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-093" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "だれも～ないです" :reading "dare mo ~ nai desu" :meaning "Express 'nobody' or 'no one' in a negative sentence." :short "Express 'nobody' or 'no one' in a negative sentence." :long "The grammar point だれも～ないです is used to express the idea of 'nobody' or 'no one' in a negative sentence. だれも is followed by the negative form of a verb or adjective, often using the polite ないです form." :formation "だれも + Verb-negative form (ないです), だれも + い-Adjective-negative form (ないです), だれも + な-Adjective-negative form (じゃないです)" :examples [{:ja "だれも知らないです。" :en "Nobody knows."} {:ja "だれも来ないです。" :en "Nobody is coming."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-094" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "だれも～ません" :reading "daremo ~masen" :meaning "Express 'nobody' or 'no one' in negative sentences." :short "Express 'nobody' or 'no one' in negative sentences." :long "The grammar point だれも is used in combination with a negative form of the verb, usually ません, to express the idea of 'nobody' or 'no one' doing something. It is used with questions or statements and can be translated as 'nobody' or 'no one' in English." :formation "だれも + Verb-negative (ません)" :examples [{:ja "だれも秘密を知りません。" :en "No one knows the secret."} {:ja "だれもその質問に答えませんでした。" :en "Nobody answered that question."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-095" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～どう しますか。" :reading "～dou shimasu ka." :meaning "Asking for someone's opinion or decision; 'what will you do?', 'how will you handle it?'." :short "Asking for someone's opinion or decision; 'what will you do?', 'how will you handle it?'." :long "The ～どうしますか grammar point is used to ask for someone's opinion, decision, or course of action in a specific situation. It can be translated as 'what will you do?' or 'how will you handle it?' in English. This grammar point is formed by attaching どうしますか to the end of a sentence." :formation "Situation + どうしますか" :examples [{:ja "雨が降っているので、どうしますか？" :en "It's raining, so what will you do?"} {:ja "この問題について、どうしますか？" :en "What are you going to do about this issue?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-096" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～どうですか。" :reading "〜dou desu ka." :meaning "Asking for opinions or suggestions; 'how about', 'what do you think of'." :short "Asking for opinions or suggestions; 'how about', 'what do you think of'." :long "The ～どうですか grammar point is used to ask for someone's opinion or suggestion on something. It can be translated as 'how about' or 'what do you think of' in English. This expression is neither casual nor super-formal, and can be used in various situations." :formation "Noun + は/が + どうですか / Verb-casual + の + どうですか / い-Adjective + どうですか / な-Adjective + な + どうですか" :examples [{:ja "この映画はどうですか？" :en "How about this movie? / What do you think of this movie?"} {:ja "晩ご飯に寿司を食べるのはどうですか？" :en "How about eating sushi for dinner?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-097" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どうやって～" :reading "douyatte～" :meaning "Ask 'how' something is done or how to do something." :short "Ask 'how' something is done or how to do something." :long "The grammar point どうやって is used to ask how something is done or how to do something, and is translated as 'how' in English. It is often used in combination with verbs to inquire about the method or process someone goes through to complete a task or action." :formation "どうやって + Verb-casual" :examples [{:ja "どうやってお寿司を作りますか？" :en "How do you make sushi?"} {:ja "どうやって日本語を勉強しましたか？" :en "How did you study Japanese?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-098" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～どう 言いますか。" :reading "〜dou iimasu ka." :meaning "Asking for the way to say something; 'how do you say'." :short "Asking for the way to say something; 'how do you say'." :long "The ～どう言いますか grammar point is used to ask someone how to express something in a particular language or the appropriate way to say it. It can be translated as 'how do you say' in English." :formation "Word/Phrase + は [language]でどう言いますか / Word/Phrase + をどう言いますか" :examples [{:ja "「ありがとう」は英語でどう言いますか。" :en "How do you say 'thank you' in English?"} {:ja "この言葉を日本語でどう言いますか。" :en "How do you say this word in Japanese?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-099" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～とき" :reading "〜toki" :meaning "Expresses the time when something happens, 'when', 'at the time'." :short "Expresses the time when something happens, 'when', 'at the time'." :long "The ～とき grammar point is used to express the time when something happens or happened. It can be translated as 'when' or 'at the time' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + とき / い-Adjective + とき / な-Adjective + なとき / Noun + のとき" :examples [{:ja "公園で遊ぶとき、帽子をかぶりましょう。" :en "When playing in the park, let's wear a hat."} {:ja "このりんごが赤いとき、食べごろです。" :en "This apple is ready to eat when it is red."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-100" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "ときどき～" :reading "tokidoki～" :meaning "Used to indicate the frequency of an event; 'sometimes', 'occasionally', 'now and then'." :short "Used to indicate the frequency of an event; 'sometimes', 'occasionally', 'now and then'." :long "The grammar point ときどき is an adverb used to describe an event or action that occurs occasionally or at irregular intervals. It can be translated as 'sometimes', 'occasionally', or 'now and then' in English. It is often used to indicate that the speaker cannot predict the exact time when a certain event occurs." :formation "ときどき + Verb/Adjective/Noun" :examples [{:ja "彼はときどきケーキを作ります。" :en "He sometimes makes cakes."} {:ja "ときどき雨が降るので、傘を持って行ったほうがいいです。" :en "It occasionally rains, so it's better to bring an umbrella."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-101" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どこ～" :reading "doko～" :meaning "Express 'where', used with question words for asking about locations." :short "Express 'where', used with question words for asking about locations." :long "The どこ～ grammar point is used to express 'where', primarily for asking about locations or places. It is often combined with question words such as どこへ (doko e), どこで (doko de), and どこに (doko ni) to ask about destinations, locations of actions, and existence, respectively." :formation "どこ + question particle" :examples [{:ja "駅はどこですか？" :en "Where is the station?"} {:ja "どこで映画を見ましたか？" :en "Where did you watch the movie?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-102" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どこか～" :reading "dokoka～" :meaning "Expresses 'somewhere', used when the specific location is unknown or uncertain." :short "Expresses 'somewhere', used when the specific location is unknown or uncertain." :long "The grammar point どこか is used when the speaker wants to express that something is happening or located 'somewhere', but the exact location is either unknown or uncertain. It can be used by itself or combined with other words." :formation "どこか + (Particle, if needed) + Verb/Noun/Adjective" :examples [{:ja "どこかで財布をなくしました。" :en "I lost my wallet somewhere."} {:ja "今日はどこかに行きたいです。" :en "I want to go somewhere today."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-103" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どこでも～" :reading "dokodemo～" :meaning "Express 'anywhere' or 'everywhere' in Japanese." :short "Express 'anywhere' or 'everywhere' in Japanese." :long "The grammar point どこでも is used to express 'anywhere' or 'everywhere' in Japanese. It is usually used in sentences to indicate that an action or situation could happen or exist in any location." :formation "Simply use the word どこでも in the sentence as an adverb to indicate 'anywhere' or 'everywhere'." :examples [{:ja "どこでも好きな場所で勉強できます。" :en "You can study anywhere you like."} {:ja "このノートパソコンは便利なので、どこでもインターネットが使えます。" :en "This laptop is convenient, so you can use the internet anywhere."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-104" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どこにも + Verb + ないです" :reading "doko ni mo + Verb + nai desu" :meaning "Express that an action cannot be performed anywhere or something cannot be found anywhere." :short "Express that an action cannot be performed anywhere or something cannot be found anywhere." :long "The grammar point どこにも + Verb + ないです is used to express that an action cannot be performed anywhere or something cannot be found anywhere. It is used with a negative verb conjugation (ない-form) and highlights the unavailability or impossibility of finding or doing something in any location." :formation "どこにも + Verb in ない-form + です" :examples [{:ja "どこにも傘が売ってないです。" :en "Umbrellas are not sold anywhere."} {:ja "彼はどこにも見つけられないです。" :en "He cannot be found anywhere."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-105" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どこにも + Verb + ません" :reading "doko ni mo + Verb + masen" :meaning "Express 'not anywhere' or 'nowhere' with a negative verb." :short "Express 'not anywhere' or 'nowhere' with a negative verb." :long "The どこにも～ません grammar point is used to express that something doesn't exist, appear, or happen anywhere. It means 'not anywhere' or 'nowhere' when used with a negative verb. The formation is quite simple as you just need to add どこにも before the negative verb." :formation "どこにも + Verb-negative" :examples [{:ja "どこにもあの猫がいません。" :en "The cat is not anywhere."} {:ja "どこにもお金を置いていません。" :en "I didn't put the money anywhere."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-106" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どこへも Verb ないです" :reading "doko e mo + Verb + nai desu" :meaning "Express that an action doesn't take place anywhere; 'nowhere', 'not anywhere'." :short "Express that an action doesn't take place anywhere; 'nowhere', 'not anywhere'." :long "The どこへも + Verb ないです grammar point is used to express a situation where an action doesn't occur or isn't applicable anywhere. It emphasizes a strong sense of not performing the action anywhere. Keep in mind that only negative verbs can follow 'どこへも' in this construction." :formation "どこへも + Verb in negative form (ないです)" :examples [{:ja "今週末どこへも行かないです。" :en "I'm not going anywhere this weekend."} {:ja "彼女はどこへも買い物に行きませんでした。" :en "She didn't go shopping anywhere."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-107" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どこへも Verb ません" :reading "doko e mo + Verb + masen" :meaning "Nowhere; Not to any place" :short "Nowhere; Not to any place" :long "The grammar point どこへも is used to express the idea of 'nowhere' or 'not to any place'. It is combined with the negative form of a verb to indicate that the action does not happen to any place or destination." :formation "どこへも + Verb-negative" :examples [{:ja "今日はどこへも行きません。" :en "I won't go anywhere today."} {:ja "彼はどこへも走りませんでした。" :en "He didn't run anywhere."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-108" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どこも Verb ないです" :reading "dokomo + Verb + naidesu" :meaning "Express that something cannot be found anywhere or in any place; 'nowhere', 'in no place'." :short "Express that something cannot be found anywhere or in any place; 'nowhere', 'in no place'." :long "The どこも～ないです grammar point is used to express that something cannot be found anywhere or in any place. It can be translated as 'nowhere' or 'in no place' in English. It is used in combination with a negative verb (ないです) to indicate the absence of something." :formation "どこも + Verb-negative" :examples [{:ja "どこもうちわがないです。" :en "I can't find fans anywhere."} {:ja "この町ではどこも美味しいラーメンがないです。" :en "There are no delicious ramen shops anywhere in this town."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-109" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どこも Verb ません" :reading "dokomo + Verb + masen" :meaning "Express that 'nowhere' meets the verb action; 'nowhere does/can'." :short "Express that 'nowhere' meets the verb action; 'nowhere does/can'." :long "The どこも + Verb ません grammar point is used to express that nowhere does or can fulfill the verb action. It is a negative statement and can be translated as 'nowhere (+ verb)' in English. Note that the first example below uses an adjective rather than a verb, which still conveys 'nowhere is ...'." :formation "どこも + Verb-ます form + ません" :examples [{:ja "どこも静かじゃありません。" :en "Nowhere is quiet."} {:ja "どこもその本を売っていません。" :en "Nowhere sells that book."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-110" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どちら～" :reading "dochira～" :meaning "Used to ask about two choices or options." :short "Used to ask about two choices or options." :long "The どちら grammar point is used to ask about two choices or options. It translates to 'which of the two' or simply 'which' in English. It is commonly used with question words like どちらが or どちらを and can be combined with verbs, adjectives, and nouns to ask about preferences or to make comparisons." :formation "どちら + が/を + verb, どちら + が/を + adjective, どちら + が/を + noun" :examples [{:ja "この二つのリンゴ、どちらが美味しいですか？" :en "Which of these two apples is delicious?"} {:ja "どちらのドレスを着たいですか？" :en "Which dress would you like to wear?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-111" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どなた～" :reading "donata～" :meaning "A polite way to ask 'who', used for asking about someone's identity or when seeking information about an unknown person." :short "A polite way to ask 'who', used for asking about someone's identity or when seeking information about an unknown person." :long "The word どなた is a polite version of the question word だれ (who). It is used when asking about someone's identity or when seeking information about an unknown person in a polite or formal context. It can be used in questions and sometimes with も to express 'no matter who'." :formation "どなた" :examples [{:ja "すみません、どなたが山田さんですか？" :en "Excuse me, who is Mr. Yamada?"} {:ja "どなたも知っている有名な俳優です。" :en "He is a famous actor whom everyone knows."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-112" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どの Noun" :reading "dono Noun" :meaning "Expresses 'which'; used to ask about specific items within a group." :short "Expresses 'which'; used to ask about specific items within a group." :long "The どの Noun grammar point is used to ask about specific items or objects within a group. It is the combination of the question word ど (which) and the particle の to connect to a noun. In English, it can be translated as 'which' depending on the context." :formation "どの + Noun" :examples [{:ja "どの本が一番面白いですか。" :en "Which book is the most interesting?"} {:ja "どの服を着たいですか。" :en "Which clothes do you want to wear?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-113" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どれでも～" :reading "dore demo～" :meaning "Indicates 'any' or 'whichever' when referring to multiple items." :short "Indicates 'any' or 'whichever' when referring to multiple items." :long "The どれでも grammar point is used to express that any option or element among multiple choices is acceptable. It can be translated as 'any' or 'whichever' in English. It is often used in questions or suggestions and is commonly seen with でも, but it can also be used with だれでも, いつでも, どこでも, etc." :formation "どれ + でも" :examples [{:ja "どれでもいいですか？" :en "Is any choice okay?"} {:ja "どれでも選んでください。" :en "Please choose whichever you like."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-114" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "どんな Noun" :reading "donna" :meaning "Used to ask or describe 'what kind' or 'what type' of something." :short "Used to ask or describe 'what kind' or 'what type' of something." :long "The どんな (donna) grammar point is used to ask or describe 'what kind' or 'what type' of something. It is usually followed by a noun. It is similar to the English expression 'What kind of' or 'What type of' when asking about a specific characteristic or attribute." :formation "どんな + Noun" :examples [{:ja "どんな音楽が好きですか？" :en "What kind of music do you like?"} {:ja "彼女はどんな映画が好きだと思う？" :en "What kind of movies do you think she likes?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-115" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "なに～" :reading "nani~" :meaning "Asking about 'what' or 'which' in Japanese" :short "Asking about 'what' or 'which' in Japanese" :long "The なに～ grammar point is used to ask questions about 'what' or 'which' in Japanese. It can be combined with various parts of speech to create different questions. The formation differs depending on whether it's used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb + なに, い-Adjective + なに, な-Adjective + なに, Noun + なに" :examples [{:ja "これはなにですか。" :en "What is this?"} {:ja "あの人はなにをしているのですか？" :en "What is that person doing?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-116" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "なにか～" :reading "nanika～" :meaning "Indicates 'something' or 'some kind of'." :short "Indicates 'something' or 'some kind of'." :long "The なにか grammar point is used to express the idea of 'something' or 'some kind of' in Japanese. It is often used when the speaker is unsure or not specific about the details of the noun being referred to. なにか is used before the noun it modifies." :formation "なにか + Noun" :examples [{:ja "なにか飲み物を持ってきてください。" :en "Please bring some kind of drink."} {:ja "なにか質問があれば、遠慮なく聞いてください。" :en "If you have any questions, please feel free to ask."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-117" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "なにも～ないです" :reading "nani mo~nai desu" :meaning "Used to express 'not any' or 'nothing' in a negative sentence." :short "Used to express 'not any' or 'nothing' in a negative sentence." :long "The なにも～ないです grammar point is used in negative sentences to express the idea of 'not any', 'not at all', 'nothing', or 'none'. It is commonly used with verbs in the negative form." :formation "なにも + Verb-negative" :examples [{:ja "彼はなにも言わなかったです。" :en "He didn't say anything."} {:ja "私はなにも食べないです。" :en "I don't eat anything."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-118" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "なにも～ません" :reading "nanimo~masen" :meaning "Used to express 'not anything', 'nothing'." :short "Used to express 'not anything', 'nothing'." :long "The grammar point なにも～ません is used to express the idea of 'not anything' or 'nothing' in negative sentences. It is placed before the verb in its negative form (～ません)." :formation "なにも + Verb-ます form + ません" :examples [{:ja "昨日はなにも食べませんでした。" :en "I didn't eat anything yesterday."} {:ja "彼はなにも話しません。" :en "He doesn't say anything."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-119" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "なん～" :reading "nan~" :meaning "Used to form interrogative questions and emphasize the question word for 'what', 'how'." :short "Used to form interrogative questions and emphasize the question word for 'what', 'how'." :long "The なん～ grammar point is used to form interrogative questions and emphasize the question word for 'what' or 'how'. It is an abbreviation of '何' meaning 'what'. Its usage can vary depending on whether it is used with a counter, verb, or adjective." :formation "なん + Counter / なん + Verb / なん + Adjective" :examples [{:ja "あなたはなん歳ですか？" :en "How old are you?"} {:ja "これはなんですか？" :en "What is this?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-120" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "なんで～" :reading "nande～" :meaning "Asking the reason or cause; 'why', 'how come'." :short "Asking the reason or cause; 'why', 'how come'." :long "The なんで grammar point is used to ask the reason or cause behind something. It can be translated as 'why' or 'how come' in English. It is a casual way of asking for an explanation, and it can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "なんで + [phrase or sentence]" :examples [{:ja "なんで遅れたの？" :en "Why were you late?"} {:ja "なんでこの部屋はこんなに汚いの？" :en "Why is this room so dirty?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-121" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "なんでも～" :reading "nandemo～" :meaning "Means 'any', 'anything', 'anyone'." :short "Means 'any', 'anything', 'anyone'." :long "The なんでも grammar point is used to express the concept of 'any', 'anything', or 'anyone' in Japanese. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, and nouns. It is often used in the meaning of 'no matter what' or 'regardless of'." :formation "なんでも + Verb / なんでも + Adjective / なんでも + Noun" :examples [{:ja "なんでも食べられるから、何を頼んでも大丈夫です。" :en "I can eat anything, so it's okay to order whatever you want."} {:ja "なんでもよければ、この本をおすすめします。" :en "If you're okay with anything, I recommend this book."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-122" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～なんと 言いますか。" :reading "〜nan to iimasu ka." :meaning "Asking how something is called or referred to; 'how do you say'" :short "Asking how something is called or referred to; 'how do you say'" :long "The ～なんと言いますか grammar point is used to ask how something is called, expressed, or referred to in a polite manner. It can be translated as 'how do you say' or 'what do you call' in English. The formation is used with a noun or a topic where you would like to know the proper expression or name for something." :formation "Noun + は/が + なんと言いますか" :examples [{:ja "この花はなんと言いますか。" :en "How do you say this flower?"} {:ja "この料理はなんと言いますか。" :en "What do you call this dish?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-123" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～に あげます" :reading "〜 ni agemasu" :meaning "Used to express giving something to someone; 'give', 'offer'." :short "Used to express giving something to someone; 'give', 'offer'." :long "The ～に あげます grammar point is used when the speaker offers or gives something to someone. It emphasizes that the action is performed for the benefit of the receiver. The formation involves using the particle 'に' after the person receiving the item, followed by the verb あげます." :formation "Receiver + に + Object + を + あげます" :examples [{:ja "私は友達に本をあげました。" :en "I gave a book to my friend."} {:ja "彼にプレゼントをあげましょう。" :en "Let's give him a present."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-124" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～に もらいます" :reading "〜ni moraimasu" :meaning "To receive something or some action from someone; 'to get', 'to receive'." :short "To receive something or some action from someone; 'to get', 'to receive'." :long "The ～に もらいます grammar point is used to express receiving something or some action from someone. It can be translated as 'to get' or 'to receive' in English. The formation involves placing に after the giver and もらいます to show the act of receiving." :formation "Giver + に + Verb-te form + もらいます / Giver + に + Object + を + もらいます" :examples [{:ja "友達にプレゼントをもらいました。" :en "I received a gift from my friend."} {:ja "先生にアドバイスをもらいました。" :en "I got advice from my teacher."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-125" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～の" :reading "〜no" :meaning "Express possession, apposition, or description using 'of' or ''s'." :short "Express possession, apposition, or description using 'of' or ''s'." :long "The ～の grammar point is used to express possession, apposition, or description. It functions like 'of' or an apostrophe + 's' (’s) in English. It can be used with nouns, adjectives, and verbs to show relationships between words." :formation "Noun1 + の + Noun2, (Adjective/Clause) + の (nominalized), etc." :examples [{:ja "これは私の鞄です。" :en "This is my bag."} {:ja "彼女は赤いバッグを持っています。" :en "She has a red bag."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-126" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～はたいへんです" :reading "〜wa taihen desu" :meaning "Express that something is difficult or tough." :short "Express that something is difficult or tough." :long "The ～はたいへんです grammar point is used to express that something is difficult or tough to do, handle, or face. It can be translated as 'is difficult' or 'is tough' in English. The formation is used with a noun followed by はたいへんです." :formation "Noun + はたいへんです" :examples [{:ja "漢字を覚えるのはたいへんです。" :en "It is difficult to remember kanji."} {:ja "この仕事はたいへんです。" :en "This job is tough."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-127" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "ほとんど〜" :reading "hotondo〜" :meaning "Used to express 'almost', 'nearly', or 'most'." :short "Used to express 'almost', 'nearly', or 'most'." :long "The ほとんど〜 grammar point is used to express that something is 'almost', 'nearly', or 'most'. It can appear in both negative contexts (hardly/barely) or positive contexts (almost all). It is typically followed by a verb or used with a noun to describe the quantity or extent of something." :formation "ほとんど + Verb / ほとんど + Noun" :examples [{:ja "彼女はほとんど英語を話せません。" :en "She can hardly speak English."} {:ja "ほとんどの人がパーティーに出席しました。" :en "Most people attended the party."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-128" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "まあまあ～" :reading "maa maa～" :meaning "Expresses something as 'so-so', 'not bad', or 'average'." :short "Expresses something as 'so-so', 'not bad', or 'average'." :long "The expression まあまあ is used to describe something that is neither particularly good nor bad, but rather average or 'so-so'. It is a neutral term and can be used in various contexts when discussing quality, feelings, or situations." :formation "Used as an adverb or short expression with no special grammar structure." :examples [{:ja "彼の料理の腕前はまあまあです。" :en "His cooking skills are so-so."} {:ja "今日の天気はまあまあですね。" :en "The weather today is not bad, huh?"}]} {:id "grammar-n5-129" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "まだ〜" :reading "mada〜" :meaning "Indicates something has not yet happened or is still happening; 'still', 'not yet'." :short "Indicates something has not yet happened or is still happening; 'still', 'not yet'." :long "The まだ〜 grammar point is used to express that something has not yet occurred or is still in progress. It can be translated as 'still' or 'not yet' in English. Depending on the context or if expressing the negative, まだ can be used with different combinations of verb, adjective, or noun." :formation "まだ + Verb (non-past / negative / te-form), まだ + い-adjective, まだ + な-adjective + じゃない/ではない, まだ + Noun + じゃない/ではない" :examples [{:ja "昼ご飯はまだ食べていません。" :en "I haven't eaten lunch yet."} {:ja "山田さんはまだ来ていません。" :en "Yamada-san has not arrived yet."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-130" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "まだ〜ないです" :reading "mada 〜 nai desu" :meaning "Expresses that something has not happened or been done yet." :short "Expresses that something has not happened or been done yet." :long "The まだ〜ないです grammar point is used to indicate that a certain action or event has not yet happened or been completed. It is used with the negative form of a verb (casual negative + です) and can be translated as 'not yet', 'still', or 'haven't' in English." :formation "まだ + Verb-negative + です" :examples [{:ja "まだ宿題をしていないです。" :en "I haven't done my homework yet."} {:ja "彼はまだ来ないです。" :en "He hasn't come yet."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-131" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "まだ～ません" :reading "mada ~masen" :meaning "Expresses that something has not happened or been done yet (polite form)." :short "Expresses that something has not happened or been done yet (polite form)." :long "The まだ～ません grammar point is used to express that something has not happened or been done yet in a polite register. It is used with the negative ます-form of a verb (～ません). まだ is placed before the verb phrase." :formation "まだ + Verb-ます form (negative)" :examples [{:ja "昼ご飯をまだ食べていません。" :en "I have not eaten lunch yet."} {:ja "宿題がまだ終わっていません。" :en "I have not finished my homework yet."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-132" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "もう～" :reading "mou～" :meaning "Expresses 'already', 'anymore', or 'soon'." :short "Expresses 'already', 'anymore', or 'soon'." :long "The もう grammar point is used to express the idea of 'already', 'anymore', or 'soon' in English. It indicates that something has already happened, won't happen anymore, or will happen soon. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, or nouns depending on context." :formation "もう + Verb / もう + Adjective / もう + Noun" :examples [{:ja "もう昼ごはんを食べました。" :en "I already ate lunch."} {:ja "もうここでは働かない。" :en "I won't work here anymore."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-133" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "もうすぐ〜" :reading "mou sugu~" :meaning "Express an action or event happening soon: 'soon', 'in a short while', 'almost'." :short "Express an action or event happening soon: 'soon', 'in a short while', 'almost'." :long "The もうすぐ grammar point is used to express that an action or event is going to happen soon or is almost happening. It can be translated as 'soon', 'in a short while', or 'almost' in English. もうすぐ is usually followed by a verb or a noun indicating an event." :formation "もうすぐ + Verb (~ます form or 〜る form)" :examples [{:ja "もうすぐ春が来ます。" :en "Spring is coming soon."} {:ja "もうすぐ彼女の誕生日です。" :en "Her birthday is coming up soon."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-134" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "もっと〜" :reading "motto〜" :meaning "Expresses 'more' or 'even more' when describing a quantity, frequency, or degree." :short "Expresses 'more' or 'even more' when describing a quantity, frequency, or degree." :long "The もっと〜 grammar point is used to emphasize the desire or need for something to be 'more' or 'even more' in terms of quantity, frequency, or degree. It is often translated as 'more' or 'even more' in English. もっと can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, or な-adjectives." :formation "もっと + Verb / い-Adjective / な-Adjective" :examples [{:ja "もっと勉強しなければなりません。" :en "I have to study more."} {:ja "このかばんをもっと大きいサイズに変えたいです。" :en "I want to change this bag to a larger size."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-135" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "よく～" :reading "yoku ~" :meaning "Often used to express 'doing something well' or 'often'." :short "Often used to express 'doing something well' or 'often'." :long "The よく～ grammar point is used to express that someone does something well, frequently, or often. It is often placed before the verb and can also mean 'well done' or 'good job' when praising someone's action." :formation "よく + Verb-casual" :examples [{:ja "彼はよく勉強します。" :en "He studies often."} {:ja "彼女はよく料理を作ります。" :en "She often cooks."}]} {:id "grammar-n5-136" :track :grammar :level "N5" :display "～（場所）に～があります" :reading "〜basho ni 〜 ga arimasu" :meaning "Express the existence or presence of something in a place." :short "Express the existence or presence of something in a place." :long "The ～（場所）に～があります grammar point is used to express the existence or presence of something in a specific location. In this structure, the place is followed by 'に', then the object, plus が, and finally あります (to exist / there is)." :formation "Place + に + Object/Thing + が + あります" :examples [{:ja "図書館にたくさんの本があります。" :en "There are many books in the library."} {:ja "公園に池があります。" :en "There is a pond in the park."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-001" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "A とか B とか" :reading "" :meaning "Used to express multiple examples or possibilities; 'such as', 'like', 'for example'." :short "Used to express multiple examples or possibilities; 'such as', 'like', 'for example'." :long "The とか grammar point is used to list multiple examples or possibilities. It can be translated as 'such as', 'like', or 'for example' in English. とか is used between the listed items and expresses that the list is not exhaustive." :formation "Noun + とか + Noun + とか" :examples [{:ja "週末は映画とか買い物とか楽しいことをしたいです。" :en "I want to do fun things like watching movies and shopping on the weekend."} {:ja "彼女は英語とかフランス語とかいろいろな言葉を話せます。" :en "She can speak various languages, like English and French."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-002" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "A は B ほど～ありません" :reading "A wa B hodo ～ arimasen" :meaning "Express that A is not as much as B; 'not as... as', 'not... to the extent of'." :short "Express that A is not as much as B; 'not as... as', 'not... to the extent of'." :long "The は B ほど～ありません grammar point is used to express that A is not as much as B or not to the same extent as B, emphasizing the difference. It can be translated as 'not as... as' or 'not... to the extent of' in English." :formation "A は B ほど Verb/adjective ありません" :examples [{:ja "今日は昨日ほど寒くありません。" :en "Today is not as cold as yesterday."} {:ja "彼は私ほど上手に英語を話せません。" :en "He cannot speak English as well as I can."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-003" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "A は B ほど～ない" :reading "A wa B hodo ~ nai" :meaning "Expressing that A is not as ~ as B; 'not as...as'" :short "Expressing that A is not as ~ as B; 'not as...as'" :long "The A は B ほど～ない grammar point is used to express that A is not as ~ as B in some aspect. It can be translated as 'not as...as' in English. This structure is commonly used with い-adjectives (without い) and な-adjective. For verbs, you can use the negative potential form." :formation "A は B ほど + い-Adjective stem (without い) + くない, な-Adjective + じゃない, Verb-negative potential form" :examples [{:ja "山田さんは鈴木さんほど背が高くない。" :en "Yamada is not as tall as Suzuki."} {:ja "このリンゴはあのリンゴほど甘くない。" :en "This apple is not as sweet as that apple."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-004" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "A より B のほうが〜" :reading "A yori B no hou ga 〜" :meaning "Comparing two things; 'B is more ~ than A'." :short "Comparing two things; 'B is more ~ than A'." :long "The より and のほうが grammar pattern is used to compare two things by means of an adjective or verb. In this structure, item A is compared to item B, suggesting that item B is more ~ than item A." :formation "A + より + B + のほうが + Adjective/Verb" :examples [{:ja "田中さんより鈴木さんのほうが背が高いです。" :en "Suzuki is taller than Tanaka."} {:ja "リンゴよりバナナのほうが甘いです。" :en "Bananas are sweeter than apples."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-005" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "い-Adjective く する/なる" :reading "i-Adjective kusuru/naru" :meaning "Express a change happening or making something change." :short "Express a change happening or making something change." :long "The い-Adjective く する/なる grammar point is used to indicate a change happening (naru) or intentionally making something change (suru). When using this grammatical structure, the い-adjective is changed to its く form, and then is followed by either する (to make the change) or なる (the change happens)." :formation "い-Adjective (remove い) + く + する/なる" :examples [{:ja "部屋を明るくします。" :en "I will make the room brighter."} {:ja "彼の顔は赤くなりました。" :en "His face turned red."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-006" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "な-adjective に する/なる" :reading "" :meaning "Used to indicate change or decision; 'to become' or 'to make' with na-adjectives." :short "Used to indicate change or decision; 'to become' or 'to make' with na-adjectives." :long "The な-adjective に する/なる grammar point is used to express a change or a decision related to a na-adjective. The なる (naru) form indicates a change that occurs by itself, while the する (suru) form indicates a conscious decision made by someone." :formation "な-Adjective + に + する/なる" :examples [{:ja "部屋をきれいにする。" :en "To make the room clean."} {:ja "彼は元気になりました。" :en "He became energetic."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-007" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun しか～ない" :reading "Noun shika~nai" :meaning "Expresses 'only', 'nothing but', or 'no other'." :short "Expresses 'only', 'nothing but', or 'no other'." :long "The grammar point Noun しか～ない is used to emphasize the small quantity or exclusiveness of something. It can be translated as 'only', 'nothing but', or 'no other' in English. It is used with a negative verb to convey the meaning." :formation "Noun + しか + Negative Verb" :examples [{:ja "私はリンゴしか食べない。" :en "I eat only apples."} {:ja "電車で行くしかないです。" :en "There is no other way but to go by train."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-008" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun に する" :reading "Noun ni suru" :meaning "Used to indicate a choice or decision, 'to decide on' or 'to choose'." :short "Used to indicate a choice or decision, 'to decide on' or 'to choose'." :long "The grammar point 'Noun に する' is used to express making a choice or decision, which can be translated as 'to decide on' or 'to choose' in English. It is often used when deciding between different options or making a decision in various situations, such as choosing a menu item, deciding on a plan, or selecting a gift." :formation "Noun + に + する" :examples [{:ja "メニューからピザにしました。" :en "I decided on pizza from the menu."} {:ja "誕生日プレゼントに時計にするつもりです。" :en "I plan to choose a watch as a birthday present."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-009" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun に なる" :reading "Noun ni naru" :meaning "To become; to change into; to turn into." :short "To become; to change into; to turn into." :long "The 'Noun に なる' grammar point is used to show a change or a transformation from one state or form into another. In English, it can be translated as 'to become', 'to change into', or 'to turn into'. It is often used with nouns that indicate profession, status, or condition." :formation "Noun + に + なる" :examples [{:ja "彼は医者になりました。" :en "He became a doctor."} {:ja "子供の頃、私は先生になりたかったです。" :en "When I was a child, I wanted to become a teacher."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-010" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "のために" :reading "no tame ni" :meaning "Used to express purpose or reason; 'for', 'for the sake of', 'in order to'." :short "Used to express purpose or reason; 'for', 'for the sake of', 'in order to'." :long "The のために grammar point is used to express the purpose or reason for doing something, or for the benefit of someone or something. It can be translated as 'for', 'for the sake of', or 'in order to' in English. The formation is straightforward, simply place のために after a noun." :formation "Noun + のために" :examples [{:ja "勉強のために、図書館で静かな場所を探しています。" :en "In order to study, I am looking for a quiet place in the library."} {:ja "彼は家族のために、一生懸命働いています。" :en "He works hard for the sake of his family."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-011" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun の 間に" :reading "〜no aida ni" :meaning "Expresses 'while' or 'during' a specific period or time." :short "Expresses 'while' or 'during' a specific period or time." :long "The Noun の 間に grammar point is used to indicate a specific period or time during which an action takes place. It is often translated as 'while' or 'during' in English. This grammar point requires a noun to represent the time or period." :formation "Noun + の間に" :examples [{:ja "昼休みの間に銀行に行きます。" :en "I will go to the bank during lunch break."} {:ja "夏休みの間に海外旅行に行く予定です。" :en "I plan to go on an overseas trip during the summer vacation."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-012" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun ばかり" :reading "〜bakari" :meaning "Expresses 'only', 'nothing but', 'always', or 'just'." :short "Expresses 'only', 'nothing but', 'always', or 'just'." :long "The ばかり grammar point is used to express that something is only, nothing but, just, or always. It can be attached to nouns, verbs, or adjectives, and has various meanings based on the context." :formation "Noun + ばかり, Verb-casual + ばかり, い-Adjective + ばかり, な-Adjective + だ ばかり" :examples [{:ja "彼はお金の話ばかりする。" :en "He talks about nothing but money."} {:ja "彼女は勉強ばかりしている。" :en "She is always studying."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-013" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun を あげる" :reading "Noun wo ageru" :meaning "To give something to someone; 'give', 'offer', 'present'." :short "To give something to someone; 'give', 'offer', 'present'." :long "The を あげる grammar point is used to express giving something to someone. It can be translated as 'give', 'offer', or 'present' in English. This construction is commonly used with nouns to indicate giving or offering a tangible item, action, or favor to someone else." :formation "Noun + を + あげる" :examples [{:ja "プレゼントをあげる。" :en "I give a present."} {:ja "友達に花をあげた。" :en "I gave flowers to my friend."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-014" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Nounをいただく" :reading "Noun wo itadaku" :meaning "To receive or accept something politely; 'to receive', 'to get', 'to accept'." :short "To receive or accept something politely; 'to receive', 'to get', 'to accept'." :long "The Nounをいただく grammar point is used to express the action of receiving or accepting something in a polite or humble manner. The verb 'いただく' is a more respectful version of the verb 'もらう' (to receive), so using 'いただく' shows humility and respect towards the person who is giving you something." :formation "Noun + を + いただく" :examples [{:ja "プレゼントをいただきありがとうございます。" :en "Thank you for the gift that I received."} {:ja "先生からアドバイスをいただきました。" :en "I received advice from my teacher."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-015" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Nounをくださる" :reading "" :meaning "Used to express someone doing something as a favor or for the benefit of the speaker." :short "Used to express someone doing something as a favor or for the benefit of the speaker." :long "The grammar point 'Nounをくださる' is a polite expression used to say that someone is giving or doing something for the benefit of the speaker. It is often used when referring to people who have a higher social status or when expressing gratitude for their actions." :formation "Noun + を + くださる" :examples [{:ja "先生が資料をくださった。" :en "The teacher gave me the materials."} {:ja "お母さんがおやつをくださる。" :en "My mother gives me snacks."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-016" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun を くれる" :reading "Noun wo kureru" :meaning "Expresses that someone gives a thing to the speaker or someone close to the speaker." :short "Expresses that someone gives a thing to the speaker or someone close to the speaker." :long "The grammar point Noun を くれる is used to express when someone (other than the speaker) gives something to the speaker or someone close to the speaker. The giver is the subject, and the receiver is the speaker or someone close to the speaker. くれる indicates that the action of giving 'favors' the speaker or someone close to the speaker." :formation "Giver + が + Receiver + に + Noun + を + くれる" :examples [{:ja "友達が私にプレゼントをくれました。" :en "My friend gave me a present."} {:ja "彼が私に花をくれた。" :en "He gave me flowers."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-017" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun を さしあげる" :reading "Noun wo sashiageru" :meaning "A polite way of expressing to give something." :short "A polite way of expressing to give something." :long "The grammar point Noun を さしあげる is used to say that you are giving something to someone in a polite way. When offering something to a person of higher status, this expression is used. It is used in conjunction with a noun and replaces the regular verb あげる (ageru) to give." :formation "Noun + を + さしあげる" :examples [{:ja "先生に本をさしあげます。" :en "I will give a book to the teacher."} {:ja "お母さんに花をさしあげたいです。" :en "I want to give flowers to my mother."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-018" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun もらう" :reading "Noun wo morau" :meaning "To receive or get something from someone; 'to receive', 'to get'." :short "To receive or get something from someone; 'to receive', 'to get'." :long "The もらう (morau) grammar point is used to express the action of receiving or getting something from someone. It usually emphasizes that the action was done for you or you were the beneficiary of the action. The formation requires a noun followed by the particle を (wo) before 'もらう'." :formation "Noun + を + もらう" :examples [{:ja "友達からプレゼントをもらいました。" :en "I received a gift from my friend."} {:ja "先生にアドバイスをもらいました。" :en "I got advice from my teacher."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-019" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Noun + 中" :reading "Noun + ちゅう" :meaning "Express 'in the middle of', 'during', or 'in the process of'." :short "Express 'in the middle of', 'during', or 'in the process of'." :long "The 中 (ちゅう) grammar point is used to express that something is in the middle of, during, or in the process of happening. It is attached to a noun to convey this meaning (e.g., 勉強中, 仕事中, 会議中). It is also sometimes read as じゅう depending on the word (e.g., 授業中)." :formation "Noun + 中 (ちゅう)" :examples [{:ja "彼は仕事中です。" :en "He is in the middle of work."} {:ja "試験中、携帯電話を使ってはいけません。" :en "During the exam, you must not use your mobile phone."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-020" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb させられる" :reading "Verb-saserareru" :meaning "Express the passive causative form; 'to be made to', 'to be forced to'." :short "Express the passive causative form; 'to be made to', 'to be forced to'." :long "The Verb させられる grammar point is the passive causative form of the verb. It is used to describe a situation where someone or something causes or forces another person to do something. This construction of passive causative is useful to show that the action was not done voluntarily by the person." :formation "Group 1 Verb: Replace ～う with ～わせられる, Group 2 Verb: Replace ～る with ～させられる, Group 3 Verb: する → させられる, くる → こさせられる" :examples [{:ja "子供たちは先生に宿題をやらせられました。" :en "The children were made to do their homework by the teacher."} {:ja "彼は会社にもっと働かせられた。" :en "He was forced to work more by the company."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-021" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb させる" :reading "Verb-saseru" :meaning "Expresses 'to make someone do something', 'to let someone do something', or 'to cause someone to do something'." :short "Expresses 'to make someone do something', 'to let someone do something', or 'to cause someone to do something'." :long "The ～させる verb form is used to describe causing or allowing someone to do something. It can be translated as 'to make someone do something', 'to let someone do something', or 'to cause someone to do something' in English. The formation of the ～させる form depends on the verb it is attached to." :formation "Group 1 Verbs: Change the last hiragana to ～せる, Group 2 Verbs: Remove ～る and add ～させる, Group 3 Verbs: する → させる, くる → こさせる" :examples [{:ja "先生が生徒に宿題をさせました。" :en "The teacher made the students do homework."} {:ja "母が私に部屋を掃除させました。" :en "My mother made me clean my room."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-022" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb た ことがある" :reading "Verb ta koto ga aru" :meaning "Expresses the experience of having done something in the past." :short "Expresses the experience of having done something in the past." :long "The ～たことがある grammar point is used to express the experience of having done an action in the past. It indicates that the speaker has had the experience of doing something at least once before. It is formed by adding ことがある to the た-form of the verb." :formation "Verb-た-form + ことがある" :examples [{:ja "日本に行ったことがあります。" :en "I have been to Japan before."} {:ja "寿司を食べたことがありますか？" :en "Have you ever eaten sushi before?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-023" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb た ときに" :reading "Verb た ときに" :meaning "Express an action or event that occurred at a specific time in the past; 'when', 'at the time'." :short "Express an action or event that occurred at a specific time in the past; 'when', 'at the time'." :long "The たときに grammar point is used to express an action that happened at a specific time in the past, usually with a focus on a specific event or moment. It can be translated as 'when' or 'at the time' in English. This grammar point requires a verb in its た-form followed by ときに to indicate the time at which the action occurred." :formation "Verb-た + ときに" :examples [{:ja "映画を見たときに、泣きました。" :en "When I watched the movie, I cried."} {:ja "彼に初めて会ったときに、遅刻していました。" :en "When I first met him, he was late."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-024" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb た ところ" :reading "Verb ta tokoro" :meaning "Expresses the situation right after an action has taken place." :short "Expresses the situation right after an action has taken place." :long "The たところ grammar point is used to describe a situation that occurs right after an action has taken place. It is often translated as 'just now', 'right after', or 'when'. The formation is relatively simple as it only requires the verb in its た form (past tense) followed by ところ." :formation "Verb (た form) + ところ" :examples [{:ja "ドアを開けたところ、猫が飛び出してきた。" :en "When I opened the door, a cat came jumping out."} {:ja "電話を切ったところ、また鳴った。" :en "Right after I hung up the phone, it rang again."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-025" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb たほうがいい" :reading "〜ta hou ga ii" :meaning "Used to give advice or make a suggestion to someone - 'it's better if...'." :short "Used to give advice or make a suggestion to someone - 'it's better if...'." :long "The たほうがいい grammar point is used to give advice or make a suggestion to someone. It can be translated as 'it's better if...', 'should', or 'it's a good idea to'. It is formed by conjugating the verb into the past tense (た-form) and adding ほうがいい." :formation "Verb (past tense) + ほうがいい" :examples [{:ja "早く寝たほうがいい。" :en "It's better if you go to bed early."} {:ja "もっと勉強したほうがいい。" :en "You should study more."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-026" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ために" :reading "tame ni" :meaning "Used to describe the purpose or reason for an action; 'for the sake of', 'in order to', 'because of'." :short "Used to describe the purpose or reason for an action; 'for the sake of', 'in order to', 'because of'." :long "The ために (tame ni) grammar point is used to express either the purpose of doing something (in which case it translates to 'for the sake of' or 'in order to') or the cause/reason for a situation (in which case it translates to 'because of'). Typically, we use the dictionary form of the verb (e.g., 合格する) for the 'in order to' meaning. For an adjective or noun, attach ために appropriately (い-Adjective + ために, な-Adjective + な + ために, Noun + の + ために)." :formation "1) Verb (dictionary form) + ために (purpose)\n2) い-Adjective + ために (reason)\n3) な-Adjective + な + ために (reason)\n4) Noun + の + ために (reason)" :examples [{:ja "試験に合格するために、毎日勉強しています。" :en "I study every day in order to pass the exam."} {:ja "健康のために、毎日運動しなければなりません。" :en "For the sake of health, I must exercise daily."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-027" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb つもり" :reading "〜tsumori" :meaning "Express the intention, plan, or determination to do something; 'I intend to', 'I plan to', 'I'm determined to'." :short "Express the intention, plan, or determination to do something; 'I intend to', 'I plan to', 'I'm determined to'." :long "The ～つもり grammar point is used to express the intention, plan, or determination to do something. It can be translated as 'I intend to', 'I plan to', or 'I'm determined to' in English. It is used with verbs in the casual form." :formation "Verb-casual + つもり" :examples [{:ja "明日、友達に会うつもりです。" :en "I plan to meet my friend tomorrow."} {:ja "今夜、晩ご飯を作るつもりです。" :en "I intend to cook dinner tonight."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-028" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb てあげる" :reading "Verb te ageru" :meaning "Used to express doing something for someone; 'do something for someone'." :short "Used to express doing something for someone; 'do something for someone'." :long "The てあげる grammar point is used to express that the speaker or someone other than the listener is doing something for someone else. It shows that the action is being done as a favor or out of kindness. This grammar point is used with verbs in their て-form, followed by あげる." :formation "Verb-て form + あげる" :examples [{:ja "友達が疲れていたから、荷物を持ってあげた。" :en "Since my friend was tired, I carried their luggage for them."} {:ja "弟は宿題がわからなかったので、手伝ってあげた。" :en "My younger brother couldn't understand the homework, so I helped him."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-029" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て ある" :reading "Verb-te aru" :meaning "Indicates that an action has been done and its result is still in effect." :short "Indicates that an action has been done and its result is still in effect." :long "The ～てある grammar point is used to express that an action has been done and its result remains in effect. It indicates that the action was intentionally performed and its effect continues. The formation is done by conjugating the verb into its て-form and then adding ある." :formation "Verb-て form + ある" :examples [{:ja "窓が開けてある。" :en "The window has been opened (and remains open)."} {:ja "宿題がもう終わってある。" :en "The homework has already been done (and remains that way)."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-030" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て いく" :reading "Verb-te iku" :meaning "Expresses an action that continues on into the future or a change that progresses over time." :short "Expresses an action that continues on into the future or a change that progresses over time." :long "The て いく grammar point is used to indicate that an action will continue or a change will progress into the future. It can be attached to the て-form of a verb and is often translated as 'to keep', 'to continue', or 'to go on' in English." :formation "Verb in て-form + いく" :examples [{:ja "このまま練習を続けていくと、上達するでしょう。" :en "If you keep practicing like this, you will improve."} {:ja "彼は成長していくうちに、自立心が強くなった。" :en "As he grew up, his independence became stronger."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-031" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ていただきたい" :reading "te itadakitai" :meaning "Express a polite request or desire that someone does something for you." :short "Express a polite request or desire that someone does something for you." :long "The ていただきたい grammar point is used to express a polite request or a desire for someone to do something for you. It is formed by attaching いただきたい to the て-form of the verb. It is more polite than the て-form + ほしい construction." :formation "Verb-て form + いただきたい" :examples [{:ja "資料をコピーしていただきたいです。" :en "I would like you to make copies of the documents."} {:ja "この仕事を手伝っていただきたいのですが。" :en "I would like to ask for your assistance with this work."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-032" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ていただく" :reading "〜te itadaku" :meaning "Used to express receiving someone's favor or help; 'to kindly do'." :short "Used to express receiving someone's favor or help; 'to kindly do'." :long "The ～ていただく grammar point is used to express that someone has kindly done a favor or provided help. It is a more polite and humble way of saying that you received someone's help. The formation is created by attaching the て-form of the verb + いただく." :formation "Verb-て form + いただく" :examples [{:ja "メールを送っていただいて、ありがとうございます。" :en "Thank you for kindly sending the email."} {:ja "荷物を運んでいただいて、助かりました。" :en "You really helped me by carrying the luggage."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-033" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て いただけませんか" :reading "Verb te itadakemasen ka" :meaning "A polite request; 'Could you please...', 'Would you mind...'" :short "A polite request; 'Could you please...', 'Would you mind...'" :long "The て いただけませんか grammar point is used to make a polite request to someone. It is a more polite form of て くれませんか, and can be translated as 'Could you please...' or 'Would you mind...' in English. To use it, change the verb to its て-form and add いただけませんか." :formation "Verb-て form + いただけませんか" :examples [{:ja "この手紙を投函していただけませんか。" :en "Could you please mail this letter for me?"} {:ja "この荷物を持っていただけませんか。" :en "Could you please carry this luggage for me?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-034" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て いる" :reading "Verb-te iru" :meaning "Expresses an ongoing action or a state resulting from a past action." :short "Expresses an ongoing action or a state resulting from a past action." :long "The ている form is used to express either an ongoing action or a state resulting from a past action. It is formed by conjugating the verb into its て-form and then adding いる. In English, this can be translated as verb-ing or 'have verb-ed'. Its usage depends on the type of verb: action verbs or state verbs." :formation "Verb (て-form) + いる" :examples [{:ja "今、本を読んでいます。" :en "I am reading a book now."} {:ja "彼は英語を勉強しています。" :en "He is studying English."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-035" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て いる ところ" :reading "Verb te iru tokoro" :meaning "Describes an action that is currently happening; 'in the middle of', 'in the process of'." :short "Describes an action that is currently happening; 'in the middle of', 'in the process of'." :long "The て いる ところ grammar point is used to express an action that is currently happening or taking place. It can be translated as 'in the middle of' or 'in the process of' in English. It is formed by using the て-form of a verb followed by いる and ところ." :formation "Verb-て form + いる ところ" :examples [{:ja "彼は宿題をしているところです。" :en "He is in the middle of doing his homework."} {:ja "私たちは映画を見ているところでした。" :en "We were in the process of watching a movie."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-036" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ている間に" :reading "te iru aida ni" :meaning "Expresses 'while' or 'during the time' something is happening." :short "Expresses 'while' or 'during the time' something is happening." :long "The ～ている間に grammar point is used to express that an action or event occurs during the time another action or event is happening. It is often translated to 'while' or 'during the time' in English. It is formed by combining the て-form of a verb with いる and adding 間に." :formation "Verb-て form + いる + 間に" :examples [{:ja "映画を見ている間に、彼は寝てしまった。" :en "While watching the movie, he fell asleep."} {:ja "子供が遊んでいる間に、私は料理を作ります。" :en "While the children are playing, I'll cook."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-037" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ておく" :reading "〜te oku" :meaning "Perform an action in preparation for a future situation; 'to do something in advance'." :short "Perform an action in preparation for a future situation; 'to do something in advance'." :long "The ～ておく grammar point is used when you want to express performing an action in preparation for a future situation or to do something in advance. It is attached to the te-form of a verb and expresses the idea of doing something beforehand, as a preparatory action." :formation "Verb-て form + おく" :examples [{:ja "駅で切符を買っておこう。" :en "Let's buy the tickets in advance at the station."} {:ja "明日早く起きるために、今晩早く寝ておきます。" :en "I will go to bed early tonight to wake up early tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-038" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て くださいませんか" :reading "Verb-te kudasaimasen ka" :meaning "Polite request for asking someone for a favor." :short "Polite request for asking someone for a favor." :long "The て くださいませんか grammar point is a polite way of asking someone for a favor or requesting them to do something. It is more formal than the simple て form followed by ください, and it is commonly used in more formal settings or when speaking to someone who is not close." :formation "Verb in て-form + くださいませんか" :examples [{:ja "コーヒーを飲んでくださいませんか。" :en "Would you please drink some coffee?"} {:ja "ドアを閉めてくださいませんか。" :en "Would you please close the door?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-039" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て くださる" :reading "Verb-te kudasaru" :meaning "Expresses gratitude for receiving someone's favor; 'to kindly do'." :short "Expresses gratitude for receiving someone's favor; 'to kindly do'." :long "The て くださる grammar point is used to express gratitude for receiving a favor or action from someone else. It is an honorific way to say that someone kindly does something for the speaker or the listener. The verb is conjugated to its ～て form and followed by くださる." :formation "Verb て-form + くださる" :examples [{:ja "先生が手伝ってくださる。" :en "The teacher kindly helps."} {:ja "お母さんが美味しい料理を作ってくださる。" :en "My mother kindly cooks delicious meals."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-040" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て くる" :reading "Verb te kuru" :meaning "An action that starts in the past and continues to the present or future; 'come to', 'begin to', 'become'." :short "An action that starts in the past and continues to the present or future; 'come to', 'begin to', 'become'." :long "The て くる grammar point is used to express an action that starts in the past and continues to the present or future. It can also show a change in state or condition. The formation involves attaching the て-form of the verb followed by くる." :formation "Verb て-form + くる" :examples [{:ja "最近、日本語が分かってくるようになりました。" :en "Recently, I've come to understand Japanese."} {:ja "寒くなってきたので、コートを着ましょう。" :en "Since it's gotten cold, let's put on a coat."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-041" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb てくれませんか" :reading "〜te kuremasen ka" :meaning "A polite way to request someone to do something for you; 'Could you please', 'Would you please', 'Won't you'." :short "A polite way to request someone to do something for you; 'Could you please', 'Would you please', 'Won't you'." :long "The ～てくれませんか grammar point is used to ask someone politely to do something for you. It is formed by using the て-form of a verb followed by くれませんか. It translates to 'Could you please', 'Would you please', or 'Won't you' in English." :formation "Verb-て form + くれませんか" :examples [{:ja "ドアを開けてくれませんか。" :en "Could you please open the door?"} {:ja "これを見てくれませんか。" :en "Would you please take a look at this?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-042" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て くれる" :reading "Verb-te kureru" :meaning "Expresses that someone does something for the speaker (or another person in speaker's group); 'to do something for me/us'." :short "Expresses that someone does something for the speaker (or another person in speaker's group); 'to do something for me/us'." :long "The て くれる grammar point is used to express when someone does something for, or gives something to the speaker (or someone in the speaker's group). The action is usually done willingly or as a favor by the person performing the action. It is usually translated into English as 'to do something for me' or 'to give something to me'." :formation "Verb-te form + くれる" :examples [{:ja "友達が宿題を手伝ってくれました。" :en "My friend helped me with my homework."} {:ja "弟が部屋を掃除してくれた。" :en "My younger brother cleaned the room for me."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-043" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て + さしあげる" :reading "Verb TE sashiageru" :meaning "A polite way to express giving or doing something for someone." :short "A polite way to express giving or doing something for someone." :long "The grammar point 'Verb て + さしあげる' is used to express the action of giving or doing something for someone in a polite manner. It is mainly used when the speaker is offering or doing something for someone of higher social status or showing respect towards the person. To form this expression, you need to conjugate the verb into its て-form and attach さしあげる." :formation "Verb-てform + さしあげる" :examples [{:ja "お茶を入れてさしあげます。" :en "I will prepare and serve tea (for you)."} {:ja "メッセージを送ってさしあげました。" :en "I have sent the message (for you)."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-044" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb てしまう" :reading "〜te shimau" :meaning "Expresses unintentional actions or actions with regrettable outcomes; 'to end up doing', 'to accidentally do'." :short "Expresses unintentional actions or actions with regrettable outcomes; 'to end up doing', 'to accidentally do'." :long "The ～てしまう grammar point is used to indicate unintentional actions or actions with regrettable outcomes. It can often be translated as 'to end up doing' or 'to accidentally do' in English. The formation requires adding しまう to the て-form of a verb." :formation "Verb-te form + しまう" :examples [{:ja "ケーキを全部食べてしまった。" :en "I ended up eating the whole cake."} {:ja "宿題を忘れてしまいました。" :en "I accidentally forgot my homework."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-045" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て ほしい" :reading "Verb-te hoshii" :meaning "Express a desire for someone else to do something; 'I want (someone) to'." :short "Express a desire for someone else to do something; 'I want (someone) to'." :long "The て ほしい grammar point is used to express the speaker's desire for someone else to do an action. It is formed by combining the て-form of a verb with ほしい. The subject is usually followed by the particle に, but it can be dropped in casual speech." :formation "Verb-て form + ほしい" :examples [{:ja "子供にもっと勉強してほしい。" :en "I want my child to study more."} {:ja "彼にもっと早く来てほしい。" :en "I want him to come earlier."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-046" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb てみる" :reading "〜te miru" :meaning "Used to express 'try to' or 'attempt to do'." :short "Used to express 'try to' or 'attempt to do'." :long "The ～てみる grammar point is used with verbs to express 'trying' or 'attempting' to do something. It emphasizes the idea of giving something a go or trying it out, often to see what the results or outcome will be." :formation "Verb-て form + みる" :examples [{:ja "この料理を作ってみます。" :en "I will try to make this dish."} {:ja "その服を着てみてください。" :en "Please try on those clothes."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-047" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb てもらいたい" :reading "～te moraitai" :meaning "Express a desire to receive a favor or service from someone else." :short "Express a desire to receive a favor or service from someone else." :long "The ～てもらいたい grammar point is used to express one's desire to receive a favor, service, or action from someone else. It is formed by attaching もらいたい to the て-form of verbs. This structure emphasizes the help or favor the speaker wants from someone else." :formation "Verb-て-form + もらいたい" :examples [{:ja "この荷物を運んでもらいたいです。" :en "I want you to carry this luggage for me."} {:ja "彼にそのニュースを教えてもらいたい。" :en "I want him to tell me the news."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-048" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb て もらう" :reading "Verb-te morau" :meaning "Express receiving a favor or action from someone." :short "Express receiving a favor or action from someone." :long "The Verb て もらう grammar point is used to express that the speaker or a third person receives a favor or action from someone else. It describes an action performed by someone for the benefit of the speaker or a third person, implying gratitude or request from the beneficiary." :formation "Verb-て form + もらう" :examples [{:ja "友達に宿題を手伝ってもらった。" :en "I had my friend help me with my homework."} {:ja "母に部屋を掃除してもらいました。" :en "I had my mother clean my room."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-049" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb てもらえませんか" :reading "～te moraemasen ka" :meaning "A polite way to ask for a favor or request someone to do something." :short "A polite way to ask for a favor or request someone to do something." :long "The ～てもらえませんか grammar point is a polite and indirect way of asking someone to do something for you or asking for a favor. It is formed by conjugating the verb into the て-form and adding もらえませんか. This form is used in conversations to show politeness and humility." :formation "Verb-て form + もらえませんか" :examples [{:ja "この手紙を見てもらえませんか。" :en "Could you take a look at this letter for me?"} {:ja "荷物を持ってもらえませんか。" :en "Could you carry this luggage for me?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-050" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ない ことがある" :reading "Verb-nai koto ga aru" :meaning "Expresses that something doesn't always happen or there are occasions when it doesn't happen." :short "Expresses that something doesn't always happen or there are occasions when it doesn't happen." :long "The ないことがある grammar point is used to indicate that there are instances or times when something doesn't happen or isn't done. It is formed by combining the negative form of a verb (ない) with ことがある." :formation "Verb-negative form + ことがある" :examples [{:ja "彼は会議に出席しないことがある。" :en "There are times when he doesn't attend meetings."} {:ja "この電車は定刻に来ないことがある。" :en "There are occasions when this train doesn't come on time."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-051" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ない + ことにする" :reading "Verb nai koto ni suru" :meaning "To decide not to do something." :short "To decide not to do something." :long "The grammar point Verb ない + ことにする is used to express the decision not to do something. This structure shows that the speaker has made a conscious choice to not take the action expressed in the verb." :formation "Verb-ない form + ことにする" :examples [{:ja "明日のパーティーに行かないことにする。" :en "I've decided not to go to tomorrow's party."} {:ja "そのケーキを食べないことにします。" :en "I've decided not to eat that cake."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-052" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ない ことになる" :reading "Verb nai koto ni naru" :meaning "Expresses a result; 'end up not', 'it will become that', 'it would mean that'." :short "Expresses a result; 'end up not', 'it will become that', 'it would mean that'." :long "The Verb ない ことになる grammar point is used to express a result or consequence. It translates to 'end up not', 'it will become that', or 'it would mean that' in English. It is formed by using the negative form (ない) of a verb followed by ことになる." :formation "Verb-negative (ない) + ことになる" :examples [{:ja "今日彼が来ないことになると、私たちの予定はキャンセルされます。" :en "If he doesn't come today, our plans will be canceled."} {:ja "このプロジェクトが進まないことになると、収益が減るでしょう。" :en "If this project doesn't progress, it would mean that revenue will decrease."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-053" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ないほうがいい" :reading "Verb nai hou ga ii" :meaning "Expresses the idea that it is better not to do something." :short "Expresses the idea that it is better not to do something." :long "The ないほうがいい grammar point is used to suggest that it's better not to do a certain action. It is formed by taking the negative form of the verb and adding ほうがいい." :formation "Verb-negative form + ほうがいい" :examples [{:ja "この薬は飲まないほうがいいです。" :en "It's better not to take this medicine."} {:ja "急いでいるときは、走らないほうがいい。" :en "When you're in a hurry, it's better not to run."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-054" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ながら" :reading "〜nagara" :meaning "Expresses 'while' or 'as' in the sense of doing two things simultaneously." :short "Expresses 'while' or 'as' in the sense of doing two things simultaneously." :long "The ～ながら grammar point is used to indicate that two actions are happening simultaneously or that one action is happening while another is taking place. This can be translated to 'while' or 'as' in English. It is primarily used with verbs." :formation "Verb-masu stem + ながら" :examples [{:ja "テレビを見ながら宿題をしました。" :en "I did my homework while watching television."} {:ja "音楽を聞きながら料理をしました。" :en "I cooked while listening to music."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-055" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb なさい" :reading "〜nasai" :meaning "A command form used to give orders or instructions; 'Please do', 'Do this'." :short "A command form used to give orders or instructions; 'Please do', 'Do this'." :long "The なさい grammar point is used to give orders or instructions, often used by teachers, parents, or other authority figures when addressing someone younger or of lower status. It is formed by attaching なさい to the stem of a verb. This form sounds polite, but can be considered strong and direct." :formation "Verb-stem + なさい" :examples [{:ja "部屋を掃除しなさい。" :en "Clean your room."} {:ja "勉強しなさい。" :en "Study."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-056" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb にくい" :reading "〜nikui" :meaning "Express difficulty or complexity; 'hard to', 'difficult to', 'not easily'." :short "Express difficulty or complexity; 'hard to', 'difficult to', 'not easily'." :long "The ～にくい grammar point is used to express that an action is difficult or relatively hard to do. It is used by attaching にくい to the stem of a verb. It can be translated as 'hard to', 'difficult to', or 'not easily' in English." :formation "Verb-stem + にくい" :examples [{:ja "この字は読みにくいです。" :en "This character is hard to read."} {:ja "このドアは開けにくい。" :en "This door is difficult to open."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-057" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb やすい" :reading "〜yasui" :meaning "Indicates that something is easy to do; 'easy to', 'easy for'." :short "Indicates that something is easy to do; 'easy to', 'easy for'." :long "The やすい grammar point is used to express that something is easy to do or easy for someone. It is attached to the verb stem and can be translated as 'easy to' or 'easy for' in English. This formation is used with verbs only." :formation "Verb-stem + やすい" :examples [{:ja "この本は読みやすいです。" :en "This book is easy to read."} {:ja "このシャツは着やすい。" :en "This shirt is easy to wear."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-058" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ようと思う" :reading "Verb-you to omou" :meaning "Express intention or plan to do something; 'I think I will', 'I plan to', 'I intend to'." :short "Express intention or plan to do something; 'I think I will', 'I plan to', 'I intend to'." :long "The ようと思う grammar point is used to express the intention or plan to do something in the future. It can be translated as 'I think I will', 'I plan to', or 'I intend to' in English. The formation involves using the volitional form of the verb followed by と思う." :formation "Verb-volitional + と思う" :examples [{:ja "明日、映画を見ようと思います。" :en "I think I will watch a movie tomorrow."} {:ja "週末に友達と遊ぼうと思っています。" :en "I plan to hang out with friends on the weekend."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-059" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ように" :reading "〜you ni" :meaning "Used to express 'like', 'as if', 'in order to', or 'so that'." :short "Used to express 'like', 'as if', 'in order to', or 'so that'." :long "The ～ように grammar point is used to express different meanings depending on the context: 'like', 'as if', 'in order to', or 'so that'. Formation patterns can include: Noun + のように, Verb dictionary/negative form + ように, い-adjective + ように, etc." :formation "Examples: Noun + のように / Verb-casual + ように / い-adjective + ように" :examples [{:ja "彼女は鳥のように軽やかに踊る。" :en "She dances lightly like a bird."} {:ja "覚えたことを忘れないように、毎日復習しましょう。" :en "In order not to forget what you've learned, let's review every day."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-060" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ようにする" :reading "Verb ~you ni suru" :meaning "Make an effort to; try to; see to it that; ensure that." :short "Make an effort to; try to; see to it that; ensure that." :long "The ようにする grammar point is used to express that someone is making an effort, trying, or seeing to it that something happens. It can be translated as 'make sure to', 'try to', or 'ensure that' in English. The formation is simple, involving a verb in the dictionary form followed by ようにする." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ようにする" :examples [{:ja "毎日運動するようにしています。" :en "I try to exercise every day."} {:ja "早く寝るようにしてください。" :en "Please try to go to bed early."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-061" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ようになる" :reading "〜you ni naru" :meaning "Express a change in ability, situation or state; 'become able to', 'come to', 'begin to'." :short "Express a change in ability, situation or state; 'become able to', 'come to', 'begin to'." :long "The ようになる grammar implies a change or development involving new abilities, situations or states. It can be translated into English as 'become able to', 'come to' or 'begin to'. This grammar is often used when talking about a skill that has been acquired or a change in habit or situation over time." :formation "Verb-dictionary/potential form + ようになる" :examples [{:ja "日本語が話せるようになりました。" :en "I have become able to speak Japanese."} {:ja "最近、早く寝るようになった。" :en "Recently, I have come to go to bed early."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-062" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ように言う" :reading "Verb-you ni iu" :meaning "Tell someone to do something; 'tell to do', 'instruct to do'." :short "Tell someone to do something; 'tell to do', 'instruct to do'." :long "The grammar point Verb ように言う is used when someone tells or instructs another person to do something. It can be translated as 'tell to do' or 'instruct to do' in English. The formation is created by adding ように言う to the casual form of a verb." :formation "Verb-casual + ように言う" :examples [{:ja "先生は私たちに宿題をやるように言いました。" :en "The teacher told us to do our homework."} {:ja "母は弟に部屋を片付けるように言いました。" :en "My mother told my younger brother to clean his room."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-063" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb られる" :reading "〜rareru" :meaning "Expresses passive voice or potential form for verbs." :short "Expresses passive voice or potential form for verbs." :long "The られる grammar point can be used to express passive voice or potential form for verbs. Passive voice indicates that the action is being done to the subject, while potential form expresses the ability or possibility to do an action. The formation of られる depends on the type of verb (う, る, or irregular verbs)." :formation "う-verb (passive): Replace the final う with われる / る-verb (passive & potential): Replace る with られる / Irregular: する → される, くる → こられる" :examples [{:ja "彼は先生に褒められました。" :en "He was praised by the teacher."} {:ja "彼は友達に笑われました。" :en "He was laughed at by his friends."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-064" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb る ことがある" :reading "〜ru koto ga aru" :meaning "Used to express that something happens sometimes or has happened before." :short "Used to express that something happens sometimes or has happened before." :long "The grammar point る ことがある is used to express that something occurs sometimes or has occurred in the past. It indicates that the action is not a regular or constant event, but rather something that takes place occasionally or has happened at least once." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ことがある" :examples [{:ja "彼は遅刻することがあります。" :en "He is sometimes late."} {:ja "私は寝坊することがあります。" :en "I sometimes oversleep."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-065" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ることができる" :reading "〜ru koto ga dekiru" :meaning "Express ability or possibility of doing an action; 'can', 'be able to', 'is possible'." :short "Express ability or possibility of doing an action; 'can', 'be able to', 'is possible'." :long "The ～ることができる grammar point is used to express the ability or possibility of doing an action in Japanese. It can be translated as 'can', 'be able to', or 'is possible' in English. The formation is made by adding the phrase 'ことができる' to the verb in its conjugated る-form." :formation "Verb-る + ことができる" :examples [{:ja "私は日本語を話すことができます。" :en "I can speak Japanese."} {:ja "彼はギターを弾くことができます。" :en "He can play the guitar."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-066" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb ることにする" :reading "〜ru koto ni suru" :meaning "Decide to do something, make a decision." :short "Decide to do something, make a decision." :long "The ～ることにする grammar point is used to express that someone has made a decision to do something or has decided on a specific course of action. It is formed by attaching ことにする to the verb in its る form." :formation "Verb-る + ことにする" :examples [{:ja "来週、ジムに行くことにしました。" :en "I decided to go to the gym next week."} {:ja "この靴を買うことにしました。" :en "I decided to buy these shoes."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-067" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb る ことになる" :reading "〜ru koto ni naru" :meaning "Indicates that something has been decided or arranged; 'it has been decided that', 'it turns out that'." :short "Indicates that something has been decided or arranged; 'it has been decided that', 'it turns out that'." :long "The る ことになる grammar point is used to express that something has been decided or arranged. It often indicates a decision made by someone else or due to circumstances. It can be translated as 'it has been decided that' or 'it turns out that' in English. The formation requires a verb in る (dictionary) form." :formation "Verb-る + ことになる" :examples [{:ja "今日は会議がなくなることになりました。" :en "It has been decided that there will be no meeting today."} {:ja "来週、友達と旅行することになった。" :en "It has been arranged that I will travel with my friends next week."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-068" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb るときに" :reading "〜ru toki ni" :meaning "Describes the time when a certain action takes place; 'when', 'at the time'." :short "Describes the time when a certain action takes place; 'when', 'at the time'." :long "The ～るときに grammar point is used to indicate the time when a certain action or event happens. It can be translated as 'when' or 'at the time' in English. The formation involves attaching ときに to the verb in its dictionary (る) form." :formation "Verb-る + ときに" :examples [{:ja "映画を見るときに、ポップコーンを食べます。" :en "When I watch a movie, I eat popcorn."} {:ja "寝るときに、部屋の電気を消します。" :en "When I go to bed, I turn off the room lights."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-069" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb る ところ" :reading "Verb-ru tokoro" :meaning "Expresses the state or moment when an action is about to happen or is happening." :short "Expresses the state or moment when an action is about to happen or is happening." :long "The grammar point 'Verb る ところ' is used to express that one is just about to do something, or is in the process of doing it. It can be translated as 'just about to' or 'in the middle of' in English, depending on context." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ところ" :examples [{:ja "彼は食べるところです。" :en "He is in the process of eating."} {:ja "私は出かけるところです。" :en "I am just about to leave."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-070" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb 出す" :reading "~dasu" :meaning "Express the action of taking something out, starting an action, or emphasizing a verb." :short "Express the action of taking something out, starting an action, or emphasizing a verb." :long "The verb 出す (~dasu) is used to express the action of taking something out, starting an action, or emphasizing the verb it is attached to. To form this grammar point, simply attach 出す (~dasu) to the stem of the verb." :formation "Verb stem + 出す (~dasu)" :examples [{:ja "本を棚から出してください。" :en "Please take the book out from the shelf."} {:ja "彼は部屋から急いで出ました。" :en "He rushed out of the room."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-071" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb 方" :reading "〜hou" :meaning "Indicates the way or method of doing something; often read as '～かた' ('how to', 'the way of')." :short "Indicates the way or method of doing something; often read as '～かた' ('how to', 'the way of')." :long "The ～方 grammar point is used to express the way or method of doing something, similar to 'how to' or 'the way of' in English. It can be attached to the stem of a verb to form a noun that indicates the method of doing the action. In practice, ～方 is usually read as '～かた' (e.g., 作り方＝つくりかた)." :formation "Verb-stem + 方 (read as かた)" :examples [{:ja "誰かがケーキの作り方を知っていますか？" :en "Does anyone know how to make a cake?"} {:ja "彼女は英語の話し方を学んでいます。" :en "She is learning how to speak English."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-072" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb 終わる" :reading "〜owaru" :meaning "Verbal action is completed; 'to finish', 'to end'." :short "Verbal action is completed; 'to finish', 'to end'." :long "The verb 終わる (owaru) is used to express that an action has been completed or something has come to an end. Despite ending in る, 終わる is a godan (u-verb), so it does not follow the typical ichidan (る-verb) conjugation. It is translated as 'to finish', 'to end' or 'to be over' in English." :formation "終わる (owaru) is a godan (u-verb)" :examples [{:ja "映画が終わったら、家に帰ります。" :en "When the movie finishes, I'll go home."} {:ja "仕事が終わるまで、待ってください。" :en "Please wait until the work is finished."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-073" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "Verb + 続ける" :reading "つづける, tsuzukeru" :meaning "To continue doing or to keep doing an action." :short "To continue doing or to keep doing an action." :long "The grammar point 続ける (tsuzukeru) is attached to the masu-stem form of verbs, and it indicates the ongoing continuity of an action. It can be translated as 'to continue doing' or 'to keep doing' in English." :formation "Verb-masu-stem + 続ける" :examples [{:ja "彼は毎日ジムで運動を続けています。" :en "He continues to exercise at the gym every day."} {:ja "彼女は勉強を続けるために図書館に行きました。" :en "She went to the library to continue studying."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-074" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～かしら" :reading "〜kashira" :meaning "Indicates uncertainty or doubt; 'I wonder', 'I'm not sure'." :short "Indicates uncertainty or doubt; 'I wonder', 'I'm not sure'." :long "The ～かしら grammar point is used to express uncertainty or doubt about a situation. It can be translated as 'I wonder' or 'I'm not sure' in English. It is primarily used by females in casual conversations but can be used by males in some contexts as well. ～かしら is used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + かしら, い-Adjective + かしら, な-Adjective + かしら, Noun + かしら" :examples [{:ja "今日は雨が降るかしら？" :en "I wonder if it will rain today?"} {:ja "このケーキは美味しいかしら？" :en "I wonder if this cake is delicious?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-075" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～かどうか" :reading "〜ka dou ka" :meaning "Used to express 'whether' or 'if' in indirect questions or statements." :short "Used to express 'whether' or 'if' in indirect questions or statements." :long "The ～かどうか grammar point is used to express 'whether' or 'if' in indirect questions or statements. It is often used to express uncertainty or to inquire about information in a more polite form. ～かどうか can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + かどうか, い-Adjective + かどうか, な-Adjective + (だ)かどうか, Noun + (だ)かどうか" :examples [{:ja "彼が来るかどうか知りません。" :en "I don't know if he will come or not."} {:ja "この本が面白いかどうか教えてください。" :en "Please tell me if this book is interesting or not."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-076" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～かなあ" :reading "〜kanaa" :meaning "Expresses uncertainty or wonder; 'I wonder', 'I'm not sure'." :short "Expresses uncertainty or wonder; 'I wonder', 'I'm not sure'." :long "The ～かなあ grammar point is used to express a feeling of uncertainty or wonder about a situation or statement. It can be translated as 'I wonder' or 'I'm not sure' in English. In casual spoken Japanese, when using nouns or な-adjectives before かなあ, the copula だ is often omitted or replaced with の (e.g., 学生かなあ or 学生なのかなあ). This grammar point should not be used when asking questions directly to someone, as it may come off as too direct or impolite." :formation "Verb-casual + かなあ, い-Adjective + かなあ, (な-Adjective/Noun) + (だ) + かなあ" :examples [{:ja "明日雨が降るかなあ。" :en "I wonder if it will rain tomorrow."} {:ja "このケーキは美味しいかなあ。" :en "I wonder if this cake is delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-077" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～かもしれない" :reading "〜kamoshirenai" :meaning "Express uncertainty; 'maybe', 'might', 'possibly'." :short "Express uncertainty; 'maybe', 'might', 'possibly'." :long "The ～かもしれない grammar point is used to express uncertainty or the possibility of something occurring. It can be translated as 'maybe', 'might', or 'possibly' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun. In everyday speech, speakers often omit だ before かもしれない for nouns and な-adjectives." :formation "Verb-casual + かもしれない, い-Adjective + かもしれない, な-Adjective + だかもしれない, Noun + だかもしれない" :examples [{:ja "明日雨が降るかもしれない。" :en "It might rain tomorrow."} {:ja "このケーキは美味しいかもしれない。" :en "This cake might be delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-078" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～から" :reading "〜kara" :meaning "Express a reason or cause; 'because', 'since', 'due to'." :short "Express a reason or cause; 'because', 'since', 'due to'." :long "The ～から grammar point is used to express a reason, cause, or the origin of something. It can be translated as 'because', 'since', or 'due to' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + から, い-Adjective + から, な-Adjective + だから, Noun + だから" :examples [{:ja "雨が降るから、傘を持って行ってください。" :en "Because it's raining, please bring an umbrella."} {:ja "このケーキは美味しいから、みんな食べるでしょう。" :en "Because this cake is delicious, everyone will probably eat it."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-079" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～けれど" :reading "〜keredo" :meaning "Indicate a soft contrast or contradiction; 'but', 'however'." :short "Indicate a soft contrast or contradiction; 'but', 'however'." :long "The ～けれど grammar point is used to express a soft contrast or contradiction between two statements. It can be translated as 'but' or 'however' in English. It is relatively informal and can be replaced by more formal expressions like けれども or が. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + けれど, い-Adjective + けれど, な-Adjective + だけれど, Noun + だけれど" :examples [{:ja "今日は忙しいけれど、明日はもっと時間がある。" :en "I'm busy today, but I have more time tomorrow."} {:ja "このレストランは高いけれど、料理が美味しい。" :en "This restaurant is expensive, but the food is delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-080" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～させてください" :reading "〜sasete kudasai" :meaning "Express a polite request to allow or permit someone to do something." :short "Express a polite request to allow or permit someone to do something." :long "The ～させてください grammar point is used to make a polite request to allow or permit someone to do something. It can be translated as 'please let me' or 'please allow me to' in English. It is formed by taking the causative form of a verb and adding ください." :formation "Verb-causative form + ください" :examples [{:ja "もう少し考えさせてください。" :en "Please let me think a little more."} {:ja "写真を撮らせてください。" :en "Please let me take a photo."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-081" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～し、～し、～" :reading "〜shi, 〜shi, 〜" :meaning "Express multiple reasons or characteristics; 'and', 'because', 'since'." :short "Express multiple reasons or characteristics; 'and', 'because', 'since'." :long "The ～し、～し、～ grammar point is used to connect multiple reasons or characteristics that support a conclusion or statement. It can be translated as 'and', 'because', or 'since' in English. This grammar can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + し, い-Adjective + し, な-Adjective + だし, Noun + だし" :examples [{:ja "彼は若いし、元気だし、フレンドリーだし、みんなと仲良くなれると思います。" :en "He is young, energetic, friendly, and I think he can get along with everyone."} {:ja "このレストランは料理が美味しいし、雰囲気がいいし、価格も手頃だからお勧めです。" :en "I recommend this restaurant because the food is delicious, the atmosphere is nice, and the prices are reasonable."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-082" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～すぎる" :reading "〜sugiru" :meaning "Expresses that something is excessive or overdone; 'too much', 'too', 'over'." :short "Expresses that something is excessive or overdone; 'too much', 'too', 'over'." :long "The ～すぎる grammar point is used to express that something is excessive or beyond a certain level. It can be translated as 'too much', 'too', or 'over' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, or な-adjective." :formation "Verb-stem + すぎる, い-Adjective (remove い) + すぎる, な-Adjective + すぎる" :examples [{:ja "この部屋は暑すぎる。" :en "This room is too hot."} {:ja "彼は早く来すぎた。" :en "He came too early."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-083" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～ずつ" :reading "〜zutsu" :meaning "Indicates equal distribution; 'each', 'per', 'apiece'." :short "Indicates equal distribution; 'each', 'per', 'apiece'." :long "The ～ずつ grammar point is used to express an equal distribution of something, whether it is an action, an amount, or an item. It is translated as 'each', 'per', or 'apiece' in English." :formation "Noun + ずつ" :examples [{:ja "りんごを2つずつ配ります。" :en "I will distribute 2 apples each."} {:ja "私たちは週に一度ずつ掃除をします。" :en "We each clean once per week."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-084" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～そうだ" :reading "〜sou da" :meaning "Express the appearance, seeming, or feeling of something; 'looks like', 'seems', 'appears'." :short "Express the appearance, seeming, or feeling of something; 'looks like', 'seems', 'appears'." :long "The ～そうだ grammar point is used to describe the appearance or feeling of something based on what is observed or inferred. It can be translated as 'looks like', 'seems', or 'appears' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, or な-adjective." :formation "Verb-ますstem + そうだ, い-Adjective (without い) + そうだ, な-Adjective + そうだ" :examples [{:ja "その花はとてもきれいそうだ。" :en "That flower looks very beautiful."} {:ja "彼は疲れそうだ。" :en "He looks tired."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-085" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "そんな (sonna) + Noun" :reading "" :meaning "Expresses 'that kind/type of'; referring to something near the listener or previously mentioned." :short "Expresses 'that kind/type of'; referring to something near the listener or previously mentioned." :long "The word 'そんな' is used before a noun to indicate 'that kind of' or 'that type of', typically referring to something near the listener or previously mentioned in the conversation. It specifies the noun based on context or shared understanding between the speaker and the listener." :formation "そんな + Noun" :examples [{:ja "そんな話、信じられない。" :en "I can't believe that kind of story."} {:ja "そんな映画、見たくない。" :en "I don't want to watch that kind of movie."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-086" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "そんなに～" :reading "sonna ni〜" :meaning "Expresses 'not so much', 'not that', or 'not so ~'." :short "Expresses 'not so much', 'not that', or 'not so ~'." :long "The grammar point そんなに is an adverb used to express that something is not as extreme as described or not to that extent. It is often translated as 'not so much', 'not that', or 'not so ~'. It is commonly followed by a negative form of a verb or adjective." :formation "そんなに + Verb-negation, そんなに + Adjective-negation" :examples [{:ja "そんなに急がなくてもいいですよ。" :en "You don't have to hurry that much."} {:ja "そんなに難しくない問題だと思います。" :en "I don't think the problem is that difficult."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-087" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～たらいい" :reading "〜tara ii" :meaning "Expressing a desire or suggestion for a particular outcome; 'it would be good if', 'should', 'I hope'." :short "Expressing a desire or suggestion for a particular outcome; 'it would be good if', 'should', 'I hope'." :long "The ～たらいい grammar point is used to express a desire, suggestion, or hope for a particular outcome. It can be translated as 'it would be good if', 'should', or 'I hope' in English. The formation is simple, and it is used with verbs in their casual past tense form." :formation "Verb-casual-past + たらいい" :examples [{:ja "もっと勉強したらいいですよ。" :en "It would be good if you studied more."} {:ja "雨が止んだらいいな。" :en "I hope the rain stops."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-088" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～たら いかがですか" :reading "〜tara ikaga desu ka" :meaning "Making polite suggestions; 'how about if', 'what if', 'it would be good if'." :short "Making polite suggestions; 'how about if', 'what if', 'it would be good if'." :long "The ～たら いかがですか grammar point is used to make polite suggestions, ask for opinions, or imply that something would be good or desirable if the condition is met. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual, past + たら いかがですか, い-Adjective + かったら いかがですか, な-Adjective + だったら いかがですか, Noun + だったら いかがですか" :examples [{:ja "図書館に行ったらいかがですか。" :en "How about going to the library?"} {:ja "寒かったらカーディガンを着るのはいかがですか。" :en "How about wearing a cardigan if it's cold?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-089" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～たら どうですか" :reading "〜tara doudesuka" :meaning "Express a suggestion or advice; 'what if', 'how about', 'why don't you'." :short "Express a suggestion or advice; 'what if', 'how about', 'why don't you'." :long "The ～たら どうですか grammar point is used to make a suggestion or give advice to someone. It can be translated as 'what if', 'how about', or 'why don't you' in English. This structure is used with the た-form of verbs to make a gentle, polite suggestion." :formation "Verb-た form + ら どうですか" :examples [{:ja "スキーに行ったらどうですか？" :en "How about going skiing?"} {:ja "少し休んだらどうですか？" :en "What if you take a little break?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-090" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～たり～たり" :reading "〜tari 〜tari" :meaning "Used to list multiple actions or states; 'doing things like', 'and so on', 'etc.'" :short "Used to list multiple actions or states; 'doing things like', 'and so on', 'etc.'" :long "The ～たり～たり grammar point is used to list two or more actions or states, without specifying their order or importance. It implies that these actions or states are representative examples, and there may be others. This construction is useful for describing general or habitual actions." :formation "Verb-ta + り + next verb-ta + り, い-Adjective + かったり + next verb-ta + り, な-Adjective + だったり + next verb-ta + り, Noun + だったり + next verb-ta + り" :examples [{:ja "週末は映画を観たり、本を読んだりする。" :en "On weekends, I do things like watching movies and reading books."} {:ja "彼女は料理が上手だったり、歌がうまかったりする。" :en "She is good at things like cooking and singing."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-091" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～だろう" :reading "〜darou" :meaning "Express probability, likelihood or certainty; 'probably', 'maybe', 'I think'." :short "Express probability, likelihood or certainty; 'probably', 'maybe', 'I think'." :long "The ～だろう grammar point is used to express probability, likelihood, or certainty about a situation or event. It can be translated as 'probably', 'maybe', or 'I think' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + だろう, い-Adjective (drop い) + だろう, な-Adjective + だろう, Noun + だろう" :examples [{:ja "明日は晴れるだろう。" :en "It will probably be sunny tomorrow."} {:ja "彼は忙しいだろう。" :en "He is probably busy."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-092" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～っていう" :reading "〜tte iu" :meaning "Used to quote, define, or specify a noun." :short "Used to quote, define, or specify a noun." :long "The ～っていう grammar point is used for quoting, defining, or specifying a noun. It can be translated as 'called', 'that is to say', or 'I mean' in English. This expression is similar to ～という, but it is more casual and commonly used in spoken conversations." :formation "Noun/Adjective/Verb + っていう + Noun" :examples [{:ja "ピザっていう料理が大好きです。" :en "I love the dish called pizza."} {:ja "彼っていう男性はすごく面白いです。" :en "That man (called ‘kare’) is really interesting."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-093" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～で" :reading "〜de" :meaning "Indicates the means, method, or place." :short "Indicates the means, method, or place." :long "The ～で grammar point is used to indicate the means or method used to perform an action, or to specify the place where an action took place. It is typically used with verbs and comes after a noun." :formation "Noun + ～で" :examples [{:ja "私は自転車で学校に行きます。" :en "I go to school by bicycle."} {:ja "彼は日本語で手紙を書いた。" :en "He wrote a letter in Japanese."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-094" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～でしょう" :reading "〜deshou" :meaning "A conjecture or probability; 'probably', 'I think', 'it seems'." :short "A conjecture or probability; 'probably', 'I think', 'it seems'." :long "The ～でしょう grammar point is used to indicate a conjecture or probability. It can be translated as 'probably', 'I think', or 'it seems' in English. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-ますstem + でしょう, い-Adjective + でしょう, な-Adjective + でしょう, Noun + でしょう" :examples [{:ja "明日は晴れでしょう。" :en "It will probably be sunny tomorrow."} {:ja "彼は元気でしょう。" :en "He is probably fine."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-095" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～てはいけない" :reading "〜te wa ikenai" :meaning "Expresses prohibition; 'must not', 'cannot'." :short "Expresses prohibition; 'must not', 'cannot'." :long "The ～てはいけない grammar point is used to express strict prohibition or something that must not be done. It can be translated as 'must not', 'cannot', or 'should not' in English. It is formed by adding て-form of a verb followed by はいけない." :formation "Verb-て-form + はいけない" :examples [{:ja "公園でたばこを吸ってはいけない。" :en "You must not smoke in the park."} {:ja "ここで写真を撮ってはいけない。" :en "You cannot take pictures here."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-096" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～てもいい" :reading "〜temo ii" :meaning "To give or ask permission; 'it's okay to', 'may', 'can'." :short "To give or ask permission; 'it's okay to', 'may', 'can'." :long "The ～てもいい grammar point is used to give or ask for permission to do something. It can be translated as 'it's okay to', 'may', or 'can' in English. The formation is derived by adding てもいい after the て-form of a verb." :formation "Verb-て form + もいい" :examples [{:ja "ここで写真を撮ってもいいですか。" :en "Is it okay to take a photo here?"} {:ja "このリンゴを食べてもいいですか。" :en "May I eat this apple?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-097" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～ても/でも" :reading "〜te mo/demo" :meaning "Expresses 'even if' or 'even though'; used to convey contradiction or contrast." :short "Expresses 'even if' or 'even though'; used to convey contradiction or contrast." :long "The ～ても/でも grammar point is used to express 'even if' or 'even though' in a sentence, showing the idea of contradiction or contrast. The formation of ても differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, or な-adjective. For nouns, でも is used." :formation "Verb-て-form + も, い-Adjective (-い) + くても, な-Adjective + でも, Noun + でも" :examples [{:ja "雨が降っても、学校に行きます。" :en "Even if it rains, I will go to school."} {:ja "この部屋が汚くても、掃除しないつもりです。" :en "Even if this room is dirty, I don't intend to clean it."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-098" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～といい" :reading "〜to ii" :meaning "Express a wish or hope; 'I hope', 'it would be good if'." :short "Express a wish or hope; 'I hope', 'it would be good if'." :long "The ～といい grammar point is used to express a wish or hope for a certain situation or outcome. It can be translated as 'I hope', 'it would be good if', or 'it would be nice if' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + といい, い-Adjective + といい, な-Adjective + だといい, Noun + だといい" :examples [{:ja "明日晴れるといいね。" :en "I hope it will be sunny tomorrow."} {:ja "この試験に合格するといい。" :en "I hope to pass this exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-099" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～という (〜to iu) Noun" :reading "" :meaning "Used to define or explain a noun more specifically." :short "Used to define or explain a noun more specifically." :long "The ～という grammar point is used to define or explain a noun more specifically. It is often translated as 'called', 'named', or 'that is to say' in English. It can be used with nouns, な-adjectives, and adjectival verbs." :formation "Noun + という + Noun, な-Adjective + という + Noun, Adjectival Verb + という + Noun" :examples [{:ja "彼は日本語が流暢なアメリカ人という人です。" :en "He is an American who speaks fluent Japanese."} {:ja "昨日、美味しかったというケーキを食べました。" :en "Yesterday, I ate a cake that was said to be delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-100" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "どういう Noun" :reading "dou iu Noun" :meaning "Asking for clarification or more information about a noun; 'what kind of', 'what sort of'." :short "Asking for clarification or more information about a noun; 'what kind of', 'what sort of'." :long "The どういう Noun grammar point is used to ask for more information or clarification about a specific noun. It can be translated as 'what kind of' or 'what sort of' in English. The formation is simply using どういう followed by the noun in question." :formation "どういう + Noun" :examples [{:ja "どういう仕事をしていますか？" :en "What kind of work do you do?"} {:ja "どういう映画が好きですか？" :en "What kind of movies do you like?"}]} {:id "grammar-n4-101" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～と思う" :reading "〜to omou" :meaning "Express one's thoughts or opinions; 'I think', 'I believe'." :short "Express one's thoughts or opinions; 'I think', 'I believe'." :long "The ～と思う grammar point is used to express one's thoughts or opinions on a particular subject or situation. It can be translated as 'I think' or 'I believe' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + と思う, い-Adjective + と思う, な-Adjective + だと思う, Noun + だと思う" :examples [{:ja "明日晴れると思います。" :en "I think it will be sunny tomorrow."} {:ja "この映画は面白いと思う。" :en "I think this movie is interesting."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-102" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～という" :reading "〜to iu" :meaning "Used to quote, express hearsay or define something." :short "Used to quote, express hearsay or define something." :long "The ～という grammar point is used to quote someone's words, express something you heard or read from someone else, or define and clarify the subject. It can be translated as 'called', 'that', or 'it is said'. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + という, い-Adjective + という, な-Adjective + という, Noun + という" :examples [{:ja "彼は「好きだ」と言った。" :en "He said 'I like you'."} {:ja "その映画は面白いという評判があります。" :en "The movie has a reputation for being interesting."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-103" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～ないといけない" :reading "〜nai to ikenai" :meaning "Express a necessity; 'must', 'have to', 'need to'." :short "Express a necessity; 'must', 'have to', 'need to'." :long "The ～ないといけない grammar point is used to express a sense of necessity or obligation, similar to 'must', 'have to', or 'need to' in English. This structure is formed by using the negative form of the verb (ending in ない) followed by といけない." :formation "Verb-negative + といけない" :examples [{:ja "明日試験があるから、今夜勉強しないといけない。" :en "I have an exam tomorrow, so I have to study tonight."} {:ja "９時に出発しないといけない。" :en "We must leave at 9 o'clock."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-104" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～なきゃいけない" :reading "〜nakya ikenai" :meaning "Express a necessity or obligation; 'must', 'have to'." :short "Express a necessity or obligation; 'must', 'have to'." :long "The ～なきゃいけない grammar point is used to express a sense of necessity or obligation, similar to saying 'must' or 'have to' in English. It is a casual and more colloquial form of the ～なければいけない grammar point. The formation is different for verbs, but it is not used with い-adjectives, な-adjectives, or nouns." :formation "Verb-casual (negative form without い) + なきゃいけない" :examples [{:ja "明日までに宿題を終わらなきゃいけない。" :en "I have to finish my homework by tomorrow."} {:ja "もうすぐ出かけるから、急いで準備しなきゃいけない。" :en "We're leaving soon, so I have to hurry and get ready."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-105" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～なくちゃいけない" :reading "〜naku cha ikenai" :meaning "Express the obligation to do something; 'have to', 'must', 'need to'." :short "Express the obligation to do something; 'have to', 'must', 'need to'." :long "The ～なくちゃいけない grammar point is used to express that there is an obligation or necessity to do something. It can be translated as 'have to', 'must', or 'need to' in English. This grammar point is mainly used with verbs." :formation "Verb-ない form (-ない ending is dropped) + なくちゃいけない" :examples [{:ja "仕事に行かなくちゃいけないので、早起きしなければなりません。" :en "I have to go to work, so I must wake up early."} {:ja "試験があるから、勉強しなくちゃいけません。" :en "I have a test, so I must study."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-106" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～なくてはいけない" :reading "〜nakute wa ikenai" :meaning "Expresses the necessity or obligation; 'must', 'have to'." :short "Expresses the necessity or obligation; 'must', 'have to'." :long "The ～なくてはいけない grammar point is used to express the necessity or obligation to do something. It can be translated as 'must' or 'have to' in English. The formation requires the negative form of a verb and is followed by いけない (cannot go/progress). It indicates that if something is not done, it will be a problem or inappropriate." :formation "Verb-negative form + なくてはいけない" :examples [{:ja "宿題をしなくてはいけない。" :en "I have to do my homework."} {:ja "この手紙を明日までに出さなくてはいけない。" :en "I must send this letter by tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-107" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～なくてもいい" :reading "〜nakutemo ii" :meaning "Expresses that something is not necessary to do; 'it's okay if not', 'don't have to'." :short "Expresses that something is not necessary to do; 'it's okay if not', 'don't have to'." :long "The ～なくてもいい grammar point is used to express that an action or situation is not necessary or required, implying that it's okay if it does not occur. It can be translated as 'it's okay if not' or 'don't have to' in English. This grammar is used with verbs." :formation "Verb-ない-form + てもいい" :examples [{:ja "コーヒーは飲まなくてもいいです。" :en "You don't have to drink the coffee."} {:ja "宿題を今日しなくてもいいです。" :en "It's okay if you don't do your homework today."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-108" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～なければ ならない" :reading "〜nakereba naranai" :meaning "Express obligation or necessity; 'must', 'have to', 'need to'." :short "Express obligation or necessity; 'must', 'have to', 'need to'." :long "The ～なければ ならない grammar point is used to express obligation or necessity. In English, it can be translated as 'must', 'have to', or 'need to'. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb. It cannot be used with い-adjectives, な-adjectives or nouns." :formation "Verb-ない form + なければ ならない" :examples [{:ja "宿題をやらなければならない。" :en "I have to do my homework."} {:ja "明日までにこの本を読まなければならない。" :en "I have to read this book by tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-109" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～に" :reading "〜ni" :meaning "Indicates direction, destination, purpose, or time." :short "Indicates direction, destination, purpose, or time." :long "The ～に grammar point is one of the most versatile Japanese particles. It is commonly used to indicate the direction or destination of an action, to show the purpose of an action or item, or to specify a point in time. Depending on the context, it can be translated as 'to', 'at', 'in', 'on', or 'for' in English." :formation "Noun + に" :examples [{:ja "母は私にケーキを作りました。" :en "My mother made a cake for me."} {:ja "彼女は東京に引っ越しました。" :en "She moved to Tokyo."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-110" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～ので" :reading "〜node" :meaning "Express a reason or a cause for something; 'because', 'since', 'so'." :short "Express a reason or a cause for something; 'because', 'since', 'so'." :long "The ～ので grammar point is used to express reason or cause for something. It can be translated as 'because', 'since', or 'so' in English. It is similar to から but is considered more polite and formal. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + ので, い-Adjective + ので, な-Adjective + なので, Noun + なので" :examples [{:ja "明日試験があるので、今晩勉強します。" :en "I will study tonight because there is a test tomorrow."} {:ja "このドレスが高いので、買わないことにします。" :en "I will not buy this dress because it is expensive."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-111" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～のです" :reading "〜no desu" :meaning "Provides an explanation or gives background information; 'the fact is', 'the reason is', 'actually'." :short "Provides an explanation or gives background information; 'the fact is', 'the reason is', 'actually'." :long "The ～のです grammar point is used to provide an explanation or give background information on a situation, often used when there is a need to justify or clarify one's statement. It can be translated as 'the fact is', 'the reason is', or 'actually' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + のです, い-Adjective + のです, な-Adjective + なのです, Noun + なのです" :examples [{:ja "この本は難しいのです。" :en "This book is difficult, you see."} {:ja "明日は忙しいのです。" :en "The fact is, I am busy tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-112" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～のに" :reading "〜no ni" :meaning "Used to express contrast between expectation and reality, or to indicate a purpose for doing something" :short "Used to express contrast between expectation and reality, or to indicate a purpose for doing something" :long "The ～のに grammar point has two main usages. The first is to express contrast between an expectation and reality, with translations like 'even though', 'despite' or 'in spite of' in English. The second is to indicate a purpose for doing something, translated as 'in order to' or 'for the purpose of'. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + のに, い-Adjective + のに, な-Adjective + なのに, Noun + なのに" :examples [{:ja "遅刻したのに、先生は怒らなかった。" :en "Even though I was late, the teacher didn't get angry."} {:ja "彼は疲れているのに、まだ働いています。" :en "Despite being tired, he is still working."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-113" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～ばいい" :reading "〜ba ii" :meaning "Express a suggestion or advice; 'should', 'it would be good if'." :short "Express a suggestion or advice; 'should', 'it would be good if'." :long "The ～ばいい grammar point is used to express a suggestion, recommendation or advice. It can be translated as 'should', or 'it would be good if' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-ば-form + いい, い-Adjective + ければいい, な-Adjective + であればいい, Noun + であればいい" :examples [{:ja "もっと勉強すればいい。" :en "You should study more."} {:ja "お腹が空いたら、何か食べればいい。" :en "If you're hungry, you should eat something."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-114" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～まで" :reading "〜made" :meaning "Indicates extent, 'until', 'up to', 'as far as', 'even'." :short "Indicates extent, 'until', 'up to', 'as far as', 'even'." :long "The ～まで grammar point is used to express extent or range in terms of time, distance, or degree. It can be translated as 'until', 'up to', 'as far as', or 'even' in English. It is used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, or nouns." :formation "Verb + まで, い-Adjective + まで, な-Adjective + まで, Noun + まで" :examples [{:ja "仕事が終わるまで待っています。" :en "I will wait until work is finished."} {:ja "ここから駅まで歩いて10分かかります。" :en "It takes 10 minutes to walk from here to the station."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-115" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～までに" :reading "〜made ni" :meaning "Express 'by' or 'before' a certain time or action." :short "Express 'by' or 'before' a certain time or action." :long "The ～までに grammar point is used to indicate that something must be completed or occurs by a certain time or before a specific action. It can be used with verbs, nouns, and time expressions." :formation "Verb-casual + までに, Noun + までに, Time expression + までに" :examples [{:ja "明日の朝８時までに起きてください。" :en "Please wake up by 8 a.m. tomorrow."} {:ja "来週の金曜日までにレポートを提出してください。" :en "Please submit the report by next Friday."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-116" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～まま" :reading "〜mama" :meaning "Express 'as it is', 'while' or 'unchanged state'." :short "Express 'as it is', 'while' or 'unchanged state'." :long "The ～まま grammar point is used to express a state or condition that remains unchanged or continues, often translated as 'as it is', 'while', or 'unchanged'. It can be used with verbs and adjectives to show that something remains in a certain state or condition." :formation "Verb-てform + いる+ まま, Adjective (い & な) + まま, Noun + のまま" :examples [{:ja "ドアを開けたまま出かけてしまった。" :en "I accidentally left with the door open."} {:ja "彼は目覚まし時計を止めたまま寝てしまった。" :en "He fell asleep with the alarm clock stopped."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-117" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～みたいだ" :reading "〜mitai da" :meaning "Indicates similarity, resemblance or 'like'; 'it seems', 'appears', 'looks like'." :short "Indicates similarity, resemblance or 'like'; 'it seems', 'appears', 'looks like'." :long "The ～みたいだ grammar point is used to express that something appears, seems or resembles something else. It can be used with nouns, verbs, い-adjectives, and な-adjectives. It can be translated as 'it seems', 'appears', or 'looks like' in English." :formation "Verb-casual + みたいだ, い-Adjective + みたいだ, な-Adjective + みたいだ, Noun + みたいだ" :examples [{:ja "彼女が病気のみたいだ。" :en "She seems to be sick."} {:ja "このドレスは高いみたいだ。" :en "This dress seems to be expensive."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-118" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～も" :reading "〜mo" :meaning "Indicates 'also', 'too', or 'as well'." :short "Indicates 'also', 'too', or 'as well'." :long "The ～も grammar point is used to express that something is also true, or to add some additional information to the previous statement. It can be translated as 'also', 'too', or 'as well' in English. The grammar point can be used with verbs, adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + も, い-Adjective + も, な-Adjective + も, Noun + も" :examples [{:ja "私もお寿司が好きです。" :en "I also like sushi."} {:ja "彼女は英語も日本語も話せます。" :en "She can speak both English and Japanese."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-119" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～ようだ" :reading "〜you da" :meaning "Expresses appearance, similarity or inference; 'it seems', 'it looks like', 'it appears'." :short "Expresses appearance, similarity or inference; 'it seems', 'it looks like', 'it appears'." :long "The ～ようだ grammar point is used to express appearance, similarity or inference about a situation or action. It can be translated as 'it seems', 'it looks like', or 'it appears' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-て form + ようだ, い-Adjective (drop い) + ようだ, な-Adjective + のようだ, Noun + のようだ" :examples [{:ja "彼は元気がないようだ。" :en "He seems to be lacking energy."} {:ja "この料理は美味しいようだ。" :en "This dish seems to be delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-120" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "～んです" :reading "〜n desu" :meaning "Provide an explanation or reason; 'it is that', 'the thing is', 'the reason is'." :short "Provide an explanation or reason; 'it is that', 'the thing is', 'the reason is'." :long "The ～んです grammar point is used to provide an explanation or reason for a situation or action. It can be translated as 'it is that', 'the thing is', or 'the reason is' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + んです, い-Adjective + んです, な-Adjective + なんです, Noun + なんです" :examples [{:ja "遅れたんですが、電車が遅れました。" :en "I was late because the train was delayed."} {:ja "疲れたんです。" :en "I'm tired (that's why)."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-121" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "文A。そのうえ 文B。" :reading "" :meaning "Shows additional information; 'furthermore', 'besides', 'in addition'." :short "Shows additional information; 'furthermore', 'besides', 'in addition'." :long "This grammar point is used to connect two sentences, where the first sentence 文A provides some information, and the second sentence 文B adds additional related information. It can be translated as 'furthermore', 'besides', or 'in addition' in English." :formation "Sentence A + そのうえ + Sentence B + 。" :examples [{:ja "このソフトウェアは使いやすい。そのうえ、価格も手ごろだ。" :en "This software is easy to use. Furthermore, the price is reasonable."} {:ja "彼女は美人だ。そのうえ、頭もいい。" :en "She is beautiful. In addition, she is smart."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-122" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "文A。それで 文B" :reading "Bun A. Sorede Bun B" :meaning "Used to connect two sentences; 'So', 'That's why', 'Therefore'." :short "Used to connect two sentences; 'So', 'That's why', 'Therefore'." :long "The grammar point 文A。それで 文B is used to connect two sentences and show that the second sentence (文B) is a result or consequence of the first sentence (文A). It can be translated as 'so', 'that's why', or 'therefore' in English." :formation "Sentence A。それで Sentence B。" :examples [{:ja "今日は暑いです。それで、エアコンをつけました。" :en "It is hot today. So, I turned on the air conditioner."} {:ja "電車が遅れました。それで、仕事に遅れてしまいました。" :en "The train was delayed. That's why I was late for work."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-123" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "文A。それに 文B" :reading "Bun A. Soreni Bun B" :meaning "Express 'in addition to; besides,' used to include additional information." :short "Express 'in addition to; besides,' used to include additional information." :long "This grammar point 文A。それに 文B is used to express an additional statement on what was mentioned earlier. It can be translated as 'in addition to,' 'besides,' or 'on top of that,' in English. It connects two sentences by highlighting supplementary information, opinions, or reasons." :formation "Sentence A + それに + Sentence B" :examples [{:ja "彼は頭がいい。それに、スポーツも得意だ。" :en "He is smart. In addition, he is good at sports."} {:ja "このケーキはおいしい。それに、値段も安い。" :en "This cake is delicious. Besides, the price is also cheap."}]} {:id "grammar-n4-124" :track :grammar :level "N4" :display "文A。だから 文B" :reading "Bun A. Dakara Bun B" :meaning "Express reason or cause; 'because', 'so', 'therefore'." :short "Express reason or cause; 'because', 'so', 'therefore'." :long "The 文A。だから 文B grammar point is used to indicate that the reason or cause of an action or event expressed in the second sentence (文B) is a result or consequence of the situation described in the first sentence (文A). It can be translated as 'because', 'so', or 'therefore' in English." :formation "Sentence A + だから + Sentence B" :examples [{:ja "明日は試験だ。だから今日勉強しなければならない。" :en "Tomorrow is the exam, so I have to study today."} {:ja "この料理は簡単だ。だから初心者でも作れる。" :en "This dish is simple, so even beginners can make it."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-001" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "A その上 B" :reading "A sono ue B" :meaning "Used to express 'in addition to', 'besides', or 'furthermore' when adding information or making a suggestion." :short "Used to express 'in addition to', 'besides', or 'furthermore' when adding information or making a suggestion." :long "The grammar point A その上 B is used to connect two statements, where B is an additional piece of information, a suggestion or reason that is related to statement A. The expression can be translated as 'in addition to', 'besides', or 'furthermore'." :formation "A [sentence/clause] + その上 + B [sentence/clause]" :examples [{:ja "彼は頭が良い。その上、スポーツも得意だ。" :en "He is smart. In addition, he is also good at sports."} {:ja "このカフェは雰囲気がいい。その上、料理も美味しい。" :en "This cafe has a nice atmosphere. Besides, the food is delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-002" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～うちに" :reading "〜uchi ni" :meaning "Express 'while', 'before', or 'during' a certain condition or situation." :short "Express 'while', 'before', or 'during' a certain condition or situation." :long "The ～うちに grammar point is used to express the idea of doing something while a certain condition or situation is still present or before it changes. It can be translated as 'while', 'before', or 'during' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, or な-adjective." :formation "Verb-てform + いる + うちに, い-Adjective + い + うちに, な-Adjective + な + うちに" :examples [{:ja "若いうちに、いろいろな経験をしておくことが大切です。" :en "It is important to have various experiences while you are young."} {:ja "雨が降っていないうちに、買い物に行こう。" :en "Let's go shopping before it starts raining."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-003" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～うとした" :reading "〜uto shita" :meaning "Express an action someone tried to do but couldn't; 'tried to', 'attempted to'." :short "Express an action someone tried to do but couldn't; 'tried to', 'attempted to'." :long "The ～うとした grammar point is used to express an action that someone tried to do but couldn't complete, for various reasons. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with verbs. This grammar point can be translated as 'tried to' or 'attempted to' in English." :formation "Verb-う stem + とした" :examples [{:ja "電話に出ようとしたが、相手は切ってしまった。" :en "I tried to answer the phone, but the other person hung up."} {:ja "彼は大声で話そうとしたが、言葉が出なかった。" :en "He tried to speak loudly, but no words came out."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-004" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～おかげで" :reading "〜okagede" :meaning "Expresses gratitude or shows a positive result due to a certain factor; 'thanks to', 'because of'." :short "Expresses gratitude or shows a positive result due to a certain factor; 'thanks to', 'because of'." :long "The ～おかげで grammar point is used to express gratitude or show a positive outcome resulting from a certain factor, event or person. It can be translated as 'thanks to' or 'because of' in English. The formation is straightforward and can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + おかげで, い-Adjective + おかげで, な-Adjective + だおかげで, Noun + のおかげで" :examples [{:ja "お母さんのおかげで、私は元気になりました。" :en "Thanks to my mother, I became healthy."} {:ja "先生の助けのおかげで、試験に合格しました。" :en "Thanks to the teacher's help, I passed the exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-005" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～かけ" :reading "〜kake" :meaning "Expresses the meaning of being in the middle of doing something or of something being partway done." :short "Expresses the meaning of being in the middle of doing something or of something being partway done." :long "The ～かけ grammar point is used to indicate that something is in the process of being done or is partway completed. This is often used with verbs to describe an action that is not yet finished or is ongoing. The verb should be in its ます-form, and かけ is added after removing the ます." :formation "Verb-ますstem + かけ" :examples [{:ja "彼は食事を食べかけです。" :en "He is in the middle of eating his meal."} {:ja "この本は読みかけで、まだ終わっていません。" :en "This book is partway read, and it is not yet finished."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-006" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～かなあ" :reading "〜kanaa" :meaning "Expressing uncertainty or a mild wish; 'I wonder', 'maybe'." :short "Expressing uncertainty or a mild wish; 'I wonder', 'maybe'." :long "The ～かなあ grammar point is used to express uncertainty or a mild wish. It can be translated as 'I wonder' or 'maybe' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + かなあ, い-Adjective + かなあ, な-Adjective + だかなあ, Noun + だかなあ" :examples [{:ja "明日晴れるかなあ。" :en "I wonder if it will be sunny tomorrow."} {:ja "このスープ、辛いかなあ。" :en "I wonder if this soup is spicy."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-007" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ないで" :reading "〜naide" :meaning "Express 'without doing ~' or 'without ~ing'." :short "Express 'without doing ~' or 'without ~ing'." :long "The ～ないで grammar point is used to express the idea of 'without doing something' or 'without ~ing' in English. It is formed by attaching the ない-form of the verb and adding で (de). This is used for expressing when an action is not taken or avoided, without interfering with the main action." :formation "Verb-ない-form + で" :examples [{:ja "会わないでその電話をかけました。" :en "I made the phone call without meeting them."} {:ja "雨が降るのを待たないで、家を出ました。" :en "I left the house without waiting for it to rain."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-008" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～から～にかけて" :reading "〜kara 〜ni kakete" :meaning "Expresses a range of something, such as time or space; 'from...to', 'between'." :short "Expresses a range of something, such as time or space; 'from...to', 'between'." :long "The ～から～にかけて grammar point is used to show a range, whether it is a range of time, space, or another measure. It can be translated as 'from...to' or 'between' in English. This grammar point is commonly used with nouns indicating time or place." :formation "Noun (time, place, or measure) + から + Noun (time, place or measure) + にかけて" :examples [{:ja "午前８時から午後５時にかけて働いています。" :en "I work from 8 am to 5 pm."} {:ja "この地域は、北から南にかけて狭いです。" :en "This area is narrow from north to south."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-009" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～かわりに" :reading "〜kawari ni" :meaning "In place of; instead of; as a substitute for." :short "In place of; instead of; as a substitute for." :long "The ～かわりに grammar point is used to express doing something in place of or as a substitute for something else. It can be translated as 'instead of', 'in place of', or 'as a substitute for' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + かわりに, い-Adjective + かわりに, な-Adjective + だかわりに, Noun + だかわりに" :examples [{:ja "映画を見るかわりに、本を読んだ。" :en "Instead of watching a movie, I read a book."} {:ja "お酒を飲むかわりに、水を飲んでください。" :en "Please drink water instead of alcohol."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-010" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～きり" :reading "〜kiri" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'only', 'since', or 'after'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'only', 'since', or 'after'." :long "The ～きり grammar point is used to express the idea of 'only', 'since', or 'after'. It indicates that a certain action or state continues without any change since a specific point in time or after a certain event. It is commonly used with verbs in the past tense, but can also be used with other forms." :formation "Verb-past + きり, Verb-てform + きり (less common), Noun + きり" :examples [{:ja "彼は出かけたきり帰ってきません。" :en "He went out and hasn't come back since."} {:ja "昨日コーヒーを飲んだきり、何も飲まなかった。" :en "I haven't had anything to drink since the coffee I had yesterday."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-011" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～くせに" :reading "〜kuse ni" :meaning "Used to express criticism or disapproval; 'even though', 'despite', 'in spite of'." :short "Used to express criticism or disapproval; 'even though', 'despite', 'in spite of'." :long "The ～くせに grammar point is used to express criticism or disapproval in a sentence. It can be translated as 'even though', 'despite', or 'in spite of' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + くせに, い-Adjective + くせに, な-Adjective + な/のくせに, Noun + のくせに" :examples [{:ja "彼はお金持ちのくせに、ケチです。" :en "Even though he's rich, he's stingy."} {:ja "この部屋が汚いくせに、文句を言うな。" :en "Don't complain even though this room is dirty. (criticizing)"}]} {:id "grammar-n3-012" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～くらい" :reading "〜kurai" :meaning "Expresses an approximate quantity or degree; 'about', 'approximately', or 'to the extent of'." :short "Expresses an approximate quantity or degree; 'about', 'approximately', or 'to the extent of'." :long "The ～くらい grammar point is used to express an approximate quantity, degree, or extent. It can be translated as 'about', 'approximately', or 'to the extent of' in English. The formation is straightforward and can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + くらい, い-Adjective + くらい, な-Adjective + くらい, Noun + くらい" :examples [{:ja "１０分くらい待ちましょう。" :en "Let's wait for about 10 minutes."} {:ja "彼女はその映画を５回くらい見ました。" :en "She watched that movie about five times."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-013" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～くらい～は～ない" :reading "〜kurai 〜wa 〜nai" :meaning "Express the least one can do; 'at least', 'the least', 'no less than'." :short "Express the least one can do; 'at least', 'the least', 'no less than'." :long "The ～くらい～は～ない grammar point is used to emphasize the minimum expectation in a situation or the least one can do. It can be translated as 'at least', 'the least', or 'no less than' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb or an adjective." :formation "Verb-casual (non-past) + くらい + は + Verb-casual (negative), Adjective (non-past) + くらい + は + Adjective (negative)" :examples [{:ja "週に一度くらいはジムに行かないと。" :en "I have to go to the gym at least once a week."} {:ja "毎日くらいは歯を磨かないと。" :en "I have to brush my teeth at least every day."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-014" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～こそ" :reading "〜koso" :meaning "Emphasizes the importance or uniqueness of something or someone; 'precisely', 'exactly', 'it is...', 'of all...'." :short "Emphasizes the importance or uniqueness of something or someone; 'precisely', 'exactly', 'it is...', 'of all...'." :long "The ～こそ grammar point is used to emphasize the importance, uniqueness, or the most suitable thing or person in a specific situation. It can be translated as 'precisely', 'exactly', or 'it is...' in English. In practice, it most often appears after a noun (e.g. 今日こそ), or with verbs in the て-form (e.g. 頑張ってこそ)." :formation "Noun + こそ, (Verb-て + こそ)" :examples [{:ja "今日こそ勉強しなければなりません。" :en "Today, of all days, I must study."} {:ja "あなたこそ私の一番の友達です。" :en "You are precisely my best friend."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-015" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～こと" :reading "〜koto" :meaning "Express an action in a nominal (noun-like) form or state fact or experience." :short "Express an action in a nominal (noun-like) form or state fact or experience." :long "The ～こと grammar point is used to turn a verb or verb phrase into a noun or noun phrase. This allows the verb to be talked about as a general concept, fact, or experience. The formation varies slightly depending on whether it is used with a verb or a verb phrase." :formation "Verb-plain form + こと, Verb-phrase in plain form + こと" :examples [{:ja "日本語を話すことができます。" :en "I can speak Japanese."} {:ja "毎日運動することは健康に良いです。" :en "Exercising every day is good for your health."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-016" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ことだ" :reading "〜koto da" :meaning "Expresses advice or recommendation; 'should', 'it is important', 'it is necessary'." :short "Expresses advice or recommendation; 'should', 'it is important', 'it is necessary'." :long "The ～ことだ grammar point is used to express a piece of advice or recommendation to someone. It can be translated as 'should', 'it is important', or 'it is necessary' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-ますstem + ことだ, い-Adjective + ことだ, な-Adjective + なことだ, Noun + のことだ" :examples [{:ja "毎日運動することだ。" :en "You should exercise every day."} {:ja "健康のために、野菜をたくさん食べることだ。" :en "For your health, you should eat lots of vegetables."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-017" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ことにしている" :reading "〜koto ni shite iru" :meaning "Express a personal rule or habit; 'I make it a rule to', 'I always make sure to'." :short "Express a personal rule or habit; 'I make it a rule to', 'I always make sure to'." :long "The ～ことにしている grammar point is used to express a personal rule, habit, or decision that the speaker has made for themselves. It can be translated as 'I make it a rule to' or 'I always make sure to' in English. This grammar point is formed by attaching ことにしている to the casual form of a verb." :formation "Verb-casual + ことにしている" :examples [{:ja "毎日運動することにしている。" :en "I make it a rule to exercise every day."} {:ja "夜遅くまで働かないことにしている。" :en "I make it a rule not to work late into the night."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-018" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ことになっている" :reading "〜koto ni natte iru" :meaning "Describes something that is decided, arranged, or expected." :short "Describes something that is decided, arranged, or expected." :long "The ～ことになっている grammar point is used to express that something has been decided or arranged, or is expected as a rule or custom. It can be translated as 'it is decided that...', 'it is arranged that...', or 'it is expected to...' in English. The formation is used with verbs in their dictionary form." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ことになっている" :examples [{:ja "この会社では、20時には仕事を終えることになっている。" :en "In this company, it is decided that work will end at 8 pm."} {:ja "日本では、18歳以上の人が選挙に投票することになっている。" :en "In Japan, it is decided that people 18 years old and above will vote in elections."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-019" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ことは…が" :reading "～koto wa... ga" :meaning "Indicates that while something may be true or valid, there is another contrasting point." :short "Indicates that while something may be true or valid, there is another contrasting point." :long "The ～ことは…が grammar point is used to express that although something might be true, there is another point or fact that is being contrasted with it. It can be translated as 'it may be true but...', 'it is that... however...', etc. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + ことは + が, い-Adjective + ことは + が, な-Adjective + だ + ことは + が, Noun + だ + ことは + が" :examples [{:ja "この映画は面白いことは面白いが、ちょっと長すぎる。" :en "This movie may be interesting, but it is a bit too long."} {:ja "彼は親切なことは親切だが、少し無口だ。" :en "He may be kind, but he is a bit quiet."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-020" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ことはない" :reading "〜koto wa nai" :meaning "Expresses that something doesn't need to be done or there's no occasion to do it; 'don't have to', 'not necessary'." :short "Expresses that something doesn't need to be done or there's no occasion to do it; 'don't have to', 'not necessary'." :long "The ～ことはない grammar point is used to express that doing something is not necessary, or there's no occasion to do it. It implies that although a certain action might not be needed, it does not mean it is forbidden. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ことはない, い-Adjective + ことはない, な-Adjective + であることはない, Noun + であることはない" :examples [{:ja "お金があるから、無理に貯金することはない。" :en "Since I have enough money, there's no need to force myself to save."} {:ja "この本は簡単だから、辞書を使うことはない。" :en "Since this book is easy, there's no need to use a dictionary."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-021" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～さ" :reading "〜sa" :meaning "Express the degree or extent of a quality; '-ness', 'degree of'." :short "Express the degree or extent of a quality; '-ness', 'degree of'." :long "The ～さ grammar point is used to express the degree or extent of a certain quality or characteristic. It is often translated as '-ness' or 'degree of' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with an い-adjective or a な-adjective." :formation "い-Adjective (drop い) + さ, な-Adjective + さ" :examples [{:ja "この料理は辛さが強い。" :en "The spiciness of this dish is strong."} {:ja "彼女の美しさは皆に憧れられる。" :en "Her beauty is admired by everyone."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-022" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～さえ" :reading "～sae" :meaning "Emphasizes a specific item or action; 'even', 'if only', 'as long as'." :short "Emphasizes a specific item or action; 'even', 'if only', 'as long as'." :long "The ～さえ grammar point is used to emphasize a specific item, person, or action, implying that it alone is sufficient, remarkable, or an exception. It can be translated as 'even', 'if only', or 'as long as' in English. The formation differs slightly depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Noun + さえ, Verb-casual + さえ, い-Adjective + さえ, な-Adjective + でさえ" :examples [{:ja "お金さえあれば、世界中を旅行できる。" :en "If only I have money, I can travel around the world."} {:ja "彼は英語さえ話せれば、その仕事ができるだろう。" :en "As long as he can speak English, he can do that job."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-023" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～しかない" :reading "〜shika nai" :meaning "Express the idea of 'only', 'no other option but', 'nothing else but'." :short "Express the idea of 'only', 'no other option but', 'nothing else but'." :long "The ～しかない grammar point is used to emphasize that there are no other options or possibilities, and the subject must follow a specified action or state. It can be translated as 'only', 'no other option but', or 'nothing else but' in English." :formation "Verb-casual + しかない, い-Adjective + しかない, な-Adjective + しかない, Noun + しかない" :examples [{:ja "この問題を解決する方法は一つしかない。" :en "There is only one way to solve this problem."} {:ja "彼が出て行ったら、泣くしかない。" :en "If he leaves, there's nothing else to do but cry."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-024" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "すこしも〜ない" :reading "sukoshimo~nai" :meaning "Express a negative state or action; 'not at all', 'none whatsoever'." :short "Express a negative state or action; 'not at all', 'none whatsoever'." :long "The すこしも〜ない grammar point is used to emphasize a negative state or action, indicating that something does not exist or happen even a little bit. It can be translated as 'not at all', 'none whatsoever', or 'not one bit' in English. This expression is used with negative verbs." :formation "すこしも + Verb-negative" :examples [{:ja "彼はすこしも悲しそうに見えません。" :en "He doesn't look sad at all."} {:ja "この部屋はすこしも寒くない。" :en "This room is not cold at all."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-025" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ずに" :reading "〜zu ni" :meaning "Express 'without doing' something or 'not doing' something." :short "Express 'without doing' something or 'not doing' something." :long "The ～ずに grammar point is used to express the meaning of not doing something or doing an action without doing another action first. It is similar to the negative ないで form (～ないで) but ～ずに is considered slightly more formal." :formation "Verb-ない form (Remove ない) + ずに" :examples [{:ja "彼は笑わずに話を聞いていました。" :en "He was listening to the story without laughing."} {:ja "食べずに寝るのは良くないです。" :en "It's not good to go to bed without eating."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-026" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～せいで" :reading "〜sei de" :meaning "Indicates that something or someone caused an unfortunate result." :short "Indicates that something or someone caused an unfortunate result." :long "The ～せいで grammar point is used to express that something or someone is the reason or cause of an unfortunate result or situation. It can be translated as 'because of' or 'due to' in English." :formation "Verb-casual + せいで, い-Adjective + せいで, な-Adjective + なせいで, Noun + のせいで" :examples [{:ja "雨のせいで試合が中止になりました。" :en "The game was cancelled because of the rain."} {:ja "彼の遅刻のせいで、みんな待たされました。" :en "Everyone had to wait because of his lateness."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-027" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～せてください" :reading "〜sete kudasai" :meaning "Requesting someone to allow or let you do something (causative request)." :short "Requesting someone to allow or let you do something (causative request)." :long "The せてください grammar point is used to request permission or ask someone to allow you to do something. It is a polite way of making a request and can be translated as 'please let me...' or 'please allow me...' in English. It is formed by using the causative form of the verb (Verb-させる) in the て-form, plus ください (e.g. 帰らせる → 帰らせて → 帰らせてください)." :formation "Verb-causative (させ) + て + ください" :examples [{:ja "もう一度説明させてください。" :en "Please let me explain it once more."} {:ja "帰らせてください。" :en "Please let me go home."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-028" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～そのために" :reading "〜sono tame ni" :meaning "For the purpose of; because of; in order to." :short "For the purpose of; because of; in order to." :long "The ～そのために grammar point is used to convey the purpose or reason behind an action or situation. It can be translated as 'for that reason', 'for the purpose of', or 'in order to' in English. It connects two sentences, where the first sentence gives a goal or reason, and the second sentence shows the resulting action." :formation "Sentence/phrase 1 + そのために + Sentence/phrase 2" :examples [{:ja "試験に合格するために、毎日勉強しています。そのために、早く寝て早く起きるようにしています。" :en "I study every day to pass the exam. For that reason, I go to bed early and wake up early."} {:ja "健康的な生活を送りたい。そのために、ジムに通って運動しています。" :en "I want to lead a healthy lifestyle. For that reason, I go to the gym and exercise."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-029" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～その結果" :reading "〜sono kekka" :meaning "Shows the result or consequence of a previous action or situation; 'as a result', 'in consequence'." :short "Shows the result or consequence of a previous action or situation; 'as a result', 'in consequence'." :long "The ～その結果 grammar point is used to show the result, outcome, or consequence of a previous action or situation. It can be translated as 'as a result', 'in consequence', or 'therefore' in English. It is typically placed between two statements to link cause and effect." :formation "Situation/ACTION 1 + その結果 + RESULT" :examples [{:ja "彼女は毎日練習した。その結果、試合に勝った。" :en "She practiced every day. As a result, she won the match."} {:ja "会社が倒産した。その結果、彼は仕事を失った。" :en "The company went bankrupt. As a result, he lost his job."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-030" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～それと～" :reading "〜sore to〜" :meaning "Used to link two or more items; 'and', 'also'." :short "Used to link two or more items; 'and', 'also'." :long "The ～それと～ grammar point is used to connect two or more items or actions, similar to how 'and' or 'also' is used in English. The expression それと can appear at the start of a new sentence or between items in a list to add another point." :formation "Item1 + それと + Item2" :examples [{:ja "買い物に行くつもりです。それと、郵便局にも行かなくては。" :en "I plan to go shopping, and I also have to go to the post office."} {:ja "今日はタコスを作りました。それと、サラダも作りました。" :en "Today, I made tacos, and I also made a salad."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-031" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～？それとも～？" :reading "～? sore tomo ～?" :meaning "Presents two alternatives or choices; 'or', 'either...or'." :short "Presents two alternatives or choices; 'or', 'either...or'." :long "The ～？それとも～？ grammar point is used to present two alternatives or choices in a single sentence. It is similar to 'or' or 'either...or' in English. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, or nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + ？それとも + Verb-casual + ？, etc." :examples [{:ja "コーヒーを飲む？それとも紅茶を飲む？" :en "Do you want to drink coffee? Or tea?"} {:ja "この映画は面白い？それともつまらない？" :en "Is this movie interesting? Or is it boring?"}]} {:id "grammar-n3-032" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～だけしか" :reading "～dake shika" :meaning "Emphasizes the scarcity or insufficiency of something; 'only', 'nothing but'." :short "Emphasizes the scarcity or insufficiency of something; 'only', 'nothing but'." :long "The ～だけしか grammar point is used to emphasize that there is nothing except what is mentioned, and it requires a negative verb. It can be translated as 'only' or 'nothing but' in English." :formation "Noun/Quantity + だけ + しか + Negative Verb" :examples [{:ja "今日は1時間だけしか勉強できなかった。" :en "Today, I could study for only one hour."} {:ja "彼はカレーだけしか食べない。" :en "He eats only curry."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-033" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "だけど" :reading "dakedo" :meaning "Expresses a contrast or contradiction; 'but', 'however', 'even though'." :short "Expresses a contrast or contradiction; 'but', 'however', 'even though'." :long "The だけど grammar point is used to express a contrast or contradiction between two statements. In written or spoken Japanese, だけど is often placed at the start of a new sentence (after a period) to mean 'however'. Within a single sentence, speakers often just say けど without だ." :formation "Sentence 1 + だけど + Sentence 2, or Sentence 1 + 。だけど、Sentence 2" :examples [{:ja "試験に合格したけど、まだ勉強しなければならない。" :en "I passed the exam, but I still have to study."} {:ja "この部屋は広いけど、家賃が高いです。" :en "This room is spacious, but the rent is high."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-034" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～たて" :reading "～tate" :meaning "Indicates an action that has just been completed; 'freshly', 'just'." :short "Indicates an action that has just been completed; 'freshly', 'just'." :long "The ～たて grammar point is used to express that an action has just been completed, and the result or condition is still fresh or new. This is often used with verbs like 焼く (bake), 作る (make), or 洗う (wash)." :formation "Verb-stem + たて" :examples [{:ja "このパンは焼きたてで、まだ温かいです。" :en "This bread is freshly baked and still warm."} {:ja "作りたてのお弁当が美味しい。" :en "A freshly made bento is delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-035" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～たとえ～ても" :reading "〜tatoe〜temo" :meaning "Express a condition with 'even if'; 'no matter how'." :short "Express a condition with 'even if'; 'no matter how'." :long "The ～たとえ～ても grammar point is used to express a hypothetical or conditional situation where the outcome remains the same even if the condition is met. It can be translated as 'even if' or 'no matter how' in English." :formation "たとえ + Verb-casual + ても, たとえ + い-Adjective + くても, たとえ + な-Adjective + であっても, たとえ + Noun + であっても" :examples [{:ja "たとえ雨が降っても、出かけましょう。" :en "Even if it rains, let's go out."} {:ja "たとえ彼が怒っても、本当のことを言いましょう。" :en "Even if he gets angry, let's tell the truth."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-036" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～たところ" :reading "〜ta tokoro" :meaning "Expresses the result or outcome of an action; 'after', 'just when', 'upon'." :short "Expresses the result or outcome of an action; 'after', 'just when', 'upon'." :long "The ～たところ grammar point is used to indicate the result or outcome right after performing an action. It can be translated as 'after', 'just when', or 'upon' in English. It is formed by attaching ところ to the past tense (た-form) of verbs." :formation "Verb-た form + ところ" :examples [{:ja "宿題をしたところ、先生からもう一つ課題が出された。" :en "Just when I finished my homework, the teacher gave us another assignment."} {:ja "友達に会ったところ、彼が海外に行くと言った。" :en "Upon meeting my friend, he told me he's going abroad."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-037" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～たとたん" :reading "〜ta totan" :meaning "Express the moment something happens; 'as soon as', 'the moment', 'the instant'." :short "Express the moment something happens; 'as soon as', 'the moment', 'the instant'." :long "The ～たとたん grammar point is used to express that something occurs immediately at the exact moment another action is completed. It can be translated as 'as soon as', 'the moment', or 'the instant' in English. It is attached to the past tense (た-form) of a verb." :formation "Verb-た form + とたん" :examples [{:ja "ドアを開けたとたん、猫が逃げ出した。" :en "As soon as I opened the door, the cat ran away."} {:ja "電話をかけたとたん、電池が切れた。" :en "The moment I made the phone call, the battery died."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-038" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～たびに" :reading "〜tabi ni" :meaning "Express 'every time' or 'whenever'." :short "Express 'every time' or 'whenever'." :long "The ～たびに grammar point is used to express that an action or event occurs every time or whenever something else happens. It can be translated as 'every time', 'whenever', or 'each time' in English. Typically, you attach たびに to a verb in its dictionary form, or to a noun plus の." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + たびに, Noun + のたびに" :examples [{:ja "彼女に会うたびに、心が弾む。" :en "Every time I meet her, my heart leaps."} {:ja "雨が降るたびに、懐かしい思い出がよみがえる。" :en "Whenever it rains, nostalgic memories come back."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-039" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～だものだ" :reading "〜da mono da" :meaning "Express the reason or cause, often used to justify something." :short "Express the reason or cause, often used to justify something." :long "The ～だものだ grammar point is used to explain a reason or cause in a subjective, explanatory tone (e.g. 'because... after all'). Often, you'll see it used as ～だものだから ('because... after all')." :formation "Verb-casual + んだものだ, い-Adjective + んだものだ, な-Adjective + なんだものだ, Noun + なんだものだ (often with から as んだものだから)" :examples [{:ja "疲れたんだものだから、休みたい。" :en "I want to rest because I'm tired."} {:ja "お腹が空いたんだものだから、何か食べたい。" :en "I want to eat something because I'm hungry."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-040" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ちゃった" :reading "〜chatta" :meaning "Express an unintentional action or a regretful action; 'ended up doing', 'did accidentally'." :short "Express an unintentional action or a regretful action; 'ended up doing', 'did accidentally'." :long "The ～ちゃった grammar point is the informal, colloquial version of ～てしまった. It expresses unintentional or regretful actions and can be translated as 'ended up doing' or 'did accidentally'." :formation "Verb-て/で form → ちゃった/じゃった (e.g. 食べてしまった → 食べちゃった)" :examples [{:ja "忘れ物をしちゃった。" :en "I accidentally left something behind."} {:ja "寝坊して遅刻しちゃった。" :en "I overslept and ended up being late."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-041" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ついでに" :reading "〜tsuide ni" :meaning "Do something while or after completing another action; 'while', 'by the way'." :short "Do something while or after completing another action; 'while', 'by the way'." :long "The ～ついでに grammar point is used to express doing something 'on the occasion of' another action. It can mean 'while you're at it', 'by the way', or 'in passing'." :formation "Verb (dictionary or た-form) + ついでに, Noun + の + ついでに, or Verb-て form + いる + ついでに" :examples [{:ja "買い物に行くついでに、郵便局で手紙を出しましょう。" :en "Let's drop off a letter at the post office while going shopping."} {:ja "図書館で本を借りたついでに、友達に会いました。" :en "I met my friend while borrowing a book from the library."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-042" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～っけ？" :reading "〜kke?" :meaning "Used to express that one is trying to recall some information." :short "Used to express that one is trying to recall some information." :long "The ～っけ？ grammar point is added at the end of a sentence when the speaker is trying to recall something or confirm a memory. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, or nouns in casual form." :formation "Verb-casual + っけ?, い-Adjective + かったっけ?, な-Adjective/Noun + だっけ?" :examples [{:ja "彼は何時に来るっけ？" :en "What time is he coming again?"} {:ja "この映画、面白かったっけ？" :en "Was this movie interesting?"}]} {:id "grammar-n3-043" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～っぱい" :reading "〜ppai" :meaning "Expresses fullness or completeness; 'full', 'full of', 'filled with'." :short "Expresses fullness or completeness; 'full', 'full of', 'filled with'." :long "The ～っぱい grammar point is used to convey that something is filled or packed with something else. Commonly used as 『Noun + でいっぱい』 (e.g., 荷物でいっぱい) to mean 'full of X'." :formation "Noun + で + いっぱい (Note: written as いっぱい, read as 'ippai')" :examples [{:ja "部屋が荷物でいっぱいです。" :en "The room is full of luggage."} {:ja "彼女は笑顔でいっぱいでした。" :en "She was full of smiles."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-044" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～っぱなし" :reading "〜ppanashi" :meaning "Indicate a state where an action is left as is (unchanged or unfinished)." :short "Indicate a state where an action is left as is (unchanged or unfinished)." :long "The ～っぱなし grammar point is used to express that an action happened and was left in that state, unattended or unchanged. It is formed by taking the ます-stem of a verb and adding ～っぱなし." :formation "Verb-ますstem + っぱなし" :examples [{:ja "窓を開けっぱなしにすると、虫が入ってくる。" :en "If you leave the window open, bugs will come in."} {:ja "洗濯物を干しっぱなしで、雨に濡れた。" :en "I left the laundry hanging out, and it got wet in the rain."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-045" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～つまり" :reading "〜tsumari" :meaning "Summarizing, restating, or clarifying a point: 'in other words', 'that is to say'." :short "Summarizing, restating, or clarifying a point: 'in other words', 'that is to say'." :long "The ～つまり grammar point is used to restate or clarify in a concise way what has been said. It can be translated as 'in other words', 'that is to say', or 'in short'." :formation "Sentence A + つまり + Sentence B (rephrasing)" :examples [{:ja "彼は大学の教授です。つまり、専門家です。" :en "He is a university professor. In other words, he is an expert."} {:ja "今日は土曜日だ。つまり、明日は日曜日だ。" :en "Today is Saturday. That is to say, tomorrow is Sunday."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-046" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～つもりでした" :reading "〜tsumori deshita" :meaning "Expressing a past intention or plan; 'was planning to', 'meant to'." :short "Expressing a past intention or plan; 'was planning to', 'meant to'." :long "The ～つもりでした grammar point is used to express a past intention or plan that did not happen, or a past belief that turned out incorrect. It can be translated as 'was planning to', 'intended to', or 'meant to'." :formation "Verb-casual (dictionary form) + つもりでした" :examples [{:ja "昨日は友達に会うつもりでしたが、風邪を引いてしまいました。" :en "I was planning to meet my friend yesterday, but I caught a cold."} {:ja "映画を見るつもりでしたが、時間がなくなりました。" :en "I intended to watch a movie, but I ran out of time."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-047" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～てくれと" :reading "〜te kureto" :meaning "Quote someone's request (telling someone to do something)." :short "Quote someone's request (telling someone to do something)." :long "The ～てくれと grammar point is used when quoting a request or command made by someone else. It literally means 'someone said “please do …”'. Typically used as 「～てくれと頼む/言う」 to indicate being asked or told to do something." :formation "Verb-て form + くれと (言う / 頼む / 伝える, etc.)" :examples [{:ja "電話をかけてくれと言われました。" :en "I was told to make a phone call."} {:ja "この荷物を運んでくれと頼まれました。" :en "I was asked to carry this luggage."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-048" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～てごらん" :reading "〜te goran" :meaning "Suggest or invite someone to try doing something; 'Why don't you try...'." :short "Suggest or invite someone to try doing something; 'Why don't you try...'." :long "The ～てごらん grammar point is used to gently suggest or invite someone to try doing something. It is softer and more casual than ～てみて, and is used among friends or family." :formation "Verb-て form + ごらん" :examples [{:ja "そのゲームをやってごらん。" :en "Why don't you try playing that game?"} {:ja "彼に話してごらん。" :en "Try talking to him."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-049" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "ですから～" :reading "desu kara" :meaning "Expresses reason or cause; 'because', 'since', 'so'." :short "Expresses reason or cause; 'because', 'since', 'so'." :long "The ですから grammar point is used to indicate the reason or cause for a statement, often in polite speech. It can be translated as 'because', 'since', or 'so'." :formation "Sentence A + ですから + Sentence B" :examples [{:ja "今日は寒いですから、コートを着てください。" :en "It's cold today, so please wear a coat."} {:ja "この映画は面白いですから、ぜひ見てください。" :en "This movie is interesting, so please watch it."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-050" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～てはじめて" :reading "〜te hajimete" :meaning "Realization or action done for the first time; 'only after', 'not until'." :short "Realization or action done for the first time; 'only after', 'not until'." :long "The ～てはじめて grammar point indicates that something is realized or happens for the first time only after another action is completed. It can be translated as 'only after' or 'not until'." :formation "Verb-て form + はじめて" :examples [{:ja "海外に行ってはじめて、日本の良さが分かりました。" :en "Only after going abroad did I realize how great Japan is."} {:ja "彼女と別れてはじめて、彼女の大切さに気づいた。" :en "I realized her importance only after breaking up with her."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-051" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～てほしい" :reading "〜te hoshii" :meaning "Express a desire for someone else to do something; 'I want you to do...'." :short "Express a desire for someone else to do something; 'I want you to do...'." :long "The ～てほしい grammar point is used to express a desire that someone else perform an action. It is formed by attaching ほしい to the て-form of a verb. It can be translated as 'I want you to...' in English." :formation "Verb-て form + ほしい" :examples [{:ja "子供にもっと勉強してほしい。" :en "I want my child to study more."} {:ja "彼に私に電話してほしい。" :en "I want him to call me."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-052" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ても" :reading "〜temo" :meaning "'Even if', 'even though', or 'although'." :short "'Even if', 'even though', or 'although'." :long "The ～ても grammar point expresses that even if a certain condition holds, the outcome or situation doesn't change. It can be translated as 'even if' or 'even though'." :formation "Verb-て form + も, い-Adjective (～く) + ても, な-Adjective/Noun (～で) + も" :examples [{:ja "雨が降っても、運動会は開催されます。" :en "Even if it rains, the sports festival will be held."} {:ja "忙しくても、毎日勉強する時間を作る。" :en "Even though I'm busy, I make time to study every day."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-053" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～といいなあ" :reading "〜to ii naa" :meaning "Express a wish or hope; 'it would be nice if', 'I hope that'." :short "Express a wish or hope; 'it would be nice if', 'I hope that'." :long "The ～といいなあ grammar point is used to express a wish or hope for something to happen or to be in a certain way. It can be translated as 'it would be nice if' or 'I hope that' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + といいなあ, い-Adjective + といいなあ, な-Adjective + だといいなあ, Noun + だといいなあ" :examples [{:ja "明日晴れるといいなあ。" :en "I hope it's sunny tomorrow."} {:ja "この試験に合格するといいなあ。" :en "I hope I pass this exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-054" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～という" :reading "〜to iu" :meaning "Used for quoting, defining, or giving an example; 'called', 'that is to say', 'like'." :short "Used for quoting, defining, or giving an example; 'called', 'that is to say', 'like'." :long "The ～という grammar point is used to quote, define, or give an example of something. It can be translated as 'called', 'that is to say', or 'like' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Noun + という + Noun, Verb-casual + という + Noun, い-Adjective + という + Noun, な-Adjective + という + Noun" :examples [{:ja "本屋で「ハリー・ポッター」という本を買いました。" :en "I bought a book called 'Harry Potter' at the bookstore."} {:ja "昨日、美味しいというケーキを食べました。" :en "Yesterday, I ate a cake that is said to be delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-055" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ということだ" :reading "〜to iu koto da" :meaning "Express a conclusion or hearsay; 'I heard', 'it means', 'it can be said'." :short "Express a conclusion or hearsay; 'I heard', 'it means', 'it can be said'." :long "The ～ということだ grammar point is used to express a conclusion, summary, or hearsay about a certain situation or statement. It can be translated as 'I heard', 'it means', or 'it can be said' in English. This grammar point is often used with verbs, adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + ということだ, い-Adjective + ということだ, な-Adjective + だということだ, Noun + だということだ" :examples [{:ja "彼は来週結婚するということだ。" :en "I heard that he is getting married next week."} {:ja "この試験は難しいということだ。" :en "It means this exam is difficult."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-056" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～というと" :reading "〜to iu to" :meaning "Used to provide an example or characteristic related to the topic; 'when it comes to', 'speaking of'." :short "Used to provide an example or characteristic related to the topic; 'when it comes to', 'speaking of'." :long "The ～というと grammar point is used to indicate an example, characteristic, or association related to the topic being discussed. It can be translated as 'when it comes to', 'speaking of', or 'in terms of' in English." :formation "Noun + というと" :examples [{:ja "日本料理というと、寿司が思い浮かぶ。" :en "When it comes to Japanese cuisine, sushi comes to mind."} {:ja "パリというと、エッフェル塔を思い出す。" :en "Speaking of Paris, I recall the Eiffel Tower."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-057" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～というの～" :reading "〜to iu no〜" :meaning "Explain or emphasize the reason for a situation or action." :short "Explain or emphasize the reason for a situation or action." :long "The ～というの〜 grammar point is used to provide an explanation or emphasize the reason for a certain situation or action. It can be translated as 'the reason is that...', or 'it's because...'." :formation "Verb-casual + というの, い-Adjective + というの, な-Adjective + だというの, Noun + だというの" :examples [{:ja "遅刻したのは、電車が遅れたというのだ。" :en "The reason I was late is because the train was delayed."} {:ja "この部屋が汚いのは、掃除をしなかったというのだ。" :en "The reason this room is dirty is because I didn't clean it."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-058" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～というのは" :reading "〜to iu no wa" :meaning "Used to provide an explanation or definition; 'that is to say...', 'by that, I mean...'." :short "Used to provide an explanation or definition; 'that is to say...', 'by that, I mean...'." :long "The ～というのは grammar point is used to provide an explanation or definition of the preceding statement or word. It can be translated as 'that is to say...', 'by that, I mean...', or 'what I mean by...'." :formation "Phrase/sentence + というのは + explanation/definition" :examples [{:ja "日本に行くというのは、日本に旅行することです。" :en "Going to Japan, that is to say, traveling to Japan."} {:ja "この問題は難しいというのは、解決に時間がかかるということです。" :en "When I say this problem is difficult, I mean it takes time to solve."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-059" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～というより" :reading "〜to iu yori" :meaning "Express a comparison with preference; 'rather than', 'more like', 'instead of'." :short "Express a comparison with preference; 'rather than', 'more like', 'instead of'." :long "The ～というより grammar point is used to express a comparison where one description is more accurate than another. Translated as 'rather than', 'more like', or 'instead of'." :formation "Verb-casual + というより, い-Adjective + というより, な-Adjective + だというより, Noun + だというより" :examples [{:ja "この部屋は狭いというより、居心地がいい。" :en "Rather than saying this room is small, it's more like it's cozy."} {:ja "彼は怖いというより、頼りがいがある。" :en "Instead of saying he's scary, he's more like a reliable person."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-060" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～といっても" :reading "〜to ittemo" :meaning "Express the meaning 'even though', 'although', 'but'." :short "Express the meaning 'even though', 'although', 'but'." :long "The ～といっても grammar point is used to show a contrast or clarify that 'even though' something is the case, it does not necessarily lead to the expected result. It can be translated as 'even though', 'although', or 'but'." :formation "Verb-casual + といっても, い-Adjective + といっても, な-Adjective + だといっても, Noun + だといっても" :examples [{:ja "彼は有名だといっても、みんなに知られているわけではありません。" :en "Even though he's famous, it doesn't mean everyone knows him."} {:ja "このスープは辛いといっても、私にはちょうどいいです。" :en "Although this soup is spicy, it's just right for me."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-061" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～とおり" :reading "〜toori" :meaning "Expresses that something is done 'as is', 'as stated', or 'as instructed'." :short "Expresses that something is done 'as is', 'as stated', or 'as instructed'." :long "The ～とおり grammar point is used to express that something is done exactly as stated, instructed, or shown. It is commonly used with verbs and nouns, and can be translated as 'just like', 'as per', or 'according to'." :formation "Verb-て form + とおり, Noun + のとおり" :examples [{:ja "先生が言ったとおり、宿題をやりました。" :en "I did my homework exactly as the teacher said."} {:ja "地図に書いてあるとおりに行けば、駅に着きます。" :en "If you follow what's written on the map, you'll arrive at the station."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-062" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～とく" :reading "〜toku" :meaning "Express doing something in advance or for future convenience (casual form of ～ておく)." :short "Express doing something in advance or for future convenience (casual form of ～ておく)." :long "The ～とく grammar point (from ～ておく) is used to express doing something in advance or preparing for something. It implies you're performing an action now to avoid inconvenience later. It is a casual, colloquial contraction of ～ておく." :formation "Verb-て form + とく (e.g., 食べておく→食べとく, 読んでおく→読んどく)" :examples [{:ja "夜遅くなるから、今晩のご飯を作っとく。" :en "Since it will be late tonight, I'll prepare dinner in advance."} {:ja "傘が必要かもしれないので、持っておく。" :en "I might need an umbrella, so I'll bring one along (just in case)."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-063" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ところが" :reading "〜tokoro ga" :meaning "Expresses a contrast or unexpected outcome; 'however', 'but', 'even though'." :short "Expresses a contrast or unexpected outcome; 'however', 'but', 'even though'." :long "The ～ところが grammar point is used to express a contrast or an unexpected outcome. It can be translated as 'however', 'but', or 'even though' in English. It emphasizes a situation that contradicts what was expected." :formation "Phrase 1 (expected situation) + ところが + Phrase 2 (unexpected result)" :examples [{:ja "彼はいつも遅刻するところが、今日は早く来ました。" :en "He's always late, but today he arrived early."} {:ja "昨日は暑かったところが、今日は寒くなりました。" :en "Yesterday was hot, but today it got cold."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-064" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ところだった" :reading "〜tokoro datta" :meaning "Expresses a near miss or a situation that almost happened; 'was just about to', 'almost', 'nearly'." :short "Expresses a near miss or a situation that almost happened; 'was just about to', 'almost', 'nearly'." :long "The ～ところだった grammar point is used to express a situation that almost occurred or something that the speaker was just about to do. It can be translated as 'was just about to', 'almost', or 'nearly' in English. Typically, it is formed with the dictionary form or ている form of a verb + ところだった." :formation "Verb (dictionary form or ている form) + ところだった" :examples [{:ja "もう少しで遅れるところだった。" :en "I was just about to be late."} {:ja "彼が危うく事故にあうところだった。" :en "He almost had an accident."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-065" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "ところで" :reading "tokorode" :meaning "Used for changing the topic or bringing up a new topic; 'by the way', 'speaking of'." :short "Used for changing the topic or bringing up a new topic; 'by the way', 'speaking of'." :long "The ところで (tokorode) grammar point is used to change the topic of conversation or introduce a new topic. It can be translated as 'by the way' or 'speaking of' in English. It is often used when the speaker wants to ask a question or share some information on a different subject. It can be placed at the beginning or in the middle of a sentence." :formation "ところで + new topic or question" :examples [{:ja "ところで、昨日の宿題はもう終わりましたか？" :en "By the way, did you finish yesterday's homework?"} {:ja "彼はプールで泳いでいます。ところで、お昼ご飯は何にしますか？" :en "He is swimming in the pool. By the way, what do you want for lunch?"}]} {:id "grammar-n3-066" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～としたら" :reading "〜to shitara" :meaning "Express a hypothetical situation; 'if', 'suppose', 'assuming'." :short "Express a hypothetical situation; 'if', 'suppose', 'assuming'." :long "The ～としたら grammar point is used to express a hypothetical situation or assumption. It can be translated as 'if', 'suppose', or 'assuming' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + としたら, い-Adjective + としたら, な-Adjective + だとしたら, Noun + だとしたら" :examples [{:ja "明日雨が降るとしたら、傘を持って行きましょう。" :en "If it rains tomorrow, let's bring an umbrella."} {:ja "このケーキが美味しくないとしたら、誰も食べないでしょう。" :en "If this cake is not delicious, nobody will eat it."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-067" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～として" :reading "〜to shite" :meaning "Indicates a role, capacity, or identity; 'as', 'in the capacity of'." :short "Indicates a role, capacity, or identity; 'as', 'in the capacity of'." :long "The ～として grammar point is used to express a role, capacity, or identity in which someone or something functions. It can be translated as 'as' or 'in the capacity of' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + として, い-Adjective + として, な-Adjective + だとして, Noun + だとして" :examples [{:ja "彼は弁護士として働いています。" :en "He is working as a lawyer."} {:ja "この料理はスナックとして食べられます。" :en "This dish can be eaten as a snack."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-068" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～どんなに～ことか" :reading "〜donna ni〜koto ka" :meaning "Expresses the extent of something or emphasizes the degree of a condition or situation." :short "Expresses the extent of something or emphasizes the degree of a condition or situation." :long "The ～どんなに～ことか grammar point is used to emphasize the extent of a condition or circumstance, and to express how much or to what degree something is happening. It is often translated as 'how much', 'how greatly', or 'to what extent' in English. This pattern is usually used with an adjective or verb in a sentence." :formation "どんなに + Adjective + ことか, どんなに + Verb + ことか" :examples [{:ja "どんなに疲れたことか、言葉では表せません。" :en "I can't put into words how tired I am."} {:ja "どんなに辛いことか、彼女は毎日頑張って働いています。" :en "How hard it must be, she works hard every day."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-069" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "どんなに～ても" :reading "donna ni ~ temo" :meaning "Expresses 'no matter how much' or 'even if'; emphasizes the degree of a situation." :short "Expresses 'no matter how much' or 'even if'; emphasizes the degree of a situation." :long "The どんなに～ても grammar point is used to emphasize the degree of a situation or condition, often translated as 'no matter how much' or 'even if' in English. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, and な-adjectives." :formation "どんなに + Verb-てform + も, どんなに + い-Adjective-てform + も, どんなに + な-Adjective + でも" :examples [{:ja "どんなに勉強しても、まだわからないことがある。" :en "No matter how much I study, there are still things I don't understand."} {:ja "どんなに速く走っても、彼には勝てません。" :en "No matter how fast I run, I can't beat him."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-070" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ないことはない" :reading "〜nai koto wa nai" :meaning "Express the idea that 'it's not that it's impossible' or 'it's not that it cannot be done'." :short "Express the idea that 'it's not that it's impossible' or 'it's not that it cannot be done'." :long "The ～ないことはない grammar point is used to express that something is not impossible, or not that it cannot be done. It creates a softer, more indirect way of saying that something is possible or can be done, but not necessarily easy or preferred." :formation "Verb-ない form + ことはない" :examples [{:ja "この問題を解けないことはないが、時間がかかりそうだ。" :en "It's not that I can't solve this problem, but it seems like it will take time."} {:ja "彼に会わないことはないけど、あまり話したくない。" :en "It's not that I can't meet him, but I don't really want to talk."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-071" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ないと" :reading "〜nai to" :meaning "Express a condition; 'if... not', 'unless', 'must'." :short "Express a condition; 'if... not', 'unless', 'must'." :long "The ～ないと grammar point is used to express a condition, focusing on the negative consequences if the action is not performed. It can be translated as 'if... not', 'unless', or 'must' in English. The formation is simply attaching ないと to the negative form of a verb." :formation "Verb-negative form + ないと" :examples [{:ja "朝食を食べないと、お腹が空く。" :en "If I don't eat breakfast, I will be hungry."} {:ja "宿題をしないと、先生に怒られる。" :en "If I don't do my homework, the teacher will be angry."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-072" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～なぜなら" :reading "〜nazenara" :meaning "Express a reason or explanation; 'because', 'the reason is'." :short "Express a reason or explanation; 'because', 'the reason is'." :long "The ～なぜなら grammar point is used to provide a reason or explanation for a preceding statement or situation. It can be translated as 'because' or 'the reason is' in English. It is often followed by から or だから to emphasize the reason or cause." :formation "Sentence/Statement + なぜなら + Reason + から/だから" :examples [{:ja "このレストランが大好きだ。なぜなら料理が美味しいから。" :en "I love this restaurant because the food is delicious."} {:ja "彼女は試験に合格しなかった。なぜなら勉強しなかったから。" :en "She didn't pass the exam because she didn't study."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-073" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～など" :reading "〜nado" :meaning "Indicates a non-exhaustive list of examples; 'such as', 'things like', 'among others'." :short "Indicates a non-exhaustive list of examples; 'such as', 'things like', 'among others'." :long "The ～など grammar point is used to give a non-exhaustive list of examples, meaning the mentioned items are only some of many possibilities. It can be translated as 'such as', 'things like', or 'among others' in English. This grammar point can be used with nouns." :formation "Noun + など" :examples [{:ja "魚、果物などを市場で買いました。" :en "I bought fish, fruits, and such at the market."} {:ja "彼は日本語、英語、中国語などを話せます。" :en "He can speak languages such as Japanese, English, and Chinese."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-074" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～なんか" :reading "〜nanka" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'things like' or 'something like', used for listing examples or downplaying something." :short "Expresses the idea of 'things like' or 'something like', used for listing examples or downplaying something." :long "The ～なんか grammar point is used for listing examples by indicating 'things like' or 'something like'. It can also be used to downplay or devalue something, making it seem less important or less significant than it really is." :formation "Noun + なんか, Verb-casual + なんか, い-Adjective + なんか, な-Adjective + なんか" :examples [{:ja "お金なんかいらない。" :en "I don't need money or anything like that."} {:ja "彼はピアノなんかも弾けます。" :en "He can play things like the piano."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-075" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～において" :reading "〜ni oite" :meaning "Indicates the place, time, or context where an action or situation takes place." :short "Indicates the place, time, or context where an action or situation takes place." :long "The ～において grammar point is used to indicate where an action or situation occurs. It can often be translated as 'in', 'at', or 'on' in English. In some cases, it can also express a specific context or aspect. This grammar point can be used with verbs, adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Noun (Place/Time/Context) + において" :examples [{:ja "日本において、お盆は家族が集まる大切な行事です。" :en "In Japan, Obon is an important event where families gather."} {:ja "会議において、彼の意見が一番説得力がありました。" :en "In the meeting, his opinion was the most persuasive."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-076" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～にかわって" :reading "〜ni kawatte" :meaning "Expresses 'in place of', 'on behalf of', or 'instead of'." :short "Expresses 'in place of', 'on behalf of', or 'instead of'." :long "The ～にかわって grammar point is used to express that someone or something is acting in place of, on behalf of, or instead of someone or something else. Typically used with nouns (e.g., 人, 会社, etc.)." :formation "Noun + にかわって" :examples [{:ja "彼が病気なので、私が会議に出席することになりました。私が彼にかわって会議に出席します。" :en "He is sick, so I will attend the meeting. I will attend the meeting on his behalf."} {:ja "母は忙しいので、私が母にかわって料理を作ります。" :en "My mother is busy, so I will cook the meal instead of her."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-077" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～にしては" :reading "〜ni shite wa" :meaning "Express 'for' or 'considering' in the sense of being unusual or unexpected." :short "Express 'for' or 'considering' in the sense of being unusual or unexpected." :long "The ～にしては grammar point is used to express that something is unusual or unexpected considering a specific condition or context. It can be translated as 'for' or 'considering' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + にしては, い-Adjective + にしては, な-Adjective + にしては, Noun + にしては" :examples [{:ja "彼女は初心者にしては、上手に水泳ができる。" :en "Considering she is a beginner, she can swim well."} {:ja "このお寿司は安いにしては、とても美味しい。" :en "Considering this sushi is cheap, it's very delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-078" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～にしても" :reading "〜ni shitemo" :meaning "Expresses 'even if', 'no matter', 'regardless of'." :short "Expresses 'even if', 'no matter', 'regardless of'." :long "The ～にしても grammar point is used to express that even if a certain condition is true or a certain situation occurs, the outcome will not change. It can be translated as 'even if', 'no matter', or 'regardless of' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + にしても, い-Adjective + にしても, な-Adjective + だにしても, Noun + だにしても" :examples [{:ja "雨が降るにしても、学校へ行かなければなりません。" :en "Even if it rains, I have to go to school."} {:ja "彼女が怒っているにしても、話し合いは避けられない。" :en "Even if she is angry, we can't avoid talking."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-079" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～について" :reading "〜ni tsuite" :meaning "Expressing 'about', 'concerning', or 'regarding'." :short "Expressing 'about', 'concerning', or 'regarding'." :long "The ～について grammar point is used to express 'about', 'concerning', or 'regarding' a certain topic or subject. It is often used in both casual and formal contexts. The formation is simple – just add について after the noun that represents the topic you are discussing." :formation "Noun + について" :examples [{:ja "日本の文化について学びたいです。" :en "I want to learn about Japanese culture."} {:ja "彼の意見について話し合いましょう。" :en "Let's discuss his opinion."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-080" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～にとって" :reading "〜ni totte" :meaning "Express the meaning 'for', 'to', 'from the perspective of'." :short "Express the meaning 'for', 'to', 'from the perspective of'." :long "The ～にとって grammar point is used to express the meaning 'for', 'to', or 'from the perspective of' when describing someone or something's point of view, feelings, benefits, or importance. It is used with all types of words, such as verbs, adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Noun (person or thing) + にとって + Verb/Adjective/Noun" :examples [{:ja "私にとって、健康は一番大切なことです。" :en "For me, health is the most important thing."} {:ja "あの会社にとって、このプロジェクトは重要な意味があります。" :en "For that company, this project has significant meaning."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-081" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～によって" :reading "〜ni yotte" :meaning "Indicates the reason or cause, difference depending on a factor, or methods used." :short "Indicates the reason or cause, difference depending on a factor, or methods used." :long "The ～によって grammar point is used to express the reason or cause of something, to show a difference depending on a certain factor, or to indicate the method or means by which something is done. It can be translated as 'by', 'depending on', 'due to', or 'according to' in English. The formation is the same for verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + によって, い-Adjective + によって, な-Adjective + によって, Noun + によって" :examples [{:ja "その事故は悪天候によって引き起こされた。" :en "The accident was caused by bad weather."} {:ja "生活習慣によって、健康状態が変わります。" :en "Health conditions change depending on lifestyle habits."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-082" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～によれば" :reading "〜ni yoreba" :meaning "Means 'according to', 'based on' or 'by'." :short "Means 'according to', 'based on' or 'by'." :long "The ～によれば grammar point is used to express information, opinions or a judgement made based on a certain source. It can be translated as 'according to', 'based on', or 'by' in English. This grammar point is typically used with a noun that represents the source of information." :formation "Noun + によれば" :examples [{:ja "天気予報によれば、明日は晴れるでしょう。" :en "According to the weather forecast, it will be sunny tomorrow."} {:ja "先生によれば、この問題は簡単だそうです。" :en "According to the teacher, this problem seems to be easy."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-083" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～に対して" :reading "～ni taishite" :meaning "Indicates a target or contrast; 'toward', 'against', 'in contrast to', 'in response to'." :short "Indicates a target or contrast; 'toward', 'against', 'in contrast to', 'in response to'." :long "The ～に対して grammar point is used to indicate the target or direction of an action, or to show a contrast between two situations or objects. It can be translated as 'toward', 'against', 'in contrast to', or 'in response to' in English. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + に対して, い-Adjective + に対して, な-Adjective + に対して, Noun + に対して" :examples [{:ja "子供たちに対して、親は常にやさしくあるべきです。" :en "Parents should always be kind towards their children."} {:ja "彼は質問に対して正直に答えました。" :en "He answered the questions honestly."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-084" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～に比べて" :reading "〜ni kurabete" :meaning "Comparing to; in comparison with; as opposed to." :short "Comparing to; in comparison with; as opposed to." :long "The ～に比べて grammar point is used to compare two things or situations by highlighting their differences. It can be translated as 'comparing to', 'in comparison with', or 'as opposed to' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + に比べて, い-Adjective + に比べて, な-Adjective + だ/である + に比べて, Noun + だ/である + に比べて" :examples [{:ja "夏に比べて、冬は寒いです。" :en "Winter is cold compared to summer."} {:ja "このトマトは赤いトマトに比べて美味しくない。" :en "This tomato is not as delicious compared to the red tomatoes."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-085" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～に関して" :reading "〜ni kanshite" :meaning "Regarding, concerning, about, with respect to." :short "Regarding, concerning, about, with respect to." :long "The ～に関して grammar point is used to express the topic or subject matter one is talking about. It can be translated as 'regarding', 'concerning', 'about', or 'with respect to' in English. The formation is simple and remains the same regardless of it being used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Noun + に関して" :examples [{:ja "健康に関して気を付けることが大切です。" :en "It is important to be careful regarding health."} {:ja "このプロジェクトに関しては、彼がすべての詳細を知っています。" :en "Regarding this project, he knows all the details."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-086" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～の～" :reading "〜no 〜" :meaning "Indicates possession, attribution, or connection between two nouns." :short "Indicates possession, attribution, or connection between two nouns." :long "The ～の～ grammar point is used to show the relationship between two nouns in Japanese. It can be translated as 'of', 'in', or 'the' in English. This grammar point is used to indicate possession, attribution, or connection between the two nouns." :formation "Noun1 + の + Noun2" :examples [{:ja "これは私の傘です。" :en "This is my umbrella."} {:ja "あのビルは東京の有名な観光スポットです。" :en "That building is a famous tourist spot in Tokyo."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-087" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ばかり" :reading "〜bakari" :meaning "Expresses 'only', 'nothing but', 'always', 'just'." :short "Expresses 'only', 'nothing but', 'always', 'just'." :long "The ～ばかり grammar point is used to express that something is only, nothing but, always, or just a certain way. It emphasizes the repeated or continuous nature of an action or state. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-ますstem + ばかり, い-Adjective + ばかり, な-Adjective + ばかり, Noun + ばかり" :examples [{:ja "彼はゲームばかりしている。" :en "He is always playing games."} {:ja "この部屋は綺麗なものばかりだ。" :en "This room has nothing but clean things."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-088" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ばかりか (〜bakarika) ～も" :reading "mo" :meaning "Not only... but also; more than just; not just" :short "Not only... but also; more than just; not just" :long "The grammar point ～ばかりか ～も is used to emphasize that something is not limited to only one thing, but also includes another thing. It can be translated as 'not only... but also', 'more than just', or 'not just' in English. This expression is typically used with verbs, adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + ばかりか/も, い-Adjective + ばかりか/も, な-Adjective + だ + ばかりか/も, Noun + だ + ばかりか/も" :examples [{:ja "彼女は英語ばかりか、フランス語も話せます。" :en "Not only can she speak English, but she can also speak French."} {:ja "このレストランは味が良いばかりか、サービスも素晴らしい。" :en "Not only does this restaurant have great taste, but the service is also wonderful."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-089" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～はずだ" :reading "〜hazu da" :meaning "Expresses an expectation, 'should' or 'it is supposed to'." :short "Expresses an expectation, 'should' or 'it is supposed to'." :long "The ～はずだ grammar point is used to express an expectation or belief that something is true, even if it has not been confirmed. It can be translated as 'should', 'it is supposed to', or 'ought to' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + はずだ, い-Adjective + はずだ, な-Adjective + なはずだ, Noun + のはずだ" :examples [{:ja "彼はもう着いたはずだ。" :en "He should have arrived by now."} {:ja "この薬を飲むと、痛みがなくなるはずだ。" :en "If you take this medicine, the pain should go away."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-090" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ば～のに" :reading "〜ba 〜noni" :meaning "Expresses regret or dissatisfaction; 'if only', 'wish'." :short "Expresses regret or dissatisfaction; 'if only', 'wish'." :long "The ～ば～のに grammar point is used to express regret or dissatisfaction about a situation that didn't happen or to express a contrary view of the actual situation. It can be translated as 'if only' or 'wish' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-ば form + ～のに, い-Adjective-ば form + ～のに, な-Adjective + であれば + ～のに, Noun + であれば + ～のに" :examples [{:ja "もっと早く来れば、電車に間に合ったのに。" :en "If only I had come earlier, I would have made it to the train."} {:ja "もっと勉強すれば、試験に合格したのに。" :en "I wish I had studied more, I would have passed the exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-091" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ば～ほど" :reading "〜ba 〜hodo" :meaning "Expresses 'the more ~, the more ~'; a proportional relationship between two phrases." :short "Expresses 'the more ~, the more ~'; a proportional relationship between two phrases." :long "The ～ば～ほど grammar point is used to express a proportional relationship between two phrases, meaning 'the more A, the more B'. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, or な-adjective." :formation "Verb-casual + ば + Verb-casual + ほど, い-Adjective + ば + い-Adjective + ほど, な-Adjective + であれば + な-Adjective + ほど" :examples [{:ja "早く起きれば起きるほど、時間がたくさんある。" :en "The earlier you wake up, the more time you have."} {:ja "練習すればするほど、上達します。" :en "The more you practice, the more you will improve."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-092" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～はもちろん～も" :reading "〜wa mochiron 〜mo" :meaning "Used for emphasizing that not only one thing but also another thing is included. It means 'not only... but also' or 'of course... and...'." :short "Used for emphasizing that not only one thing but also another thing is included. It means 'not only... but also' or 'of course... and...'." :long "The ～はもちろん～も grammar point is used to emphasize that not only the first thing mentioned is included, but also the second thing. It can be translated as 'not only... but also' or 'of course... and...' in English. This grammar phrase can be used with nouns." :formation "Noun1 + はもちろん + Noun2 + も" :examples [{:ja "彼は数学はもちろん、英語も得意です。" :en "Not only is he good at math, but he is also good at English."} {:ja "このレストランは和食はもちろん、洋食も美味しいです。" :en "This restaurant not only has delicious Japanese food but also delicious Western food."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-093" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ばよかった" :reading "〜ba yokatta" :meaning "Express a sense of regret; 'I wish', 'it would have been better if'." :short "Express a sense of regret; 'I wish', 'it would have been better if'." :long "The ～ばよかった grammar point is used to express a sense of regret or to convey that one wishes something had been done differently. It can be translated as 'I wish' or 'it would have been better if' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, or な-adjective." :formation "Verb-ば-form + よかった, い-Adjective-く + ばよかった, な-Adjective + ならよかった" :examples [{:ja "もっと早く起きればよかった。" :en "I wish I had woken up earlier."} {:ja "昨日のパーティーに行けばよかった。" :en "I wish I had gone to yesterday's party."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-094" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ふりをする" :reading "〜furi wo suru" :meaning "Pretending or acting as if; 'pretend', 'act like'." :short "Pretending or acting as if; 'pretend', 'act like'." :long "The ～ふりをする grammar point is used to express that someone is pretending or acting as if they are doing something, feeling a certain way, or possessing a certain quality. It can be translated as 'pretend' or 'act like' in English. The formation is the same for verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb (dictionary form) + ふりをする, い-Adjective + ふりをする, な-Adjective + ふりをする, Noun + ふりをする" :examples [{:ja "彼は知らないふりをしました。" :en "He pretended not to know."} {:ja "彼女は病気のふりをして、学校を休みました。" :en "She pretended to be sick and skipped school."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-095" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～べきだ" :reading "〜beki da" :meaning "Express an obligation or something that should be done (mainly with verbs)." :short "Express an obligation or something that should be done (mainly with verbs)." :long "The ～べきだ grammar point is used to express a sense of obligation or to indicate something that should be done. In modern Japanese, it is almost always used with verbs in the dictionary form. In English, it can be translated as 'should' or 'ought to'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + べきだ" :examples [{:ja "勉強するべきだ。" :en "You should study."} {:ja "早く寝るべきだ。" :en "You should sleep early."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-096" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ほど～" :reading "〜hodo〜" :meaning "Expresses the degree or extent; 'about', 'to the extent', 'as much as'." :short "Expresses the degree or extent; 'about', 'to the extent', 'as much as'." :long "The ～ほど grammar point is used to express the degree or extent of something. It can be translated as 'about', 'to the extent', or 'as much as' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + ほど, い-Adjective + ほど, な-Adjective + ほど, Noun + ほど" :examples [{:ja "彼女は私ほど速く走ることができません。" :en "She cannot run as fast as me."} {:ja "このケーキはあのケーキほど美味しくない。" :en "This cake is not as delicious as that cake."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-097" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ますように" :reading "〜masu you ni" :meaning "Used to express a wish or hope for oneself or someone else." :short "Used to express a wish or hope for oneself or someone else." :long "The ～ますように grammar point is used to express a wish or hope. It is often used as a closing phrase in letters or messages, and can be applied to both oneself and others. The phrase is typically used with the ます form of the verb, with the final る replaced by ように." :formation "Verb-ます form, remove final る + ように" :examples [{:ja "明日のテストで良い点をとりますように。" :en "I hope I get good grades on tomorrow's test."} {:ja "彼が無事に帰ってきますように。" :en "I hope he comes back safely."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-098" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "まったく～ない" :reading "mattaku ~nai" :meaning "Completely not; not at all." :short "Completely not; not at all." :long "The まったく～ない grammar point is used to express that something is not the case at all, or to emphasize the negativity of a situation or action. It is used with negative forms of verbs, い-adjectives, and な-adjectives. It can be translated as 'completely not' or 'not at all' in English." :formation "まったく + Verb-negative, まったく + い-Adjective-negative, まったく + な-Adjective-negative" :examples [{:ja "彼は、まったく覚えていない。" :en "He doesn't remember at all."} {:ja "この部屋はまったく暖かくない。" :en "This room is not warm at all."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-099" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～まで" :reading "〜made" :meaning "Express 'until', 'up to', or 'as far as' to indicate the end point of an action or range." :short "Express 'until', 'up to', or 'as far as' to indicate the end point of an action or range." :long "The ～まで grammar point is used to express the end point, limit, or extent of an action or range in terms of time, space, or quantity. It can be translated as 'until', 'up to', or 'as far as' in English. The formation depends on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + まで, い-Adjective + まで, な-Adjective + まで, Noun + まで" :examples [{:ja "10時まで働きます。" :en "I will work until 10 o'clock."} {:ja "東京まで電車で行きます。" :en "I will go by train as far as Tokyo."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-100" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～まま" :reading "〜mama" :meaning "Expresses the state or condition remaining unchanged." :short "Expresses the state or condition remaining unchanged." :long "The 〜まま grammar point is used to indicate that a state or condition remains unchanged or continues as it is, without any change or intervention. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns to express the idea of something being left, kept, or maintained in its current state." :formation "Verb-ます stem + まま, い-Adjective + まま, な-Adjective + なまま, Noun + のまま" :examples [{:ja "部屋の窓を開けたまま寝ました。" :en "I slept with the window of the room open."} {:ja "熱いままのコーヒーを飲んで、舌を焦がしました。" :en "I drank the coffee while it was still hot and burned my tongue."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-101" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "まるで～よう" :reading "maru de ~ you" :meaning "Used to express a strong resemblance or similarity; 'as if', 'just like', 'as though'." :short "Used to express a strong resemblance or similarity; 'as if', 'just like', 'as though'." :long "The まるで～よう grammar point is used to emphasize a strong resemblance or similarity between two things or situations. It can be translated as 'as if', 'just like', or 'as though' in English. The formation is まるで + phrase/sentence + よう." :formation "まるで + phrase/sentence + よう" :examples [{:ja "彼女はまるで天使のように美しい。" :en "She is as beautiful as an angel."} {:ja "彼はまるでロボットのように仕事をしている。" :en "He works as if he is a robot."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-102" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～てみる" :reading "〜te miru" :meaning "A grammar structure used to express the action of trying or attempting something to see what happens." :short "A grammar structure used to express the action of trying or attempting something to see what happens." :long "The 〜てみる grammar point is used with a verb in the 〜て form followed by みる. It emphasizes doing something as a trial or to see the result. It often translates as 'to try doing something' or 'try it out'." :formation "Verb-て form + みる" :examples [{:ja "彼は走ってみたが、まだ膝が痛いと感じた。" :en "He tried running, but he still felt pain in his knee."} {:ja "彼女は料理を作ってみたが、失敗してしまった。" :en "She tried cooking but ended up failing."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-103" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～みたいだ" :reading "〜mitai da" :meaning "Express similarity or appearance; 'like', 'seems', 'looks'." :short "Express similarity or appearance; 'like', 'seems', 'looks'." :long "The ～みたいだ grammar point is used to express similarity, appearance or resemblance to something. It can be translated as 'like', 'seems', or 'looks' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + みたいだ, い-Adjective + みたいだ, な-Adjective + みたいだ, Noun + みたいだ" :examples [{:ja "彼は怒っているみたいだ。" :en "He seems to be angry."} {:ja "そのビルは古いみたいだ。" :en "That building looks old."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-104" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～めったに～ない" :reading "〜metta ni 〜nai" :meaning "Used to express that something rarely, hardly ever or seldom happens." :short "Used to express that something rarely, hardly ever or seldom happens." :long "The ～めったに～ない grammar point is used to express that something rarely, hardly ever or seldom happens. It is a negative expression and always needs to be used with ～ない (negative form) at the end." :formation "めったに + Verb[ない-form] / めったに + い-Adjective[ない-form] / めったに + な-Adjective[じゃない/ではない-form]" :examples [{:ja "彼はめったに外食しない。" :en "He hardly ever eats out."} {:ja "私はめったに寿司を食べない。" :en "I seldom eat sushi."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-105" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～めったにない" :reading "〜metta ni nai" :meaning "Expresses the rarity of an action, event or state; 'hardly', 'rarely', 'seldom'." :short "Expresses the rarity of an action, event or state; 'hardly', 'rarely', 'seldom'." :long "The ～めったにない grammar point is used to express that an action, event or state rarely, hardly, or seldom happens. It is usually combined with a negative verb form. Though it can be used with positive verbs, it is uncommon and sounds unnatural." :formation "Verb-negative + めったにない, い-Adjective-negative + めったにない, な-Adjective-negative + めったにない, Noun-negative + めったにない" :examples [{:ja "この町では雪が降ることはめったにない。" :en "It hardly ever snows in this town."} {:ja "彼はめったに外食しない。" :en "He rarely eats out."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-106" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "もしかすると〜かもしれない" :reading "moshikasuru to 〜kamoshirenai" :meaning "Express the possibility of something; 'maybe', 'perhaps', 'might'." :short "Express the possibility of something; 'maybe', 'perhaps', 'might'." :long "The もしかすると〜かもしれない grammar point is used to indicate that there might be a possibility of a certain situation happening or someone feeling a certain way. This structure is often used to describe uncertain situations and can be translated into English as 'maybe', 'perhaps', or 'might'." :formation "もしかすると + Sentence + かもしれない" :examples [{:ja "もしかすると彼は遅刻するかもしれない。" :en "Maybe he might be late."} {:ja "もしかすると彼女は疲れているかもしれない。" :en "Perhaps she might be tired."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-107" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "もし～たなら" :reading "moshi ~ tanara" :meaning "Express a hypothetical condition, 'if' or 'in case'." :short "Express a hypothetical condition, 'if' or 'in case'." :long "The もし～たなら grammar point is used to express a hypothetical condition or situation in a more polite manner. It emphasizes the conditional part of a sentence and can be translated as 'if' or 'in case' in English. It is mainly used with verbs in the past tense, casual form." :formation "もし + Verb-casual past + なら, もし + い-Adjective-casual past + なら, もし + な-Adjective + だったなら, もし + Noun + だったなら" :examples [{:ja "もし雨が降ったなら、傘を持って行きましょう。" :en "If it rains, let's bring an umbrella."} {:ja "もしケーキが美味しくなかったなら、誰も食べないでしょう。" :en "If the cake was not delicious, nobody would eat it."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-108" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "もし～ても" :reading "moshi ~ temo" :meaning "Express a hypothetical situation or assumption with the nuance of 'even if'." :short "Express a hypothetical situation or assumption with the nuance of 'even if'." :long "The もし～ても grammar point is used to describe a situation in which even if the stated condition is true, the result or decision does not change. It can be translated as 'even if', 'no matter if', or 'suppose ~'." :formation "もし + Verb-て form + も / もし + い-Adjective(く) + ても / もし + な-Adjective/Noun + でも" :examples [{:ja "もし雨が降っても、ピクニックに行きます。" :en "Even if it rains, we will go on a picnic."} {:ja "もし彼が謝っても、まだ怒っているでしょう。" :en "Even if he apologizes, I will still be angry."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-109" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "もしも～なら" :reading "moshimo ~ nara" :meaning "Express a hypothetical condition or situation; 'if', 'in case', 'suppose'." :short "Express a hypothetical condition or situation; 'if', 'in case', 'suppose'." :long "The もしも～なら grammar point is used to express a hypothetical condition or situation, often with a sense of uncertainty. It can be translated as 'if', 'in case', or 'suppose'. The formation varies slightly based on whether it is used with a verb, an い-adjective, a な-adjective, or a noun." :formation "Verb-casual + なら, い-Adjective + なら, な-Adjective + なら, Noun + なら" :examples [{:ja "もしも明日雨が降ったなら、出かけません。" :en "If it rains tomorrow, I won't go out."} {:ja "もしも彼が来なかったなら、私たちだけでパーティーをします。" :en "If he doesn't come, we'll have the party just by ourselves."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-110" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ようとしない" :reading "〜you to shinai" :meaning "Not showing any sign or intention to do something." :short "Not showing any sign or intention to do something." :long "The ～ようとしない grammar point indicates that someone is not showing any sign or intention of doing an action. It is used to express a refusal or unwillingness to do something. It is formed by using the volitional form of a verb followed by としない." :formation "Verb-volitional form + としない" :examples [{:ja "彼は勉強しようとしない。" :en "He doesn't show any sign of trying to study."} {:ja "彼女は謝ろうとしない。" :en "She doesn't show any intention of apologizing."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-111" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ようと思う" :reading "〜you to omou" :meaning "Expresses the speaker's intention or plan to do something." :short "Expresses the speaker's intention or plan to do something." :long "The ～ようと思う grammar point is used to express the speaker's intention or plan to do something in the future. It can be translated as 'I think I will...' or 'I am planning to...' in English. It is formed by taking the volitional form of a verb and adding と思う." :formation "Verb-volitional + と思う" :examples [{:ja "今日は早く寝ようと思います。" :en "I think I will go to bed early today."} {:ja "明日美術館に行こうと思っています。" :en "I am planning to go to the art museum tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-112" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ように" :reading "〜you ni" :meaning "Expressing a purpose, a goal or a way of doing something; 'in order to', 'so that', 'like', 'as'" :short "Expressing a purpose, a goal or a way of doing something; 'in order to', 'so that', 'like', 'as'" :long "The ～ように grammar point is used to express a purpose, a goal, or a way of doing something. It can be translated as 'in order to', 'so that', 'like', or 'as' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + ように, い-Adjective + ように, な-Adjective + に, Noun + のように" :examples [{:ja "早く家に帰るように、仕事を急いで終わらせました。" :en "In order to get home early, I hurried to finish my work."} {:ja "彼女は美しい花のように見えました。" :en "She looked like a beautiful flower."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-113" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ように" :reading "〜you ni" :meaning "Used to express a purpose, a way, or a natural tendency." :short "Used to express a purpose, a way, or a natural tendency." :long "The ～ように grammar point is used to express a purpose, a way or a method, or a natural tendency. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, or nouns. It is frequently used with the verb する (to do) to express the purpose or method." :formation "Verb-casual + ように, い-Adjective + ように, な-Adjective + だように, Noun + のように" :examples [{:ja "子供が安全に過ごせるように、家の中を整理します。" :en "I will organize the inside of the house so that the children can spend time safely."} {:ja "美しい花のように、彼女は笑顔が素敵です。" :en "Like a beautiful flower, her smile is lovely."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-114" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ように" :reading "〜you ni" :meaning "Used to express purpose or how something is done; 'like', 'as if', 'in order to'." :short "Used to express purpose or how something is done; 'like', 'as if', 'in order to'." :long "The ～ように grammar point is used to express the purpose of an action or the manner in which something is done. It can be translated as 'like', 'as if', or 'in order to' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + ように, い-Adjective + ように, な-Adjective + に, Noun + のように" :examples [{:ja "分かりやすいように説明してください。" :en "Please explain it in a way that is easy to understand."} {:ja "彼は鳥のように歌います。" :en "He sings like a bird."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-115" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ようにしましょう" :reading "〜you ni shimashou" :meaning "A suggestion to make an effort or try to do something; 'let's try to', 'let's make sure to'." :short "A suggestion to make an effort or try to do something; 'let's try to', 'let's make sure to'." :long "The ～ようにしましょう grammar point is used to suggest making an effort or trying to do something. It can be translated as 'let's try to' or 'let's make sure to' in English. Typically, we use the dictionary or negative form of the verb with ようにする/ようにしましょう to show the effort we want to make." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ようにしましょう / Verb-negative form + ようにしましょう" :examples [{:ja "毎日運動するようにしましょう。" :en "Let's try to exercise every day."} {:ja "早く寝るようにしましょう。" :en "Let's make sure to go to bed early."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-116" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ようになった" :reading "〜you ni natta" :meaning "Express an event or state that became possible; 'became able to', 'started to', 'now can'." :short "Express an event or state that became possible; 'became able to', 'started to', 'now can'." :long "The ～ようになった grammar point is used to express that an event, ability, or state has become possible or started to occur. It can be translated as 'became able to', 'started to', or 'now can' in English. Usually this is used with verbs in their dictionary or potential form (e.g., 食べられるようになった)." :formation "Verb-casual + ようになった" :examples [{:ja "彼は料理を作るようになった。" :en "He became able to cook."} {:ja "その子は読めるようになった。" :en "That child started to be able to read."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-117" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ように言う" :reading "〜you ni iu" :meaning "To convey a message, instruction or request to someone; 'to tell (someone) to', 'to say to (someone)'." :short "To convey a message, instruction or request to someone; 'to tell (someone) to', 'to say to (someone)'." :long "The ～ように言う grammar point is used to convey a message, instruction or request to someone. It can be translated as 'to tell (someone) to' or 'to say to (someone)'. It is commonly formed by taking the dictionary (or negative) form of the verb plus ように言う." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ように言う / Verb-nai form + ように言う" :examples [{:ja "先生は私にもっと勉強するように言った。" :en "The teacher told me to study more."} {:ja "彼女は彼に待つように言いました。" :en "She told him to wait."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-118" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～らしい" :reading "〜rashii" :meaning "Expresses hearsay; 'I heard', 'it seems', 'apparently'." :short "Expresses hearsay; 'I heard', 'it seems', 'apparently'." :long "The ～らしい grammar point is used to indicate that the information provided is based on hearsay or an indirect source. In English, this can be translated as 'I heard', 'it seems', or 'apparently'. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + らしい, い-Adjective + らしい, な-Adjective + だらしい, Noun + だらしい" :examples [{:ja "彼はインフルエンザらしい。" :en "Apparently, he has the flu."} {:ja "明日雪が降るらしい。" :en "I heard it will snow tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-119" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～られた" :reading "〜rareta" :meaning "Express a passive action, 'was/were', 'has/have been'." :short "Express a passive action, 'was/were', 'has/have been'." :long "The ～られた grammar point is used to express a passive action or situation. It is formed by conjugating the verb into its passive form in the past tense, which can be translated into English as 'was/were' or 'has/have been'. This grammar is used in situations where the subject is affected by an action rather than performing the action itself." :formation "Verb (passive form, past tense) + られた" :examples [{:ja "この本はたくさんの人に読まれた。" :en "This book was read by many people."} {:ja "彼は友達に騙された。" :en "He was deceived by his friend."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-120" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～ている" :reading "〜te iru" :meaning "Expresses a continuing action or a resulting state; 'is ~ing', 'has been', 'has'." :short "Expresses a continuing action or a resulting state; 'is ~ing', 'has been', 'has'." :long "The ～ている grammar point is used to describe a continuing or repeated action, or the result/state of a completed action. Depending on the context, it can be translated as 'is ~ing', 'has been ~ed', or simply 'has/are/is'. In passive contexts (e.g., 食べられている, 開けられている), it shows that something has been done and remains in that state. With intransitive or non-passive verbs, it shows an ongoing or resultant state (e.g., 寝込んでいる, 終わっている)." :formation "Verb-て form + いる" :examples [{:ja "窓が開けられている。" :en "The window is open (someone opened it and it remains open)."} {:ja "ケーキが全部食べられている。" :en "The whole cake has been eaten."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-121" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～わけがない" :reading "〜wake ga nai" :meaning "Expresses that something is impossible or unreasonable; 'there's no way', 'can't be'." :short "Expresses that something is impossible or unreasonable; 'there's no way', 'can't be'." :long "The ～わけがない grammar point is used to show that something is impossible, non-existent, or unreasonable based on logic or context. It is often translated as 'there's no way' or 'it's impossible'." :formation "Verb-casual + わけがない, い-Adjective + わけがない, な-Adjective + なわけがない, Noun + のわけがない" :examples [{:ja "彼がそんなことを言うわけがない。" :en "There's no way he would say something like that."} {:ja "あのレストランがまずいわけがない。" :en "There's no way that restaurant tastes bad."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-122" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～わけだ" :reading "〜wake da" :meaning "Expresses a logical conclusion or reason based on information; 'that's why', 'no wonder', 'it means'." :short "Expresses a logical conclusion or reason based on information; 'that's why', 'no wonder', 'it means'." :long "The ～わけだ grammar point is used to express a conclusion, reason, or interpretation based on a certain situation or piece of information. It can be translated as 'that's why', 'no wonder', or 'it means'." :formation "Verb-casual + わけだ, い-Adjective + わけだ, な-Adjective + なわけだ, Noun + のわけだ" :examples [{:ja "彼は風邪を引いている。だから、声がかすれているわけだ。" :en "He has a cold. That's why his voice is hoarse."} {:ja "今日は忙しかった。だから、疲れたわけだ。" :en "Today was busy. No wonder I'm tired."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-123" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～わけではない" :reading "〜wake dewa nai" :meaning "Denies a general assumption; 'it does not mean that', 'it's not the case'." :short "Denies a general assumption; 'it does not mean that', 'it's not the case'." :long "The ～わけではない grammar point is used to deny or correct a general assumption or statement without completely negating the situation. It often emphasizes one particular point that is not true, while acknowledging other possibilities." :formation "Verb-casual + わけではない, い-Adjective + わけではない, な-Adjective + だわけではない, Noun + だわけではない" :examples [{:ja "彼が忙しいからといって、友達がいないわけではない。" :en "Just because he's busy doesn't mean he has no friends."} {:ja "この映画は面白くないわけではないが、好みではない。" :en "It's not that this movie isn't interesting; it's just not my taste."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-124" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～わけにはいかない" :reading "〜wake ni wa ikanai" :meaning "Expresses that something cannot be allowed or permitted due to social/moral obligations." :short "Expresses that something cannot be allowed or permitted due to social/moral obligations." :long "The ～わけにはいかない grammar point is used to express that a particular action cannot be taken due to social or moral obligations, responsibilities, or natural consequences. It often translates as 'I can't afford to...' or 'I can't just...' in English." :formation "Verb-casual + わけにはいかない" :examples [{:ja "こんなところで寝るわけにはいかない。" :en "I can't possibly sleep in a place like this."} {:ja "試験が明日だから、遊びに行くわけにはいかない。" :en "I can't afford to go out to have fun, since the exam is tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-125" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～わりには" :reading "〜wari ni wa" :meaning "Expresses contrast between expectation and reality; 'despite', 'considering', 'even though'." :short "Expresses contrast between expectation and reality; 'despite', 'considering', 'even though'." :long "The ～わりには grammar point is used to highlight a contrast between what is expected from a certain condition and the actual result. It can be translated as 'despite', 'considering', or 'even though'." :formation "Verb-casual + わりには, い-Adjective + わりには, な-Adjective + だわりには, Noun + だわりには" :examples [{:ja "彼はあまり勉強しないわりには、テストの点数が高いです。" :en "Despite not studying much, he gets high scores on tests."} {:ja "この料理は見た目が悪いわりには、味は美味しいです。" :en "Even though this dish doesn't look good, it tastes delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-126" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～んだって" :reading "〜n datte" :meaning "Conveys information heard from someone else; 'I heard', 'They said'." :short "Conveys information heard from someone else; 'I heard', 'They said'." :long "The ～んだって grammar point is used to relay information the speaker has heard from someone else. It's a casual way of quoting what was said, effectively combining explanatory のだ (んだ) with the informal quoting particle って. It can be translated as 'I heard...' or 'They said...'." :formation "Verb-casual + んだって, い-Adjective + んだって, な-Adjective + なんだって, Noun + なんだって" :examples [{:ja "彼は東京に行くんだって。" :en "I heard he's going to Tokyo."} {:ja "この映画、面白いんだって。" :en "They said this movie is interesting."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-127" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～んだもん" :reading "〜nda mon" :meaning "Used to convey a sense of justification; 'because', 'that's why', 'it's just that'." :short "Used to convey a sense of justification; 'because', 'that's why', 'it's just that'." :long "The ～んだもん grammar point is used when someone wants to give a reason, justification, or explanatory information in a more casual and emphatic way. It can be translated as 'because', 'that's why', or 'it's just that' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + んだもん, い-Adjective + んだもん, な-Adjective + なんだもん, Noun + なんだもん" :examples [{:ja "遅れたんだもん、ごめん。" :en "I was late, that's why I'm sorry."} {:ja "おいしいんだもん、もうちょっと食べたい。" :en "It's delicious, that's why I want to eat more."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-128" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～上げる" :reading "〜ageru" :meaning "Indicates to finish doing something completely." :short "Indicates to finish doing something completely." :long "The ～上げる grammar point is used to express that an action has been completed or finished entirely. It's often used with verbs that have a ます stem, and can be translated as 'to finish (doing something)', 'to complete', or 'to get something done'." :formation "Verb-ますstem + 上げる" :examples [{:ja "レポートを書き上げるまで、寝られません。" :en "I cannot sleep until I finish writing the report."} {:ja "彼女は早速部屋の掃除を始め、一時間で掃除をやり上げた。" :en "She started cleaning the room right away and finished cleaning it in an hour."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-129" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～切れない" :reading "～kirenai" :meaning "Expresses inability to completely finish or do something." :short "Expresses inability to completely finish or do something." :long "The ～切れない grammar point is used to express the inability to fully complete, finish, or do something. It is formed by attaching 切れない to the stem of a verb. It often implies that some portion of the action is done, but finishing it entirely is not possible for some reason." :formation "Verb-stem + 切れない" :examples [{:ja "この本を一晩で読み切れない。" :en "I cannot finish reading this book in one night."} {:ja "彼は泣き切れないほど嬉しかった。" :en "He was so happy that he couldn't stop crying."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-130" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "必ずしも～とは限らない" :reading "kanarazushimo ～ towa kagiranai" :meaning "Not necessarily; not always." :short "Not necessarily; not always." :long "The 必ずしも～とは限らない grammar point is used to express that something is not necessarily or not always the case, indicating that a certain assumption may not hold true in all situations." :formation "必ずしも + Statement + とは限らない" :examples [{:ja "高いものが必ずしも良いとは限らない。" :en "Expensive things are not necessarily better."} {:ja "有名な人が必ずしも幸せとは限らない。" :en "Famous people are not necessarily happy."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-131" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "～最中に" :reading "～saichuu ni" :meaning "In the middle of / while doing something." :short "In the middle of / while doing something." :long "The ～最中に grammar point is used to indicate that something happens during the middle of an action or situation. It can be translated as 'in the middle of' or 'while' in English. It often involves appending 最中に to a verb in 〜ている form or a noun plus の." :formation "Verb-て form + いる + 最中に, Noun + の + 最中に" :examples [{:ja "昼寝をしている最中に電話が鳴った。" :en "The phone rang while I was taking a nap."} {:ja "料理を作っている最中に火事が起こった。" :en "A fire broke out in the middle of cooking."}]} {:id "grammar-n3-132" :track :grammar :level "N3" :display "決して～ない" :reading "kesshite ~ nai" :meaning "Express 'never', 'by no means', or 'not at all' when negating a statement." :short "Express 'never', 'by no means', or 'not at all' when negating a statement." :long "The 決して～ない grammar point is used to emphasize a negative statement or action. It can be translated as 'never', 'by no means', or 'not at all'. This grammar point must be paired with a negative verb form to maintain its negative meaning." :formation "決して + Verb (negative form)" :examples [{:ja "彼は決して遅刻しない。" :en "He never arrives late."} {:ja "この秘密を決して誰にも言わないでください。" :en "Please never tell this secret to anyone."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-001" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A あるいは B" :reading "A aruiwa B" :meaning "Indicates alternatives; 'A or B', 'either A or B'." :short "Indicates alternatives; 'A or B', 'either A or B'." :long "The あるいは grammar point is used to show alternatives or options, similar to 'A or B' or 'either A or B' in English. It highlights the possibility of more than one option in a situation. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Phrase A + あるいは + Phrase B" :examples [{:ja "今日は映画を見るあるいはショッピングをする予定です。" :en "Today, I plan to either watch a movie or go shopping."} {:ja "彼女は英語あるいはドイツ語で話すことができます。" :en "She can speak either English or German."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-002" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。おまけに B。" :reading "~omake ni" :meaning "Expresses that in addition to A, there is also B; 'moreover', 'besides', 'not only', 'but also'." :short "Expresses that in addition to A, there is also B; 'moreover', 'besides', 'not only', 'but also'." :long "The A。おまけに B。grammar point is used to express that in addition to the first statement A, there is also the second statement B which often emphasizes the negative aspect or difficulty of the situation. It can be translated as 'moreover', 'besides', 'not only', or 'but also' in English. The formation is used with two sentences, placing おまけに between those two sentences." :formation "Sentence A + おまけに  + Sentence B" :examples [{:ja "彼は遅刻した。おまけに宿題も忘れた。" :en "He was late. Moreover, he forgot his homework."} {:ja "このアパートは狭い。おまけに家賃が高い。" :en "This apartment is small. Besides, the rent is high."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-003" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。さて B。" :reading "A. Sate B." :meaning "Indicates a sudden change or shift in the topic or situation; 'now then', 'well', 'so'." :short "Indicates a sudden change or shift in the topic or situation; 'now then', 'well', 'so'." :long "The さて grammar point is used to indicate a sudden change or shift in the topic or situation. It often expresses a sense of moving on to the next topic or action, acting as a connector between sentences. It can be loosely translated as 'now then', 'well', or 'so' in English." :formation "A + さて + B" :examples [{:ja "今日の仕事が終わった。さて、これから帰るか。" :en "Today's work is done. Now then, shall we go home?"} {:ja "話がちょっと長くなったね。さて、もっと喜美子さんのことを教えてください。" :en "The conversation got a bit lengthy. Well, please tell me more about Kimiko."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-004" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。しかも B。" :reading "A. Shikamo B." :meaning "Expressing additional information; 'moreover', 'besides', 'furthermore'." :short "Expressing additional information; 'moreover', 'besides', 'furthermore'." :long "The しかも grammar point is used to express additional information, supplementing or emphasizing what was mentioned before. It can be translated as 'moreover', 'besides', or 'furthermore' in English. Both A and B are independent statements, and しかも is used to connect them." :formation "Statement A + 。しかも + Statement B。" :examples [{:ja "この料理は美味しいです。しかも、安いです。" :en "This dish is delicious. Moreover, it is cheap."} {:ja "彼は優秀な学生です。しかも、スポーツが得意です。" :en "He is an excellent student. Besides, he is good at sports."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-005" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。したがって B。" :reading "A. Shitagatte B." :meaning "Express a cause-and-effect relationship; 'therefore', 'consequently', 'so'." :short "Express a cause-and-effect relationship; 'therefore', 'consequently', 'so'." :long "The したがって grammar point is used to express a cause-and-effect relationship between two sentences, where 'A' is the cause and 'B' is the effect, result or conclusion. It can be translated as 'therefore', 'consequently', or 'so' in English." :formation "A + したがって + B" :examples [{:ja "今日は雨が降っています。したがって、傘を持って行きましょう。" :en "It is raining today. Therefore, let's bring an umbrella."} {:ja "彼は忙しいです。したがって、彼に連絡するのはやめましょう。" :en "He is busy. Consequently, let's not contact him."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-006" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A すなわち B。" :reading "A sunawachi B" :meaning "Express that A is equivalent to B; 'in other words', 'namely', 'that is to say'." :short "Express that A is equivalent to B; 'in other words', 'namely', 'that is to say'." :long "The grammar point A すなわち B is used to express that two phrases are equivalent or that the second phrase is further explanation, clarification or the true meaning of the first phrase. This can be translated as 'in other words', 'namely', or 'that is to say' in English." :formation "Phrase A + すなわち + Phrase B" :examples [{:ja "私は立派な成果を出したい、すなわち、成功したいのです。" :en "I want to achieve great results, in other words, I want to succeed."} {:ja "彼は言葉に詰まった、すなわち、何も言えなかった。" :en "He was at a loss for words, that is to say, he couldn't say anything."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-007" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。すると B。" :reading "~suruto" :meaning "Indicates that when action A is done, action or event B follows; 'then', 'when', 'if'." :short "Indicates that when action A is done, action or event B follows; 'then', 'when', 'if'." :long "The A。すると B。grammar point is used to express that when action A is done or takes place, action or event B follows. It can be translated as 'then', 'when', or 'if' in English. It is used to show a natural consequence or result of action A." :formation "A (Sentence) + すると + B (Sentence)" :examples [{:ja "電話をかけると、彼女が出ました。" :en "When I made the phone call, she answered."} {:ja "このボタンを押すと、ドアが開きます。" :en "If you press this button, the door will open."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-008" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。そういえば B。" :reading "~souieba" :meaning "Recalling something related to A; 'speaking of which', 'by the way', 'now that you mention it'." :short "Recalling something related to A; 'speaking of which', 'by the way', 'now that you mention it'." :long "The A。そういえば B。grammar point is used when something related to A is recalled or brought up. It can be translated as 'speaking of which', 'by the way', or 'now that you mention it' in English. It is commonly used to connect two related topics or ideas in a conversation and indicates a change of focus. The formation consists of two clauses: the first being A and the second being B, with 'そういえば' in between as a connector." :formation "A + そういえば + B" :examples [{:ja "映画。そういえば、彼は映画館で働いているんだ。" :en "Movies. Speaking of which, he works at a movie theater."} {:ja "山田さん。そういえば、彼女は今週末旅行しています。" :en "Ms. Yamada. By the way, she is traveling this weekend."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-009" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。そこで B。" :reading "~sokode" :meaning "Expresses 'therefore' or 'because of that'." :short "Expresses 'therefore' or 'because of that'." :long "The A。そこで B。grammar point is used to describe the reason for an action or situation in the A part and the result or decision made in the B part. It can be translated as 'therefore' or 'because of that' in English." :formation "A + そこで + B" :examples [{:ja "明日は試験があります。そこで今晩勉強しようと思います。" :en "There is an exam tomorrow. Therefore, I plan to study tonight."} {:ja "このレストランは混んでいます。そこで別の場所でランチを食べましょう。" :en "This restaurant is crowded. Therefore, let's eat lunch at a different place."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-010" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。それがB。" :reading "~sorega" :meaning "Expressing 'that's exactly what A is; in other words, B'." :short "Expressing 'that's exactly what A is; in other words, B'." :long "The grammar point A。それがB。can be used to restate or clarify that B is precisely what A means. It emphasizes the essence or definition of A by stating B. It can be translated as 'that’s what A is,' 'that is precisely A/B,' or 'in other words, B.'" :formation "A (Statement)。それが B (Restatement or explanation of A)。" :examples [{:ja "彼は勉強ができる。それが天才というものだ。" :en "He can study well. That's what you call a genius."} {:ja "美味しい料理を作る。それが私の趣味なんです。" :en "I make delicious food. That's my hobby."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-011" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。それで B。" :reading "~sore de" :meaning "Express a sequence of events or cause-and-effect; 'and then', 'so', 'because of that'." :short "Express a sequence of events or cause-and-effect; 'and then', 'so', 'because of that'." :long "The A。それで B。grammar point is used to connect two sentences or events, expressing a sequence of events or explaining the reason for the second event based on the first event. Essentially, it means 'and then', 'so', or 'because of that' in English." :formation "Sentence A +。それで+ Sentence B。" :examples [{:ja "昨日は雨が降っていた。それで公園に行かなかった。" :en "It was raining yesterday. So, I didn't go to the park."} {:ja "試験に落ちた。それで再試験を受けなければならない。" :en "I failed the exam. So, I have to take a re-exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-012" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。それでも B。" :reading "~sore demo" :meaning "Expresses 'even so', 'nevertheless', 'still', or 'but'; used to contrast two statements." :short "Expresses 'even so', 'nevertheless', 'still', or 'but'; used to contrast two statements." :long "The A。それでも B。grammar point is used to show a contrast between two statements. The first statement (A) presents a situation, and the second statement (B) presents an action or thought that might be unexpected or contrary to the initial situation. It can be translated as 'even so', 'nevertheless', 'still', or 'but' in English." :formation "A (situation) + 。それでも + B (action or thought)" :examples [{:ja "今日は日曜日です。それでも、仕事に行かなければなりません。" :en "Today is Sunday. Nevertheless, I have to go to work."} {:ja "彼は若いです。それでも、経験が豊富です。" :en "He is young. Even so, he has a lot of experience."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-013" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。それなのに B。" :reading "~sorenanoni" :meaning "Express contrast or a contradiction between two situations or facts; 'even though', 'despite', 'but still'." :short "Express contrast or a contradiction between two situations or facts; 'even though', 'despite', 'but still'." :long "The A。それなのにB。grammar point is used to express contrast, contradiction or unexpectedness between two situations or facts. It can be translated as 'even though', 'despite', or 'but still' in English. The formation is simple, with A and B representing the two contrasting or contradicting statements." :formation "A。それなのに B。" :examples [{:ja "彼は頭がいい。それなのに、試験に落ちた。" :en "He is smart. Despite that, he failed the exam."} {:ja "私は早く起きた。それなのに、バスに遅れた。" :en "I woke up early. Even so, I was late for the bus."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-014" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。それなら B。" :reading "A. Sore nara B." :meaning "Express 'if that's the case, then'; used to show a logical consequence or decision based on a given situation." :short "Express 'if that's the case, then'; used to show a logical consequence or decision based on a given situation." :long "The A。それなら B。grammar point is used to express a logical consequence or decision based on the given situation in the first part of the sentence (A). The phrase それなら can be translated as 'if that's the case', 'in that case', or 'then' in English." :formation "Situation A (sentence) + 。それなら + Situation B (sentence)" :examples [{:ja "試験が近い。それなら、もっと勉強しなければならない。" :en "The exam is coming soon. If that's the case, I must study more."} {:ja "明日は雨が降るだろう。それなら、傘を持って行こう。" :en "It will probably rain tomorrow. In that case, let's bring an umbrella."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-015" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A それはそうと B。" :reading "A Sore wa sou to B" :meaning "Changing the topic; 'by the way', 'speaking of which'." :short "Changing the topic; 'by the way', 'speaking of which'." :long "The grammar point A それはそうと B is used to change the topic or bring up a new subject in a conversation. It can be translated as 'by the way' or 'speaking of which' in English. It's used to connect two unrelated sentences or ideas, with A being the original topic and B being the new topic brought up." :formation "Sentence A + それはそうと + Sentence B" :examples [{:ja "それはそうと、今日の天気はいいですね。" :en "By the way, the weather is nice today, isn't it?"} {:ja "映画は面白かったです。それはそうと、この店のラーメンはおいしいですよ。" :en "The movie was interesting. Speaking of which, this restaurant's ramen is delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-016" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。だが B。" :reading "~daga" :meaning "Expresses contrasting ideas or statements; 'but', 'however'." :short "Expresses contrasting ideas or statements; 'but', 'however'." :long "The A。だが B。grammar point is used to express contrasting ideas or statements. It connects two sentences or phrases with a contrasting meaning. It can be translated as 'but' or 'however' in English." :formation "Sentence A + だが + Sentence B" :examples [{:ja "このレストランは値段が高い。だが、味はとてもいい。" :en "This restaurant is expensive. But, the taste is very good."} {:ja "彼は疲れている。だが、仕事を終わらせるまで休まない。" :en "He is tired. However, he will not rest until he finishes work."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-017" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。ただB。" :reading "~tada" :meaning "Express contrast between two situations; 'although', 'but', 'however'." :short "Express contrast between two situations; 'although', 'but', 'however'." :long "The ただ grammar point is used to express contrast between two situations or facts. It can often be translated as 'although', 'but', or 'however' in English. It is used to show a contradiction or a different aspect of a situation." :formation "Sentence A + ただ + Sentence B" :examples [{:ja "彼は頭がいい。ただ、努力はあまりしない。" :en "He is smart; however, he doesn't make much effort."} {:ja "外は雨が降っている。ただ、家にいると暇だ。" :en "It's raining outside, but I'm bored at home."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-018" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。ただしB。" :reading "A. Tadashi B" :meaning "Adding a condition or exception to a statement; 'however', 'provided that', 'but only if'." :short "Adding a condition or exception to a statement; 'however', 'provided that', 'but only if'." :long "The grammar point A。ただしB is used to add a condition or exception to a previous statement. It can be translated to 'however', 'provided that', or 'but only if' in English. This structure consists of two parts: A (statement) and B (condition/exception). The word ただし is used to connect these two parts." :formation "Statement A + ただし + Condition B" :examples [{:ja "この部屋を使ってもいいです。ただし、使った後で掃除してください。" :en "You can use this room. However, please clean up after you use it."} {:ja "明日から出張で不在になります。ただし、緊急の場合は携帯に連絡してください。" :en "I'll be away on a business trip starting tomorrow. However, in case of emergency, please contact me on my cell phone."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-019" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。だって B。" :reading "Datte~" :meaning "Used to give a reason, express cause or bring up a topic; 'because', 'since', 'it's just that'." :short "Used to give a reason, express cause or bring up a topic; 'because', 'since', 'it's just that'." :long "The A。だって B。grammar point is used to give a reason, express cause, or introduce a topic in a casual manner. It can be translated as 'because', 'since', or 'it's just that'. A is the reason and B is the statement that follows due to the reason. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + だって、い-Adjective + だって、な-Adjective + だって、Noun + だって" :examples [{:ja "暇だって、映画を見に行こうよ。" :en "Since we are free, let's go watch a movie."} {:ja "寒いだって、コートを着てください。" :en "Because it's cold, please wear a coat."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-020" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。ちなみに B。" :reading "A. Chinamini B." :meaning "Used to give additional information about the topic; 'by the way', 'incidentally'." :short "Used to give additional information about the topic; 'by the way', 'incidentally'." :long "The ちなみに grammar point is used to provide additional information or a side note about a topic that has been previously mentioned. It can be translated as 'by the way' or 'incidentally' in English. This grammar point is typically used to give further context, background information, or a slight change in topic." :formation "A。ちなみに B。" :examples [{:ja "今日は授業がありません。ちなみに、明日も休みです。" :en "There's no class today. By the way, tomorrow is also a day off."} {:ja "この店のラーメンは美味しいです。ちなみに、餃子もおすすめです。" :en "The ramen at this shop is delicious. By the way, their gyoza is also recommended."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-021" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。ということは B。" :reading "A. To iu koto wa B." :meaning "Expresses the meaning 'if A, that means B' or 'A implies B'." :short "Expresses the meaning 'if A, that means B' or 'A implies B'." :long "The A。ということは B。grammar point is used to express the logical conclusion that if A is true, then B must also be true. It can be translated as 'if A, that means B' or 'A implies B' in English. This pattern is used when the speaker wants to infer or deduce B based on the information provided by A." :formation "A + ということは + B" :examples [{:ja "彼女が泣いている。ということは何か悲しいことがあったに違いない。" :en "She is crying. That means something sad must have happened."} {:ja "このケーキが残っている。ということは、みんなお腹がいっぱいだったのかな。" :en "This cake is left over. That means everyone must have been full, I guess."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-022" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。というのは B。" :reading "Toiu no wa~" :meaning "Used to give an explanation or provide additional information for A; 'that is to say', 'in other words'." :short "Used to give an explanation or provide additional information for A; 'that is to say', 'in other words'." :long "The grammar point A。というのは B。 is used to provide an explanation, definition, or further information about statement A. It can be translated as 'that is to say', 'in other words', or 'namely' in English." :formation "[Statement A]。というのは、[Statement B]。" :examples [{:ja "彼は非常に疲れています。というのは、昨夜、車で遠くまで行ってきたためです。" :en "He is very tired. That is to say, because he went far by car last night."} {:ja "このレストランはおすすめです。というのは、料理が美味しいし、雰囲気もいいからです。" :en "I recommend this restaurant. That is to say, because the food is delicious and the atmosphere is nice."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-023" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。なおB。" :reading "A. Nao B." :meaning "Provides additional information; 'moreover', 'besides', 'in addition'." :short "Provides additional information; 'moreover', 'besides', 'in addition'." :long "The なお grammar point is used to provide additional information or emphasize an existing fact. It can be translated as 'moreover', 'besides', or 'in addition' in English. なお is placed between two sentences to connect them and provide supplementary information." :formation "A (sentence) + なお + B (sentence)" :examples [{:ja "明日は休みです。なお、月曜日も休みです。" :en "Tomorrow is a holiday. Moreover, Monday is also a holiday."} {:ja "彼は頭が良いです。なお、スポーツも得意です。" :en "He is smart. Besides, he is also good at sports."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-024" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。もっとも B。" :reading "Motto mo ~" :meaning "Express contrasting opinions or facts; 'although', 'but', 'however'." :short "Express contrasting opinions or facts; 'although', 'but', 'however'." :long "The A。もっとも B。 grammar point is used to express contrasting opinions or facts. It can be translated as 'although', 'but', or 'however' in English. This structure connects two clauses and highlights the contrast between them." :formation "Clause A + もっとも + Clause B" :examples [{:ja "彼は頭がいい。もっとも、勉強も大切だ。" :en "He is smart. However, studying is also important."} {:ja "このレストランは美味しい。もっとも、値段が高い。" :en "This restaurant is delicious. But, the prices are high."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-025" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "A。要するに B。" :reading "A. Yousuru ni B." :meaning "Expressing 'in short', 'in a nutshell', or 'to sum up' when restating or summarizing A as B." :short "Expressing 'in short', 'in a nutshell', or 'to sum up' when restating or summarizing A as B." :long "The A。要するに B。grammar point is used to restate or summarize the information provided in A more concisely as B. It can be translated as 'in short', 'in a nutshell', or 'to sum up' in English. The formation consists of two sentences or statements, A and B, with 要するに (yousuru ni) in between to indicate the summary or restatement of A as B." :formation "A。要するに B。" :examples [{:ja "このプロジェクトは成功しました。要するに、私たちのチームは素晴らしい仕事をしました。" :en "This project was successful. In short, our team did a great job."} {:ja "彼は料理が上手です。要するに、彼は優れたシェフです。" :en "He is good at cooking. In a nutshell, he is an excellent chef."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-026" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun につき" :reading "〜ni tsuki" :meaning "Indicates the reason, cause, or condition for something; 'due to', 'regarding', 'per'." :short "Indicates the reason, cause, or condition for something; 'due to', 'regarding', 'per'." :long "The につき grammar point is used to express the reason, cause, or condition for an event or situation. In English, it can be translated as 'due to', 'regarding', 'per', or 'for each'. It can be used with nouns." :formation "Noun + につき" :examples [{:ja "試験につき、今日は静かにしてください。" :en "Due to the exam, please be quiet today."} {:ja "このエリアは工事中につき、通行止めです。" :en "The area is closed for passage due to construction."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-027" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun にて" :reading "Noun nite" :meaning "Expresses location or means; 'at', 'in', 'by', 'with'." :short "Expresses location or means; 'at', 'in', 'by', 'with'." :long "The にて grammar point is used to indicate the location, means, or method of an action. It can be translated as 'at', 'in', 'by', or 'with' in English. It functions similarly to the particle で, but にて is more formal and often used in written language, formal speaking, and announcements." :formation "Noun + にて" :examples [{:ja "駅前広場にてイベントが開催されます。" :en "An event will be held at the station square."} {:ja "ホテルにて会議が行われます。" :en "The meeting will take place at the hotel."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-028" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun の ことだから" :reading "Noun no koto dakara" :meaning "Used to provide a reason or explanation based on one's knowledge about the noun; 'since', 'because', 'knowing'." :short "Used to provide a reason or explanation based on one's knowledge about the noun; 'since', 'because', 'knowing'." :long "The の ことだから (no koto dakara) grammar point is used when giving a reason or explanation based on one’s knowledge or understanding of a particular person or thing. It often implies 'given that this is the kind of person/thing it is'." :formation "Noun + のことだから" :examples [{:ja "料理が得意な彼のことだから、美味しい料理を作ってくれるでしょう。" :en "Since he’s good at cooking, he’ll probably make delicious food."} {:ja "いつも遅刻する彼女のことだから、今日も遅れるかもしれない。" :en "Because she’s always late, she might be late again today."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-029" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun を はじめ" :reading "Noun wo hajime" :meaning "Meaning 'starting with', 'including', 'not only... but also'." :short "Meaning 'starting with', 'including', 'not only... but also'." :long "The grammar point Nounをはじめ is used to show that there are other things or people being included apart from the mentioned noun. It can be used to provide an example, indicate a representative or list items. This can be translated as 'starting with', 'including', or 'not only... but also...' in English." :formation "Noun + をはじめ" :examples [{:ja "日本料理をはじめ、さまざまな料理がこのレストランで楽しめます。" :en "You can enjoy various dishes in this restaurant, starting with Japanese cuisine."} {:ja "彼は絵画をはじめ、彫刻や写真もコレクションしている。" :en "He collects not only paintings but also sculptures and photographs."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-030" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun を はじめとして" :reading "Noun wo hajime to shite" :meaning "Expresses 'starting with', 'beginning with', or 'including'." :short "Expresses 'starting with', 'beginning with', or 'including'." :long "The grammar point Noun を はじめとして is used to emphasize a particular item or example along with others. It translates to 'starting with', 'beginning with', or 'including' in English. It is used to present a specific case and imply there are more related items." :formation "Noun + をはじめとして" :examples [{:ja "東京をはじめとして、日本にはたくさんの観光スポットがあります。" :en "There are many tourist spots in Japan, starting with Tokyo."} {:ja "彼は、ピアノをはじめとして、たくさんの楽器を演奏できます。" :en "He can play many instruments, starting with piano."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-031" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun を はじめとする Noun" :reading "Noun o hajime to suru Noun" :meaning "Indicates a representative example of a group; 'starting with', 'including', 'such as'." :short "Indicates a representative example of a group; 'starting with', 'including', 'such as'." :long "The grammar pattern 'Noun を はじめとする Noun' is used to indicate a representative example of a group and suggests that there are other things besides the mentioned example. It can be translated as 'starting with', 'including', or 'such as' in English." :formation "Noun1 を はじめとする Noun2" :examples [{:ja "彼はフランス語をはじめとする外国語が話せる。" :en "He can speak foreign languages, starting with French."} {:ja "このコンサートには、BTSをはじめとする人気アーティストが出演する予定です。" :en "This concert is scheduled to feature popular artists, including BTS."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-032" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun を めぐって" :reading "Noun wo megutte" :meaning "Concerning; with regard to; about" :short "Concerning; with regard to; about" :long "The grammar point Noun を めぐって is used to express that something is concerning, with regard to or about a certain noun. It covers a topic or an issue that is central to a discussion or a situation." :formation "Noun + を + めぐって" :examples [{:ja "環境問題をめぐって、多くの議論が行われています。" :en "Many discussions are held concerning environmental issues."} {:ja "彼の死をめぐって、いくつかの陰謀説が浮かび上がっている。" :en "Several conspiracy theories have emerged regarding his death."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-033" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun を めぐる Noun" :reading "Noun o meguru Noun" :meaning "Indicate a situation or event revolving around a particular noun; 'concerning', 'regarding', 'pertaining to'." :short "Indicate a situation or event revolving around a particular noun; 'concerning', 'regarding', 'pertaining to'." :long "The NounをめぐるNoun grammar point is used to express that a situation or event is centered around a particular noun. It can be translated as 'concerning', 'regarding', or 'pertaining to' in English. The formation consists of the particle 'を' placed between two nouns, and the verb 'めぐる' (to revolve around) following the first noun." :formation "Noun1 + を + めぐる + Noun2" :examples [{:ja "この事件をめぐる議論が続いています。" :en "The debate concerning this incident continues."} {:ja "環境問題をめぐる国際会議が開かれます。" :en "An international conference regarding environmental issues will be held."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-034" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun を もとに" :reading "Noun o moto ni" :meaning "Based on; drawing from; using as a reference." :short "Based on; drawing from; using as a reference." :long "The grammar point Nounをもとに is used to convey that something is based on, drawn from, or using the noun as a reference. It is commonly used when one thing is being used as a foundation, source of information or inspiration for something else." :formation "Noun + をもとに" :examples [{:ja "この映画は実話をもとに作られました。" :en "This movie was made based on a true story."} {:ja "彼の経験をもとにアドバイスが必要だ。" :en "Advice is needed based on his experience."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-035" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Noun を もとにして" :reading "Noun wo moto ni shite" :meaning "Based on; using; utilizing." :short "Based on; using; utilizing." :long "The 'Noun をもとにして' grammar point is used to express that something is done based on, using, or utilizing the noun. It indicates that the noun is the foundation, source or basis for the action or statement." :formation "Noun + をもとにして" :examples [{:ja "彼の意見をもとにして、計画を変更しました。" :en "Based on his opinion, we changed the plan."} {:ja "この小説は実話をもとにして書かれています。" :en "This novel is written based on a true story."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-036" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Verb ことなく" :reading "~kotonaku" :meaning "Means 'without doing', 'not doing', or 'without happening'." :short "Means 'without doing', 'not doing', or 'without happening'." :long "The grammar point ことなく is added to the stem of a verb to express that something doesn't happen or isn't done. It can be translated as 'without doing', 'not doing', or 'without happening'. The formation depends on whether it is used with a verb." :formation "Verb-stem + ことなく" :examples [{:ja "彼女は泣くことなく帰ってしまいました。" :en "She went home without crying."} {:ja "彼は注意することなく突然出かけた。" :en "He suddenly left without giving any warning."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-037" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "Verb ないことには Verb ない" :reading "~nai koto ni wa ~ nai" :meaning "Express that something cannot be done without meeting a certain condition." :short "Express that something cannot be done without meeting a certain condition." :long "The ないことには grammar point is used to indicate that something cannot be done or achieved unless a certain condition is met. This pattern is formed by attaching ないことには to the negative form of a verb, followed by a negative outcome statement. It often appears as V-ない + ことには + V-ない or a negative expression." :formation "Verb-ない + ことには + Verb-ない" :examples [{:ja "薬を飲まないことには、病気が治らない。" :en "Unless you take the medicine, the illness won't get better."} {:ja "この問題によく考えないことには、解決できない。" :en "Unless you think carefully about this problem, you can't solve it."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-038" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～あげく" :reading "~ageku" :meaning "Expresses the outcome of a series of actions or events; 'in the end', 'finally', 'after'." :short "Expresses the outcome of a series of actions or events; 'in the end', 'finally', 'after'." :long "The ～あげく grammar point is used to express the outcome or result after a series of actions or events. It can usually be translated into English as 'in the end', 'finally', or 'after'. The typical formation is adding あげく to the past form of a verb (Verb-た), or adding のあげく to a noun. This indicates that, after a long process or a number of attempts, a certain final outcome is reached—often with a sense that it took considerable effort or time." :formation "Verb-た + あげく / Noun + の + あげく" :examples [{:ja "長い時間待ったあげく、電車が来なかった。" :en "After waiting a long time, in the end, the train didn't come."} {:ja "いろいろな方法を試したあげく、成功した。" :en "After trying various methods, I finally succeeded."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-039" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～あまり" :reading "〜amari" :meaning "Expresses the result of doing something excessively or beyond a certain point." :short "Expresses the result of doing something excessively or beyond a certain point." :long "The ～あまり grammar point is used to express a negative or undesirable outcome caused by doing something excessively or beyond a certain point. It often follows verbs, adjectives, and nouns, and is commonly translated as 'so much', 'too much', or 'to the extent that'." :formation "Verb-ます stem + あまり, い-Adjective + あまり, な-Adjective + のあまり, Noun + のあまり" :examples [{:ja "昨日は疲れすぎたあまり、朝まで眠り続けました。" :en "Yesterday, I was so tired that I slept until morning."} {:ja "彼女はうれしいあまり、泣き出してしまった。" :en "She was so happy that she ended up crying."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-040" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～うちに" :reading "〜uchi ni" :meaning "Expresses doing something while a certain condition still holds; 'while', 'before', 'as long as'." :short "Expresses doing something while a certain condition still holds; 'while', 'before', 'as long as'." :long "The ～うちに grammar point is used to indicate that an action should be done while a certain condition still exists or before a situation changes. It can be translated as 'while', 'before', or 'as long as' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + うちに, い-Adjective + うちに, な-Adjective + なうちに, Noun + のうちに" :examples [{:ja "元気なうちに、旅行を楽しんでください。" :en "Enjoy traveling while you are healthy."} {:ja "若いうちに、いろいろなことを学ぶべきだ。" :en "One should learn various things while they are young."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-041" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～がい" :reading "〜gai" :meaning "Expresses 'worth doing' for verbs." :short "Expresses 'worth doing' for verbs." :long "The ～がい grammar point is used to express that an action is worth doing or to emphasize the value and importance of doing something. It is attached to verbs in their stem form and can be translated as 'worth doing' in English." :formation "Verb-stem + がい" :examples [{:ja "この本は読みがいがあります。" :en "This book is worth reading."} {:ja "早起きしがいがあると思いますよ。" :en "I think it's worth waking up early."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-042" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～かいがあって" :reading "〜kaiga atte" :meaning "Used to express that an effort is worth it or has value; 'to be worth the effort' or 'to pay off'." :short "Used to express that an effort is worth it or has value; 'to be worth the effort' or 'to pay off'." :long "The ～かいがあって grammar point is used to convey the idea that an action or effort is worth it, or that it has value. It is often translated as 'to be worth the effort' or 'to pay off' in English. It is typically associated with actions or efforts that lead to a successful or desirable result." :formation "Verb-past + かいがあって / Noun + の + かいがあって" :examples [{:ja "毎日勉強したかいがあって、テストに合格しました。" :en "Studying every day paid off—I passed the test."} {:ja "練習のかいがあって、試合に勝つことができた。" :en "The practice paid off, and we were able to win the match."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-043" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～かいもなく" :reading "〜kai mo naku" :meaning "Expresses the meaning of 'without worth', 'in vain', 'fruitless'." :short "Expresses the meaning of 'without worth', 'in vain', 'fruitless'." :long "The ～かいもなく grammar point is used to express that something has been done without worth, in vain, or fruitlessly. It often appears as 'Verb-た + かいもなく' or 'Noun + の + かいもなく' to indicate that an effort did not produce the hoped-for result." :formation "Verb-た + かいもなく / Noun + の + かいもなく" :examples [{:ja "長い間苦労したかいもなく、失敗してしまった。" :en "I struggled for a long time, but in the end, it was all in vain and I failed."} {:ja "このプロジェクトにたくさん時間をかけたかいもなく、うまくいかなかった。" :en "I spent a lot of time on this project, but it ended fruitlessly."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-044" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～かける" :reading "〜kakeru" :meaning "Indicates a process or action is 'in progress' or 'halfway'." :short "Indicates a process or action is 'in progress' or 'halfway'." :long "The ～かける grammar point is used to describe a process or action that is in progress, halfway, or not yet completed. It can be attached to verbs to imply that the action is still ongoing or something is in a certain state of partial completion." :formation "Verb-stem + かける" :examples [{:ja "彼女は食べかけのリンゴを置いて出かけた。" :en "She left a partially eaten apple and went out."} {:ja "子供たちはやりかけの宿題を机の上に残した。" :en "The children left their half-finished homework on the desk."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-045" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～がち" :reading "〜gachi" :meaning "Expresses that something tends to occur frequently or habitually." :short "Expresses that something tends to occur frequently or habitually." :long "The ～がち grammar point is used to express that something has a tendency to occur frequently or habitually. It can be attached to verbs and adjectives to indicate that the action or state tends to occur often." :formation "Verb-ます stem + がち / い-Adjective stem + がち / な-Adjective stem + がち" :examples [{:ja "彼は風邪を引きがちです。" :en "He tends to catch colds easily."} {:ja "この地域は雨が降りがちです。" :en "This area tends to get a lot of rain."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-046" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～か～ないかのうちに" :reading "〜ka〜naika no uchi ni" :meaning "Expresses an action that occurs immediately after another action; 'as soon as', 'no sooner than', 'the moment'." :short "Expresses an action that occurs immediately after another action; 'as soon as', 'no sooner than', 'the moment'." :long "The ～か～ないかのうちに grammar point is used to express that an action occurs immediately after another action. In English, it can be translated as 'as soon as', 'no sooner than', or 'the moment'. This grammar point is used with verbs, showing the verb in both affirmative and negative forms with か and ないか, followed by のうちに." :formation "Verb-casual + か + Verb-negative form + かのうちに" :examples [{:ja "駅に着くか着かないかのうちに、雨が降り出した。" :en "No sooner had I arrived at the station than it started raining."} {:ja "ケータイを買うか買わないかのうちに、新しいモデルが発売された。" :en "As soon as I decided to buy the cell phone, a new model was released."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-047" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～かねない" :reading "〜kane nai" :meaning "Express the likelihood of a negative outcome or consequence." :short "Express the likelihood of a negative outcome or consequence." :long "The ～かねない grammar point is used to express the possibility or likelihood of a negative outcome or consequence, often used when there is concern about the result. It is typically used with verbs in their stem form and can be understood as 'might (have a bad result)'." :formation "Verb-stem + かねない" :examples [{:ja "彼はあまりにも無責任で、約束を破りかねない。" :en "He is so irresponsible that he might break his promise."} {:ja "注意しないと、事故を起こしかねない。" :en "If you don't pay attention, you might cause an accident."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-048" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～かねる" :reading "〜kaneru" :meaning "Express inability or impossibility of doing an action (often as a polite refusal)." :short "Express inability or impossibility of doing an action (often as a polite refusal)." :long "The ～かねる grammar point is used to express that someone cannot do an action or that something is impossible to do. It typically has a nuance of reluctance or polite refusal and is formed by adding かねる to the ます-stem of the verb." :formation "Verb-ます stem + かねる" :examples [{:ja "その問題は難しいので、私には解決しかねます。" :en "Since that problem is difficult, I cannot solve it."} {:ja "彼の言っていることが真実かどうか判断しかねます。" :en "I cannot judge whether what he is saying is true or not."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-049" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～かのようだ" :reading "〜ka no you da" :meaning "Express a similarity or comparison; 'as if', 'like', 'as though'." :short "Express a similarity or comparison; 'as if', 'like', 'as though'." :long "The ～かのようだ grammar point is used to express similarity or comparison between two things or situations. It can be translated as 'as if', 'like', or 'as though' in English. The standard patterns differ depending on whether it’s used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, or nouns. For な-adjectives and nouns, we usually insert である before かのようだ." :formation "Verb-casual + かのようだ, い-Adjective + かのようだ, な-Adjective + であるかのようだ, Noun + であるかのようだ" :examples [{:ja "彼は犬が怖いかのようだ。" :en "He seems as if he is scared of dogs."} {:ja "この部屋は寒いかのようだ。" :en "This room feels as if it's cold."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-050" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～か～まいか" :reading "〜ka 〜maika" :meaning "Used to express a dilemma or indecision between two options or actions." :short "Used to express a dilemma or indecision between two options or actions." :long "The ～か～まいか grammar point expresses uncertainty or indecision, often with a more formal or literary feel. Typically, it is seen with verbs in the volitional form plus まい (the negative volitional). In more academic or stiff writing, い／な-adjectives and nouns plus である can also appear before まいか." :formation "Verb-volitional + か + Verb-volitional + まいか, い-Adjective + であるか + い-Adjective + であるまいか, な-Adjective + であるか + な-Adjective + であるまいか, Noun + であるか + Noun + であるまいか" :examples [{:ja "このプロジェクトを引き受けようか引き受けまいか、まだ決めていない。" :en "I haven't decided whether to take on this project or not."} {:ja "彼が成功しようか成功するまいか、今は予測できない。" :en "Whether he will succeed or not is unpredictable at the moment."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-051" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～からこそ" :reading "〜kara koso" :meaning "Express the emphasis on the reason; 'precisely because', 'it is precisely because'." :short "Express the emphasis on the reason; 'precisely because', 'it is precisely because'." :long "The ～からこそ grammar point is used to emphasize the reason or cause of a situation, often implying that the effect is especially notable or even surprising. It can be translated as 'precisely because' or 'it is precisely because.'" :formation "Verb-casual + からこそ, い-Adjective + からこそ, な-Adjective + だからこそ, Noun + だからこそ" :examples [{:ja "失敗したからこそ、成功の意味がわかる。" :en "It is precisely because I failed that I understand the meaning of success."} {:ja "苦しかったからこそ、今日の私がある。" :en "It is precisely because I went through hardships that I am who I am today."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-052" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "〜からして" :reading "〜kara shite" :meaning "Expresses that something is exceptional, unusual, or negative based on a particular aspect or point." :short "Expresses that something is exceptional, unusual, or negative based on a particular aspect or point." :long "The 〜からして grammar point is used when you want to make a conclusion or emphasize that something is exceptional (often negative or surprising) from a certain aspect or starting point. The part before からして serves as the base or clue for the judgment." :formation "Noun + からして" :examples [{:ja "彼の顔つきからして、何か悪いことがあったのだろう。" :en "From his facial expression, something bad must have happened."} {:ja "この料理の見た目からして、おいしくなさそうだ。" :en "Judging from the appearance of this dish, it doesn't look delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-053" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～からすると" :reading "〜kara suru to" :meaning "Expresses a judgment or evaluation based on a certain standard; 'from this perspective', 'considering', 'judging from'." :short "Expresses a judgment or evaluation based on a certain standard; 'from this perspective', 'considering', 'judging from'." :long "The ～からすると grammar point is used to make a judgment or evaluation based on some criterion, perspective, or standard. It can be translated as 'from this perspective', 'considering', or 'judging from' in English." :formation "Noun + からすると" :examples [{:ja "経験からすると、この仕事は難しいでしょう。" :en "Considering their experience, this job might be difficult."} {:ja "評判からすると、あのレストランはとても美味しいです。" :en "Judging from the reputation, that restaurant is very delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-054" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～からといって" :reading "〜kara to itte" :meaning "Expresses 'just because...doesn't mean' or 'even though...not necessarily'." :short "Expresses 'just because...doesn't mean' or 'even though...not necessarily'." :long "The ～からといって grammar point is used to convey that even though something is true, it does not necessarily mean a certain result will occur. It can be translated as 'just because...doesn't mean' or 'even though...not necessarily.'" :formation "Verb-casual + からといって, い-Adjective + からといって, な-Adjective + だからといって, Noun + だからといって" :examples [{:ja "雨が降ったからといって、試合が中止になるわけではありません。" :en "Just because it rained doesn't mean the game will be canceled."} {:ja "彼が有名だからといって、彼の意見がいつも正しいわけではない。" :en "Just because he is famous doesn't mean his opinions are always correct."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-055" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～から～にかけて" :reading "〜kara 〜ni kakete" :meaning "Expresses a range of time, space, or quantities; 'from...to', 'between...and', 'over'." :short "Expresses a range of time, space, or quantities; 'from...to', 'between...and', 'over'." :long "The ～から～にかけて grammar pattern is used to indicate a range or scope—often in time or space. It can be translated as 'from...to', 'between...and', or 'over' in English." :formation "Noun 1 + から + Noun 2 + にかけて" :examples [{:ja "午前９時から午後５時にかけて、仕事をしています。" :en "I work from 9 in the morning to 5 in the evening."} {:ja "東京から大阪にかけて、新幹線が走っています。" :en "The bullet train runs from Tokyo to Osaka."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-056" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～からには" :reading "〜kara niwa" :meaning "Express a sense of responsibility or determination; 'since', 'now that', 'if'." :short "Express a sense of responsibility or determination; 'since', 'now that', 'if'." :long "The ～からには grammar point is used to show a sense of responsibility or strong resolve once a certain condition is met. It can be translated as 'since', 'now that', or 'if'." :formation "Noun + だ + からには, な-Adjective + だ + からには, い-Adjective + からには, Verb-casual + からには" :examples [{:ja "試験に合格したからには、一所懸命働かなければならない。" :en "Since I passed the exam, I have to work hard."} {:ja "約束したからには、守らなければなりません。" :en "Since I made a promise, I must keep it."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-057" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～から見ると" :reading "〜kara miru to" :meaning "Express a perspective or point of view; 'from the standpoint of', 'in terms of', 'from the perspective of'." :short "Express a perspective or point of view; 'from the standpoint of', 'in terms of', 'from the perspective of'." :long "The ～から見ると grammar point is used to express a particular perspective or point of view. In English, it translates to 'from the standpoint of', 'from the perspective of', or 'in terms of'. It is usually used with a noun that indicates the perspective." :formation "Noun + から見ると" :examples [{:ja "費用から見ると、このプランはお得です。" :en "In terms of cost, this plan is a good deal."} {:ja "彼女の意見から見ると、社長の決定は理解できる。" :en "From her perspective, the president's decision is understandable."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-058" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～から言うと" :reading "〜kara iuto" :meaning "Express a judgment or opinion based on a specific criterion; 'speaking from', 'in terms of', 'from the viewpoint of'." :short "Express a judgment or opinion based on a specific criterion; 'speaking from', 'in terms of', 'from the viewpoint of'." :long "The ～から言うと grammar point is used to express a judgment or opinion based on a specific criterion or aspect. It can be translated as 'speaking from', 'in terms of', or 'from the viewpoint of' in English. It is most commonly used with nouns, although you may occasionally see other forms (like adjectives)." :formation "Noun + から言うと (common), [Verb-casual / Adjective + から言って] (less common)" :examples [{:ja "値段から言うと、この商品が最もお得です。" :en "Speaking from the viewpoint of price, this product is the best deal."} {:ja "味から言って、このレストランはおいしいと思います。" :en "In terms of taste, I think this restaurant is delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-059" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～くせに" :reading "〜kuse ni" :meaning "Used to criticize someone for doing something contrary to what one would expect from their nature or situation." :short "Used to criticize someone for doing something contrary to what one would expect from their nature or situation." :long "The grammar point ～くせに is used to express criticism or disapproval toward someone who does something contradictory to their expected nature, role, or prior statement. It's used in fairly casual or spoken contexts and can sound quite critical or accusing. Note that there's a colloquial variant ～くせして, but ～くせに is the standard form taught in most references." :formation "Verb (casual) + くせに / い-Adjective + くせに / な-Adjective + な(or の) + くせに / Noun + の + くせに" :examples [{:ja "彼はいつも遅刻してくるくせに、自分が待たされるのが嫌いだ。" :en "He’s always late, yet he hates being kept waiting."} {:ja "悪いなと言ってるくせに、また同じことを繰り返している。" :en "He says he's sorry, yet he keeps doing the same thing."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-060" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ことから" :reading "〜koto kara" :meaning "Indicates the reason or basis for something; 'because', 'since', 'due to'." :short "Indicates the reason or basis for something; 'because', 'since', 'due to'." :long "The ～ことから grammar point is used to indicate the reason or basis for a conclusion, fact, or premise. It can be translated as 'because', 'since', or 'due to' in English. The same pattern is used whether it’s with verbs, adjectives, or nouns (with the appropriate こと or の)." :formation "Verb-casual + ことから / い-Adjective + ことから / な-Adjective + なことから / Noun + のことから" :examples [{:ja "彼は毎日運動することから、体が丈夫です。" :en "Because he exercises every day, he is physically strong."} {:ja "この店のサービスがいいことから、いつも混んでいます。" :en "This store is always crowded because of its good service."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-061" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ことに" :reading "〜koto ni" :meaning "Used to express feelings or emotions about a situation, action, or outcome." :short "Used to express feelings or emotions about a situation, action, or outcome." :long "The ～ことに grammar point is used to highlight the speaker’s feelings or emotional reaction toward a situation, action, or outcome. It often appears in constructions like 驚いたことに, 残念なことに, 不思議なことに, etc." :formation "Verb-casual + ことに / い-Adjective + ことに / な-Adjective + なことに / Noun + のことに" :examples [{:ja "彼が急に帰国することに驚いた。" :en "I was surprised that he suddenly returned to his home country."} {:ja "彼女が優勝したことに感動した。" :en "I was moved by the fact that she won the championship."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-062" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ことになっている" :reading "〜koto ni natte iru" :meaning "Expresses that something is supposed to be or has been decided (rule, plan, custom)." :short "Expresses that something is supposed to be or has been decided (rule, plan, custom)." :long "The ～ことになっている grammar point is used when something has been decided, planned, or there is a rule/custom that everyone follows. It can be translated as 'it is supposed to be,' 'it is decided,' or 'it is customary that.'" :formation "Verb-plain form + ことになっている / い-Adjective + ことになっている / な-Adjective + だ + ことになっている / Noun + だ + ことになっている" :examples [{:ja "日本では、左側を運転することになっています。" :en "In Japan, it is decided that people drive on the left side."} {:ja "会社の規則では、制服を着ることになっている。" :en "According to the company rules, we are supposed to wear uniforms."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-063" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～さえ～ば" :reading "〜sae ~ba" :meaning "Expresses that, as long as a certain condition is met, something else will follow; 'if only', 'provided that'." :short "Expresses that, as long as a certain condition is met, something else will follow; 'if only', 'provided that'." :long "The ～さえ～ば grammar point is used to indicate that if a certain single condition is fulfilled, everything else will be fine or a certain result will occur. It often translates as 'as long as...' or 'if only...'." :formation "Noun + さえ + Verb-conditional / Verb-ますstem + さえすれば / etc. (varies by part of speech)" :examples [{:ja "お金さえあれば、幸せです。" :en "As long as I have money, I’m happy."} {:ja "試験に合格しさえすれば、卒業できます。" :en "If only I pass the exam, I can graduate."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-064" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ざるを得ない" :reading "〜zaru wo enai" :meaning "Have no choice but to; cannot help but." :short "Have no choice but to; cannot help but." :long "The ～ざるを得ない grammar point is used to indicate that one has no choice but to do something or cannot avoid doing it. It is formed from the negative form (ない) but replaces ない with ざる, followed by を得ない. (Note: する → せざるを得ない.)" :formation "Verb-ない form (drop ない) + ざるを得ない" :examples [{:ja "この仕事は締め切りが近いので、残業せざるを得ない。" :en "Since the deadline is near, I have no choice but to work overtime."} {:ja "電車が遅れたため、タクシーに乗るざるを得なかった。" :en "Because the train was delayed, I had no choice but to take a taxi."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-065" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ずにはいられない" :reading "〜zu ni wa irarenai" :meaning "Cannot help but; cannot resist doing; an irresistible urge." :short "Cannot help but; cannot resist doing; an irresistible urge." :long "The ～ずにはいられない grammar point expresses that one cannot resist or help doing something. In other words, there is a strong urge that one cannot suppress. It's formed from the negative stem of a verb plus ずにはいられない. (Note: する → せずにはいられない.)" :formation "Verb-ない form (drop ない) + ずにはいられない" :examples [{:ja "この曲を聞くと、歌わずにはいられない。" :en "When I hear this song, I can’t help but sing."} {:ja "彼女が泣いているのを見ると、慰めずにはいられない。" :en "When I see her crying, I can’t resist comforting her."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-066" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～そうにない" :reading "〜sou ni nai" :meaning "Express the unlikelihood of something happening; 'not likely', 'doesn't seem'." :short "Express the unlikelihood of something happening; 'not likely', 'doesn't seem'." :long "The ～そうにない grammar point is used to indicate that something does not appear likely to happen. Often translated as 'doesn't seem like' or 'not likely to...'. Attach そうにない to the verb stem or adjective stem." :formation "Verb-stem + そうにない / い-Adjective (drop い) + そうにない / な-Adjective + そうにない" :examples [{:ja "この雨は止みそうにない。" :en "It doesn't seem like this rain will stop."} {:ja "彼が試験に合格しそうにない。" :en "It doesn't look like he'll pass the exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-067" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～たかと思ったら" :reading "〜ta ka to omottara" :meaning "Express a sequence of events happening in quick succession; 'as soon as', 'no sooner than'." :short "Express a sequence of events happening in quick succession; 'as soon as', 'no sooner than'." :long "The ～たかと思ったら grammar point indicates that one action occurs almost immediately after another, often conveying surprise at how quickly it happens. It is frequently translated as 'as soon as...' or 'no sooner than...'." :formation "Verb-た form + かと思ったら" :examples [{:ja "電話をかけたかと思ったら、すぐに彼女が出た。" :en "No sooner had I called than she picked up."} {:ja "離陸したかと思ったら、飛行機が揺れ始めた。" :en "As soon as we took off, the plane started shaking."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-068" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～たきり" :reading "〜takiri" :meaning "Express 'since then' or 'after'; used to indicate that an action occurred once and has not been repeated or that a state has continued since a certain event or time." :short "Express 'since then' or 'after'; used to indicate that an action occurred once and has not been repeated or that a state has continued since a certain event or time." :long "The ～たきり grammar point is used to express that an action occurred once and has not been repeated, or that a situation or state has continued since a certain event or time. It can be translated as 'since then', 'after', or 'one time only' in English." :formation "Verb-past + きり" :examples [{:ja "彼は会社を辞めたきり、連絡がない。" :en "Ever since he quit the company, there's been no contact."} {:ja "昨日の朝、メールを送ったきり、返事がない。" :en "I sent an email yesterday morning, and there's been no reply since then."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-069" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～だけあって" :reading "〜dake atte" :meaning "Expresses that something is as expected or deserves its reputation." :short "Expresses that something is as expected or deserves its reputation." :long "The ～だけあって grammar point is used to indicate that something is as expected or deserves its reputation, based on certain facts or qualities. It can be translated as 'no wonder', 'as expected', or 'it is natural that' in English." :formation "Verb-casual + だけあって, い-Adjective + だけあって, な-Adjective + なだけあって, Noun + だけあって" :examples [{:ja "彼は日本語が上手だけあって、日本人のように話すことができる。" :en "As expected from his good Japanese skills, he can speak like a native."} {:ja "このレストランは有名だけあって、料理がとても美味しい。" :en "No wonder this restaurant is famous; the food is very delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-070" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～だけましだ" :reading "〜dake mashi da" :meaning "Expresses that although the situation isn’t ideal, it could be worse; 'it’s not great, but at least...'." :short "Expresses that although the situation isn’t ideal, it could be worse; 'it’s not great, but at least...'." :long "The ～だけましだ grammar point is used to compare two undesirable or less-than-ideal situations, saying that one is not good, but still better than the worse alternative. It often translates as 'at least (it’s only...)' or 'it could be worse.'" :formation "Verb-casual + だけましだ, い-Adjective + だけましだ, な-Adjective + だけましだ, Noun + だけましだ" :examples [{:ja "失敗するだけましだ。もっと大変なことにならなくてよかった。" :en "At least we only failed—it could’ve been much worse."} {:ja "遅れるだけましだ。来られないよりはいい。" :en "At least I’m only late. It’s better than not being able to come at all."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-071" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～たところ" :reading "〜ta tokoro" :meaning "Used to describe an action that has just been completed, often followed by a result or observation." :short "Used to describe an action that has just been completed, often followed by a result or observation." :long "The ～たところ grammar point is used when an action has just been completed and the speaker wants to describe an observation or result that follows. It can be translated as 'just finished doing something' or 'as soon as (I did something)...'." :formation "Verb-past + ところ" :examples [{:ja "掃除したところ、部屋がずっとキレイになりました。" :en "I just finished cleaning, and the room is so much cleaner now."} {:ja "彼に聞いたところ、その質問の答えは分からないみたいです。" :en "I just asked him, and it seems he doesn't know the answer."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-072" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～たとたん" :reading "〜ta totan" :meaning "Express the moment something happens; 'as soon as', 'just when'." :short "Express the moment something happens; 'as soon as', 'just when'." :long "The ～たとたん grammar point is used to express that an action or event occurred at the same moment another action or event happened. It can be translated as 'as soon as' or 'just when.' It requires the past tense of a verb." :formation "Verb-past + たとたん" :examples [{:ja "ドアを開けたとたん、雨が降り始めた。" :en "As soon as I opened the door, it started raining."} {:ja "電話をかけたとたん、バッテリーが切れた。" :en "Just when I made the phone call, the battery died."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-073" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～だらけ" :reading "〜darake" :meaning "Indicates that something is full of or covered with a certain (usually negative) characteristic." :short "Indicates that something is full of or covered with a certain (usually negative) characteristic." :long "The ～だらけ grammar point is used to express that something is full of, covered with, or filled with a certain characteristic. It frequently has a negative or undesirable nuance." :formation "Noun + だらけ" :examples [{:ja "彼の部屋はゴミだらけだ。" :en "His room is full of trash."} {:ja "このビーチは岩だらけで泳ぎにくい。" :en "This beach is full of rocks and is difficult to swim at."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-074" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～っこない" :reading "〜kkonai" :meaning "Expresses the impossibility or very low probability of something happening; 'no way', 'impossible'." :short "Expresses the impossibility or very low probability of something happening; 'no way', 'impossible'." :long "The ～っこない grammar point is used to express that something is impossible or extremely unlikely. It’s formed by attaching ～っこない to the negative stem of a verb. It can be translated as 'no way', 'no chance', or 'impossible.'" :formation "Verb-negative stem + っこない" :examples [{:ja "こんなに雪が降っているんだから、学校に間に合いっこない。" :en "With snow falling this heavily, there's no way we'll make it to school on time."} {:ja "彼は泳げないので、プールの向こう側に泳いで行きっこない。" :en "Since he can’t swim, there’s no way he’ll make it to the other side of the pool."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-075" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～つつ" :reading "〜tsutsu" :meaning "Shows two actions happening simultaneously or a contrast; 'while', 'although'." :short "Shows two actions happening simultaneously or a contrast; 'while', 'although'." :long "The ～つつ grammar point is used to express two actions occurring simultaneously, or to show a contrast between an ongoing action and another factor. It can be translated as 'while' or 'although.' It is attached to a verb in its stem form." :formation "Verb-stem + つつ" :examples [{:ja "彼はテレビを見つつ、宿題をしていました。" :en "He was doing his homework while watching TV."} {:ja "最近忙しいと思いつつ、毎日運動を続けています。" :en "Although I feel busy these days, I still exercise every day."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-076" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～つつある" :reading "〜tsutsu aru" :meaning "Expresses an ongoing process or gradual change." :short "Expresses an ongoing process or gradual change." :long "The ～つつある grammar point is used to describe something that is in the middle of a gradual change or development. It can be translated as '(something) is in the process of ~ing.' The most common usage is with the verb stem plus つつある." :formation "Verb-stem + つつある" :examples [{:ja "地球温暖化は進行しつつある。" :en "Global warming is in progress."} {:ja "この町は急速に発展しつつある。" :en "This town is rapidly developing."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-077" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～っぱなし" :reading "〜ppanashi" :meaning "Express a continuous action or state; 'left as is', 'non-stop'." :short "Express a continuous action or state; 'left as is', 'non-stop'." :long "The ～っぱなし grammar point is used to express that something is left in a certain condition for an extended time or that an action continues without stopping. It attaches to the ます-stem of verbs." :formation "Verb-ます stem + っぱなし" :examples [{:ja "窓を開けっぱなしにして、部屋が寒くなってしまった。" :en "I left the window open, and the room got cold."} {:ja "彼は音楽を聴くのが好きで、いつもイヤホンをつけっぱなしです。" :en "He loves listening to music and always has his earphones on."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-078" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～っぽい" :reading "〜ppoi" :meaning "Indicates that something has the appearance, characteristics, or behavior of something else." :short "Indicates that something has the appearance, characteristics, or behavior of something else." :long "The ～っぽい grammar point is used when something or someone appears to have characteristics, traits, or a vibe that resemble something else. It can mean 'looks like,' 'seems like,' or 'has the quality of.'" :formation "Noun + っぽい, Verb-casual + っぽい, い-Adjective + っぽい, な-Adjective + っぽい" :examples [{:ja "彼女は大人っぽい服を着ている。" :en "She’s wearing clothes that look mature."} {:ja "この説明は分かりにくっぽい。" :en "This explanation seems hard to understand."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-079" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ていられない" :reading "〜te irarenai" :meaning "Expresses that one cannot afford to do something; 'can't keep on', 'unable to continue'." :short "Expresses that one cannot afford to do something; 'can't keep on', 'unable to continue'." :long "The ～ていられない grammar point is used to express a feeling of not being able to continue doing something or remaining in a certain state. It can be translated as 'can't keep on', 'unable to continue', or 'can't afford to' in English. The formation is the same for all verb types." :formation "Verb-て form + いられない" :examples [{:ja "こんなに忙しいのに、無理に遊んでいられない。" :en "I'm so busy that I can't afford to just play around."} {:ja "寒すぎて、外で待っていられない。" :en "It's too cold; I can't keep waiting outside."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-080" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～てかなわない" :reading "〜te kanawanai" :meaning "Expresses that something cannot be endured, tolerated, or is unbearable." :short "Expresses that something cannot be endured, tolerated, or is unbearable." :long "The ～てかなわない grammar point is typically used with adjectives (or a rephrased verb) in 〜て form to show an unbearable situation or feeling. It means you 'can’t stand' or 'can’t endure' something. It often appears with expressions like 「暑くてかなわない」「うるさくてかなわない」 and so on." :formation "Adjective (〜く / 〜で) + て + かなわない / Verb phrase (reworded to an adjective/negative) + て + かなわない" :examples [{:ja "こんなに暑くてかなわない。" :en "I can’t stand this heat."} {:ja "彼の話は失礼すぎてかなわない。" :en "His remarks are so rude I can’t put up with them."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-081" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～てからでないと" :reading "〜te kara denai to" :meaning "Indicates that something cannot be done before a certain action is completed; 'not until', 'only after'." :short "Indicates that something cannot be done before a certain action is completed; 'not until', 'only after'." :long "The ～てからでないと grammar point is used to express that an action cannot be done before another action has been completed. It can be translated as 'not until' or 'only after' in English. It attaches to a verb in the て-form, plus からでないと." :formation "Verb-て + からでないと" :examples [{:ja "宿題をしてからでないと、遊びに行けません。" :en "You cannot go play until you’ve finished your homework."} {:ja "食事をしてからでないと、デザートを食べられません。" :en "You cannot eat dessert until after having a meal."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-082" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～てこそ" :reading "〜te koso" :meaning "Emphasizes the condition; 'only when', 'precisely because'." :short "Emphasizes the condition; 'only when', 'precisely because'." :long "The ～てこそ grammar point is used to emphasize that 'only when' or 'precisely because' the stated condition is met, a certain result or meaning arises. For nouns and adjectives, similar forms appear (e.g., Noun + でこそ, い-Adjective くてこそ, な-Adjective でこそ)." :formation "Verb-て form + こそ / い-Adjective + くてこそ / な-Adjective + でこそ / Noun + でこそ" :examples [{:ja "勉強してこそ、テストに合格できる。" :en "Only when you study can you pass the test."} {:ja "素直になってこそ、相手の気持ちが分かります。" :en "Only when you’re honest can you understand the other person’s feelings."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-083" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～でしょうがない" :reading "〜deshou ga nai" :meaning "Expresses an intense emotional or physical state; 'extremely', 'can’t help but'." :short "Expresses an intense emotional or physical state; 'extremely', 'can’t help but'." :long "The pattern ～(て)しょうがない (or 〜でしょうがない) indicates a condition or feeling so intense that one is almost 'overcome' by it. It’s often used with emotions (e.g. 退屈で(しょうがない), 嬉しくてしょうがない, 寒くてしょうがない)." :formation "い-Adjective(く) + てしょうがない / な-Adjective + で(は)しょうがない / Verb-て form + しょうがない" :examples [{:ja "今日は暇で(は)しょうがない。" :en "I’m extremely bored today."} {:ja "試験が近いから不安でしょうがない。" :en "I’m so anxious because the exam is coming up."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-084" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～でたまらない" :reading "〜de tamaranai" :meaning "Express extremely strong feelings or sensations; 'can't stand', 'unbearably'." :short "Express extremely strong feelings or sensations; 'can't stand', 'unbearably'." :long "The ～でたまらない grammar point is used to convey very strong emotion or sensation—so strong it’s almost unbearable. It’s common with expressions like 暑くてたまらない, うれしくてたまらない, etc." :formation "い-Adjective (く) + てたまらない / な-Adjective + でたまらない / Verb-て form + たまらない" :examples [{:ja "この暑さは本当に暑くてたまらない。" :en "This heat is absolutely unbearable."} {:ja "彼女のことが好きでたまらない。" :en "I like her so much I can hardly stand it."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-085" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～でならない" :reading "〜de naranai" :meaning "Indicates an uncontrollable emotion or feeling; 'can’t help but', 'overwhelmingly'." :short "Indicates an uncontrollable emotion or feeling; 'can’t help but', 'overwhelmingly'." :long "The ～でならない grammar pattern expresses a very strong, often involuntary feeling—e.g., '不思議でならない' (it’s so mysterious I can’t help thinking about it), '残念でならない' (I’m so disappointed), '心配でならない' (I’m extremely worried)." :formation "な-Adjective + でならない / い-Adjective (く) + てならない / Verb-て + ならない" :examples [{:ja "彼の演技が素晴らしくてならない。" :en "I can’t help but be amazed at his wonderful performance."} {:ja "合格発表が気になってならない。" :en "I can’t stop worrying about the exam results."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-086" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～でばかりはいられない" :reading "〜de bakari wa irarenai" :meaning "Cannot afford to only do something; cannot always rely on it." :short "Cannot afford to only do something; cannot always rely on it." :long "The ～でばかりはいられない grammar point is used when one realizes they cannot keep doing only that one thing or cannot always rely on a certain situation. It implies the need to take other actions or responsibilities." :formation "Verb-て form + ばかりはいられない / Noun + ばかりはいられない (with appropriate particles)" :examples [{:ja "いつも助けてもらってばかりはいられない。" :en "I can’t just keep letting people help me all the time."} {:ja "好きな仕事ばかりしてはいられない。" :en "I can’t afford to only do the work I love."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-087" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ではないか" :reading "〜de wa nai ka" :meaning "Used to show a strong assertion or emphasis on one’s opinion; 'isn't it?', 'aren't you?', 'right?'." :short "Used to show a strong assertion or emphasis on one’s opinion; 'isn't it?', 'aren't you?', 'right?'." :long "The ～ではないか grammar point is used at the end of a sentence to make a rhetorical question or a strong, emphatic statement. It often translates as 'isn't it…?' 'aren't you…?' or 'wouldn’t you say…?'." :formation "Noun + ではないか / い-Adjective + ではないか / な-Adjective + ではないか / Verb-casual + ではないか" :examples [{:ja "彼は優秀な学生ではないか。" :en "Isn’t he an excellent student?"} {:ja "この料理は美味しいではないか。" :en "Isn’t this dish delicious?"}]} {:id "grammar-n2-088" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～てはならない" :reading "〜te wa naranai" :meaning "Express prohibition; 'must not', 'cannot', 'should not'." :short "Express prohibition; 'must not', 'cannot', 'should not'." :long "The ～てはならない grammar point is used to express a strong prohibition. It is often more formal or strict and can be translated as 'must not', 'cannot', or 'should not.'" :formation "Verb-て form + はならない" :examples [{:ja "ここでタバコを吸ってはならない。" :en "You must not smoke here."} {:ja "秘密を他人に話してはならない。" :en "You cannot reveal this secret to others."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-089" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～てまで" :reading "〜te made" :meaning "Express the extent someone goes in order to achieve something; 'to the point of', 'even', 'so far as to'." :short "Express the extent someone goes in order to achieve something; 'to the point of', 'even', 'so far as to'." :long "The ～てまで grammar point is used to convey the extent someone goes to achieve something, highlighting the action one takes or the sacrifice one makes for a certain purpose. It can be translated as 'to the point of', 'even', or 'so far as to' in English. It is used with verbs, not adjectives or nouns." :formation "Verb-て form + まで" :examples [{:ja "彼は試験に合格するために、徹夜してまで勉強しました。" :en "He studied to the point of staying up all night in order to pass the exam."} {:ja "この商品がほしかったので、朝早く起きてまで並んで買いました。" :en "I wanted this product so much that I went so far as to get up early and wait in line to buy it."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-090" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～て当然だ" :reading "〜te tōzen da" :meaning "Expresses that something is natural or expected." :short "Expresses that something is natural or expected." :long "The ～て当然だ grammar point is used to express that something is natural, obvious, or expected under the circumstances. It can be translated as 'it's natural that', 'as expected', or 'no wonder' in English." :formation "Verb-て form + 当然だ" :examples [{:ja "彼は毎日勉強しているから、試験に合格して当然だ。" :en "Since he studies every day, it's only natural that he passes the exam."} {:ja "そのレストランは料理が美味しいので、予約がいっぱいになって当然だ。" :en "Because that restaurant’s food is delicious, no wonder its reservations are always full."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-091" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～というものだ" :reading "〜to iu mono da" :meaning "Expressing a general rule or common sense." :short "Expressing a general rule or common sense." :long "The ～というものだ grammar point is used to emphasize a general rule, a piece of common sense, or an indisputable fact. It often appears in comments or observations about something commonly accepted as true." :formation "Sentence + というものだ" :examples [{:ja "若者の熱情は尊敬に値するというものだ。" :en "The passion of young people is something that deserves respect."} {:ja "勉強は一日でもサボれば遅れるというものだ。" :en "If you skip even a day of studying, you'll fall behind—that's just how it is."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-092" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "どうにか～ないものか" :reading "dō ni ka ~ nai mono ka" :meaning "Express a strong desire or wish for something to be possible." :short "Express a strong desire or wish for something to be possible." :long "どうにか～ないものか is used to express a strong desire or wish for something to be resolved or made possible. It translates roughly as 'isn't there some way...?', 'can't we somehow...?', or 'is there no way...?'" :formation "どうにか + Verb-negative form + ないものか" :examples [{:ja "どうにかこの仕事を終わらせないものか。" :en "Isn't there some way to finish this job?"} {:ja "どうにか次の試験で合格しないものか。" :en "Can't we somehow pass the next exam?"}]} {:id "grammar-n2-093" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～とおり" :reading "〜toori" :meaning "Express doing something as instructed or describing how something matches a rule or guideline; 'as', 'according to'." :short "Express doing something as instructed or describing how something matches a rule or guideline; 'as', 'according to'." :long "The ～とおり grammar point is used to express doing something exactly as instructed or describing how something matches a guideline or is exactly as one heard/expected. It can be translated as 'as', 'according to', or 'the same way...'." :formation "Verb-casual + とおり, い-Adjective + とおり, な-Adjective + だとおり, Noun + だとおり" :examples [{:ja "先生が言ったとおりに宿題をしました。" :en "I did my homework exactly as the teacher said."} {:ja "この地図のとおりに歩いてください。" :en "Please walk according to this map."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-094" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～とか" :reading "〜to ka" :meaning "Used to list examples or express uncertainty; 'such as', 'like', 'or something like that'." :short "Used to list examples or express uncertainty; 'such as', 'like', 'or something like that'." :long "The ～とか grammar point can be used to list examples or express a somewhat vague mention of items/activities. It can be translated as 'such as', 'like', or 'or something like that'." :formation "Verb-casual + とか, い-Adjective + とか, な-Adjective + だとか, Noun + だとか" :examples [{:ja "休日は映画を見るとか、買い物に行くとかして過ごします。" :en "On my days off, I do things like watch movies or go shopping."} {:ja "彼女はオーストラリアとかニュージーランドとかに行きたがっています。" :en "She wants to go to places like Australia or New Zealand."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-095" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ところ" :reading "〜tokoro" :meaning "Express a specific point in time or state; 'when', 'just about', or 'in the middle of'." :short "Express a specific point in time or state; 'when', 'just about', or 'in the middle of'." :long "The ～ところ grammar point is used to describe a specific point in time or stage of action. Depending on the verb tense, it can mean 'just about to do something', 'in the middle of doing something', or 'just did something'." :formation "Verb-casual + ところ, い-Adjective + ところ, な-Adjective + なところ, Noun + のところ" :examples [{:ja "出かけるところです。" :en "I'm just about to go out."} {:ja "映画を見ているところです。" :en "I'm in the middle of watching a movie."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-096" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～どころか" :reading "〜dokoro ka" :meaning "Express 'far from', 'not just', 'not only', or 'to say nothing of'." :short "Express 'far from', 'not just', 'not only', or 'to say nothing of'." :long "The ～どころか grammar point is used to emphasize that reality is far from what one might expect or that something is not only one way, but actually goes beyond it. It can be translated as 'far from', 'not just', or 'to say nothing of'." :formation "Verb-casual + どころか, い-Adjective + どころか, な-Adjective + どころか, Noun + どころか" :examples [{:ja "彼は歌が上手どころか、プロの歌手みたいだ。" :en "Far from just being good at singing, he sounds like a professional singer."} {:ja "この料理はおいしいどころか、最高の味だ。" :en "This dish is not just delicious, it’s absolutely the best."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-097" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～どころではない" :reading "〜dokoro de wa nai" :meaning "Not the time/place for; far from; can't possibly." :short "Not the time/place for; far from; can't possibly." :long "The ～どころではない grammar point is used to express that circumstances make it impossible or inappropriate to do something—there’s a much bigger issue at hand. It can also mean ‘far from’ in a figurative sense." :formation "Verb-casual + どころではない, い-Adjective + どころではない, な-Adjective + どころではない, Noun + どころではない" :examples [{:ja "彼の失敗を笑うどころではない。私たちも同じミスをしている。" :en "This is not the time to laugh at his mistakes—we’re making the same ones."} {:ja "病気で寝ている時は、遊ぶどころではない。" :en "When you’re sick in bed, it's far from the time to play around."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-098" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～としたら" :reading "〜to shitara" :meaning "Express a hypothetical situation; 'if', 'suppose', 'assuming'." :short "Express a hypothetical situation; 'if', 'suppose', 'assuming'." :long "The ～としたら grammar point is used to express a hypothetical situation or assumption. It can be translated as 'if', 'suppose', or 'assuming' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it’s used with verbs, adjectives, or nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + としたら, い-Adjective + としたら, な-Adjective + だとしたら, Noun + だとしたら" :examples [{:ja "明日雨が降るとしたら、傘を持っていきましょう。" :en "If it rains tomorrow, let’s bring umbrellas."} {:ja "このケーキが美味しくないとしたら、誰も食べないだろう。" :en "If this cake isn’t tasty, probably no one will eat it."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-099" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～としても" :reading "〜to shite mo" :meaning "Even if, despite, although" :short "Even if, despite, although" :long "The ～としても grammar point is used to say ‘even if...’ or ‘even assuming that...’, often followed by a conclusion or stance. It’s similar to ～ても, but puts more emphasis on the hypothetical assumption or viewpoint." :formation "Verb-casual + としても, い-Adjective + としても, な-Adjective + だとしても, Noun + だとしても" :examples [{:ja "雨が降るとしても、レースは中止しません。" :en "Even if it rains, we won't cancel the race."} {:ja "このケーキが美味しくないとしても、私は食べるつもりです。" :en "Even if this cake isn't delicious, I plan on eating it."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-100" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～と～ともに" :reading "〜to 〜tomoni" :meaning "Expresses 'together with' or 'along with'; used to describe two events or actions happening simultaneously or in parallel." :short "Expresses 'together with' or 'along with'; used to describe two events or actions happening simultaneously or in parallel." :long "The ～と～ともに grammar point is used to express that two events or actions are happening simultaneously or in parallel. It is translated as 'together with' or 'along with' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + とともに, い-Adjective + とともに, な-Adjective + とともに, Noun + とともに" :examples [{:ja "彼が来るとともに、パーティーが始まりました。" :en "The party started as soon as he arrived."} {:ja "季節が変わるとともに、服のファッションも変わります。" :en "As the seasons change, clothing fashion also changes."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-101" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～とは限らない" :reading "〜to wa kagiranai" :meaning "Expresses that something is not necessarily true; 'not always', 'not necessarily'." :short "Expresses that something is not necessarily true; 'not always', 'not necessarily'." :long "The ～とは限らない grammar point is used to express that something is not necessarily true or does not always occur. It can be translated as 'not always' or 'not necessarily' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + とは限らない, い-Adjective + とは限らない, な-Adjective + だとは限らない, Noun + だとは限らない" :examples [{:ja "いつも勉強しているとは限らない。" :en "It's not always true that I'm studying."} {:ja "高い商品がいいとは限らない。" :en "Expensive products are not necessarily good."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-102" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ないことはない" :reading "〜nai koto wa nai" :meaning "Express that something is not impossible, meaning it is possible or could be done." :short "Express that something is not impossible, meaning it is possible or could be done." :long "The ～ないことはない grammar point is used to express a double negative, meaning that something is not impossible, implying that it is possible or could be done. Essentially, it downplays the possibility or ability to do something but still acknowledges that it's possible." :formation "Verb-ないform + ことはない" :examples [{:ja "彼が来ないことはない。" :en "It's not that he won't come (it's possible that he will come)."} {:ja "あの問題が解けないことはない。" :en "It's not impossible to solve that problem (it can be solved)."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-103" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ないこともない" :reading "〜nai koto mo nai" :meaning "Used to express an action or state is 'not impossible', but emphasizes reluctance or doubt." :short "Used to express an action or state is 'not impossible', but emphasizes reluctance or doubt." :long "The ～ないこともない grammar point is used to express that something is not impossible, but the speaker is reluctant, has doubts, or deems it as a less favorable option. It can be translated as 'not that one can't', 'not that it's impossible', or 'it's not out of the question'." :formation "Verb-ないForm + こともない, い-adjective + こともない" :examples [{:ja "この問題は解けないこともないけど、難しいです。" :en "It's not that I can't solve this problem, but it's difficult."} {:ja "彼に会わないこともないが、あまり話す時間がない。" :en "It's not that I can't meet him, but there's not much time to talk."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-104" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ないではいられない" :reading "〜nai de wa irarenai" :meaning "Cannot help but; cannot avoid; unable to refrain from." :short "Cannot help but; cannot avoid; unable to refrain from." :long "The ～ないではいられない grammar point is used to express a strong feeling, urge, or uncontrollable action that someone cannot resist or help doing, even if it may be undesirable. It can be translated as 'cannot help but', 'cannot avoid', or 'unable to refrain from' in English." :formation "Verb-ない form + ではいられない or じゃいられない" :examples [{:ja "この映画はとても感動的で、泣かないではいられなかった。" :en "This movie was so touching, I couldn't help but cry."} {:ja "彼女の笑顔を見ると、笑わないではいられない。" :en "When I see her smile, I can't help but laugh."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-105" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ながら" :reading "〜nagara" :meaning "Express doing two things simultaneously or at the same time." :short "Express doing two things simultaneously or at the same time." :long "The ～ながら grammar point is used to express the concept of doing two things simultaneously or at the same time. In English, it can be translated as 'while', 'as', or 'during'. ～ながら can be attached to the stem of a verb to convey this meaning." :formation "Verb-stem + ながら" :examples [{:ja "テレビを見ながら宿題をしています。" :en "I am doing my homework while watching TV."} {:ja "彼女は歌いながら料理を作っていました。" :en "She was cooking while singing."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-106" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にあたり" :reading "〜ni atari" :meaning "Expresses the meaning of 'at the time of', 'in', 'on', or 'upon'." :short "Expresses the meaning of 'at the time of', 'in', 'on', or 'upon'." :long "The ～にあたり grammar point is used to describe an important event or situation that occurs at a specific moment. It can be translated as 'at the time of', 'in', 'on', or 'upon' in English. The formation is quite simple, as it only requires a noun before it." :formation "Noun + にあたり" :examples [{:ja "入学にあたり、必要な書類を準備してください。" :en "Please prepare the necessary documents upon entering the school."} {:ja "新年にあたり、抱負を立てる人が多いです。" :en "Many people set goals at the time of the new year."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-107" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～において" :reading "〜ni oite" :meaning "Indicates the place, time, or context where an action or event occurs; 'in', 'at', 'on', 'regarding'." :short "Indicates the place, time, or context where an action or event occurs; 'in', 'at', 'on', 'regarding'." :long "The ～において grammar point is used to show the place, time, or context where an action or event takes place. It can be translated as 'in', 'at', 'on', or 'regarding' in English. It is often used in formal situations and can be replaced by more casual equivalents such as で, に, or は in informal contexts." :formation "Noun + において" :examples [{:ja "この大学において、英語のクラスが一番人気があります。" :en "In this university, English classes are the most popular."} {:ja "彼の研究において新しい発見がありました。" :en "In his research, there were new discoveries."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-108" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にかかわらず" :reading "〜ni kakawarazu" :meaning "Regardless of; irrespective of; (often used as) 'whether or not'." :short "Regardless of; irrespective of; (often used as) 'whether or not'." :long "The ～にかかわらず grammar point is used to express that something happens or is true regardless of a particular fact, condition, or circumstance. It can be translated as 'regardless of', 'irrespective of', or 'whether or not'. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, or nouns." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + にかかわらず, い-Adjective + にかかわらず, な-Adjective + にかかわらず, Noun + にかかわらず" :examples [{:ja "天気にかかわらず、今日の試合は行われます。" :en "The game today will be held, regardless of the weather."} {:ja "彼は年齢にかかわらず、新しいことを学び続けています。" :en "He continues to learn new things, regardless of his age."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-109" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にかけては" :reading "〜ni kakete wa" :meaning "Indicates a specific area or aspect in which someone or something excels." :short "Indicates a specific area or aspect in which someone or something excels." :long "The grammar point ～にかけては is used to emphasize a particular area or aspect in which someone or something excels or is exceptional. This structure is often used with positive connotations regarding the subject's expertise or outstanding qualities." :formation "Noun + にかけては" :examples [{:ja "料理の腕前にかけては、彼女は誰にも負けません。" :en "When it comes to cooking skills, she is second to none."} {:ja "この町にかけては、彼の店が一番おいしいラーメンを売っています。" :en "As far as this town is concerned, his store sells the most delicious ramen."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-110" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にしたがって" :reading "〜ni shitagatte" :meaning "Expresses a natural consequence or a change that occurs according to something else; 'as', 'with', 'in accordance with'." :short "Expresses a natural consequence or a change that occurs according to something else; 'as', 'with', 'in accordance with'." :long "The ～にしたがって grammar point is used to express a natural consequence or a change that occurs according to something else. It can be translated as 'as', 'with', or 'in accordance with' in English. This grammar point is used with nouns and can be used to show a change in proportion or in accordance with a rule or principle." :formation "Noun + にしたがって" :examples [{:ja "成長にしたがって、この植物の葉が大きくなります。" :en "As it grows, the leaves of this plant become larger."} {:ja "説明書にしたがって、家具を組み立ててください。" :en "Please assemble the furniture according to the instructions."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-111" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にしたら" :reading "〜ni shitara" :meaning "Express a point of view; 'from the perspective of', 'if I were'." :short "Express a point of view; 'from the perspective of', 'if I were'." :long "The ～にしたら grammar point is used to express a point of view or opinion from someone else's perspective. It can be translated as 'from the perspective of' or 'if I were' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a noun or a pronoun." :formation "Noun + にしたら, Pronoun + にしたら" :examples [{:ja "彼にしたら、早く帰りたいだろう。" :en "From his perspective, he probably wants to go home early."} {:ja "子供たちにしたら、この話はつまらないかもしれない。" :en "If I were the children, this story might be boring."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-112" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にしろ～にしろ" :reading "〜ni shiro 〜ni shiro" :meaning "Expresses 'either way', 'in any case', 'be it...', used when presenting two alternatives." :short "Expresses 'either way', 'in any case', 'be it...', used when presenting two alternatives." :long "The ～にしろ～にしろ grammar point is used to present two alternatives and emphasize that regardless of which one is chosen or occurs, the result or conclusion remains the same. It can be translated as 'either way', 'in any case', or 'be it...'. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb (dictionary form or negative form) + にしろ, い-Adjective + にしろ, な-Adjective + にしろ, Noun + にしろ" :examples [{:ja "行くにしろ行かないにしろ、早く決めてください。" :en "Either way, whether you go or not, please decide quickly."} {:ja "彼が来るにしろ来ないにしろ、パーティーは始めましょう。" :en "Be it if he comes or not, let's start the party either way."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-113" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～につけ～につけ" :reading "〜ni tsuke 〜ni tsuke" :meaning "Expresses 'whenever', 'each time', 'whether...or'." :short "Expresses 'whenever', 'each time', 'whether...or'." :long "The ～につけ～につけ grammar point is used to express that something happens 'each time' or 'whenever' a certain event or situation occurs, and can also mean 'whether...or'. This grammar point indicates that the same event or feeling occurs no matter which of the following situations happen." :formation "Verb-casual + につけ, い-Adjective + につけ, な-Adjective + につけ, Noun + につけ" :examples [{:ja "外国へ行くにつけ、勉強するにつけ、言葉の壁にぶつかる。" :en "Whether I go abroad or study, I always encounter language barriers."} {:ja "彼と話すにつけ、彼が考えるにつけ、彼の知識に驚かされる。" :en "Whenever I talk to him or hear his thoughts, I'm always amazed by his knowledge."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-114" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～につれて" :reading "〜ni tsurete" :meaning "Expresses the idea that as one thing changes, so does another; 'as', 'along with', 'while'." :short "Expresses the idea that as one thing changes, so does another; 'as', 'along with', 'while'." :long "The ～につれて grammar point is used to express the idea that as one thing changes or progresses, another thing changes or progresses simultaneously. In English, it can be translated as 'as', 'along with', or 'while'." :formation "Verb (dictionary form) + につれて, Noun + につれて" :examples [{:ja "年を取るにつれて、健康に気をつけなければなりません。" :en "As you get older, you have to pay more attention to your health."} {:ja "春が近づくにつれて、気温が上がる。" :en "As spring approaches, the temperature rises."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-115" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にともなって" :reading "〜ni tomonatte" :meaning "Expresses 'along with' or 'as a result of'; indicates that one event occurs along with another." :short "Expresses 'along with' or 'as a result of'; indicates that one event occurs along with another." :long "The ～にともなって grammar point is used to indicate that one event occurs along with another event, or as a result of another event. It can be translated as 'along with', 'as a result of', or 'in accordance with' in English." :formation "Noun (event) + にともなって, Verb-casual + にともなって" :examples [{:ja "人口が増えるにともなって、交通渋滞も悪化しています。" :en "As the population increases, traffic congestion is also getting worse."} {:ja "季節の変わり目にともなって、体調を崩しやすくなります。" :en "As the seasons change, it's easier to get out of shape."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-116" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にほかならない" :reading "〜ni hoka naranai" :meaning "Express that something is 'nothing but' or 'none other than'." :short "Express that something is 'nothing but' or 'none other than'." :long "The ～にほかならない grammar point is used to emphasize that something is no more or no less than what is specified. It can be seen as a strong way to say that something is 'nothing but' or 'none other than'." :formation "Verb-plain + にほかならない, い-Adjective + にほかならない, な-Adjective + にほかならない, Noun + にほかならない" :examples [{:ja "彼の成功は努力にほかならない。" :en "His success is nothing but hard work."} {:ja "この痛みは寒さにほかならない。" :en "This pain is nothing but the cold."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-117" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にもかかわらず" :reading "〜ni mo kakawarazu" :meaning "Expresses 'despite', 'in spite of' or 'even though'." :short "Expresses 'despite', 'in spite of' or 'even though'." :long "The ～にもかかわらず grammar point is used to express that something occurs, or a situation exists, despite or in spite of a contrasting or unfavorable condition. It can be translated as 'despite', 'in spite of', or 'even though' in English." :formation "Verb (any tense/form) + にもかかわらず, い-Adjective + にもかかわらず, な-Adjective (or Noun) + であるにもかかわらず" :examples [{:ja "忙しいにもかかわらず、彼は毎日運動しています。" :en "Despite being busy, he exercises every day."} {:ja "雨が降っているにもかかわらず、彼女は散歩に行きました。" :en "Even though it was raining, she went for a walk."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-118" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～により" :reading "〜ni yori" :meaning "Indicates the reason, method, or means by which something happens; 'by', 'through', 'due to'." :short "Indicates the reason, method, or means by which something happens; 'by', 'through', 'due to'." :long "The ～により grammar point is used to indicate the reason, method, or means by which something happens or is done. It can be often translated as 'by', 'through', or 'due to' in English. It can be attached directly to nouns or verbs in plain form." :formation "Noun + により, Verb + により" :examples [{:ja "この事故は運転手の不注意により起こりました。" :en "This accident occurred due to the driver's carelessness."} {:ja "その映画は小説により制作されました。" :en "The movie was produced based on the novel."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-119" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～にわたって" :reading "〜ni watatte" :meaning "Indicates a range or extent; 'over', 'throughout', 'across'." :short "Indicates a range or extent; 'over', 'throughout', 'across'." :long "The ～にわたって grammar point is used to express a range or extent of a particular action, event, or situation. It can be translated as 'over', 'throughout', or 'across' in English. This structure can be used with various types of words such as time, distance, and quantity." :formation "Noun + にわたって" :examples [{:ja "彼女は一年にわたって日本語を勉強しました。" :en "She studied Japanese for over a year."} {:ja "このプロジェクトは50キロにわたって道路を建設します。" :en "This project will construct roads across 50 kilometers."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-120" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に先立ち" :reading "〜ni sakidachi" :meaning "Express an action or event that occurs before another; 'before', 'prior to', 'preceding'." :short "Express an action or event that occurs before another; 'before', 'prior to', 'preceding'." :long "The ～に先立ち grammar point is used to indicate that a certain action or event occurs before another action or event. It can be translated as 'before', 'prior to', or 'preceding' in English. It is often used with nouns (Noun + に先立ち) or with dictionary-form verbs (Verb-dictionary form + に先立って)." :formation "Noun + に先立ち / Verb-dictionary form + に先立って" :examples [{:ja "会議に先立ち、資料を確認しましょう。" :en "Before the meeting, let's check the materials."} {:ja "出発に先立ち、荷物を整理しました。" :en "Before departure, I organized my luggage."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-121" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に反して" :reading "〜ni hanshite" :meaning "Contrary to, against, in spite of" :short "Contrary to, against, in spite of" :long "The ～に反して grammar point is used to express that something is contrary to or against an expectation or belief. It can be translated as 'contrary to', 'against', or 'in spite of' in English. It is generally used with nouns." :formation "Noun + に反して" :examples [{:ja "天気予報に反して、今日は雨が降っている。" :en "Contrary to the weather forecast, it is raining today."} {:ja "彼の予想に反して、試合に勝った。" :en "We won the match against his prediction."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-122" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に基づいて" :reading "〜ni motozuite" :meaning "Based on, according to, depending on" :short "Based on, according to, depending on" :long "The ～に基づいて grammar point is used to express that something is based on, according to, or depending on a certain source or foundation. It can be used with nouns that represent a source, rule, principle, or standard." :formation "Noun + に基づいて" :examples [{:ja "研究結果に基づいて、新しい治療法が開発されました。" :en "A new treatment method was developed based on the research results."} {:ja "法律に基づいて、犯罪者は逮捕されました。" :en "The criminal was arrested according to the law."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-123" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に対して" :reading "〜ni taishite" :meaning "Expresses 'toward', 'against', 'in contrast to', or 'in response to'." :short "Expresses 'toward', 'against', 'in contrast to', or 'in response to'." :long "The ～に対して grammar point is used to indicate a direction or target, showing an action or attitude towards something or someone. It can also be used to show a contrast between two situations or to express 'in response to' an action or situation." :formation "Noun + に対して" :examples [{:ja "日本の文化に対して尊敬の念を持っています。" :en "I have a sense of respect towards Japanese culture."} {:ja "彼に対して無礼な態度を取らないでください。" :en "Please don't have a rude attitude towards him."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-124" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に応えて" :reading "〜ni kotaete" :meaning "Responding to; living up to; meeting." :short "Responding to; living up to; meeting." :long "The ～に応えて grammar point is used to express an action in response to something (an expectation, request, or situation). It can be translated as 'responding to', 'living up to', or 'meeting' in English. This grammar point is generally used with nouns or noun phrases." :formation "Noun + に応えて" :examples [{:ja "お客様の要望に応えて、新しいメニューを作りました。" :en "Responding to the customers' requests, we created a new menu."} {:ja "彼はチームの期待に応えて素晴らしいプレーを見せた。" :en "He lived up to the team's expectations and showed a great performance."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-125" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に応じて" :reading "〜ni oujite" :meaning "In accordance with, depending on, in response to." :short "In accordance with, depending on, in response to." :long "The ～に応じて grammar point is used to express something that varies according to a certain condition or responds to a change. It can be translated as 'in accordance with', 'depending on', or 'in response to' in English. The formation is used with nouns." :formation "Noun + に応じて" :examples [{:ja "仕事の量に応じて給料が変わります。" :en "The salary changes depending on the amount of work."} {:ja "季節に応じて服装を選びましょう。" :en "Choose your clothes according to the season."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-126" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に決まっている" :reading "〜ni kimatte iru" :meaning "Express certainty; 'it is obvious', 'it must be', 'no doubt'." :short "Express certainty; 'it is obvious', 'it must be', 'no doubt'." :long "The ～に決まっている grammar point is used to express certainty or a strong conviction about a situation or event. It can be translated in English as 'it is obvious', 'it must be', or 'no doubt'. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + に決まっている, い-Adjective + に決まっている, な-Adjective + だに決まっている, Noun + だに決まっている" :examples [{:ja "こんなに暑い日に運動したら、汗をかくに決まっている。" :en "It's obvious that you'll sweat if you exercise on such a hot day."} {:ja "彼が試験に落ちたら、がっかりするに決まっている。" :en "If he fails the exam, it's obvious he will be disappointed."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-127" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に沿って" :reading "〜ni sotte" :meaning "Along with; in accordance with; following" :short "Along with; in accordance with; following" :long "The ～に沿って grammar point is used to express that something happens or is done along with, in accordance with, or following a particular line, path, or guideline. It is often used in instructions or explanations. The に沿って is attached to the noun representing the line, path or guideline." :formation "Noun + に沿って" :examples [{:ja "川に沿って歩いていくと、橋が見えてきます。" :en "If you walk along the river, you will see a bridge."} {:ja "このレシピに沿って料理を作れば、間違いありません。" :en "If you cook according to this recipe, there will be no mistakes."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-128" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に過ぎない" :reading "〜ni suginai" :meaning "Expresses that something is 'just' or 'nothing more than'; 'merely', 'only', 'simply'." :short "Expresses that something is 'just' or 'nothing more than'; 'merely', 'only', 'simply'." :long "The ～に過ぎない grammar point is used to indicate that something is 'just' or 'nothing more than' the stated quality or condition. It implies that the thing is not as significant or important as it might seem at first glance. This grammar point can be translated as 'merely', 'only', or 'simply' in English. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + に過ぎない, い-Adjective + に過ぎない, な-Adjective + に過ぎない, Noun + に過ぎない" :examples [{:ja "彼の失敗はミスに過ぎない。" :en "His failure is nothing more than a mistake."} {:ja "この試験は簡単な問題に過ぎない。" :en "This test is nothing more than a simple question."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-129" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に関わって" :reading "〜ni kakawatte" :meaning "Indicates that something is related to or influenced by a certain matter or factor; 'concerning', 'in connection with', 'regarding'." :short "Indicates that something is related to or influenced by a certain matter or factor; 'concerning', 'in connection with', 'regarding'." :long "The ～に関わって grammar point is used to express that something is related to, influenced by, or connected with a certain matter or factor. It can be translated as 'concerning', 'in connection with', or 'regarding' in English. The formation depends on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + に関わって, い-Adjective + に関わって, な-Adjective + に関わって, Noun + に関わって" :examples [{:ja "試験の内容に関わって、先生に質問があります。" :en "I have a question for the teacher regarding the content of the exam."} {:ja "このプロジェクトに関わって、意見があれば、遠慮なく言ってください。" :en "If you have any opinions regarding this project, please feel free to express them."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-130" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に限り" :reading "〜ni kagiri" :meaning "Expresses limitation; 'only', 'limited to', 'exclusive to'." :short "Expresses limitation; 'only', 'limited to', 'exclusive to'." :long "The ～に限り grammar point is used to indicate that something is limited or exclusive to a certain condition or situation. It can be translated as 'only', 'limited to', or 'exclusive to' in English. The formation is simple and can be used with nouns." :formation "Noun + に限り" :examples [{:ja "今日に限り、この商品が半額です。" :en "Today only, this product is half price."} {:ja "このクーポンはこの店に限り有効です。" :en "This coupon is valid only at this store."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-131" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～に際して" :reading "〜ni saishite" :meaning "Used to express the moment or the occasion of an action; 'at the time of', 'upon', 'when'." :short "Used to express the moment or the occasion of an action; 'at the time of', 'upon', 'when'." :long "The ～に際して grammar point describes an action that occurs during a specific event, occasion, or when something takes place. It can be translated into English as 'at the time of', 'upon', 'when', or 'in the event of'. This grammar is used with nouns to describe the context or situation during which an action takes place." :formation "Noun + に際して" :examples [{:ja "卒業式に際して、校長先生がスピーチをします。" :en "At the graduation ceremony, the principal gives a speech."} {:ja "この仕事に際して、責任感が大切です。" :en "Responsibility is important when doing this job."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-132" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ねばならない" :reading "〜neba naranai" :meaning "Expresses obligation or necessity; 'must', 'have to', 'need to'." :short "Expresses obligation or necessity; 'must', 'have to', 'need to'." :long "The ～ねばならない grammar point is used to express obligation or necessity. It can be translated as 'must', 'have to', or 'need to' in English. This grammar point is mainly used with verbs, indicating that the action must be done." :formation "Verb-ない form → (remove い) → ね + ばならない" :examples [{:ja "試験に合格するために、もっと勉強せねばならない。" :en "To pass the exam, I must study more."} {:ja "飛行機に間に合うためには、急がねばならない。" :en "To catch the flight, I must hurry."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-133" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～のみならず～も" :reading "〜nomi narazu 〜mo" :meaning "Not only... but also; as well as." :short "Not only... but also; as well as." :long "The ～のみならず～も grammar point is used to show that something applies not only to one thing, but also to another thing. This can be translated as 'not only... but also', or 'as well as' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + のみならず + (も), い-Adjective + のみならず + (も), な-Adjective + のみならず + (も), Noun + のみならず + (も)" :examples [{:ja "彼は英語のみならず、フランス語も話せます。" :en "He can speak not only English but also French."} {:ja "このレストランは美味しいのみならず、雰囲気も素晴らしいです。" :en "Not only is this restaurant delicious, but the atmosphere is also great."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-134" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～のももっともだ" :reading "〜no mo mottomo da" :meaning "Express that something is understandable or reasonable." :short "Express that something is understandable or reasonable." :long "The ～のももっともだ grammar point is used to express that a situation, action, or feeling is understandable or reasonable, considering the circumstances. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + のももっともだ, い-Adjective + のももっともだ, な-Adjective + なのももっともだ, Noun + なのももっともだ" :examples [{:ja "彼が緊張するのももっともだ。" :en "It's understandable that he is nervous."} {:ja "この問題が難しいのももっともだ。" :en "It's reasonable that this problem is difficult."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-135" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "〜の上では" :reading "〜no ue de wa" :meaning "Indicates 'on the basis of', 'in terms of', or 'from the standpoint of'." :short "Indicates 'on the basis of', 'in terms of', or 'from the standpoint of'." :long "The 〜の上では grammar point is used to show that you are expressing a view or making a judgment based on a specific standpoint or perspective. It can be translated as 'on the basis of', 'in terms of', or 'from the standpoint of'. The formation is straightforward, which involves attaching 〜の上では to a noun or a phrase." :formation "Noun or Phrase + の上では" :examples [{:ja "効率の上では、この方法が最適です。" :en "In terms of efficiency, this method is the best."} {:ja "法律の上では、彼の行為は違法ではありません。" :en "From a legal standpoint, his actions are not illegal."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-136" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～の下で" :reading "〜no shita de" :meaning "Under the context or condition of; under." :short "Under the context or condition of; under." :long "The ～の下で grammar point is used to express that something occurs under a certain context, condition, or circumstance. It can be translated as 'under' or 'in' in English. The formation is simple, as it can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-casual + の下で, い-Adjective + の下で, な-Adjective + の下で, Noun + の下で" :examples [{:ja "先生の下で勉強するのはとても楽しいです。" :en "Studying under the teacher is very enjoyable."} {:ja "晴天の下でピクニックをしましょう。" :en "Let's have a picnic under the clear sky."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-137" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ばかりか〜も" :reading "〜bakari ka 〜 mo" :meaning "Expresses 'not only... but also'; emphasizing the extent of something." :short "Expresses 'not only... but also'; emphasizing the extent of something." :long "The ～ばかりか〜も grammar point is used to express the idea of 'not only... but also' in a sentence. It emphasizes the extent to which something applies, showing a certain component or factor is not the only relevant one." :formation "Noun/Verb/Adjective + ばかりか + Noun/Verb/Adjective + も" :examples [{:ja "彼はかわいいばかりか、賢くもある。" :en "He is not only cute but also smart."} {:ja "このレストランは味が良いばかりかサービスも素晴らしいです。" :en "This restaurant not only has great taste but also wonderful service."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-138" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ばかりだ" :reading "〜bakari da" :meaning "Express a continuing state or negative tendency; 'just', 'only', 'always'." :short "Express a continuing state or negative tendency; 'just', 'only', 'always'." :long "The ～ばかりだ grammar point is used to express a continuing or unchanging state, often emphasizing a negative or undesired tendency. It can be translated as 'just', 'only', or 'always' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-てform + ばかりだ, い-Adjective + ばかりだ, な-Adjective + ばかりだ, Noun + ばかりだ" :examples [{:ja "彼は文句を言ってばかりだ。" :en "He is always complaining."} {:ja "この映画は面白くなくて、退屈してばかりだ。" :en "This movie isn't interesting, and I'm just bored."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-139" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ばかりに" :reading "〜bakari ni" :meaning "Express an undesired situation or result caused by something; 'simply because', 'due to', 'on account of'." :short "Express an undesired situation or result caused by something; 'simply because', 'due to', 'on account of'." :long "The ～ばかりに grammar point is used to express an undesired situation or result that arises simply because of some factor. It can be translated as 'simply because', 'due to', or 'on account of' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + ばかりに, い-Adjective + ばかりに, な-Adjective + なばかりに, Noun + なばかりに" :examples [{:ja "雨が降ったばかりに、試合が中止になりました。" :en "Simply because it rained, the match was cancelled."} {:ja "彼が遅れたばかりに、私たちも遅刻しました。" :en "Because he was late, we ended up being late too."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-140" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ば～というものでもない" :reading "〜ba 〜to iu mono demo nai" :meaning "Not necessarily true that if A, then B." :short "Not necessarily true that if A, then B." :long "The ～ば～というものでもない grammar point is used to express that something is not necessarily true even if a certain condition is met. In other words, if A happens, it doesn't always mean B will happen." :formation "Verb-ば form + というものでもない" :examples [{:ja "高いものがいいものばかりではない。" :en "Expensive things are not always good."} {:ja "英語ができれば、成功するというものでもない。" :en "Just because you can speak English doesn’t mean you’ll succeed."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-141" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～はともかく～は" :reading "〜wa tomokaku 〜wa" :meaning "Used to compare or contrast two elements; 'as for...'." :short "Used to compare or contrast two elements; 'as for...'." :long "The grammar point ～はともかく～は is used to compare or contrast two elements in a sentence. It implies that the first element can be set aside or is less important compared to the second element. It often translates to 'putting A aside, B...'." :formation "Noun1 はともかく Noun2 は" :examples [{:ja "デザインはともかく、機能はこの携帯電話が一番です。" :en "As for design, the functionality of this phone is the best."} {:ja "彼の容姿はともかく、性格は素晴らしいです。" :en "Putting aside his looks, his personality is wonderful."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-142" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～はまだしも" :reading "〜wa mada shimo" :meaning "Expresses that while the first item is somewhat acceptable, the second item is not." :short "Expresses that while the first item is somewhat acceptable, the second item is not." :long "The ～はまだしも grammar point is used to show a contrast where the first item or situation might still be understandable or tolerable, whereas the second one is harder to accept. It’s often translated as 'X is one thing, but Y...'." :formation "Noun1 + はまだしも + Noun2" :examples [{:ja "失敗するのはまだしも、やらないで後悔する方がつらい。" :en "Failing is one thing, but regretting not even trying is worse."} {:ja "このピザは野菜が少ないのはまだしも、味も悪い。" :en "It’s not just that this pizza has few vegetables, it also tastes bad."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-143" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～はもとより" :reading "〜wa moto yori" :meaning "Expressing that something is obvious, and there is also something else." :short "Expressing that something is obvious, and there is also something else." :long "The ～はもとより grammar point is used to emphasize that something is obvious or a given, and on top of that, there’s another aspect to consider. It translates to 'not only…but also…' or 'of course…and also…'." :formation "Noun1 + はもとより, Noun2 + も" :examples [{:ja "英語はもとより、スペイン語も勉強しています。" :en "I'm studying not only English, but also Spanish."} {:ja "彼女は料理はもとより、お菓子作りも得意です。" :en "She’s not only good at cooking, but also at making sweets."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-144" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～は抜きにして" :reading "〜wa nuki ni shite" :meaning "Leaving aside, excluding." :short "Leaving aside, excluding." :long "The ～は抜きにして grammar point is used to express the idea of 'leaving aside' or 'excluding' a certain aspect. It is often used to focus the discussion or judgment on something else by disregarding the omitted point." :formation "Noun + は抜きにして" :examples [{:ja "値段は抜きにして、味は美味しい。" :en "Leaving aside the price, the taste is delicious."} {:ja "彼の性格は抜きにして、彼の実力は認めざるを得ない。" :en "Putting aside his personality, I have to acknowledge his ability."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-145" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～べきではない" :reading "〜beki dewa nai" :meaning "Express that an action should not be done; 'should not', 'ought not to'." :short "Express that an action should not be done; 'should not', 'ought not to'." :long "The ～べきではない grammar point is used to indicate that an action is not recommended or should not be done. It can be translated as 'should not' or 'ought not to' in English. The typical formation is “Verb-dictionary form + べきではない.”" :formation "Verb-dictionary form + べき + ではない" :examples [{:ja "遅れる理由がないなら、遅れるべきではありません。" :en "If there's no reason to be late, you shouldn’t be late."} {:ja "子供に悪口を言うべきではありません。" :en "You shouldn’t say hurtful things to children."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-146" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～まい" :reading "〜mai" :meaning "Express a negative conjecture or determination; 'probably not' or 'I will not'." :short "Express a negative conjecture or determination; 'probably not' or 'I will not'." :long "The ～まい grammar point is used to express a negative conjecture or a strong determination not to do something. In modern usage, it is generally used with the dictionary form of verbs (来る→来るまい, する→するまい, etc.). It can be translated as 'probably not' or 'I will not' in English." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + まい" :examples [{:ja "彼は今晩来るまい。" :en "He probably won't come tonight."} {:ja "こんなに遅れては、電車に間に合うまい。" :en "Being this late, I probably won't make it for the train."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-147" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～まで～て" :reading "〜made 〜te" :meaning "Express doing something until a certain time or condition; 'until', 'to'." :short "Express doing something until a certain time or condition; 'until', 'to'." :long "The ～まで～て grammar point is used to show that an action continues until a certain time or condition. In your examples, a time expression (such as 9時) is followed by まで, then the action in the て-form. It can be translated as 'until' or 'up to the point when/where'." :formation "Time/Noun + まで + Verb(て-form)" :examples [{:ja "9時まで働いて、その後休憩しました。" :en "I worked until 9 o'clock, and then took a break."} {:ja "夜まで勉強して、試験に合格しました。" :en "I studied until night, and passed the exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-148" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ままに" :reading "〜mama ni" :meaning "Act according to; leave as is; unchanged." :short "Act according to; leave as is; unchanged." :long "The ～ままに grammar point is used to express leaving something in its current state or doing something exactly as is. It can also imply doing something naturally or without altering the circumstances. Depending on the part of speech, different particles are used: Verb-て + まま, い-Adjective + のまま, な-Adjective + なまま, Noun + のまま." :formation "Verb-て + まま, い-Adjective + のまま, な-Adjective + なまま, Noun + のまま" :examples [{:ja "子供が寝ているままに、静かに部屋を出ました。" :en "I quietly left the room while the child was still sleeping."} {:ja "窓が開いたままで、寒くなりました。" :en "It got cold with the window left open."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-149" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～もかまわず" :reading "〜mo kamawazu" :meaning "Express indifference or disregard; 'without hesitating', 'without worrying about'." :short "Express indifference or disregard; 'without hesitating', 'without worrying about'." :long "The ～もかまわず grammar point is used to express indifference or disregard toward possible consequences or external factors. It can be translated as 'without hesitating', 'without worrying about', or 'regardless of'. It attaches to verbs in their dictionary form or nouns." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + もかまわず, Noun + もかまわず" :examples [{:ja "彼は周りの人もかまわず、大声で話しました。" :en "Without worrying about the people around him, he spoke loudly."} {:ja "彼女は時間もかまわず、ゆっくりと本を読み続けました。" :en "She kept reading the book leisurely, without worrying about the time."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-150" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ものか" :reading "〜mono ka" :meaning "Definitely not; no way; no chance." :short "Definitely not; no way; no chance." :long "The ～ものか grammar point is used to express a strong denial, refusal, or astonishment. It often appears with a rhetorical question tone in casual forms, such as Verb (dictionary/volitional/～たい) + ものか, Adjective + ものか, or Noun + ものか, meaning 'No way...', 'As if I'd ever...', etc." :formation "Verb-casual + ものか, い-Adjective + ものか, な-Adjective + (な)ものか, Noun + (な)ものか" :examples [{:ja "そんなこと、僕がやりたいものか。" :en "No way do I want to do such a thing."} {:ja "こんな仕事、誰が引き受けようものか。" :en "Who on earth would take on a job like this? (No one would!)"}]} {:id "grammar-n2-151" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ものがある" :reading "〜mono ga aru" :meaning "Express a strong impression or feeling; 'there's something about it that...'" :short "Express a strong impression or feeling; 'there's something about it that...'" :long "「～ものがある」 is used to convey that there is a certain intangible quality or strong feeling about something that the speaker cannot ignore. It is often used with verbs (plain form), い-adjectives, or な-adjectives + な, to show that one ‘feels strongly that...’ or ‘there’s something about it that…’." :formation "Verb-casual + ものがある, い-Adjective + ものがある, な-Adjective + なものがある" :examples [{:ja "彼のスピーチには心を打たれるものがある。" :en "There's something moving about his speech."} {:ja "この映画には考えさせられるものがある。" :en "There's something about this movie that really makes you think."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-152" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ものだ" :reading "〜mono da" :meaning "Expresses something natural or expected; 'it is normal that...', 'should', 'ought to'." :short "Expresses something natural or expected; 'it is normal that...', 'should', 'ought to'." :long "「～ものだ」 can indicate that something is regarded as a general truth, common sense, or natural behavior. It can also express strong sentiment or emotion. In the past tense (Verb-た form + ものだ), it often shows nostalgia or recollection ('I used to...')." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ものだ, な-Adjective + なものだ, (Also: Verb-た form + ものだ for recollections)" :examples [{:ja "若い時はたくさん遊ぶものだ。" :en "It is natural to have a lot of fun when you are young."} {:ja "約束をしたら、守るものだ。" :en "If you make a promise, you should keep it."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-153" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ものだから" :reading "〜mono dakara" :meaning "Express the reason or cause for something; 'because', 'since', 'due to'." :short "Express the reason or cause for something; 'because', 'since', 'due to'." :long "「～ものだから」 is used to explain a reason or cause from the speaker’s point of view. It can be translated as 'because', 'since', or 'due to'. It often carries the nuance of 'you see, that's why ...'." :formation "Verb-plain + ものだから, い-Adjective + ものだから, な-Adjective + なものだから, Noun + なものだから" :examples [{:ja "試験が近いものだから、毎日勉強しています。" :en "Because the exam is near, I'm studying every day."} {:ja "このレストランはとても人気があるものだから、予約しないと席がないかもしれません。" :en "Because this restaurant is very popular, there might not be any seats if we don't reserve."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-154" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ものではない" :reading "〜mono dewa nai" :meaning "Expresses a strong negative suggestion or prohibition: 'should not', 'must not'." :short "Expresses a strong negative suggestion or prohibition: 'should not', 'must not'." :long "「～ものではない」 is used to indicate that something should not or must not be done. It often appears in written or formal contexts, meaning 'it is not proper to...' or 'you shouldn’t...'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ものではない" :examples [{:ja "そんなことを言うものではありません。" :en "You should not say such a thing."} {:ja "公共の場で大声を出すものではない。" :en "You should not raise your voice in public places."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-155" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ものなら" :reading "〜mono nara" :meaning "Expresses a conditional wish or unlikely possibility; 'if only', 'if it were possible'." :short "Expresses a conditional wish or unlikely possibility; 'if only', 'if it were possible'." :long "「～ものなら」 is used when expressing a strong wish under a hypothetical or difficult condition. It often implies that fulfilling this condition is unlikely or very hard, but 'if only it could happen, then...'." :formation "Verb-(potential/plain) form + ものなら, etc." :examples [{:ja "帰れるものなら、今すぐ国へ帰りたい。" :en "If I could possibly go back, I'd return to my country right now."} {:ja "できるものなら、世界を一周してみたい。" :en "If it were possible, I'd love to travel around the world."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-156" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ものの、～" :reading "〜mono no、～" :meaning "Indicates contrast or contradiction between two clauses; 'although', 'but'." :short "Indicates contrast or contradiction between two clauses; 'although', 'but'." :long "「～ものの」 is used to connect two parts of a sentence where the first part is true, yet the second part shows a contrasting or unexpected outcome. It can be translated as 'although', 'even though', or 'but still'." :formation "Verb-casual + ものの, い-Adjective + ものの, な-Adjective + なものの, Noun + であるものの" :examples [{:ja "早起きしたものの、バスに間に合わなかった。" :en "Even though I got up early, I didn't catch the bus in time."} {:ja "この部屋は広いものの、家具が少ない。" :en "Although this room is spacious, there isn't much furniture."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-157" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～も～ば～も～" :reading "〜mo〜ba〜mo〜" :meaning "Lists multiple attributes or actions applying to the same subject: 'both…and…', 'not only…but also…'." :short "Lists multiple attributes or actions applying to the same subject: 'both…and…', 'not only…but also…'." :long "「～も～ば～も～」 is used when describing multiple characteristics or actions. It often appears with the conditional form (ば) plus も, indicating that 'A is true, and also B is true.' For example, 'He can do X, and he can also do Y.' It's different from '～ば～ほど', which means 'the more… the more…'." :formation "Noun/Verb/Adjective + も + (Verb/Adjective in ば-form) + も + (Verb/Adjective)" :examples [{:ja "彼は勉強もできればスポーツもできる。" :en "He is good at studying and also at sports."} {:ja "このレストランは値段も安ければ味もいい。" :en "This restaurant is cheap, and the food is also good."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-158" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～も同然だ" :reading "〜mo douzen da" :meaning "Indicates 'as good as', 'virtually', or 'practically' the same as something." :short "Indicates 'as good as', 'virtually', or 'practically' the same as something." :long "「～も同然だ」 means something is practically the same as (X). It emphasizes that although it may not be literally identical, there is hardly any difference in reality." :formation "Noun + (も)同然だ, Verb-casual + (も)同然だ, etc." :examples [{:ja "彼とは長い付き合いだから、家族も同然だ。" :en "I've known him for so long that he's practically family."} {:ja "毎日遅刻するなんて、来ていないも同然だ。" :en "Being late every single day is as good as not coming at all."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-159" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～やら～やら" :reading "〜yara〜yara" :meaning "Expresses various items or actions, 'things like..., and so on'." :short "Expresses various items or actions, 'things like..., and so on'." :long "「～やら～やら」 is used to list multiple things or actions in a somewhat non-exhaustive way. It can be translated as 'things like A and B' or 'A, B, and so on.' The grammar is often Verb-casual + やら, Noun + やら, い-Adjective + やら, etc." :formation "Verb-casual + やら, い-Adjective + やら, な-Adjective + やら, Noun + やら" :examples [{:ja "宿題やら掃除やら、やることがたくさんある。" :en "I have lots of things to do, like homework and cleaning."} {:ja "野菜やら果物やら、色々な食べ物を買いました。" :en "I bought all sorts of foods, such as vegetables and fruits."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-160" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～ようがない" :reading "〜you ga nai" :meaning "Expresses that there is no way (method) to do something; it's impossible to do." :short "Expresses that there is no way (method) to do something; it's impossible to do." :long "「～ようがない」 is used with the ます-stem of a verb to indicate that there is no means or method to carry out that action. It conveys the sense that it's impossible to do something or that there's absolutely no way to achieve it." :formation "Verb-ます stem + ようがない" :examples [{:ja "彼の連絡先がわからないので、助けようがない。" :en "I don't know his contact information, so there's no way I can help him."} {:ja "ここまで壊れてしまったら、もう直しようがない。" :en "Now that it's broken this badly, there's no way to fix it."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-161" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～よりほかない" :reading "〜yori hoka nai" :meaning "Express that there is no other choice; 'have no choice but', 'cannot do anything but'." :short "Express that there is no other choice; 'have no choice but', 'cannot do anything but'." :long "The ～よりほかない grammar point is used to express that there is no other choice or alternative in a given situation. It can be translated as 'have no choice but to...', 'nothing but...', or 'cannot do anything but...'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + よりほかない" :examples [{:ja "試験が近いので、勉強するよりほかない。" :en "Since the exam is near, there is no choice but to study."} {:ja "風邪がひどいので、早く寝るよりほかない。" :en "My cold is severe, so I have no choice but to go to bed early."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-162" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～わけがない" :reading "〜wake ga nai" :meaning "Expresses 'there’s no way' or 'it’s impossible', indicating a high degree of certainty." :short "Expresses 'there’s no way' or 'it’s impossible', indicating a high degree of certainty." :long "The ～わけがない grammar point is used to express that something is highly unlikely or impossible. It can be translated as 'there's no way' or 'it's impossible'." :formation "Verb-casual + わけがない, い-Adjective + わけがない, な-Adjective + なわけがない, Noun + のわけがない" :examples [{:ja "彼が怪我をしたから、試合に勝てるわけがない。" :en "He got injured, so there's no way he can win the match."} {:ja "嘘ばかり言っている人を、信じるわけがない。" :en "There's no way I can believe someone who constantly lies."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-163" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～わけだ" :reading "〜wake da" :meaning "Express a logical conclusion or reason; 'it means', 'no wonder', 'the reason is'." :short "Express a logical conclusion or reason; 'it means', 'no wonder', 'the reason is'." :long "The ～わけだ grammar point is used to express a logical conclusion or reason. It can be translated as 'it means...', 'no wonder...', or 'that explains why...'." :formation "Verb-casual + わけだ, い-Adjective + わけだ, な-Adjective + なわけだ, Noun + のわけだ" :examples [{:ja "彼女が遅れたのは、電車が遅れたわけだ。" :en "The reason she was late is that the train was delayed."} {:ja "授業が面白かったから、みんな熱心に聞いていたわけだ。" :en "The class was interesting, so that's why everyone was listening intently."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-164" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～わけではない" :reading "〜wake dewa nai" :meaning "Not necessarily; it doesn't mean that ~." :short "Not necessarily; it doesn't mean that ~." :long "The ～わけではない grammar point is used to express that something is not necessarily the case or 'it doesn’t mean that...' It negates a broad assumption or generalization." :formation "Verb-plain + わけではない, い-Adjective + わけではない, な-Adjective + だわけではない, Noun + だわけではない" :examples [{:ja "彼が勉強しないわけではないが、成績があまり良くない。" :en "It's not that he doesn't study, but his grades aren't very good."} {:ja "このレストランが高いわけではないけど、毎日通うには少し高いね。" :en "It's not that this restaurant is expensive, but it's a bit too pricey to go there every day."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-165" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～わけにはいかない" :reading "〜wake ni wa ikanai" :meaning "Expresses that one cannot (or must not) do something due to responsibility, social pressure, etc." :short "Expresses that one cannot (or must not) do something due to responsibility, social pressure, etc." :long "The ～わけにはいかない grammar point is used to express that you cannot afford to do something or are not allowed to do it. It often implies a sense of responsibility, moral/social obligation, or external pressure that prevents you from doing it." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + わけにはいかない" :examples [{:ja "試験が近いから、遊ぶわけにはいかない。" :en "Since the exam is close, I can't afford to just play around."} {:ja "田中さんが待っているので、遅れるわけにはいかない。" :en "Mr. Tanaka is waiting, so I absolutely must not be late."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-166" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～をきっかけに" :reading "〜wo kikkake ni" :meaning "Indicates that something serves as a trigger or starting point for another action/event." :short "Indicates that something serves as a trigger or starting point for another action/event." :long "「～をきっかけに」 is used with a noun to show that an event, thing, or situation became a trigger or motive that led to a new development or action. It translates as 'taking ... as an opportunity,' 'due to ...,' or 'inspired by ...'." :formation "Noun + をきっかけに" :examples [{:ja "結婚をきっかけに、彼は真剣に仕事を頑張り始めた。" :en "Getting married was the catalyst for him to start working seriously."} {:ja "あの出会いをきっかけに、私たちは友達になりました。" :en "That encounter led us to become friends."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-167" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～を～として" :reading "〜wo〜toshite" :meaning "Express 'as', 'in the capacity of', or 'taking something as...'." :short "Express 'as', 'in the capacity of', or 'taking something as...'." :long "The grammar pattern 「～を～として」 is used with nouns to indicate taking one thing 'as' or 'in the role of' another. It shows how a certain noun is being viewed or utilized." :formation "Noun1 + を + Noun2 + として" :examples [{:ja "彼は教師を職業として働いています。" :en "He works as a teacher."} {:ja "彼女はリーダーを役割として受け入れました。" :en "She accepted the role of leader."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-168" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～を中心に" :reading "〜wo chuushin ni" :meaning "Indicates that something is centered around or focused on a main topic or area." :short "Indicates that something is centered around or focused on a main topic or area." :long "「～を中心に」 is used with nouns to show that an action or situation is mainly or primarily about the specified noun. It can be translated as 'centered on', 'focusing on', or 'with ... as the main focus'." :formation "Noun + を中心に" :examples [{:ja "このイベントは音楽を中心に楽しまれています。" :en "This event is mainly enjoyed for its music."} {:ja "彼の仕事は東京を中心に行われています。" :en "His work is primarily conducted in and around Tokyo."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-169" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～を問わず" :reading "〜wo towazu" :meaning "Means 'regardless of' or 'irrespective of'." :short "Means 'regardless of' or 'irrespective of'." :long "「～を問わず」 is used to express that something applies or is available without any distinction regarding the stated nouns/conditions. It means 'regardless of...' or 'irrespective of...'." :formation "Noun + を問わず" :examples [{:ja "このイベントは、年齢を問わず、誰でも参加できます。" :en "This event is open to everyone, regardless of age."} {:ja "このスーパーは、日曜日を問わず、毎日開いています。" :en "This supermarket is open every day, regardless of Sunday."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-170" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～を込めて" :reading "〜wo komete" :meaning "To express that something is done with a certain feeling, intention, or emotion." :short "To express that something is done with a certain feeling, intention, or emotion." :long "The ～を込めて grammar point is used to express that an action is done with a certain feeling, intention or emotion. It can be translated as 'with' or 'filled with' in English. ～を込めて is typically used with feelings or emotions that the speaker wishes to convey through their actions." :formation "Feeling/emotion/intention Noun + を + 込めて + Verb" :examples [{:ja "愛を込めて料理を作りました。" :en "I cooked the meal with love."} {:ja "感謝を込めて先生にお礼の手紙を書きました。" :en "I wrote a thank-you letter to my teacher, filled with gratitude."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-171" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～を通じて" :reading "〜wo tsuujite" :meaning "Expresses 'through', 'throughout', or 'during'." :short "Expresses 'through', 'throughout', or 'during'." :long "The ～を通じて grammar point is used to express actions or conditions that occur through or throughout a specific period, medium, or method. It can be translated as 'through', 'throughout', or 'during' in English. It is formed by attaching 'を通じて' to a noun that represents time, place, or method." :formation "Noun + を通じて" :examples [{:ja "インターネットを通じて友達と連絡を取ります。" :en "I keep in touch with my friends through the internet."} {:ja "彼は一日を通じて熱心に働いています。" :en "He works diligently throughout the day."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-172" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～を頼りに" :reading "〜wo tayori ni" :meaning "To rely on or depend on something or someone." :short "To rely on or depend on something or someone." :long "The ～を頼りに grammar point is used to express that someone is relying on or depending on something or someone. It can be translated as 'to rely on', 'to depend on', or 'counting on' in English. This phrase is used with nouns (people or objects) that act as a source of support or help." :formation "Noun + を頼りに" :examples [{:ja "彼女は地図を頼りに旅行をしている。" :en "She is traveling relying on a map."} {:ja "多くの人が彼の力を頼りに生活している。" :en "Many people are living depending on his strength."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-173" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～一方" :reading "〜ippou" :meaning "Indicates that while one action or situation is happening, another action or situation is also occurring or is in contrast." :short "Indicates that while one action or situation is happening, another action or situation is also occurring or is in contrast." :long "The ～一方 grammar point is used to express that while one action or situation is taking place, another is occurring at the same time or in contrast. The clauses connected by 一方 often show simultaneous actions, opposing characteristics, or parallel developments." :formation "Verb-casual + 一方(で), い-Adjective + 一方(で), な-Adjective + な/である一方(で), Noun + である一方(で)" :examples [{:ja "技術が進化する一方、生活が忙しくなっている。" :en "While technology advances, life is getting busier."} {:ja "日本語が上達する一方、英語力が下がっている気がする。" :en "While my Japanese improves, I feel like my English ability is declining."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-174" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～一方だ" :reading "〜ippou da" :meaning "Expresses that a situation or condition is continuing to increase or decrease in one direction." :short "Expresses that a situation or condition is continuing to increase or decrease in one direction." :long "The ～一方だ grammar point is used when something is trending in a single direction over time, often increasing or worsening. It emphasizes that the tendency keeps going in one direction." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + 一方だ" :examples [{:ja "世界の人口は増える一方だ。" :en "The world's population continues to increase."} {:ja "この町の治安は悪化する一方だ。" :en "Public safety in this town is getting worse and worse."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-175" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～上で" :reading "〜ue de" :meaning "Used to express that something is done after or on the basis of something else; 'after', 'upon'." :short "Used to express that something is done after or on the basis of something else; 'after', 'upon'." :long "The ～上で grammar point is used to indicate that an action or decision is made after another action or condition, or based on certain information. It can be translated as 'after doing something' or 'on the basis of...'." :formation "Verb-ますstem + 上で, Noun + の上で" :examples [{:ja "調査を行った上で、結論を出しましょう。" :en "Let's draw a conclusion after conducting the investigation."} {:ja "就職活動を終えた上で、進学するかどうか決めます。" :en "I will decide whether to continue my studies after finishing my job hunting."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-176" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～上に" :reading "〜ue ni" :meaning "Express 'in addition to', 'besides', 'not only... but also'." :short "Express 'in addition to', 'besides', 'not only... but also'." :long "The ～上に grammar point is used to indicate that there is an additional piece of information or situation besides the first one. It can be translated as 'in addition to', 'besides', or 'not only... but also'. It can be used with verbs, い-adjectives, な-adjectives, and nouns." :formation "Verb-plain + 上に, い-Adjective + 上に, な-Adjective + な上に, Noun + の上に" :examples [{:ja "彼は頭がいい上に、スポーツも得意です。" :en "In addition to being smart, he is also good at sports."} {:ja "このドレスは美しい上に、値段も安いです。" :en "This dress is not only beautiful but also affordable."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-177" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～上は" :reading "～ue wa" :meaning "Expresses 'now that', 'since', or 'once'." :short "Expresses 'now that', 'since', or 'once'." :long "The ～上は grammar point is used to express that since a certain condition or situation exists, a certain action or result follows. It can be translated as 'now that', 'since', or 'once' in English. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, い-adjective, な-adjective, or noun." :formation "Verb-casual + 上は, い-Adjective + 上は, な-Adjective + な上は, Noun + の上は" :examples [{:ja "試験に合格した上は、パーティーを開こう。" :en "Now that I've passed the exam, let's have a party."} {:ja "この仕事を引き受けた上は、最後までやり遂げるつもりだ。" :en "Now that I have taken on this job, I intend to see it through to the end."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-178" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～以上" :reading "〜ijou" :meaning "Expresses 'since' or 'as long as' in a conditional context." :short "Expresses 'since' or 'as long as' in a conditional context." :long "The ～以上 grammar point is used to express a condition or requirement, and it can be translated as 'since', 'as long as', or 'if'. It often emphasizes that if a certain condition is met, a certain action or consequence will follow." :formation "Verb-casual + 以上, い-Adjective + 以上, な-Adjective + な以上, Noun + の以上" :examples [{:ja "彼は忙しい以上、手伝いに行っても無駄だろう。" :en "Since he's busy, it would be pointless to try helping him."} {:ja "約束した以上、守るべきだ。" :en "Since I made a promise, I should keep it."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-179" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～以来" :reading "〜irai" :meaning "Indicates something happening 'since', 'ever since', or 'after' a particular time." :short "Indicates something happening 'since', 'ever since', or 'after' a particular time." :long "The ～以来 grammar point is used to express a period of time that has continued uninterrupted since a certain point in the past. It can be translated as 'since', 'ever since', or 'after'. The formation differs depending on whether it is used with a verb, an adjective, or a noun." :formation "Verb-past + 以来, い-Adjective + 以来, Noun + 以来" :examples [{:ja "卒業以来、彼女に会っていない。" :en "I haven't seen her since we graduated."} {:ja "子供が生まれて以来、忙しくなった。" :en "I've been busy ever since my child was born."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-180" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～切る" :reading "〜kiru" :meaning "To do something completely or thoroughly." :short "To do something completely or thoroughly." :long "The ～切る grammar point is added to the verb stem (masu-form) to express doing something completely or thoroughly, often implying a strong sense of finishing or using up everything." :formation "Verb-stem + 切る" :examples [{:ja "彼は試験勉強をがんばり切るつもりだ。" :en "He intends to give his absolute all to exam study."} {:ja "この仕事を終わり切るまで、帰らないでください。" :en "Please don't go home until you've completely finished this job."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-181" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～反面" :reading "〜hanmen" :meaning "Express contrast between two aspects; 'on the one hand', 'on the other hand'." :short "Express contrast between two aspects; 'on the one hand', 'on the other hand'." :long "The ～反面 grammar point is used to show a contrast between two aspects of something. It can be translated as 'on the one hand' or 'on the other hand'." :formation "Verb-casual + 反面, い-Adjective + 反面, な-Adjective + である反面, Noun + である反面" :examples [{:ja "この車は速い反面、燃費が悪いです。" :en "On the one hand, this car is fast, but on the other hand, it has poor fuel efficiency."} {:ja "彼は頭がいい反面、運動が苦手です。" :en "On the one hand, he is smart, but on the other hand, he's not good at sports."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-182" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～向け" :reading "〜muke" :meaning "Indicating target or intended audience." :short "Indicating target or intended audience." :long "The ～向け grammar point is used to indicate the target or intended audience for a product, service, or event. It can be translated as 'for', 'aimed at', or 'intended for'." :formation "Noun + 向け" :examples [{:ja "この雑誌は若者向けです。" :en "This magazine is aimed at young people."} {:ja "スーパーで家族向けの食品セットが売っています。" :en "The supermarket sells food sets intended for families."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-183" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～恐れがある" :reading "〜osore ga aru" :meaning "Express a concern or fear that something undesirable might occur." :short "Express a concern or fear that something undesirable might occur." :long "The ～恐れがある grammar point indicates that there is a concern or fear that something undesirable or negative might happen. It is often used for potential problems or risks, and can be translated as 'there is a risk' or 'there is a possibility'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + 恐れがある" :examples [{:ja "地震が来る恐れがあるため、避難所に行ってください。" :en "Please go to the evacuation center because there is a risk of an earthquake."} {:ja "彼は事故に遭う恐れがあるので、注意して運転してください。" :en "There's a possibility he might have an accident, so please drive carefully."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-184" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～折には" :reading "〜ori ni wa" :meaning "Expresses 'when' or 'in case of' specific situations or timings." :short "Expresses 'when' or 'in case of' specific situations or timings." :long "The ～折には grammar point is used to emphasize a specific moment or situation (often not very frequent). It can be translated as 'when (the occasion arises)' or 'in case of'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + 折には, Verb-た form + 折には, い-Adjective + 折には, な-Adjective + な折には, Noun + の折には" :examples [{:ja "喉が渇く折には、このペットボトルの水を飲んでください。" :en "When you get thirsty, please drink water from this bottle."} {:ja "この地域に来る折には、この神社に必ずお参りしましょう。" :en "When you visit this area, make sure to go to this shrine."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-185" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～末" :reading "～sue" :meaning "Express the outcome after a period of time or series of actions; 'after', 'as a result of'." :short "Express the outcome after a period of time or series of actions; 'after', 'as a result of'." :long "The ～末 grammar point is used to indicate an outcome or result after a period of time, effort, or a series of actions. It can be translated as 'after', 'as a result of', or 'following'. It is typically used with nouns (e.g., '相談の末', '努力の末')." :formation "Noun + 末" :examples [{:ja "長い議論の末、ようやく意見がまとまった。" :en "After a long discussion, we finally reached a consensus."} {:ja "一週間の勉強の末、試験に合格した。" :en "After a week of studying, I passed the exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-186" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～次第" :reading "〜shidai" :meaning "Expresses 'as soon as' (when used with Verb-ますstem) or 'depending on' (when used with a noun)." :short "Expresses 'as soon as' (when used with Verb-ますstem) or 'depending on' (when used with a noun)." :long "The grammar pattern ～次第 can mean 'as soon as' when used with a verb in its ます-stem, and 'depending on' when used with a noun. For instance, 仕事が終わり次第 (as soon as work finishes) or 天気次第 (depending on the weather)." :formation "Verb-ますstem + 次第, Noun + 次第" :examples [{:ja "仕事が終わり次第、帰ります。" :en "I will go home as soon as I finish work."} {:ja "お客様のご意見次第で、サービスを改善します。" :en "We will improve our services depending on customer feedback."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-187" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～次第で" :reading "〜shidai de" :meaning "Indicates 'depending on; based on' a certain factor or situation." :short "Indicates 'depending on; based on' a certain factor or situation." :long "The ～次第で pattern typically follows a noun to show that something is decided or changes 'depending on' that factor. It can be translated as 'based on...' or 'depending on...'." :formation "Noun + 次第で" :examples [{:ja "仕事の終わり次第で、家に帰る時間が変わります。" :en "Depending on when I finish work, the time I go home may change."} {:ja "天気次第で、ピクニックに行く予定です。" :en "We plan to go on a picnic depending on the weather."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-188" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～次第です" :reading "〜shidai desu" :meaning "A polite/formal way to say 'it depends on...' or 'as soon as...', often used in concluding statements." :short "A polite/formal way to say 'it depends on...' or 'as soon as...', often used in concluding statements." :long "「～次第です」 is frequently used in more formal contexts, either to mean that something depends on certain conditions or to indicate an action will happen as soon as something else is completed. It’s also used as a set phrase to conclude an explanation in writing." :formation "Verb-ますstem + 次第です (or Noun + 次第です)" :examples [{:ja "天気次第ですが、明日はピクニックに行こうと思います。" :en "It depends on the weather, but I’m thinking of going for a picnic tomorrow."} {:ja "試験の結果次第ですが、留学したいと考えています。" :en "It depends on my exam results, but I'm considering studying abroad."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-189" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～気味" :reading "〜gimi" :meaning "Indicates a slight feeling or tendency toward a particular state." :short "Indicates a slight feeling or tendency toward a particular state." :long "The suffix ～気味 generally attaches to nouns (or nominalized words) to express a slight feeling, tendency, or inclination toward a particular state. It often translates to '-ish' or 'a bit' in English (e.g., 'sleep-deprived-ish', 'nervous-ish')." :formation "Noun + 気味 (e.g., 風邪気味, 寝不足気味, 緊張気味)" :examples [{:ja "最近寝不足気味で、ちょっと疲れています。" :en "Lately, I'm feeling somewhat sleep-deprived and a bit tired."} {:ja "この部屋は暗い気味だから、もっと明るい照明をつけよう。" :en "This room is a bit dark, so let's install brighter lighting."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-190" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～限り" :reading "〜kagiri" :meaning "Expresses 'as long as', 'while', or 'to the extent that' a certain condition holds true." :short "Expresses 'as long as', 'while', or 'to the extent that' a certain condition holds true." :long "The phrase ～限り is used to indicate that something continues or is true 'as long as' or 'while' a certain condition is met. It can also mean 'to the extent that'." :formation "Verb-casual + 限り, い-Adjective + 限り, な-Adjective + な限り, Noun + の限り" :examples [{:ja "元気な限り、運動し続けます。" :en "As long as I'm healthy, I'll keep exercising."} {:ja "お金がある限り、旅行を楽しみたい。" :en "As long as I have money, I'd like to travel."}]} {:id "grammar-n2-191" :track :grammar :level "N2" :display "～際に" :reading "〜sai ni" :meaning "Means 'when', 'while', or 'on the occasion of' a certain event or situation." :short "Means 'when', 'while', or 'on the occasion of' a certain event or situation." :long "～際に is used to specify a time or occasion when something happens. It’s more formal than a simple 'とき' and often appears in announcements or polite instructions." :formation "Verb-casual + 際に, い-Adjective + 際に, な-Adjective + な際に, Noun + の際に" :examples [{:ja "出発の際に、パスポートを忘れないでください。" :en "When departing, please don't forget your passport."} {:ja "地震が起こる際に、すぐに避難してください。" :en "When an earthquake occurs, please evacuate immediately."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-001" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A うが B うが" :reading "A uga B uga" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'no matter how... or', 'even if... or'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'no matter how... or', 'even if... or'." :long "The ～うが ～うが grammar pattern is used to express that regardless of the conditions in A and B, the result remains the same. Often you’ll see volitional forms or i-adjective '-かろうが' forms. It can be translated as 'no matter how... or...', 'even if... or...'." :formation "Verb-volitional form + うが + Verb-volitional form + うが, or Adjective (-かろう) + が + Adjective (-かろう) + が" :examples [{:ja "朝早かろうが夜遅かろうが、いつもバスが遅れる。" :en "No matter whether it's early morning or late at night, the bus is always late."} {:ja "彼に話そうが話すまいが、結果は変わらない。" :en "Even if I talk to him or don't, the outcome won't change."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-002" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A うと B うと" :reading "A uto B uto" :meaning "Shows that the speaker is indifferent to both conditions; 'whether A or B'." :short "Shows that the speaker is indifferent to both conditions; 'whether A or B'." :long "The うと うと pattern indicates that A and B are both possibilities, yet the speaker's stance or the outcome doesn’t change. It's often translated as 'whether A or B'. For i-adjectives, the '-かろうと' form is used. For nouns or な-adjectives, you’ll see 'Noun/な-adjective + であろうと'." :formation "い-adjective (-かろう) + と, な-adjective / Noun + であろうと" :examples [{:ja "雨が降ろうと晴れようと、私は毎日ジョギングをします。" :en "Whether it's raining or sunny, I jog every day."} {:ja "試験が難しかろうと易しかろうと、一生懸命勉強します。" :en "Whether the exam is difficult or easy, I will study hard."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-003" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A かたわら B" :reading "A katawara B" :meaning "Expresses 'while also', 'at the same time as'." :short "Expresses 'while also', 'at the same time as'." :long "The ～かたわら grammar point is used when someone is doing B while also doing A. It often implies that both tasks require considerable commitment or skill. It can be translated as 'while also' or 'at the same time as'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + かたわら, Noun + のかたわら" :examples [{:ja "彼は仕事をするかたわら、大学に通っている。" :en "While working, he also attends university."} {:ja "私は学生のかたわら、レストランでアルバイトをしている。" :en "While being a student, I work part-time at a restaurant."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-004" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A かれ B かれ" :reading "A kare B kare" :meaning "Expresses 'whether A or B'; both situations are included." :short "Expresses 'whether A or B'; both situations are included." :long "The grammar point ～AかれBかれ is a somewhat literary/old-fashioned pattern often seen with i-adjectives (e.g., 早かれ遅かれ). It can mean 'whether A or B'. Here it's shown with nouns or verbs, which is also possible in older or set expressions." :formation "Commonly i-adjective stems + かれ + i-adjective stems + かれ (e.g. 早かれ遅かれ). Also sometimes Noun + かれ + Noun + かれ in older usage." :examples [{:ja "冬かれ夏かれ、毎日ジョギングする。" :en "Whether it's winter or summer, I jog every day."} {:ja "彼が来るかれ来ないかれ、パーティーは始める。" :en "Whether he comes or not, the party will start."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-005" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A だの B だの" :reading "A dano B dano" :meaning "Used to list examples (often with a critical or complaining tone); 'things like A, B, etc.'." :short "Used to list examples (often with a critical or complaining tone); 'things like A, B, etc.'." :long "The ～だの～だの grammar point is used to list examples, typically in a casual or complaining tone. It can be translated as 'things like... and so on'." :formation "Noun/Verb casual + だの + Noun/Verb casual + だの" :examples [{:ja "彼はいつも遅刻だの、仕事を忘れるだので困る。" :en "I'm troubled because he's always late, forgets work, and so on."} {:ja "彼女は新しい服だの、化粧品だのによくお金を使います。" :en "She often spends money on new clothes, cosmetics, and so on."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-006" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A であれ B であれ" :reading "A deare B deare" :meaning "Indicates the same statement applies to both A and B; 'whether A or B'." :short "Indicates the same statement applies to both A and B; 'whether A or B'." :long "The grammar point A であれ B であれ is used to say a statement applies equally whether it's A or B. Often the nouns/conditions are contrasting pairs, and it's translated as 'whether A or B'." :formation "Noun A + であれ + Noun B + であれ" :examples [{:ja "雨であれ雪であれ、試合は中止にならない。" :en "Whether it's rain or snow, the match will not be canceled."} {:ja "男であれ女であれ、平等に扱われるべきだ。" :en "Whether man or woman, everyone should be treated equally."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-007" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A というか B というか" :reading "A to iu ka B to iu ka" :meaning "Means 'rather than say A, it's more like B', or 'whether to call it A or B'." :short "Means 'rather than say A, it's more like B', or 'whether to call it A or B'." :long "The grammar 'A というか B というか' is used when the speaker isn't sure which expression (A or B) describes the situation better. It can be translated as 'rather than A, it's more B' or 'should I call it A or B?'." :formation "Phrase A + というか + Phrase B + というか" :examples [{:ja "彼は無礼というか、直接的というか、とにかくはっきりしている。" :en "He's rather rude, or maybe just direct; in any case, he is very blunt."} {:ja "この映画は面白いというか、変というか、とにかく不思議な雰囲気がある。" :en "This movie is interesting or maybe strange; anyway, it has a mysterious vibe."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-008" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A とも B とも" :reading "A tomo B tomo" :meaning "Expresses two possibilities; 'either A or B', 'whether A or B'." :short "Expresses two possibilities; 'either A or B', 'whether A or B'." :long "The 'A とも B とも' grammar pattern indicates two possible scenarios. It can be translated as 'whether A or B' or 'either A or B'." :formation "Noun + とも + Noun + とも (or sometimes Verb forms for each A, B)" :examples [{:ja "決断とも誤りとも、結果は時間が教えてくれるでしょう。" :en "Whether it's a wise decision or a mistake, time will show the result."} {:ja "彼は父とも兄とも、もう何年も話していない。" :en "He hasn't spoken to either his father or his brother in years."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-009" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A にしろ B にしろ" :reading "A nishiro B nishiro" :meaning "Means 'whether it is A or B'; 'regardless of A or B'." :short "Means 'whether it is A or B'; 'regardless of A or B'." :long "A にしろ B にしろ is used to indicate that even though A and B are different possibilities, the statement or conclusion applies regardless. Often translated as 'whether it’s A or B' or 'regardless of A or B'." :formation "Noun A + にしろ + Noun B + にしろ, or Verb-casual A + にしろ + Verb-casual B + にしろ" :examples [{:ja "暑いにしろ寒いにしろ、毎日運動するべきです。" :en "Whether it's hot or cold, you should exercise every day."} {:ja "合格するにしろ不合格になるにしろ、最善を尽くしました。" :en "Whether I pass or fail, I did my best."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-010" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A にせよ B にせよ" :reading "A ni seyo B ni seyo" :meaning "Either A or B; Regardless of whether it's A or B." :short "Either A or B; Regardless of whether it's A or B." :long "The 〜にせよ 〜にせよ grammar point is used to express two possibilities A and B, indicating that regardless of whether it is A or B, the statement remains valid. It can be translated as 'whether it's A or B.'" :formation "Noun + にせよ + Noun + にせよ (also works with verbs in dictionary form + にせよ)" :examples [{:ja "雨にせよ雪にせよ、試合は中止しない。" :en "Whether it's rain or snow, the match won't be cancelled."} {:ja "彼にせよ彼女にせよ、誰か一人が行くべきだ。" :en "Whether it's him or her, someone should go."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-011" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A につけ B につけ" :reading "A ni tsuke B ni tsuke" :meaning "Expresses 'whether... or...', 'both... and...', 'in either case'." :short "Expresses 'whether... or...', 'both... and...', 'in either case'." :long "The ～AにつけBにつけ pattern is used to show that some state or action applies in both situation A and situation B. It often pairs contrasting or related conditions and can be translated as 'whether A or B', 'in either case', etc." :formation "Phrase A (dictionary/adjective form) + につけ、Phrase B (dictionary/adjective form) + につけ" :examples [{:ja "彼には笑うにつけ泣くにつけ、とても魅力的だ。" :en "He is very charming whether he’s laughing or crying."} {:ja "嬉しいにつけ悲しいにつけ、彼はいつも音楽を聴いている。" :en "Whether he's happy or sad, he's always listening to music."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-012" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "A のやら B のやら" :reading "A no yara B no yara" :meaning "Used when the speaker is unsure or confused between multiple possibilities." :short "Used when the speaker is unsure or confused between multiple possibilities." :long "The A のやら B のやら grammar expresses the speaker's confusion or inability to decide between possibility A and B. It can be translated roughly as 'whether it's A or B (I don't know).'" :formation "Noun1 + のやら + Noun2 + のやら (also works with adjectives and verbs + のやら)" :examples [{:ja "彼が怒っているのやら、悲しいのやら、私には分からない。" :en "I don't know whether he's angry or sad."} {:ja "試験の結果が良いのやら悪いのやら、気になって仕方がない。" :en "I can’t help worrying whether my exam results are good or bad."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-013" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun1 が Noun1 なら、 Noun2 も Noun2 だ" :reading "A ga A nara, B mo B da" :meaning "Indicates a parallel or matching situation; 'if Noun1 is (truly) Noun1, then Noun2 is also Noun2'." :short "Indicates a parallel or matching situation; 'if Noun1 is (truly) Noun1, then Noun2 is also Noun2'." :long "This structure conveys a comparison: 'If Noun1 is indeed that kind of thing (Noun1), then naturally Noun2 is also that kind of thing (Noun2)'. It’s often used to show parallels or equivalences in a rhetorical way." :formation "Noun1 + が + (repeat Noun1) + なら、Noun2 + も + (repeat Noun2) + だ" :examples [{:ja "鬼が鬼なら、人間も人間だ。" :en "If demons are indeed demons, humans are likewise humans."} {:ja "彼が社長なら、私も社長だ。" :en "If he is the president, then I'm the president too."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-014" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun1 も Noun1 なら、Noun2 も Noun2 だ" :reading "A mo A nara, B mo B da" :meaning "Highlights two parallel facts or characteristics; 'if it’s said that ~, then it can also be said that ~'." :short "Highlights two parallel facts or characteristics; 'if it’s said that ~, then it can also be said that ~'." :long "This grammar point sets two items or facts side by side, emphasizing that both follow a similar logic or share a parallel characteristic. Often it can be rendered as 'If you say Noun1 is Noun1, then Noun2 is also Noun2.'" :formation "Noun1 + も + (repeat Noun1) + なら、Noun2 + も + (repeat Noun2) + だ" :examples [{:ja "君も君なら、私も私だ。" :en "If you are you, then I am also me."} {:ja "夏も夏なら、冬も冬だ。" :en "If it’s said that summer is summer, then winter is also winter."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-015" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun + あっての + Noun" :reading "A atte no B" :meaning "Expresses 'B cannot exist without A'; 'B only exists because of A'." :short "Expresses 'B cannot exist without A'; 'B only exists because of A'." :long "The 'Noun + あっての + Noun' pattern emphasizes that the second noun (B) exists only thanks to, or on the foundation of, the first noun (A). It's often translated as 'B is nothing without A' or 'B exists only because of A'." :formation "Noun1 + あっての + Noun2" :examples [{:ja "家族あっての私です。" :en "I am who I am thanks to my family."} {:ja "お客様あってのビジネスです。" :en "A business is nothing without its customers."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-016" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun かたがた" :reading "Noun kata gata" :meaning "Do one thing while also doing another; 'while', 'and at the same time'." :short "Do one thing while also doing another; 'while', 'and at the same time'." :long "「Noun かたがた」 is a somewhat formal expression for doing one thing while also doing another. The first action is often the main purpose, and the second is done simultaneously or incidentally." :formation "Noun + かたがた" :examples [{:ja "東京へ出張のかたがた、友人に会いに行った。" :en "While on a business trip to Tokyo, I also went to see a friend."} {:ja "観光のかたがた、お土産を買いました。" :en "While sightseeing, I bought some souvenirs."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-017" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun がてら" :reading "Noun gatera" :meaning "Means 'while...at the same time', 'on the occasion of', or 'along with'." :short "Means 'while...at the same time', 'on the occasion of', or 'along with'." :long "The suffix ～がてら is used to show doing one activity alongside another, taking advantage of the opportunity. It can often be translated as 'while', 'at the same time', 'along with', or 'on the occasion of'." :formation "Noun + がてら" :examples [{:ja "散歩がてらに、近くの公園で花を摘みました。" :en "While taking a walk, I picked some flowers in the nearby park."} {:ja "買い物がてら、友達に会いに行きました。" :en "While I was out shopping, I went to see a friend."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-018" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun からある" :reading "〜kara aru" :meaning "Used with large numbers or measurements to emphasize 'at least that many' or 'no less than.'" :short "Used with large numbers or measurements to emphasize 'at least that many' or 'no less than.'" :long "「Noun + Counter + からある」indicates a large or minimum number, emphasizing that the quantity is at least that high (often more). It conveys the idea that the figure is notably large or impressive." :formation "Number + Counter + からある" :examples [{:ja "彼のコレクションは500個からある。" :en "He has at least 500 items in his collection."} {:ja "このビルは40階からある。" :en "This building has at least 40 floors."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-019" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun からする" :reading "Noun kara suru" :meaning "Used to indicate that something is judged, estimated, or considered from a certain point or aspect." :short "Used to indicate that something is judged, estimated, or considered from a certain point or aspect." :long "「Noun + からする」expresses a judgment or estimation based on a particular perspective or aspect. It translates to 'judging from', 'based on', or 'from the viewpoint of' in English, allowing the speaker to form an opinion or conclusion from that point." :formation "Noun + からする" :examples [{:ja "彼の態度からすると、彼はその計画に反対しているようだ。" :en "Judging from his attitude, he seems to be against the plan."} {:ja "このレストランの雰囲気からすると、料理もすばらしいはずだ。" :en "Judging from the atmosphere of this restaurant, the food must be wonderful."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-020" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun からの" :reading "~kara no" :meaning "Defines the starting point or the origin; 'from'." :short "Defines the starting point or the origin; 'from'." :long "「Noun + からの」indicates the place, source, or origin of something. It simply means 'from (Noun)'." :formation "Noun + からの" :examples [{:ja "日本からの手紙を受け取りました。" :en "I received a letter from Japan."} {:ja "彼は東京からの旅行者です。" :en "He is a traveler from Tokyo."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-021" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun から言わせれば" :reading "~kara iwasereba" :meaning "Express the perspective of the noun; 'from ~'s point of view.'" :short "Express the perspective of the noun; 'from ~'s point of view.'" :long "「Noun + から言わせれば」is used to express an opinion or perspective from that noun's point of view. It can translate as 'from ~'s perspective' or 'if ~ had a say...'." :formation "Noun + から言わせれば" :examples [{:ja "先生から言わせれば、毎日勉強するべきだ。" :en "From the teacher's point of view, you should study every day."} {:ja "子供たちから言わせれば、もっと遊ぶ時間がほしい。" :en "From the children's perspective, they want more time to play."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-022" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun + ぐるみ" :reading "〜gurumi" :meaning "Describes 'entirely', 'all together', or 'as a whole group'." :short "Describes 'entirely', 'all together', or 'as a whole group'." :long "「Noun + ぐるみ」emphasizes that an action or state involves the entire group or everything related to that noun. It can mean 'altogether', 'as a whole', or 'with everyone/everything included.'" :formation "Noun + ぐるみ" :examples [{:ja "家族ぐるみで旅行に行きました。" :en "We went on a trip with the whole family."} {:ja "クラスぐるみで遠足に行きました。" :en "The entire class went on an excursion."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-023" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun こそあれ" :reading "~koso are" :meaning "Expresses 'though there is ... (some negative or burdensome aspect), still ...'." :short "Expresses 'though there is ... (some negative or burdensome aspect), still ...'." :long "「Noun こそあれ」is a somewhat formal expression meaning 'Though there is X, (still) Y'. It acknowledges one (often negative) aspect while asserting that it does not prevent or change another fact or outcome." :formation "Noun + こそあれ + (result/continuation)" :examples [{:ja "問題こそあれ、プロジェクトは順調に進んでいます。" :en "Though there are problems, the project is progressing smoothly."} {:ja "多少のリスクこそあれ、挑戦する価値はある。" :en "Though there is some risk, it's worth taking on the challenge."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-024" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun こそ～が" :reading "~koso~ga" :meaning "Emphasizes that 'it is precisely Noun that ...', then adds a contrasting or additional point." :short "Emphasizes that 'it is precisely Noun that ...', then adds a contrasting or additional point." :long "「Noun こそ～が」highlights the importance or uniqueness of the noun. It often translates as 'Yes, Noun is indeed ... , but ...'. The 'が' introduces a second, sometimes contrasting statement." :formation "Noun + こそ + (statement) + が + (contrasting/additional statement)" :examples [{:ja "お金こそ必要だが、それだけが人生のすべてではない。" :en "Money is indeed necessary, but it isn't everything in life."} {:ja "努力こそ大事だが、結果も見逃せない。" :en "Effort is certainly crucial, but results also matter."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-025" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun こそすれ" :reading "~koso sure" :meaning "An emphatic pattern meaning 'it may be X, but definitely not Y.' Often pairs with a negative statement." :short "An emphatic pattern meaning 'it may be X, but definitely not Y.' Often pairs with a negative statement." :long "「Noun/Verb-ます形 + こそすれ」emphasizes that while one aspect (X) might exist or be true, another aspect (Y) absolutely does not. It's frequently used with negative expressions (e.g., 〜ない、〜できない) to strongly deny the second possibility." :formation "Noun + こそすれ + (negative or contrasting statement)" :examples [{:ja "感謝こそすれ、非難などできません。" :en "I might feel grateful, but I could never criticize."} {:ja "忙しさこそすれ、退屈することはありません。" :en "I may be busy, but I'm never bored."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-026" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun ごとき / Noun ごとく" :reading "〜gotoki/〜gotoku" :meaning "Means 'like a...', 'as if...', expressing a simile or metaphor." :short "Means 'like a...', 'as if...', expressing a simile or metaphor." :long "「〜ごとき／〜ごとく」conveys similarity, as if comparing one thing to another. ごとき often modifies nouns, while ごとく can modify verbs or adjectives as well. It's more formal and literary in tone than simple expressions like みたい or よう." :formation "Noun + ごとき, Noun + ごとく, Verb-casual + ごとく, い-Adjective + ごとく" :examples [{:ja "彼は天使のごとく純粋だ。" :en "He is as pure as an angel."} {:ja "悪魔のごとき行為をするな。" :en "Don't engage in acts like those of a devil."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-027" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun じゃあるまいし" :reading "~ja aru mai shi" :meaning "Expressing that something 'is not'; 'it's not like...'. Colloquial/masculine form of ではあるまいし." :short "Expressing that something 'is not'; 'it's not like...'. Colloquial/masculine form of ではあるまいし." :long "The phrase 「Noun (or Na-Adjective) + じゃあるまいし」 is a somewhat casual, sometimes masculine-sounding expression meaning 'it's not like...', 'it's not as if...', or 'there's no need to...' in certain contexts. It dismisses the idea that the noun or adjective is the reason or cause for something." :formation "Noun or Na-Adjective + じゃあるまいし" :examples [{:ja "彼は子供じゃあるまいし、その問題をしっかりと解決するべきだ。" :en "He’s not a child, so he should solve that problem properly."} {:ja "急ぐ必要はない、時間じゃあるまいし。" :en "We don’t need to hurry; it’s not like we’re short of time."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-028" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun ずくめ" :reading "~zukume" :meaning "Denotes 'entirely' or 'nothing but' the preceding noun." :short "Denotes 'entirely' or 'nothing but' the preceding noun." :long "「Noun + ずくめ」expresses that something consists entirely of that noun or is 'nothing but' that noun. It describes a situation with a predominance of one thing." :formation "Noun + ずくめ" :examples [{:ja "彼は最近、失敗ずくめだ。" :en "He has had nothing but failures recently."} {:ja "この冬は雪ずくめで大変だ。" :en "This winter has been nothing but snow, which is tough."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-029" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun だけではすまない" :reading "Noun dake dewa sumanai" :meaning "Expresses 'it can't be settled with just...', 'cannot be resolved merely by...'." :short "Expresses 'it can't be settled with just...', 'cannot be resolved merely by...'." :long "「Noun + だけではすまない」indicates that simply doing or providing the noun is not sufficient to resolve or finish a matter. Something more is required." :formation "Noun + だけではすまない" :examples [{:ja "この問題は謝罪だけではすまない。" :en "This problem cannot be settled with just an apology."} {:ja "彼女が怒っているから、お菓子をあげただけではすまないよ。" :en "She’s angry, so just giving her sweets won't fix it."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-030" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun たりとも～ない" :reading "~tari tomo ~nai" :meaning "Used to show a strong negative: 'not even ~', 'not a single ~'." :short "Used to show a strong negative: 'not even ~', 'not a single ~'." :long "「Noun + たりとも + Negative Verb」expresses a strong negation such as 'not even one', 'not even once', or 'none at all'." :formation "Noun + たりとも + Negative Verb" :examples [{:ja "一分たりとも無駄にするな。" :en "Don’t waste even a single minute."} {:ja "彼は一言たりとも話さない。" :en "He doesn't speak even a single word."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-031" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun たる Noun" :reading "~taru~" :meaning "Indicates 'being in the position/state of', used for roles or statuses." :short "Indicates 'being in the position/state of', used for roles or statuses." :long "「Noun1 たる Noun2」expresses that the second noun is in the 'role/state/condition' of the first. It often has a formal or literary tone, emphasizing that someone/something rightly holds or embodies that status." :formation "Noun1 + たる + Noun2" :examples [{:ja "リーダーたる者は、常に先を見て行動すべきだ。" :en "A person who is a leader should always look ahead and act accordingly."} {:ja "教師たる者、学生に模範を示すべきである。" :en "Someone who is a teacher should set a good example for the students."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-032" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun ですら" :reading "~desura" :meaning "Means 'even', 'as much as', used for emphasis in formal contexts." :short "Means 'even', 'as much as', used for emphasis in formal contexts." :long "「Noun + ですら」emphasizes 'even' in a formal or literary tone. It can carry nuance similar to さえ or すら but is used more in writing or formal speech." :formation "Noun + ですら" :examples [{:ja "明日試験があるのに、彼はテキストですら読まない。" :en "He doesn’t even read the textbook, despite having a test tomorrow."} {:ja "この問題は専門家ですら解けない。" :en "Even an expert can’t solve this problem."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-033" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun でなくてなんだろう" :reading "〜de nakute nandarou" :meaning "A strong rhetorical question: 'If not X, then what is it?'" :short "A strong rhetorical question: 'If not X, then what is it?'" :long "「Noun + でなくてなんだろう」expresses the speaker’s strong conviction that something must be that noun—no other possibility. Literally, 'If it isn't Noun, then what is it?'" :formation "Noun + でなくてなんだろう" :examples [{:ja "これが真実でなくてなんだろう。" :en "If this isn’t the truth, then what is?"} {:ja "彼がリーダーでなくてなんだろう。" :en "If he isn’t the leader, then who is?"}]} {:id "grammar-n1-034" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun ではあるまいし" :reading "~dewa aru maishi" :meaning "Means 'it’s not as if...', 'it’s not that...', more formal than じゃあるまいし." :short "Means 'it’s not as if...', 'it’s not that...', more formal than じゃあるまいし." :long "「Noun + ではあるまいし」indicates that the noun is not the situation or reason being implied. It dismisses a possibility or justification, e.g. 'It’s not as if X, so don’t worry/do that/etc.' This is the more formal counterpart to じゃあるまいし." :formation "Noun + ではあるまいし" :examples [{:ja "彼女はプロの歌手ではあるまいし、完璧を求めるのは酷だ。" :en "She’s not a professional singer, so it’s rather harsh to demand perfection."} {:ja "彼は子供ではあるまいし、そんなにチョコレートばかり食べるのはどうかと思う。" :en "He’s not a child, so I’m not sure about him eating that much chocolate."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-035" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun と Noun を兼ねて" :reading "Noun to Noun o kanete" :meaning "Used to indicate doing two things at once or fulfilling two roles/purposes." :short "Used to indicate doing two things at once or fulfilling two roles/purposes." :long "「Noun と Noun を兼ねて」means that one action or thing serves the purpose of both nouns. It can be translated as 'serving as both… and…', or 'for both… and…'." :formation "Noun + と + Noun + を兼ねて" :examples [{:ja "お父さんは社長と経理部長を兼ねています。" :en "My father serves as both the company president and the head of accounting."} {:ja "彼女は音楽教師とピアニストを兼ねています。" :en "She works as both a music teacher and a pianist."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-036" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun といい Noun といい" :reading "〜to ii〜to ii" :meaning "Used to express 'both A and B'; 'whether it's A or B'; 'not to mention A and also B'." :short "Used to express 'both A and B'; 'whether it's A or B'; 'not to mention A and also B'." :long "This grammar structure is used to provide two examples or two situations to demonstrate a certain characteristic or situation. It's a way of mentioning two items of the same theme to generalize or emphasize something applicable to them both. In English, it can be understood as 'both A and B', 'whether it's A or B', or 'not to mention A and also B'." :formation "Noun1 + といい + Noun2 + といい + Comment" :examples [{:ja "日本の食べ物といい観光地といい、すべてが素晴らしいです。" :en "Both Japanese food and tourist spots are all wonderful."} {:ja "彼の優しさといい、体力といい、彼は理想的なパートナーだ。" :en "Whether it's his kindness or stamina, he is an ideal partner."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-037" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun という Noun" :reading "~to iu~" :meaning "Used to specify or define one noun with another; 'the noun called', 'the noun that is', or 'a certain'." :short "Used to specify or define one noun with another; 'the noun called', 'the noun that is', or 'a certain'." :long "The Noun という Noun construction is used to specify or define one noun with another. It usually translates as 'the noun called', 'the noun that is' or simply 'a certain'. It is a very common pattern for clarifying what something is named or known as." :formation "Noun1 という Noun2" :examples [{:ja "宮崎駿という人は有名なアニメーション監督です。" :en "The person called Hayao Miyazaki is a famous animation director."} {:ja "その恋人という女性は彼にとても厳しいです。" :en "The woman called his girlfriend is very strict with him."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-038" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun というところだ" :reading "Noun to iu tokoro da" :meaning "Means 'it's approximately...' or 'it's about...'. Used to show approximation or a general assessment." :short "Means 'it's approximately...' or 'it's about...'. Used to show approximation or a general assessment." :long "The というところだ grammar point is used to show an approximation or general assessment of something. It is often translated as 'it's approximately...', 'around...', or 'it's about...'." :formation "Noun + というところだ" :examples [{:ja "今回の試験の合格者は半数というところだろう。" :en "The passers in this exam would be approximately half."} {:ja "彼の力は80％というところだ。" :en "His strength is about 80%."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-039" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun + というもの" :reading "~ to iu mono" :meaning "Used to express a generic concept: 'all ~', 'any ~ of that kind'." :short "Used to express a generic concept: 'all ~', 'any ~ of that kind'." :long "The grammar pattern 'Noun + というもの' refers to the entirety or general nature of that noun—often implying 'all of this category' or 'this thing called...'. It can also highlight a universal truth about the noun mentioned." :formation "Noun + というもの" :examples [{:ja "子供というものは、毎日たくさん遊びます。" :en "Children, as a group, play a lot every day."} {:ja "大学生というものは研究に時間をたくさんかけます。" :en "University students spend a lot of time on research."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-040" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun といったところだ" :reading "Noun to itta tokoro da" :meaning "Expresses 'at most', 'as much as', or 'limited to'." :short "Expresses 'at most', 'as much as', or 'limited to'." :long "Noun といったところだ describes an upper limit or a maximum extent, often translating to 'at most' or 'around...'. It suggests the speaker doesn’t expect anything beyond that limit." :formation "Noun + といったところだ" :examples [{:ja "彼の英語レベルは初級といったところだ。" :en "His English level is at most beginner level."} {:ja "私の日本語の語彙は1000語といったところだ。" :en "My Japanese vocabulary is limited to about 1000 words."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-041" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun といわず Noun といわず" :reading "A to iwazu B to iwazu" :meaning "Expresses 'both A and B' or 'be it A or B'; applies to both without distinction." :short "Expresses 'both A and B' or 'be it A or B'; applies to both without distinction." :long "The grammar pattern AといわずBといわず means 'be it A or B' or 'both A and B.' It emphasizes that something applies widely, without distinguishing between A and B." :formation "Noun1 + といわず + Noun2 + といわず + (rest of the sentence)" :examples [{:ja "冬といわず夏といわず、彼はいつもアイスクリームを食べます。" :en "Be it winter or summer, he always eats ice cream."} {:ja "老いといわず若きといわず、全ての人がそのイベントを楽しめます。" :en "Whether old or young, everyone can enjoy that event."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-042" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun + ときたら" :reading "〜tokitara" :meaning "Expresses strong feelings about a topic; 'speaking of', 'when it comes to'." :short "Expresses strong feelings about a topic; 'speaking of', 'when it comes to'." :long "Noun + ときたら emphasizes strong feelings—often frustration, admiration, or surprise—about that noun. It can translate as 'when it comes to...', 'speaking of...', or 'honestly, about...'." :formation "Noun + ときたら" :examples [{:ja "寿司ときたら、彼は何も食べられない。" :en "When it comes to sushi, he can't eat anything."} {:ja "数学の問題ときたら、彼女はいつも困っている。" :en "When it comes to math problems, she's always struggling."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-043" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun とは比べものにならない" :reading "~to wa kurabemono ni naranai" :meaning "Indicates something is not comparable; 'beyond comparison'." :short "Indicates something is not comparable; 'beyond comparison'." :long "Use とは比べものにならない to emphasize that two things differ so greatly they cannot be compared—one is vastly superior or different in scale, quality, etc." :formation "Noun + とは比べものにならない" :examples [{:ja "新しい車の速さは古い車とは比べものにならない。" :en "The speed of the new car is not comparable to the old one."} {:ja "彼の英語のうまさは私とは比べものにならない。" :en "His English skills are beyond comparison with mine."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-044" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun + ともあろう + Noun" :reading "~tomoarou~" :meaning "Used to criticize or question unbecoming behavior by someone of a certain status." :short "Used to criticize or question unbecoming behavior by someone of a certain status." :long "Noun + ともあろう + Noun is used to express that someone with a certain role or status has done something unbefitting their position. It often carries disapproval or surprise: 'For someone who is supposed to be X, how could they...?'" :formation "Noun + ともあろう + Noun" :examples [{:ja "先生ともあろう者が、そんな間違いをするなんて。" :en "For a teacher, it's shocking to make such a mistake."} {:ja "大人ともあろう者が子供みたいにふるまってはいけない。" :en "It's not right for an adult to behave like a child."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-045" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun ともなると" :reading "〜to mo naru to" :meaning "'When it comes to...', emphasizes a change of situation or status." :short "'When it comes to...', emphasizes a change of situation or status." :long "Noun + ともなると indicates a change of state or circumstances once that noun becomes relevant or once one attains that position. It conveys that 'once it’s X, things change' or 'when it comes to X...'." :formation "Noun + ともなると" :examples [{:ja "クリスマスともなると、街中がきらきらと輝いて見えます。" :en "When it comes to Christmas time, the whole city sparkles."} {:ja "子供が自分の過ちを認めるともなると、話は別ですね。" :en "When a child admits their mistake, that’s a different story."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-046" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun ともなれば" :reading "〜to mo nareba" :meaning "Expresses 'if it comes to', 'when', or 'once'." :short "Expresses 'if it comes to', 'when', or 'once'." :long "The grammar point ～ともなれば is used to stress the likelihood of a change or consequence that comes about due to the situation expressed before ともなれば. It can be translated as 'when it comes to', 'once', or 'if it comes to' in English. It's usually used with nouns." :formation "Noun + ともなれば" :examples [{:ja "試験ともなれば、大抵の人が緊張するものです。" :en "When it comes to exams, most people get nervous."} {:ja "彼がリーダーともなれば、チームの性能はさらに向上するでしょう。" :en "If he becomes the leader, the team's performance will improve even more."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-047" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun と相まって" :reading "~ to aimatte" :meaning "Used to express 'in combination with', 'together with', 'along with'." :short "Used to express 'in combination with', 'together with', 'along with'." :long "The Noun と相まって grammar point is used to indicate that something happens or is the result of two or more factors working together. It can be translated as 'in combination with', 'together with' or 'along with' in English. It is used with nouns." :formation "Noun + と相まって" :examples [{:ja "天気の良さと相まって、ピクニックは大成功でした。" :en "In combination with the good weather, the picnic was a great success."} {:ja "努力と運と相まって、彼は試験に合格しました。" :en "Thanks to both effort and luck, he passed the exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-048" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun なくして～はない" :reading "Noun nakushite ~ wa nai" :meaning "Expresses 'without ~, there can't be ~'; 'can't do ~ without first doing ~'." :short "Expresses 'without ~, there can't be ~'; 'can't do ~ without first doing ~'." :long "The なくして grammar point is used to indicate that something is indispensable or necessary for something else to occur. 'Without' the first noun, the second part 'cannot exist/occur'. It demonstrates the principle of causality or necessity." :formation "Noun + なくして + Sentence ～はない" :examples [{:ja "努力なくして成功はない。" :en "There is no success without effort."} {:ja "時間なくして完璧な作品は作れない。" :en "You cannot create a perfect work without time."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-049" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun なしでは～ない" :reading "Noun nashi de wa ~nai" :meaning "Expresses the necessity of something; 'cannot do without', 'not possible without'." :short "Expresses the necessity of something; 'cannot do without', 'not possible without'." :long "Noun なしでは～ない is used to emphasize that something is indispensable. It can be translated as 'cannot do without' or 'not possible without.'" :formation "Noun + なしでは～ない" :examples [{:ja "水なしでは生きられない。" :en "We cannot live without water."} {:ja "彼の助けなしでは、この問題を解決することはできない。" :en "Without his help, I cannot solve this problem."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-050" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun なしには～ない" :reading "Noun nashi ni wa ~nai" :meaning "Means 'without', 'cannot do without', 'not possible without'." :short "Means 'without', 'cannot do without', 'not possible without'." :long "The なしには～ない grammar point is used to express that without the mentioned noun, something is not possible. It can be translated as 'cannot (do) without ~', or 'not possible without ~'." :formation "Noun + なしには + Verb-negative" :examples [{:ja "お金なしには生活ができない。" :en "I cannot get by without money."} {:ja "愛情なしには子育てができない。" :en "You cannot raise a child without love."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-051" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun ならいざ知らず" :reading "~nara izashirazu" :meaning "Expresses 'maybe X is possible, but Y is definitely not'. Often 'let alone...'." :short "Expresses 'maybe X is possible, but Y is definitely not'. Often 'let alone...'." :long "「Xならいざ知らず、Yは…」 emphasizes that while X might be possible or acceptable, Y is out of the question or even more difficult. It can be translated as 'I could understand X, but Y is impossible' or 'Maybe X, but definitely not Y.'" :formation "Noun1 + ならいざ知らず + Noun2 (comment)" :examples [{:ja "数学ならいざ知らず、物理なんて全然わからない。" :en "Math might be manageable, but as for physics, I'm completely clueless."} {:ja "5キロならいざ知らず、10キロなんて走れません。" :en "Maybe I could run 5 kilometers, but 10 kilometers is out of the question."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-052" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun + ならでは" :reading "~nara de wa" :meaning "Expresses something unique or characteristic only of that noun; 'only found in', 'only possible with'." :short "Expresses something unique or characteristic only of that noun; 'only found in', 'only possible with'." :long "The pattern Noun + ならでは highlights something that is unique or distinctive to that noun, suggesting that only this place, person, or thing could possess or create it." :formation "Noun + ならでは" :examples [{:ja "これは日本ならではの風景ですね。" :en "This is a landscape you can only find in Japan."} {:ja "新鮮な魚はこの町ならではの特産品です。" :en "Fresh fish is a specialty unique to this town."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-053" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun なり Noun なり" :reading "A nari B nari" :meaning "Lists alternatives of the same kind; 'either A or B', 'A or B, for example'." :short "Lists alternatives of the same kind; 'either A or B', 'A or B, for example'." :long "The pattern AなりBなり is used to suggest alternatives or options, usually of the same category. It can be translated as 'whether A or B' or 'A or B, for example'." :formation "Noun + なり + Noun + なり" :examples [{:ja "映画なり、音楽なり、何か趣味はありますか。" :en "Do you have any hobbies, such as movies or music?"} {:ja "紅茶なり、コーヒーなり、何か飲み物をください。" :en "Please give me something to drink, like tea or coffee."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-054" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun なりとも" :reading "~nari tomo" :meaning "Indicates even a small amount or degree; 'at least', 'even a little'." :short "Indicates even a small amount or degree; 'at least', 'even a little'." :long "Noun + なりとも is used to show that even a small amount or degree is valuable or needed. It translates as 'at least' or 'even a little' in English." :formation "Noun + なりとも" :examples [{:ja "時間が限られているので、一時間なりとも練習すべきだ。" :en "Since time is limited, you should practice for at least an hour."} {:ja "彼には一言なりとも謝罪の言葉を聞きたい。" :en "I’d like to hear at least a word of apology from him."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-055" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun に Noun を重ねて" :reading "A ni B wo kasanete" :meaning "This expression is used to describe repeated actions or states, 'one after another' or 'pile up'." :short "This expression is used to describe repeated actions or states, 'one after another' or 'pile up'." :long "The structure Noun に Noun を重ねて is used to indicate the repetition of actions or states. In English, this could be translated as 'one after another' or 'pile up'. It's typically used to emphasize a great deal of continuation or accumulation of something." :formation "Noun に Noun を重ねて" :examples [{:ja "彼女は失敗に失敗を重ねて、とうとう会社を辞めた。" :en "She piled up failures upon failures and finally quit the company."} {:ja "仕事に仕事を重ねて、彼は疲れ果てた。" :en "He piled up work upon work and was completely exhausted."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-056" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun にあっては" :reading "Noun ni atte ha" :meaning "Indicates a specific situation or environment in which something happens or is the case." :short "Indicates a specific situation or environment in which something happens or is the case." :long "The にあっては grammar point is used after a noun that indicates a specific situation or environment. It expresses the context in which an event takes place, or the circumstances that pertain to the information to be explained or highlighted. It can be translated as 'in', 'at' or 'under' in English." :formation "Noun + にあっては" :examples [{:ja "戦争時にあっては、すべての人々が困難に直面していました。" :en "In wartime, all people were facing difficulties."} {:ja "経済危機にあっては、多くの企業が倒産の危機に瀕している。" :en "In the economic crisis, many companies are on the verge of bankruptcy."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-057" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun にあるまじき Noun" :reading "Noun ni aru majiki Noun" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'unfitting', 'unbecoming' or 'inappropriate'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'unfitting', 'unbecoming' or 'inappropriate'." :long "The grammar point Noun にあるまじき Noun is used to express that the noun doesn't fit or is inappropriate in some way for the other noun. It can translate loosely as 'unfitting for', 'unbecoming of' or 'something that must not be for a/ the noun'." :formation "Noun1 にあるまじき Noun2" :examples [{:ja "彼は教師にあるまじき言動をとった。" :en "He took actions that are unbecoming of a teacher."} {:ja "これは人間にあるまじき行為だ。" :en "This is an act that is unfitting for a human."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-058" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun にして" :reading "Noun ni shite" :meaning "Expresses the idea 'even for', 'even though', or 'in spite of'." :short "Expresses the idea 'even for', 'even though', or 'in spite of'." :long "The grammar point Noun にして expresses a sense of surprise, disbelief or unexpectedness in response to a situation or circumstance. Generally, it is used when the speaker wants to convey that something is unexpected even for someone (noun) or in their situation." :formation "Noun + にして" :examples [{:ja "彼は医者にしてその基本的な知識がないのが信じられない。" :en "I can't believe that even as a doctor, he doesn't have that basic knowledge."} {:ja "彼は経験豊富な運転手にして、事故を起こした。" :en "Even as an experienced driver, he had an accident."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-059" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun にして初めて" :reading "Noun nishite hajimete" :meaning "Expresses 'only when/after... for the first time'." :short "Expresses 'only when/after... for the first time'." :long "The phrase Noun にして初めて is used to express a sense of realization or experience that only came about or was felt after reaching that particular stage or status. In English, it could be translated as 'only when/after... for the first time'." :formation "Noun + にして初めて" :examples [{:ja "大人にして初めて、親の苦労が分かった。" :en "Only when I became an adult did I understand my parents' struggles for the first time."} {:ja "彼は40歳にして初めて、海外旅行を経験した。" :en "Only when he turned 40 did he travel abroad for the first time."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-060" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun にすら" :reading "〜ni sura" :meaning "Expresses the concept of 'even'; 'even in', 'even as', 'even to'." :short "Expresses the concept of 'even'; 'even in', 'even as', 'even to'." :long "The 〜にすら grammar point is used to show surprise that an action or situation applies even to the noun in question. It can be translated into English as 'even in', 'even as', or 'even to'. It's often used with negative or unexpected statements, emphasizing that 'even' in this particular case something can't or doesn't happen." :formation "Noun + にすら" :examples [{:ja "彼にすらその問題は解けなかった。" :en "Even he could not solve that problem."} {:ja "彼女はテストにすら合格しなかった。" :en "She did not even pass the test."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-061" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun にとどまらず～も" :reading "~ ni todomarazu ~ mo" :meaning "This expresses the idea of 'not only...but also', 'not stopping at...'." :short "This expresses the idea of 'not only...but also', 'not stopping at...'." :long "This grammar point is used to express that something is not limited to just one thing (the thing before にとどまらず), but also includes other things (the thing after も). It can be translated as 'not only... but also...' or 'not stopping at...'." :formation "Noun + にとどまらず + Noun + も" :examples [{:ja "このアプリは、日本語にとどまらず、他の言語もサポートしています。" :en "This app not only supports Japanese, but also other languages."} {:ja "彼は、音楽にとどまらず、映画製作にも興味がある。" :en "He is not only interested in music, but also in filmmaking."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-062" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun にひきかえ Noun は" :reading "~ni hikikae ~ wa" :meaning "Represents 'in contrast to', 'as opposed to'." :short "Represents 'in contrast to', 'as opposed to'." :long "The grammar point Noun にひきかえ Noun は is used in Japanese to express contrast or opposition between two nouns. It translates to 'as opposed to' or 'in contrast to' in English. The first noun represents what is being contrasted against and the second noun represents the main subject." :formation "Noun 1 + にひきかえ + Noun 2 + は" :examples [{:ja "彼の親切さにひきかえ、彼女は無感情だ。" :en "In contrast to his kindness, she is emotionless."} {:ja "夏にひきかえ、冬は雪で寒い。" :en "As opposed to summer, winter is cold with snow."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-063" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun にもまして" :reading "〜ni mo mashite" :meaning "This expresses 'Even more than; exceeding'." :short "This expresses 'Even more than; exceeding'." :long "The Nounにもまして grammar point is used to express that something is even more or exceeds the noun that it’s being compared to. It translates to expressions like 'even more than' or 'exceeding' in English. It is used when making a comparison to emphasize the greater degree of something." :formation "Noun + にもまして" :examples [{:ja "私は彼にもまして、音楽を愛しています。" :en "I love music even more than he does."} {:ja "彼女は年齢にもまして、経験豊富です。" :en "She has even more experience than her age would suggest."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-064" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun によらず" :reading "～ni yorazu" :meaning "Regardless of, irrespective of." :short "Regardless of, irrespective of." :long "The ～によらず grammar point is used to express the meaning 'regardless of' or 'irrespective of' in English. It is used when the outcome does not change regardless of differences in conditions." :formation "Noun + によらず" :examples [{:ja "結果は勉強時間によらず変わらないでしょう。" :en "The result will not change regardless of the study time."} {:ja "季節によらず、この地域は常に暖かいです。" :en "This region is always warm, regardless of the season."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-065" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun に先駆けて" :reading "〜ni saki gakete" :meaning "Meaning 'ahead of', 'before', or 'preceding in time'." :short "Meaning 'ahead of', 'before', or 'preceding in time'." :long "The grammar point 〜に先駆けて indicates something happening ahead or before something else in terms of time. Typically used in written language or formal speech." :formation "Noun + に先駆けて" :examples [{:ja "この映画は日本に先駆けて、アメリカで公開されました。" :en "This film was released in America ahead of Japan."} {:ja "彼は周りの人々に先駆けて、その発表を聞いた。" :en "He heard the announcement before the people around him."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-066" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun に即した Noun" :reading "A ni sokushita B" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'in accordance with', 'based on'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'in accordance with', 'based on'." :long "The grammar point 'Noun に即した Noun' is used to express the idea of something being done in accordance with or based on something else. Usually, the first noun is principle, rule, reality, etc. and the second noun could be action, method, plan, etc." :formation "Noun1 + に即した + Noun2" :examples [{:ja "社会のニーズに即した教育改革が必要だ。" :en "We need an educational reform that is in accordance with the needs of society."} {:ja "国際法に即した対応をとるべきだ。" :en "We should respond in accordance with international law."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-067" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun に即して Verb" :reading "〜ni soku shite ~" :meaning "The grammar point Noun に即して Verb is used to mean 'to act in accordance with', 'in line with' or 'based on'." :short "The grammar point Noun に即して Verb is used to mean 'to act in accordance with', 'in line with' or 'based on'." :long "The formation Noun に即して Verb is used when someone is doing something based on or in accordance with some norms, rules, decisions or principles. This structure is often used in business or official contexts." :formation "Noun + に即して + Verb" :examples [{:ja "会社のポリシーに即して行動しましょう。" :en "Let's act in accordance with the company's policy."} {:ja "法律に即して判断を下すことが重要です。" :en "It's important to make judgments in accordance with the law."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-068" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun に言わせれば" :reading "Noun ni iwasereba" :meaning "Expresses 'according to', 'if you ask', 'in the opinion of'." :short "Expresses 'according to', 'if you ask', 'in the opinion of'." :long "The に言わせれば grammar point is used when we want to express someone's opinion or viewpoint. It can be translated as 'according to', 'if you ask', or 'in the opinion of'. This form is mostly used in formal or written language." :formation "Noun + に言わせれば" :examples [{:ja "先生に言わせれば、毎日勉強することが重要だと言う。" :en "According to the teacher, it's important to study every day."} {:ja "彼に言わせれば、早く結婚する方がいいと思っている。" :en "If you ask him, he thinks it's better to get married early."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-069" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun に限ったことではない" :reading "〜ni kagitta koto dewa nai" :meaning "Indicates that something is not only limited to; 'not only', 'not just'." :short "Indicates that something is not only limited to; 'not only', 'not just'." :long "This grammar point is used to express that the statement is not only limited to the noun mentioned before it. In English, it can be translated as 'not only', 'not just', or sometimes 'not necessarily'. It underscores that what is being said is also true for other things or situations, not just for the specific noun stated." :formation "Noun + に限ったことではない" :examples [{:ja "この問題は日本に限ったことではない。" :en "This issue is not only limited to Japan."} {:ja "英語はアメリカに限ったことではない。" :en "English is not just limited to America."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-070" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun に限ったことでもない" :reading "~ni kagitta koto demo nai" :meaning "'Not limited to'; 'not just', 'not exclusively'." :short "'Not limited to'; 'not just', 'not exclusively'." :long "The grammar point 'Noun に限ったことでもない' typically expresses that something is not only limited to a specific instance or category. It insinuates that there are also other instances or categories involved. In English, it can be translated as 'not limited to', 'not just', or 'not exclusively'." :formation "Noun + に限ったことでもない" :examples [{:ja "この試験が難しいのは、数学に限ったことでもない。" :en "The exam being difficult is not limited to just math."} {:ja "彼女が可愛いのは顔だけに限ったことでもない。性格も好きだ。" :en "Her being cute is not just limited to her face. I also like her personality."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-071" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun に限る" :reading "~ni kagiru" :meaning "Expresses the idea that 'nothing but', 'nothing is better than'." :short "Expresses the idea that 'nothing but', 'nothing is better than'." :long "The noun に限る grammar point is used to express the idea that there is nothing better than the noun mentioned in the specific context. It is often used to give personal opinions, advice, or recommendations." :formation "Noun + に限る" :examples [{:ja "こういう時には家族に話すに限る。" :en "At times like this, there's nothing better than talking to your family."} {:ja "寒い冬には温かいお茶に限る。" :en "In cold winter, there's nothing better than warm tea."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-072" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun ぬいた Noun" :reading "A nuita B" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'taking something out from something else'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'taking something out from something else'." :long "This grammar point is used to denote the action of removing or taking out one thing from another. It is usually used with nouns." :formation "Noun1 + をぬいた + Noun2" :examples [{:ja "このサラダはにんじんをぬいたサラダです。" :en "This is a salad with the carrots removed."} {:ja "彼はコーヒーから砂糖をぬいた。" :en "He removed the sugar from the coffee."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-073" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun + ぬいて（~nuite)" :reading "" :meaning "This is a way to express 'minus' or 'except for' some specific noun." :short "This is a way to express 'minus' or 'except for' some specific noun." :long "The grammar point Noun + ぬいて is used when you want to exclude a certain noun from the whole or mention something about everything else except for a specific noun. It's usually translated in English as 'minus' or 'except for'. " :formation "Noun + ぬいて" :examples [{:ja "私たちは、彼ぬいて全員が集まることができました。" :en "We were able to gather everyone except for him."} {:ja "このレストランはお子様メニューをぬいてすべてが美味しい。" :en "Everything at this restaurant is delicious except for the kids' menu."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-074" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display " ～ぬく" :reading "~nuku" :meaning "Expresses the concept of doing something completely or thoroughly." :short "Expresses the concept of doing something completely or thoroughly." :long "'Noun ぬく' is a phrase that conveys the idea of doing an action thoroughly or completely. It demonstrates a determination to carry out a task until the end. While the formation commonly uses verbs, it does convey a meaning similar to the English phrase 'to do completely'." :formation "Verb-stem + ぬく" :examples [{:ja "彼は一日中本を読みぬく。" :en "He will read the book all day long."} {:ja "私はこのプロジェクトを終わらせぬく。" :en "I will finish this project completely."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-075" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun+のいかんだ" :reading "Noun no ikan da" :meaning "Depends on; up to" :short "Depends on; up to" :long "The のいかんだ(Noun no ikan da) grammar point is used to express that something depends on a particular situation or fact. It can be translated as 'Depends on' or 'up to' in English. The construction no ikan is usually used with nouns and naru verbs." :formation "Noun + のいかんだ/Noun + のいかんでは" :examples [{:ja "彼が来るのいかんで決めよう。" :en "Let's decide depending on whether he comes or not."} {:ja "試験の結果のいかんで留年が決まります。" :en "Whether you repeat a year or not depends on the test results."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-076" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun のいかんでは" :reading "Noun no ikan de wa" :meaning "It depends on the Noun" :short "It depends on the Noun" :long "The のいかんでは grammar point is used when you want to express that the outcome or decision hinges on the condition described in the noun preceding this phrase. It can be translated as 'depending on ___' or 'it depends on ___' in English." :formation "Noun + のいかんでは" :examples [{:ja "試験の結果のいかんでは、大学の入学許可を得られるかどうかが決まります。" :en "Whether you can get permission to enter university depends on the result of the exam."} {:ja "天気のいかんでは、ピクニックを延期するかもしれません。" :en "Depending on the weather, we might delay the picnic."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-077" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun のいかんにかかわらず" :reading "Noun no ikan ni kakawarazu" :meaning "Regardless of, irrespective of." :short "Regardless of, irrespective of." :long "The phrase 'Noun のいかんにかかわらず' is used to express that something is the case irrespective of a certain condition. It can be translated as 'regardless of' or 'irrespective of' in English. This phrase is used with nouns." :formation "Noun + のいかんにかかわらず" :examples [{:ja "結果のいかんにかかわらず、彼は決定を後悔しなかった。" :en "Regardless of the outcome, he didn't regret his decision."} {:ja "年齢のいかんにかかわらず、どんな人でも学ぶことは可能です。" :en "Regardless of age, anyone can learn."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-078" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun のいかんによっては" :reading "Noun no ikan ni yotte wa" :meaning "Depending on the matter; depending on noun" :short "Depending on the matter; depending on noun" :long "The phrase のいかんによっては (no ikan ni yotte wa) is used to express the concept of 'depending on the matter or situation'. It is used to indicate that the outcome may change based on the condition or quality of the noun (matter) in question." :formation "Noun + のいかんによっては" :examples [{:ja "天気のいかんによっては、ピクニックを延期しましょう。" :en "Depending on the weather, we should postpone the picnic."} {:ja "試験の結果のいかんによっては、進学できます。" :en "You can go on to further education, depending on the result of the exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-079" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun のいかんによらず" :reading "~ no ikan ni yorazu" :meaning "Means 'regardless of', 'irrespective of', or 'no matter'" :short "Means 'regardless of', 'irrespective of', or 'no matter'" :long "The Noun のいかんによらず grammar point is used to express that something is happening, will occur, or is true, regardless of a particular situation or condition. It is often translated as 'irrespective of', 'regardless of', or 'no matter' in English." :formation "Noun + のいかんによらず" :examples [{:ja "試験の結果のいかんによらず、自分を信じ続けてください。" :en "Regardless of the test results, keep believing in yourself."} {:ja "天気のいかんによらず、毎日、ジョギングをします。" :en "Regardless of the weather, I jog every day."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-080" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun のごとき Noun" :reading "A no gotoki B" :meaning "Expresses 'like' or 'as if'; something/someone resembles something else strongly." :short "Expresses 'like' or 'as if'; something/someone resembles something else strongly." :long "The のごとき pattern is used to convey the concept of 'just like' or 'as if'. The comparison here is strong, indicating that the thing/person is very similar to or almost the same as the thing/person compared to. It is a bit archaic and thus is more often found in writings than in everyday conversation." :formation "Noun1 + のごとき + Noun2" :examples [{:ja "彼は神のごとき存在だ。" :en "He is an existence like a god."} {:ja "彼女の美しさは花のごときものだ。" :en "Her beauty is like a flower."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-081" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun のことだから" :reading "〜no koto dakara" :meaning "Because it is about ~, considering it's ~" :short "Because it is about ~, considering it's ~" :long "The のことだから grammar point is used to express an expectation or decision that is made specifically because of the person or thing mentioned. Usually it implies that the speaker is familiar with the character or typical behavior of the person or thing being talked about." :formation "Noun + のことだから" :examples [{:ja "彼のことだから、きっと大丈夫だと思う。" :en "Because it's him, I think it will certainly be fine."} {:ja "この店のことだから、料理は美味しいはずです。" :en "Considering it's this restaurant, the food should be delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-082" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun の 嫌いがある" :reading "Noun no kirai ga aru" :meaning "Have a dislike for a particular Noun" :short "Have a dislike for a particular Noun" :long "The grammar point 'Nounの嫌いがある' is used to express a dislike for a particular thing, action or situation, indicated by the noun. This structure is somewhat formal or old-fashioned. In casual speech, people more commonly say 'Noun が嫌い'." :formation "Noun + の嫌いがある" :examples [{:ja "彼は魚の嫌いがあるので、寿司を食べません。" :en "He has a dislike for fish, so he doesn't eat sushi."} {:ja "彼女は高いところの嫌いがあるから、山登りはしない。" :en "She has a dislike for high places, so she doesn't go mountain climbing."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-083" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun の手前" :reading "~no temae" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'Before doing something; on the verge of'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'Before doing something; on the verge of'." :long "This grammar point is used to express the idea of being on the verge of doing something, or just before some major action/event. Nouns that are frequently used with this structure often denote an action or event (試験の手前, 選挙の手前, etc.)." :formation "Noun + の手前" :examples [{:ja "試験の手前、遊びに行くのはやめた方がいい。" :en "Just before the exam, it's better to stop going out to play."} {:ja "彼女と別れる手前、思い直しました。" :en "Just before breaking up with her, I changed my mind."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-084" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun の極み" :reading "〜no kiwami" :meaning "Used to express the extreme or utmost point of something." :short "Used to express the extreme or utmost point of something." :long "The の極み grammar point is used to express the highest level or the extreme of something. It often describes an extreme state or condition. The noun before の極み usually indicates some situation, state, or quality (e.g., 美の極み, 感動の極み)." :formation "Noun + の極み" :examples [{:ja "彼の料理の極みは、誰もマネできない。" :en "The pinnacle of his cooking is something nobody can imitate."} {:ja "彼女の努力の極みが、この成功を作り出した。" :en "The extreme of her effort has created this success."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-085" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun の 至り" :reading "~no itari" :meaning "Expresses 'the height of', 'the extreme of' something." :short "Expresses 'the height of', 'the extreme of' something." :long "The 'Nounの至り' grammar point is used to emphasize an extreme or the highest level of something, often an emotional state or an honor. Common nouns include 光栄, 若気, 感激, 恥, etc." :formation "Noun + の至り" :examples [{:ja "彼の冗談には驚きの至りだ。" :en "His jokes are the height of surprise."} {:ja "父がプロジェクトのリーダーになったのは、光栄の至りです。" :en "It is the height of honor that my father became the project leader."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-086" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun はいざ知らず" :reading "~ wa iza shirazu" :meaning "‘I can understand it for ~, but for …? (surprising)’" :short "‘I can understand it for ~, but for …? (surprising)’" :long "The はいざ知らず grammar point is used when contrasting two scenarios: the first being understandable, and the second being unexpected or surprising by comparison. It often translates as “Well, for A, I can understand, but for B…?!”" :formation "Noun + はいざ知らず, Noun + とは/なんて/しかし etc." :examples [{:ja "彼が遅れるのはいざ知らず、彼女まで遅刻するとは意外だ。" :en "Well, I can understand him being late, but even her...? That's surprising."} {:ja "夏はいざ知らず、冬にアイスクリームを食べるのは寒すぎませんか？" :en "I can see eating ice cream in summer, but in winter…isn't it too cold?"}]} {:id "grammar-n1-087" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun はおろか～すら" :reading "Noun wa oroka ～sura" :meaning "Expresses 'not to mention ~, even ~'." :short "Expresses 'not to mention ~, even ~'." :long "The grammar point Noun はおろか～すら is used to emphasize that a particular statement applies not only to the first noun mentioned, but even more so to what comes after すら or さえ. It can be translated as ‘let alone ~, even ~’." :formation "Noun1 はおろか, Noun2 すら/さえ ～" :examples [{:ja "彼は走るはおろか、速く歩くことすらできない。" :en "He can't even walk fast, let alone run."} {:ja "日本語はおろか、英語さえ話しません。" :en "He doesn't even speak English, let alone Japanese."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-088" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun はおろか～まで" :reading "~wa oroka ~made" :meaning "Means 'not just A, but even B'; 'let alone A, even B'." :short "Means 'not just A, but even B'; 'let alone A, even B'." :long "The phrase 'Noun はおろか' emphasizes something beyond the obvious. It can be translated as 'not only ..., but even ...'. The '～まで' part extends that idea further to show an unexpected or more advanced case." :formation "Noun1 + はおろか + Noun2 + まで" :examples [{:ja "彼は英語はおろか、日本語までペラペラなんです。" :en "He is not just fluent in English, but even in Japanese."} {:ja "彼女は数学はおろか、物理まで得意なんです。" :en "She is not just good at math, but even at physics."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-089" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun はおろか～も" :reading "Noun wa oroka ～ mo" :meaning "'Not only...but also', 'not to mention', 'to say nothing of'" :short "'Not only...but also', 'not to mention', 'to say nothing of'" :long "The 'Noun はおろか～も' grammar point is used to list items with an emphasis on how far the situation extends. It often appears in negative contexts to show that something supposedly more advanced (first part) is out of the question, and even something simpler (second part) is also not possible. It can be translated as 'Needless to say X, (he/she) can't even do Y.'" :formation "Noun + はおろか + Noun + も" :examples [{:ja "絵を描くことはおろか、字さえ書けない。" :en "Needless to say drawing pictures, he can’t even write letters."} {:ja "彼は英語はおろか、日本語もわからない。" :en "Needless to say English, he doesn’t even understand Japanese."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-090" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun はさておき" :reading "~ wa sateoki" :meaning "Means 'putting aside', 'aside from', 'notwithstanding'." :short "Means 'putting aside', 'aside from', 'notwithstanding'." :long "The grammar point 'Noun はさておき' is used when highlighting something as an exception from an overall generalization, or when putting aside a particular matter before progressing to another point of discussion. The literal translation would be 'putting Noun aside', 'aside from Noun' or 'notwithstanding Noun.'" :formation "Noun + はさておき" :examples [{:ja "試験の結果はさておき、この学期はとても学ぶことが多かった。" :en "Aside from the exam results, I learned a lot this semester."} {:ja "天候はさておき、明日のピクニックは楽しみにしています。" :en "Putting aside the weather, I am looking forward to tomorrow's picnic."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-091" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun + はどうであれ" :reading "~ wa dou de are" :meaning "This phrase means 'regardless of' or 'no matter how…'." :short "This phrase means 'regardless of' or 'no matter how…'." :long "It is used to convey indifference about the state or condition of a certain thing or matter. You can think of it as 'no matter what' or 'regardless of' in English. The phrase shows the speaker’s strong intention to do something or an assertion that doesn’t change regardless of the noun’s state or condition." :formation "Noun + はどうであれ" :examples [{:ja "天候はどうであれ、試合は行われます。" :en "No matter what the weather is, the match will be held."} {:ja "結果はどうであれ、最善を尽くすだけです。" :en "No matter what the result is, all we can do is try our best."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-092" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun まみれ" :reading "~mamire" :meaning "Expresses a state in which something is entirely covered or filled with something else." :short "Expresses a state in which something is entirely covered or filled with something else." :long "The まみれ grammar point is used to express a state where someone or something is completely covered, smeared, or filled with another thing. The noun before まみれ indicates what the person or thing is covered in. This grammar point often has a negative connotation." :formation "Noun + まみれ" :examples [{:ja "彼は泥まみれになって帰ってきた。" :en "He returned home covered in mud."} {:ja "彼女は涙まみれになりながら告白した。" :en "She confessed while crying her eyes out."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-093" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun もさることながら Noun も" :reading "A mo saru koto nagara B mo" :meaning "Means 'not only... but also'; the second noun is especially emphasized." :short "Means 'not only... but also'; the second noun is especially emphasized." :long "The grammar point 'Noun もさることながら Noun も' is used to express that both Nouns are true, important, or relevant. It can be translated as 'not only... but also' in English. However, among the two nouns, the one coming after もさることながら is more emphasized in comparison to the one before it." :formation "Noun1 + もさることながら + Noun2 + も" :examples [{:ja "彼の歌唱力もさることながら、ダンスも素晴らしい。" :en "Not only his singing ability but also his dancing is wonderful."} {:ja "このレストラン、味もさることながら、サービスもすばらしい。" :en "At this restaurant, not only the taste but also the service is wonderful."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-094" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun も兼ねて" :reading "~mo kanete" :meaning "Used to express 'as well as', 'also', or 'at the same time'." :short "Used to express 'as well as', 'also', or 'at the same time'." :long "The も兼ねて grammar point indicates that two actions or purposes are being served simultaneously. The noun before も兼ねて tells what the additional action or purpose is." :formation "Noun + も兼ねて" :examples [{:ja "散歩も兼ねて、公園に行きました。" :en "I went to the park, also for a walk."} {:ja "今日は日本語の勉強も兼ねて、日本の映画を見ます。" :en "Today, I will watch a Japanese movie, also to study Japanese."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-095" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun も相まって" :reading "~mo aimatte" :meaning "Used to express that combined with something else, it caused a certain outcome." :short "Used to express that combined with something else, it caused a certain outcome." :long "The grammar point Noun も相まって is used to express the meaning that, combined with something else, it caused a certain result. Literally, it translates to 'also contributes to'. It is generally used with nouns and points out that there are multiple contributing factors." :formation "Noun + も相まって" :examples [{:ja "気候も相まって、この地域は農業に適しています。" :en "Combined with the climate, this area is suitable for agriculture."} {:ja "彼の才能も相まって、彼は急速に昇進した。" :en "Combined with his talent, he was rapidly promoted."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-096" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を おいて他に Verb ない" :reading "〜wo oite hoka ni〜nai" :meaning "Means 'apart from ~ there's nothing else,' implying the Noun is the best or most suitable choice." :short "Means 'apart from ~ there's nothing else,' implying the Noun is the best or most suitable choice." :long "The structure Nounをおいて他に〜ない is used to express that there is no substitute for the Noun, no other choice, or that the Noun is the best or the only one to choose among various options. This form is often used in praising or making strong positive comments about someone or something." :formation "Noun + をおいて他に + Verb negative form" :examples [{:ja "この仕事を彼に任せる人をおいて他にいない。" :en "Apart from him, there's no one else to entrust this job to."} {:ja "この問題を解決できるのは彼をおいて他にいない。" :en "Apart from him, there's no one else who can solve this problem."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-097" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun をもって" :reading "~wo motte" :meaning "Used to indicate means, methods, or time; 'with', 'by means of', 'at'." :short "Used to indicate means, methods, or time; 'with', 'by means of', 'at'." :long "The をもって grammar is used to express a means or method to achieve something, or to indicate a point in time. It can be translated as 'with' or 'by means of' when indicating means, and 'at' when indicating a point in time." :formation "Noun + をもって" :examples [{:ja "彼は誠実さをもって人々に信頼されています。" :en "He is trusted by people with his honesty."} {:ja "この賞をもって、彼女の努力が認められました。" :en "With this award, her efforts were recognized."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-098" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun をものともせずに" :reading "Noun wo mono tomo sezu ni" :meaning "Expresses the idea that 'despite something, action was carried out', translating to 'without being fazed by something'." :short "Expresses the idea that 'despite something, action was carried out', translating to 'without being fazed by something'." :long "The 'Nounをものともせずに' grammar point indicates that something which could potentially be a hindrance or challenge was not taken into account when carrying out an action. The literal idea is ‘not making something an obstacle’, meaning actions were performed without being fazed or distracted by it." :formation "Noun + をものともせずに" :examples [{:ja "彼女は怖さをものともせずに、暗闇の中へ進んで行った。" :en "Despite her fear, she went ahead into the darkness."} {:ja "試験のプレッシャーをものともせずに、彼は自信満々に答えた。" :en "Unfazed by the pressure of the exam, he answered with full confidence."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-099" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun をよそに" :reading "~wo yoso ni" :meaning "Despite; Notwithstanding; Regardless of" :short "Despite; Notwithstanding; Regardless of" :long "The Japanese phrase 'Nounをよそに' is used to express a situation where, despite some circumstance (represented by the noun), a certain action takes place. It can convey a feeling of disregard or nonchalance. In English, it can be translated as 'despite', 'notwithstanding', or 'regardless of'." :formation "Noun + をよそに" :examples [{:ja "経済危機をよそに、彼は高級な車を買った。" :en "Despite the economic crisis, he bought a luxury car."} {:ja "試験が迫るをよそに、彼は遊び続けている。" :en "Despite the approaching test, he keeps on playing."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-100" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を余儀なくされる" :reading "Noun wo yogi naku sareru" :meaning "Expresses the idea of being forced into a certain situation, 'to be compelled', 'to be obliged'." :short "Expresses the idea of being forced into a certain situation, 'to be compelled', 'to be obliged'." :long "The grammar pattern 'Noun を余儀なくされる' is used to describe circumstances where a person or party is compelled or obliged to do something due to necessity or unavoidable circumstances. It translates as 'to be forced to', 'to be compelled to', or 'to have no choice but to'. This form is often used in formal or written Japanese." :formation "Noun + を + 余儀なくされる" :examples [{:ja "彼は経済的困難により、退学を余儀なくされた。" :en "Due to financial difficulties, he was forced to drop out of school."} {:ja "厳しい天候によって、私たちは遠足を中止する余儀なくされた。" :en "We were forced to cancel the excursion due to the severe weather."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-101" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を前提として" :reading "Noun wo zentei toshite" :meaning "Used to express 'on the assumption of', 'based on'." :short "Used to express 'on the assumption of', 'based on'." :long "The grammar point 'Noun を前提として' is used to convey that something is done based on or assuming something else. It indicates a preconceived condition or assumption on which the following action or plan relies." :formation "Noun + を前提として" :examples [{:ja "成功を前提として、ビジネスプランを作成しました。" :en "We made a business plan on the assumption of success."} {:ja "彼の勝利を前提として、パーティーの準備を始めました。" :en "We started preparing for the party assuming his victory."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-102" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を前提にして" :reading "Noun wo zentei ni shite" :meaning "Assuming; presupposing; on the assumption of." :short "Assuming; presupposing; on the assumption of." :long "The grammar point 'Noun を前提にして' is used to show that something is done assuming or presupposing the noun that comes before it. In English, it can be translated as 'on the assumption of', 'assuming', or 'presupposing'." :formation "Noun + を前提にして" :examples [{:ja "好天を前提にして、明日のピクニックの計画を立てました。" :en "We planned tomorrow's picnic assuming fine weather."} {:ja "彼女の合格を前提にして、祝いのパーティーの準備を始めました。" :en "We started preparing for the celebration party assuming she would pass the exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-103" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を境にして" :reading "Noun wo sakai ni shite" :meaning "Means 'as a turning point', 'since (time)', or 'from (time) on'." :short "Means 'as a turning point', 'since (time)', or 'from (time) on'." :long "The grammar point 'Noun を境にして' is used to indicate a significant change or turning point marked by a specific event or time. It means that after this boundary, some situation changes or a new state begins." :formation "Noun + を境に(して)" :examples [{:ja "父の死を境にして、彼の性格が一変した。" :en "His personality changed completely since his father's death."} {:ja "試験を境にして、休暇を取る予定です。" :en "I plan to take a vacation after the exam."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-104" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を機にして" :reading "~wo ki ni shite" :meaning "To take the opportunity to; taking the occasion to; on the occasion of." :short "To take the opportunity to; taking the occasion to; on the occasion of." :long "The grammar pattern 'Noun を機にして' expresses the idea of using a certain event or situation as an opportunity to do something else. It is often used when some change or new step is taken as a result of a specific event." :formation "Noun + を機にして" :examples [{:ja "引っ越しを機にして、家具を新しくしました。" :en "I took the opportunity of moving to get new furniture."} {:ja "卒業を機にして、新しい仕事を探し始めた。" :en "I started looking for a new job on the occasion of graduation."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-105" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を皮切りに / を皮切りにして" :reading "Noun wo kawakiri ni / wo kawakiri ni shite" :meaning "Used to indicate the starting point of some series of events or actions; 'starting with', 'beginning with'." :short "Used to indicate the starting point of some series of events or actions; 'starting with', 'beginning with'." :long "The grammar point 'Noun を皮切りに / を皮切りにして' is used to convey the idea of something beginning or starting with a certain event or action. This pattern is often used when the speaker wants to highlight the starting point of a series or string of events." :formation "Noun + を皮切りに / を皮切りにして" :examples [{:ja "彼はその論文を皮切りにして複数の研究を開始した。" :en "Starting with that thesis, he started multiple research projects."} {:ja "彼女はこのコンペティションを皮切りにして彼女のダンスキャリアが飛躍的に成長した。" :en "Starting with this competition, her dance career drastically grew."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-106" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を皮切りにして" :reading "Noun wo kawakiri ni shite" :meaning "Used to express 'Starting with', 'Beginning with'" :short "Used to express 'Starting with', 'Beginning with'" :long "'Noun を皮切りにして' is a Japanese grammatical form used when one wants to express that something begins with a certain event or action, then continues on in a sequence. It is the same as saying 'starting with...' or 'beginning with...' in English." :formation "Noun + を皮切りに (して)" :examples [{:ja "彼はその日を皮切りにして毎日ジムに通い始めました。" :en "Starting from that day, he began to go to the gym every day."} {:ja "新年会を皮切りにして、今年はたくさんのイベントが開催されます。" :en "Starting with the New Year's party, a lot of events will be held this year."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-107" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を禁じ得ない" :reading "〜wo kinjienai" :meaning "'Cannot help but do', or 'inevitable'" :short "'Cannot help but do', or 'inevitable'" :long "The 〜を禁じ得ない (wo kinjienai) grammar point is used to express a situation or emotion that is so strong it can't be helped or is inevitable. It is used with nouns to express an uncontrollable feeling or action." :formation "Noun + を禁じ得ない" :examples [{:ja "この結果には驚きを禁じ得ない。" :en "I can't help but be surprised at this result."} {:ja "そのニュースを聞いて、彼の成功を祝福する気持ちを禁じ得ない。" :en "Hearing that news, I can't help but feel the urge to celebrate his success."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-108" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を経て" :reading "〜wo hete" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'through' or 'via'; going through some process or phase." :short "Expresses the idea of 'through' or 'via'; going through some process or phase." :long "The を経て grammar point is used to express the concept of going through some process, phase, place, or period of time as a means of achieving something or reaching some end point. It can be literally translated as 'through' or 'via' in English, and can be used both literally and metaphorically." :formation "Noun + を経て" :examples [{:ja "彼は苦労を経て成功を手に入れた。" :en "He achieved success through hardships."} {:ja "彼女はニューヨークを経て日本に帰国した。" :en "She returned to Japan via New York."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-109" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を踏まえて" :reading "〜wo fumaete" :meaning "Based on; taking into account; in light of" :short "Based on; taking into account; in light of" :long "The verb 踏まえる (fumaeru) means 'to be based on' or 'to take into consideration'. When ～を踏まえて is connected with a noun, it implies that actions or decisions are made based on or taking into account the noun. It also expresses looking at a situation from the standpoint of the noun before proceeding to the following action." :formation "Noun + を踏まえて" :examples [{:ja "過去の結果を踏まえて、新たな計画を立てました。" :en "Based on previous results, we have made a new plan."} {:ja "現地の文化を踏まえて、プレゼンテーションを準備します。" :en "I will prepare a presentation taking into account the local culture."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-110" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun を限りに" :reading "Noun wo kagiri ni" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'as of', 'with', or 'from', indicating the end of something or a point of change." :short "Expresses the idea of 'as of', 'with', or 'from', indicating the end of something or a point of change." :long "The grammar point Noun を限りに is used to indicate the end point of something or a point of change from that time onward. The noun preceding this expression usually refers to a point in time, signifying that something ends or changes starting at that point." :formation "Noun + を限りに" :examples [{:ja "今年度を限りに、この大学での教授活動を終えるつもりです。" :en "I intend to end my teaching activities at this university as of this academic year."} {:ja "今日を限りに、タバコを吸うのをやめます。" :en "As of today, I will stop smoking."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-111" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun 並み" :reading "~nami" :meaning "Indicates 'like' or 'as' something or someone; 'equivalent to', 'around'." :short "Indicates 'like' or 'as' something or someone; 'equivalent to', 'around'." :long "The Noun 並み grammar point is used to show a comparison, often translated as 'like' or 'equivalent to'. It can also indicate approximate quantity or extent, similar to 'around'." :formation "Noun + 並み" :examples [{:ja "彼はプロのボクサー並みの力を持っています。" :en "He has power equivalent to a professional boxer."} {:ja "彼女の料理の腕前はレストラン並みです。" :en "Her cooking skills are like those of a restaurant."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-112" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Noun + 前提で" :reading "Noun + zentei de" :meaning "Expresses a condition or requirement for something to be established: 'on the assumption that', 'under the condition of'." :short "Expresses a condition or requirement for something to be established: 'on the assumption that', 'under the condition of'." :long "The Noun + 前提で grammar point is used to define the premise, assumption, or requirement necessary for some event or action. It translates to 'on the assumption that', 'under the condition of', or 'with the understanding that'. The following clause is based on that premise." :formation "Noun + 前提で" :examples [{:ja "説明できる英語力がある前提で、この仕事を引き受けてください。" :en "Please take on this job on the assumption that you have enough English skills to explain."} {:ja "彼が一緒に行く前提で、旅行の計画を立てました。" :en "I planned the trip on the assumption that he would go together."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-113" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる / Noun(である) + 限り(は) (kagiri (wa))" :reading "" :meaning "Expresses 'as long as', 'while this condition is true'." :short "Expresses 'as long as', 'while this condition is true'." :long "This grammar is used to express that something holds true as long as a particular condition remains in effect. When using a noun, it can appear as Noun(である) + 限り(は). With a verb, use the dictionary form + 限り(は)." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + 限り(は)\nNoun(である) + 限り(は)" :examples [{:ja "私が生きている限りは、あなたを支えます。" :en "As long as I'm alive, I will support you."} {:ja "子供が学校に通っている限り、私たちは彼らを守ります。" :en "As long as the kids are going to school, we will protect them."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-114" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb がてら" :reading "~ gatera" :meaning "Expresses doing something while doing another; 'while', 'on the occasion of'." :short "Expresses doing something while doing another; 'while', 'on the occasion of'." :long "The ～がてら grammar point is used to express doing something else in the middle of or on the occasion of another activity. Often used with verbs of movement or activities that easily combine with a second action." :formation "Verb (dictionary or -ます stem) + がてら" :examples [{:ja "散歩がてらに郵便局へ行った。" :en "I went to the post office while taking a walk."} {:ja "買い物がてらに友達に会いに行った。" :en "I went to see a friend while doing some shopping."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-115" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb こそすれ" :reading "~koso sure" :meaning "Often used in the pattern 'Xこそすれ、Y〜ない', meaning 'One might do X, but definitely not Y'." :short "Often used in the pattern 'Xこそすれ、Y〜ない', meaning 'One might do X, but definitely not Y'." :long "Verb-ますstem + こそすれ is typically used to emphasize that while someone might certainly do or be X, there's no way they would do or be Y. The sentence that follows is usually a strong negative, denying the possibility of Y." :formation "Verb-ますstem + こそすれ、(絶対に)～ない" :examples [{:ja "彼は友達を助けこそすれ、自分を卑下することはありません。" :en "He might help his friends, but he never belittles himself."} {:ja "彼女は料理こそすれ、掃除はほとんどしない。" :en "She might cook, but she rarely cleans."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-116" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb させられる" :reading "~saserareru" :meaning "Expresses 'being forced to do something' or 'being made to do something'." :short "Expresses 'being forced to do something' or 'being made to do something'." :long "The させられる grammar point is used to express the passive causative form in Japanese. This form is used when you are forced or made to do something by someone or something else. It's a combination of causative and passive form, and it often implies having been made to do something against one's will." :formation "Verb-casual (non-past) + させられる" :examples [{:ja "彼に無理やり飲ませられた。" :en "He forced me to drink against my will."} {:ja "私は父に勉強させられました。" :en "My father made me study."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-117" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ざるを得ない" :reading "~ zaru wo enai" :meaning "'Cannot but', 'have no choice but to', 'forced to'" :short "'Cannot but', 'have no choice but to', 'forced to'" :long "The Verb ざるを得ない construction is used when the subject is compelled or forced to do something out of necessity, leaving no other choice. It conveys the idea of 'cannot but...' or 'have no choice but...' in English." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ざるを得ない (する → せざるを得ない)" :examples [{:ja "この仕事が嫌ならもう辞めざるを得ない。" :en "If you hate this job, you have no choice but to quit."} {:ja "試験が近いので、夜遅くまで勉強せざるを得ない。" :en "The exam is near, so I have no choice but to study late into the night."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-118" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ずじまい" :reading "~zu jimai" :meaning "Expressing regret for not having done something; not being able to; missing the chance to do it." :short "Expressing regret for not having done something; not being able to; missing the chance to do it." :long "The grammar point ～ずじまい (~zu jimai) is used to express regret for not doing something, not being able to do it, or not getting the chance to do it. It highlights an action that ultimately never happened, despite intentions or opportunities." :formation "Verb-ない form (remove ない) + ずじまい" :examples [{:ja "日本へ来たのに、一度も富士山を見ずじまいで帰国してしまった。" :en "I came to Japan but ended up going home without ever getting to see Mt. Fuji."} {:ja "出張のため、彼女に会わずじまいだった。" :en "Because I was on a business trip, I ended up not meeting her."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-119" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ずとも" :reading "〜zu tomo" :meaning "Means 'Even without doing (X), (Y) can still happen'." :short "Means 'Even without doing (X), (Y) can still happen'." :long "The ～ずとも grammar point is used to express that, even if something isn't done, the desired result can still be achieved or the situation remains unaffected. It can be translated as 'even without' or 'even if not'." :formation "Verb-negative stem + ずとも" :examples [{:ja "朝ごはんを食べずとも、働くことができます。" :en "Even without eating breakfast, I can work."} {:ja "車がなくとも、バスで行くことができます。" :en "Even without a car, you can go by bus."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-120" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ずにはおかない" :reading "~zuni wa okanai" :meaning "Expresses 'cannot help but...', 'will definitely...', or 'must do something'." :short "Expresses 'cannot help but...', 'will definitely...', or 'must do something'." :long "The ずにはおかない grammar point conveys the sentiment of an inevitable action or a strong emotional compulsion. It can mean 'I can’t let it pass without doing X' or 'I definitely will do X'." :formation "Verb-ない form (remove ない) + ずにはおかない" :examples [{:ja "彼が困っていると聞いたら、助けずにはおかない。" :en "If I hear he is in trouble, I cannot help but assist him."} {:ja "食事の準備がこのままだと、手伝わずにはおかないだろう。" :en "If the meal prep stays like this, I won't be able to stop myself from helping."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-121" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ずにはすまない" :reading "Verb zuni wa sumanai" :meaning "'Cannot do without...', 'unavoidable', 'have to'." :short "'Cannot do without...', 'unavoidable', 'have to'." :long "The grammar point ずにはすまない is used when something must be done due to social/moral obligation, necessity, or rules. It implies that there is no way around taking that action." :formation "Verb-ない form (remove ない) + ずにはすまない" :examples [{:ja "この問題を解決しないと、社会全体が影響を受けるので、解決せずにはすまない。" :en "Since this issue will affect the entire society if not resolved, we cannot do without solving it."} {:ja "これは子供たちの未来を思うと、教育改革を行わずにはすまないことだ。" :en "For the sake of the children’s future, we cannot avoid carrying out educational reforms."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-122" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb そうにない" :reading "Verb sou ni nai" :meaning "Expresses unlikelihood or that something does not seem likely to happen." :short "Expresses unlikelihood or that something does not seem likely to happen." :long "The そうにない grammar point is typically formed from the verb’s ます-stem plus そうにない, and is used to express that something seems unlikely to happen or that someone seems unlikely to do something." :formation "Verb-ますstem + そうにない" :examples [{:ja "彼はそんなに早く走れそうにない。" :en "He doesn't seem likely to run that fast."} {:ja "この書類を明日までに終わりそうにない。" :en "It doesn’t seem like I’ll be able to finish these documents by tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-123" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb そうもない" :reading "〜sou mo nai" :meaning "Expresses negative potential; 'it doesn't seem like...', 'it's not likely...'." :short "Expresses negative potential; 'it doesn't seem like...', 'it's not likely...'." :long "～そうもない is used to express a low possibility or unlikelihood of something happening. It is typically attached to the ます-stem of a verb, similar to そうにない but with an even stronger sense of improbability in many contexts." :formation "Verb-ますstem + そうもない" :examples [{:ja "今日中に仕事を終えそうもない。" :en "It doesn't seem like I'll finish my work today."} {:ja "雨が止みそうもない。" :en "It doesn't seem like the rain will stop."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-124" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb そばから" :reading "〜soba kara" :meaning "Expresses that as soon as one action finishes, another begins immediately." :short "Expresses that as soon as one action finishes, another begins immediately." :long "The 〜そばから grammar point is used to indicate that right after a certain action is completed, another action takes place—often suggesting a repetitive or frustrating cycle." :formation "Verb-ますstem + そばから" :examples [{:ja "掃除をするそばから子供たちが散らかす。" :en "As soon as I clean, the kids mess it up."} {:ja "仕事を終えるそばから新たな仕事が舞い込んできた。" :en "As soon as I finished my work, a new task came in."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-125" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb たが最後" :reading "〜ta ga saigo" :meaning "Emphasizes 'once something happens, there is no going back'; 'once', 'as soon as', etc." :short "Emphasizes 'once something happens, there is no going back'; 'once', 'as soon as', etc." :long "The 〜たが最後 grammar point indicates that once an action is taken or event occurs, some (often irreversible) consequence follows. In English, it can be translated as 'once', 'as soon as', or 'the moment that...'." :formation "Verb-casual, past (た form) + が最後" :examples [{:ja "彼が一度怒ったが最後、誰も彼を止めることはできません。" :en "Once he gets angry, no one can stop him."} {:ja "このゲームを始めたが最後、一晩中やり続けた。" :en "As soon as I started this game, I played it all night."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-126" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb たことにしてください" :reading "~ ta koto ni shite kudasai" :meaning "Asking someone to pretend or accept that something has already happened or taken a certain state." :short "Asking someone to pretend or accept that something has already happened or taken a certain state." :long "The grammar point 'Verb たことにしてください' is used to request someone to act as if or pretend that a certain action has already occurred or a certain state exists. It is used in various contexts to express the idea of accepting a hypothetical situation for convenience or to avoid conflicts." :formation "Verb (past, casual) + ことにしてください" :examples [{:ja "昨日のことは忘れたことにしてください。" :en "Please act as if you have forgotten about yesterday."} {:ja "私がここに来たことを誰にも言わないで、秘密にしたことにしてください。" :en "Please don't tell anyone that I came here; act as if it’s a secret."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-127" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb たら Verb たで" :reading "~ tara ~ tade" :meaning "Conveys that if something happens (or doesn’t happen), there is a particular consequence or situation either way." :short "Conveys that if something happens (or doesn’t happen), there is a particular consequence or situation either way." :long "The pattern VerbたらVerbたで is used for scenarios where doing something creates a certain issue or reaction, and not doing it also creates a different concern. It often shows that no matter which side of the condition you end up on, there’s some related result or trouble." :formation "Verb-た form + ら + (same Verb-た form) + で" :examples [{:ja "行ったら行ったで大変だし、行かなかったら行かなかったで後悔しそうだ。" :en "If I go, it’s a hassle; if I don’t go, I’ll regret it."} {:ja "買ったら買ったでお金がなくなるし、買わなかったら買わなかったで後で欲しくなる。" :en "If I buy it, I’ll run out of money; if I don’t buy it, I’ll want it later."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-128" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb たら きりがない" :reading "Verb tara kiri ga nai" :meaning "Indicates an endless or unending cycle once an action is started; 'if one starts doing something, it will not stop.'" :short "Indicates an endless or unending cycle once an action is started; 'if one starts doing something, it will not stop.'" :long "The たらきりがない grammar pattern is used when something negative or undesirable continues indefinitely once it begins. The implication is that you should avoid initiating the action because it’ll just go on and on." :formation "Verb-た form + ら + きりがない" :examples [{:ja "このエンジンを修理したら、またすぐ壊れて、直したらきりがない。" :en "If I fix this engine, it breaks again, and if I fix it again, it never ends."} {:ja "飲み始めたらきりがない。" :en "Once I start drinking, I never stop."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-129" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb たら最後" :reading "〜tara saigo" :meaning "Expresses that once a certain action happens, the situation changes irreversibly—usually in a negative way." :short "Expresses that once a certain action happens, the situation changes irreversibly—usually in a negative way." :long "The たら最後 grammar point indicates that once an action (in た-form) occurs, there is no going back and an unpleasant or unavoidable state follows. Often translated as 'once...then it's all over' or 'the moment...there’s no turning back.'" :formation "Verb-た form + ら最後" :examples [{:ja "このゲームを始めたら最後、時間の経つのを忘れてしまいます。" :en "Once you start this game, you completely lose track of time."} {:ja "彼はビールを一杯飲み始めたら最後、止まらなくなります。" :en "Once he starts drinking a beer, he can't stop."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-130" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb つ Verb つ" :reading "Verb tsu Verb tsu" :meaning "Expresses two alternating actions happening repeatedly." :short "Expresses two alternating actions happening repeatedly." :long "The 〜つ〜つ pattern is used with two verb ます-stems to express two ongoing, repeated or alternating actions. For example, 'push and pull,' 'go and come back,' 'eat and play,' etc. The same subject is typically implied for both verbs." :formation "Verb-ますstem + つ + Verb-ますstem + つ" :examples [{:ja "朝から晩まで働きつ勉強しつで、疲れてしまった。" :en "I’ve been working and studying from morning till night, and now I’m exhausted."} {:ja "彼は話しつ笑いつして楽しんでいた。" :en "He was talking and laughing, having a good time."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-131" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb てからというもの" :reading "Verb te kara to iu mono" :meaning "Expresses that ever since a certain event happened, a condition has continued until now." :short "Expresses that ever since a certain event happened, a condition has continued until now." :long "The てからというもの grammar point indicates that from the time a certain action happened, a significant change or condition has persisted continuously. It emphasizes that the new situation contrasts with what came before." :formation "Verb-て form + からというもの" :examples [{:ja "彼女と別れてからというもの、一人で過ごす時間が増えました。" :en "Ever since I broke up with her, I've spent more time alone."} {:ja "子供が生まれてからというもの、自由な時間がなくなった。" :en "Since the baby was born, I have had no free time."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-132" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb てこそ" :reading "Verb te koso" :meaning "Emphasizes 'only by doing something, a certain result or realization can occur.'" :short "Emphasizes 'only by doing something, a certain result or realization can occur.'" :long "The ～てこそ grammar point is used to highlight that only by performing the verb in て-form can a certain important outcome be achieved or recognized. It often translates as 'only by (doing)' or 'only when (doing).'" :formation "Verb-て form + こそ" :examples [{:ja "実際に使ってこそ、その価値を理解できる。" :en "You can only understand its value by actually using it."} {:ja "自分で経験してこそ、大切なことが学べる。" :en "Only by experiencing it yourself can you learn what’s truly important."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-133" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ては" :reading "～te wa" :meaning "Expresses a repeated pattern or cycle, often implying a frustrating outcome; 'whenever X happens, Y happens again.'" :short "Expresses a repeated pattern or cycle, often implying a frustrating outcome; 'whenever X happens, Y happens again.'" :long "The grammar ～ては is used to show that whenever one action or situation occurs, another action or situation repeatedly follows. It can also be used in an alternating pattern: 'do this, then that, then this, then that.'" :formation "Verb-て form + は" :examples [{:ja "彼女に会っては、いつも緊張してしまいます。" :en "Whenever I meet her, I get nervous."} {:ja "この部屋を掃除しては乱雑になり、片づけては散らかる。" :en "Whenever I clean this room, it gets messy again, and whenever I tidy it, it gets cluttered again."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-134" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ては Verb" :reading "~ te wa ~" :meaning "Expresses a repeated pattern of action; translates as 'every time', 'whenever', etc." :short "Expresses a repeated pattern of action; translates as 'every time', 'whenever', etc." :long "This grammar point is used when you want to express a repeated or cyclical pattern of actions or events. It can be translated as 'every time', 'whenever', or 'if at all'. The typical formation is a verb in the て-form, followed by は, and then another verb (often in the same tense or a continuing action)." :formation "Verb-て form + は + Verb" :examples [{:ja "私が通勤しては、電子書籍を読んでいます。" :en "Whenever I commute, I read e-books."} {:ja "友達が部屋に来ては、ゲームをしています。" :en "Every time my friend comes to my room, we play games."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-135" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb てまでも" :reading "~ temademo" :meaning "Expresses 'even if it means doing...', 'no matter what it takes'." :short "Expresses 'even if it means doing...', 'no matter what it takes'." :long "This grammar point is used when the speaker wants to emphasize the lengths they would go to in order to achieve something. It attaches to the verb's て-form to mean 'even if it means doing X', 'even at the cost of X', or 'no matter what it takes'." :formation "Verb-て form + までも" :examples [{:ja "試験に合格するために、寝る時間を削ってまでも勉強します。" :en "I will study to pass the exam, even if it means cutting down on sleep."} {:ja "彼女と結婚できるなら、どんな苦労をしてまでもお金を貯めたい。" :en "If I can marry her, I'll save money no matter what hardships it takes."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-136" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb てみせる" :reading "Verb te miseru" :meaning "'Show by doing', 'demonstrate', 'prove by action'." :short "'Show by doing', 'demonstrate', 'prove by action'." :long "The ～てみせる grammar point is used to express the action of demonstrating or proving something by actually doing it. It can be translated as 'show by doing', 'demonstrate', or 'prove by action' in English." :formation "Verb-て form + みせる" :examples [{:ja "彼はそのパズルを解いてみせた。" :en "He solved the puzzle to show me."} {:ja "私はこの仕事ができることをやってみせます。" :en "I will do this job to prove that I can."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-137" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb てやまない" :reading "Verb te yamanai" :meaning "Used with certain emotional/mental verbs to show a strong, unceasing emotion: 'can't stop', 'never cease to'." :short "Used with certain emotional/mental verbs to show a strong, unceasing emotion: 'can't stop', 'never cease to'." :long "The grammar ～てやまない is typically used with verbs like 願う, 祈る, 愛する, 尊敬する, 期待する, etc., expressing a deep, continuous emotional state (love, respect, hope, prayer). It emphasizes that the feeling 'never ceases.'" :formation "Verb-て form + やまない (common with certain verbs)" :examples [{:ja "私は母を思ってやまない。" :en "I can't stop thinking of my mother."} {:ja "彼は彼女を愛してやまない。" :en "He loves her beyond measure."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-138" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ないではおかない" :reading "~ nai de wa okanai" :meaning "Means 'cannot help but do', 'will definitely do', or 'is bound to happen'." :short "Means 'cannot help but do', 'will definitely do', or 'is bound to happen'." :long "The pattern Verb ないではおかない indicates that something definitely occurs as a logical or emotional consequence. It can be translated as 'cannot not do', 'can’t help but do', or 'will inevitably do'. The verb is put in the negative form followed by ないではおかない." :formation "Verb-ない form + ではおかない" :examples [{:ja "彼女は毎回遅刻をしては、先生に怒られないではおかない。" :en "Every time she's late, she inevitably gets scolded by the teacher."} {:ja "美味しいものがあれば、彼は食べないではおかない。" :en "If there's delicious food, he can't help but eat it."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-139" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ないではすまない" :reading "Verb nai dewa sumanai" :meaning "'Cannot end without doing', 'must do'—an unavoidable moral or social necessity." :short "'Cannot end without doing', 'must do'—an unavoidable moral or social necessity." :long "The grammar Verb ないではすまない expresses that something must be done (often for moral, social, or personal reasons) and cannot be left undone. It implies an obligation or inevitability, akin to 'I can't get away without doing it.'" :formation "Verb-ない form + ではすまない" :examples [{:ja "失敗をしたら、謝らないではすまない。" :en "If you make a mistake, you have to apologize—there’s no way around it."} {:ja "この問題は重大なので、報告しないではすまない。" :en "This issue is so serious that I must report it."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-140" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ないまでも" :reading "Verb nai made mo" :meaning "'Even if not to the extent of...', 'although not completely...'." :short "'Even if not to the extent of...', 'although not completely...'." :long "The pattern ないまでも is used when acknowledging that something doesn’t reach a certain extreme, but at least some part or degree of it applies. Often translated as 'even if not entirely...' or 'though not as far as...'." :formation "Verb-ない form + までも" :examples [{:ja "全てを理解しないまでも、基本的なことは分かってほしい。" :en "Even if you don't understand everything, I'd like you at least to grasp the basics."} {:ja "会えないまでも、電話で声を聞きたい。" :en "Even if we can’t meet, I still want to hear your voice over the phone."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-141" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ないものだろうか" :reading "Verb nai mono darou ka" :meaning "Expressing hope or wonder if something can be done: 'Isn't there a way...?', 'Can't we...?'" :short "Expressing hope or wonder if something can be done: 'Isn't there a way...?', 'Can't we...?'" :long "This grammar point is used when the speaker expresses the hope, wish, or wonder if an action could be possible. It creates the nuance of 'Isn't there some way...?' or 'Can't I/you/we...?' in English, often with the negative potential form." :formation "Verb-negative (often negative potential) + ものだろうか" :examples [{:ja "もっと時間があれば、ここに来られないものだろうか。" :en "If I had more time, wouldn't it be possible to come here?"} {:ja "彼女にもっとよく説明すれば、理解できないものだろうか。" :en "If I explain it better to her, wouldn't she understand?"}]} {:id "grammar-n1-142" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ないものでもない" :reading "Verb nai mono demo nai" :meaning "Expresses the idea that it's not entirely impossible or non-existent: 'It's not that I don't...', 'It's not that there aren't...'" :short "Expresses the idea that it's not entirely impossible or non-existent: 'It's not that I don't...', 'It's not that there aren't...'" :long "The grammar point Verbないものでもない is used to express that a situation is not completely impossible or nonexistent. Literally, it can be translated as 'it's not that there aren't...' or 'it's not that I never...'. It implies that some possibility or partial truth exists." :formation "Verb-negative form (ない form) + ものでもない" :examples [{:ja "旅行に行きたいと思わないものでもない。" :en "It's not that I never want to travel."} {:ja "彼の言ったこと全部が間違いないものでもない。" :en "It's not that everything he said is completely free from mistakes."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-143" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb の ない Noun" :reading "~ no nai ~" :meaning "Used to modify a noun with a negative verb form; 'a Noun without (Verb)'." :short "Used to modify a noun with a negative verb form; 'a Noun without (Verb)'." :long "VerbのないNoun modifies a noun by describing the absence of the verb's action. It indicates 'a Noun where the action does not happen' or 'a Noun without that action.'" :formation "Verb in negative form + の + Noun" :examples [{:ja "笑いのない人生は、つまらない。" :en "A life without laughter is boring."} {:ja "水のない場所には、人は住めない。" :en "People cannot live in a place without water."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-144" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ば きりがない" :reading "〜ba kiri ga nai" :meaning "Indicates there's no end once you start listing, doing, or saying something: 'If one starts to..., there will be no end.'" :short "Indicates there's no end once you start listing, doing, or saying something: 'If one starts to..., there will be no end.'" :long "The pattern ～ばきりがない is used when expressing that if you start an action—like listing examples or complaining—there is no end. It literally means 'if (you) do X, there's no limit/end.'" :formation "Verb-conditional form (ば) + きりがない" :examples [{:ja "悪口を言い始めればきりがない。" :en "Once I start speaking ill of someone, there's no end."} {:ja "彼の欠点を数えればきりがない。" :en "If I start counting his flaws, there will be no end."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-145" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb もしないで" :reading "~ mo shinai de" :meaning "Means 'without even doing'." :short "Means 'without even doing'." :long "This pattern is used to express that one has not done (or not even tried to do) some action before proceeding to do something else. It emphasizes doing something 'without even' doing the prior action." :formation "Verb (masu stem) + もしないで" :examples [{:ja "調べもしないで、答えを書きました。" :en "I wrote the answer without even investigating."} {:ja "彼は試験を勉強もしないで、テストに合格した。" :en "He passed the test without even studying."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-146" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb やしない" :reading "~ yashinai" :meaning "Expresses a strong denial or refusal: 'I would never...', 'No way I would...'." :short "Expresses a strong denial or refusal: 'I would never...', 'No way I would...'." :long "This grammar adds a nuance of strong denial or dismissal of the idea that the speaker (or someone) would do the action. Formed by adding やしない to the verb's stem, it often appears in casual or spoken language." :formation "Verb (masu stem) + やしない" :examples [{:ja "こんな時に泣きやしない。" :en "I would never cry at a time like this."} {:ja "私が彼を助けやしない。" :en "There's no way I'd help him."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-147" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb よう" :reading "~ you / ~ you ni" :meaning "Expresses 'the way/manner of doing something' or 'in order to / so that / try to'." :short "Expresses 'the way/manner of doing something' or 'in order to / so that / try to'." :long "This usage of よう most commonly appears as 「Verb辞書形 + ように」. It can mean 'in order to do something', 'so that something happens', or 'in the way someone does something'. It is also used in expressions like ～ようにする (to make an effort to) or ～ように言う (to tell someone to do something)." :formation "Verb (dictionary or negative form) + ように (する / etc.)" :examples [{:ja "この問題を解くように努力します。" :en "I will try to solve this problem."} {:ja "彼が言ったようにやりましょう。" :en "Let's do it the way he said."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-148" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ようか Verbるまいか" :reading "Verb you ka Verb ru mai ka" :meaning "Expresses indecision: 'Should I do it or not?'." :short "Expresses indecision: 'Should I do it or not?'." :long "This grammar shows hesitation about whether to do something or not. The volitional form plus まい (negative volitional) suggests an internal debate about two opposite choices of the *same* verb." :formation "Verb-volitional form + か + Verb-volitional negative form (まい) + か" :examples [{:ja "彼に話そうか話すまいか、本当に迷っている。" :en "I'm really unsure whether to talk to him or not."} {:ja "試験を受けようか受けまいか迷っています。" :en "I'm wondering whether to take the exam or not."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-149" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ようが Verb るまいが" :reading "Verb you ga Verb ru mai ga" :meaning "Expresses 'whether or not', 'no matter if something happens or not'." :short "Expresses 'whether or not', 'no matter if something happens or not'." :long "Verbようが Verbるまいが indicates that whatever happens—whether the action is done or not—the outcome remains unchanged or the speaker's stance remains the same." :formation "Verb-volitional form + が + Verb-negative volitional form + が" :examples [{:ja "彼が来ようが来るまいが、パーティーは始まります。" :en "Whether he comes or not, the party will start."} {:ja "勉強しようがしまいが、テストの結果は変わらない。" :en "Whether you study or not, the test results won't change."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-150" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ようがない" :reading "〜you ga nai" :meaning "Expresses 'no way to do something,' 'impossible to do'." :short "Expresses 'no way to do something,' 'impossible to do'." :long "〜ようがない is used to say that there's no way or method to do something, even if you wanted to." :formation "Verb (masu stem) + ようがない" :examples [{:ja "電車が混んでいて、動きようがない。" :en "The train is so crowded that I can't move."} {:ja "彼はとても早く走るので、追いつきようがない。" :en "He runs so fast, there's no way I can catch up."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-151" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ようと Verbる まいと" :reading "Verb you to Verb ru mai to" :meaning "This structure indicates that regardless of whether one does (verb) or not, the outcome/attitude remains unchanged." :short "This structure indicates that regardless of whether one does (verb) or not, the outcome/attitude remains unchanged." :long "The expression 'Verb-volitional form + と + Verb-negative volitional (～まい) + と' means 'regardless of whether one does X or not, it doesn't change the situation'. It shows the speaker's firm stance or the unchanging nature of some outcome." :formation "Verb-volitional + と + Verb-negative volitional (～まい) + と" :examples [{:ja "あなたが来ようと来るまいと、パーティーは予定通りに始まります。" :en "Whether you come or not, the party will start as scheduled."} {:ja "彼があそこに行こうと行くまいと、私たちは止めることができません。" :en "Whether he tries to go there or not, we can't stop him."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-152" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ようにも" :reading "〜you ni mo" :meaning "Used to express 'even though one wants to do something, one can't for some reason'." :short "Used to express 'even though one wants to do something, one can't for some reason'." :long "The ～ようにも grammar point indicates that a person wants or tries to do something (volitional form), but for some reason cannot. Often, there's a second clause explaining what prevents the action." :formation "Verb-volitional form + にも + (reason/potential negative, etc.)" :examples [{:ja "もう少し速く走ろうにも、足が痛くて無理だ。" :en "I'd like to run a bit faster, but I can’t because my leg hurts."} {:ja "彼に話そうにも、彼は私を無視している。" :en "I want to talk to him, but he's ignoring me."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-153" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ようにも Verb れない" :reading "〜you ni mo 〜renai" :meaning "Expresses the inability to do something even if one wants/tries." :short "Expresses the inability to do something even if one wants/tries." :long "This grammar shows that you want (or try) to do something (volitional form), but cannot due to some obstacle. Often it appears as 'Verb-volitional + にも + Verb (potential negative)' or simply 'Verb-volitional + にも + (reason you can't)'." :formation "Verb-volitional form + にも + (potential negative form / reason)" :examples [{:ja "彼を信じようにも信じられない証拠がある。" :en "Even if I want to believe him, there's evidence that prevents me from doing so."} {:ja "早く出発しようにも車が故障してしまった。" :en "Even if I want to leave early, my car has broken down."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-154" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verb ようもない" :reading "~you mo nai" :meaning "Expresses 'it's impossible to do something'." :short "Expresses 'it's impossible to do something'." :long "～ようもない typically attaches to the verb's ます-stem, adding ようもない to indicate that there is 'no way' or 'no method' to perform the action. It’s the same nuance as 'Verbようがない' in many references." :formation "Verb-ます stem + ようもない" :examples [{:ja "この問題は解きようもない。" :en "It's impossible to solve this problem."} {:ja "これ以上我慢しようもない。" :en "It's impossible to endure any further."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-155" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる がままに" :reading "〜ga mama ni" :meaning "Expresses 'letting something happen as it is'; 'just as something does'." :short "Expresses 'letting something happen as it is'; 'just as something does'." :long "がままに describes an action happening exactly 'as is,' without intervention or change, often suggesting doing something passively or naturally." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + がままに" :examples [{:ja "窓を開けるがままにしておいたら、風が部屋に入ってきました。" :en "I left the window open as it was, and the wind came into the room."} {:ja "彼女は涙を流すがままに、ただ黙って座っていた。" :en "She just sat quietly, letting her tears flow."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-156" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる が早いか" :reading "verb-ru ga hayai ka" :meaning "Expresses 'as soon as...' or 'the moment when...'." :short "Expresses 'as soon as...' or 'the moment when...'." :long "Verbる が早いか indicates that something happens immediately after something else. It's similar to 'as soon as...' or 'no sooner than...'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + が早いか" :examples [{:ja "部屋に入るが早いか、彼はテレビをつけた。" :en "No sooner had he entered the room than he turned on the TV."} {:ja "電車に乗るが早いか、私はすぐに眠ってしまった。" :en "As soon as I got on the train, I immediately fell asleep."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-157" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる くらいなら" :reading "〜ru kurai nara" :meaning "Expresses preference to avoid something: 'rather than...', 'if it comes to doing X, I'd rather...'." :short "Expresses preference to avoid something: 'rather than...', 'if it comes to doing X, I'd rather...'." :long "This grammar is used when the speaker would rather not do something or chooses another option rather than face the stated action. It’s often translated as 'rather than... I'd prefer...' or 'if it comes down to doing X, I'd rather...'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + くらいなら" :examples [{:ja "一人で行くくらいなら、家にいるほうがましです。" :en "I'd rather stay at home than go alone."} {:ja "負けるくらいなら、試合をしないほうがいい。" :en "Rather than lose, it's better not to compete."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-158" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる こと なし に" :reading "Verb-ru koto nashi ni" :meaning "Used to express 'without doing something'." :short "Used to express 'without doing something'." :long "Verbることなしに indicates doing something else 'without' performing the stated action. It can imply skipping or omitting the verb action entirely." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ことなしに" :examples [{:ja "試験を勉強することなしに、合格しました。" :en "I passed the exam without studying."} {:ja "試着することなしに、服を買いました。" :en "I bought the clothes without trying them on."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-159" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる ことのないように" :reading "Verb-ru koto no nai you ni" :meaning "Expresses the desire or effort to prevent something: 'so as not to...', 'to avoid...'." :short "Expresses the desire or effort to prevent something: 'so as not to...', 'to avoid...'." :long "Verbる ことのないように is used to show that one is trying or intending to prevent some action from happening. It's like 'so that this doesn't happen' or 'in order not to...'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ことのないように" :examples [{:ja "車をなくすことのないように、駐車場で必ずロックしましょう。" :en "Lock your car in the parking lot so you don't lose it."} {:ja "風邪を引くことのないように、手をよく洗いましょう。" :en "Wash your hands well so you don't catch a cold."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-160" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる ときりがない" :reading "verb-ru to kiri ga nai" :meaning "Used to indicate that something can go on endlessly or without limits." :short "Used to indicate that something can go on endlessly or without limits." :long "The ～るときりがない grammar point is used to express that once you start doing the verb, there’s no end or limit. The phrase 'きりがない' means 'no end' or 'endless.'" :formation "Verb-casual + と + きりがない" :examples [{:ja "彼の悪口を言うときりがない。" :en "If we start talking about his faults, there's no end."} {:ja "その問題を考えるときりがない。" :en "If you start thinking about that problem, there's no end."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-161" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる ともなく Verb" :reading "Verb-ru tomonaku Verb" :meaning "Used to express 'without a particular intention', 'unconsciously', or 'before one knows it'." :short "Used to express 'without a particular intention', 'unconsciously', or 'before one knows it'." :long "The phrase VerbるともなくVerb indicates doing something unintentionally or unconsciously— you just find yourself doing it without a clear aim or intention." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ともなく + Verb" :examples [{:ja "遠くを見るともなく窓の外を眺めた。" :en "I found myself gazing out the window, not really looking at anything in particular."} {:ja "知るともなく、彼が去ったことを感じた。" :en "Without really knowing, I felt that he had left."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-162" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる ともなしに Verb" :reading "Verb-ru tomonashi ni Verb" :meaning "Expresses doing something unintentionally or without conscious thought." :short "Expresses doing something unintentionally or without conscious thought." :long "VerbるともなしにVerb is used to show that you do some action without particularly intending to. Often you ‘end up doing’ something by habit or casually." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ともなしに + Verb" :examples [{:ja "本を読むともなしに眠ってしまった。" :en "I ended up falling asleep while reading the book without really meaning to."} {:ja "軽い気持ちで手紙を書くともなしに、彼に本心を告白した。" :en "While writing a letter casually, I ended up confessing my true feelings to him."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-163" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる なり" :reading "Verb-ru nari" :meaning "Expresses 'as soon as' or 'the moment'." :short "Expresses 'as soon as' or 'the moment'." :long "Verbるなり indicates that the following action happens immediately after the verb action. It can be translated as 'no sooner than...', 'as soon as...', or 'the moment...'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + なり" :examples [{:ja "家に帰るなり、すぐに寝た。" :en "As soon as I got home, I went to sleep."} {:ja "彼が駅に着くなり、電話をかけてきた。" :en "He called me the moment he arrived at the station."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-164" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる にとどまらず～も" :reading "Verb-ru ni todomarazu ~ mo" :meaning "Expresses 'not only… but also', 'not limited to…'." :short "Expresses 'not only… but also', 'not limited to…'." :long "にとどまらず is used to indicate that something goes beyond just the stated verb or noun. It can be translated as 'not just… but also…' or 'not limited to…'." :formation "Noun / Verb-dictionary form + にとどまらず + も/ほかのこと" :examples [{:ja "彼は仕事にとどまらず、ボランティア活動もしている。" :en "He does not just work; he also does volunteer activities."} {:ja "この機能は写真撮影にとどまらず、動画撮影も可能です。" :en "This function is not limited to taking photos; it can also record videos."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-165" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる にはあたらない" :reading "Verb-ru ni wa ataranai" :meaning "Used to say 'there is no need to...', 'it’s not necessary to...'." :short "Used to say 'there is no need to...', 'it’s not necessary to...'." :long "Verbる にはあたらない expresses that something is not worth the reaction or there is no need to do the verb. It's somewhat formal and often used in writing." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + にはあたらない" :examples [{:ja "パニックに陥るにはあたらない。" :en "There’s no need to panic."} {:ja "彼に謝るにはあたらない。" :en "There’s no need to apologize to him."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-166" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる にも" :reading "Verb-ru ni mo" :meaning "Indicates that doing the verb is impossible or difficult, even if one wants to." :short "Indicates that doing the verb is impossible or difficult, even if one wants to." :long "Verbる にも is often used with an additional clause explaining why the action can’t be done. It can be translated as 'even if (I) want to do X, I can’t (because...)'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + にも + (reason it can't be done)" :examples [{:ja "明日雨が降ったとしても、傘を持つにも荷物が多すぎる。" :en "Even if it rains tomorrow, I have too much luggage to bring an umbrella."} {:ja "試験に落ちたが、もう一度受けるにもお金が足りない。" :en "I failed the exam, but I don’t have the money to take it again even if I wanted to."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-167" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる にも Verb れない" :reading "Verb-ru ni mo Verb-re nai" :meaning "Expresses: 'Even if I try/want to do something, I cannot.'" :short "Expresses: 'Even if I try/want to do something, I cannot.'" :long "This pattern emphasizes that a person wants or attempts to do something (often in volitional form), but is incapable of doing so. It is often used with potential verbs in negative form: 'Even if I try to ~, I can't ~.'" :formation "Verb-volitional + にも + Verb-potential negative" :examples [{:ja "彼を理解しようにも理解できない。" :en "Even if I try to understand him, I can't."} {:ja "走ろうにも走れない。" :en "Even if I want to run, I can't."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-168" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる べからざる Noun" :reading "Verb-ru bekara zaru Noun" :meaning "Used to express what should not be done; 'must not', 'should not'." :short "Used to express what should not be done; 'must not', 'should not'." :long "The Japanese grammar point Verbる べからざる Noun is used to indicate something that should not be done, or is not allowed to be done. It translates to 'must not' or 'should not' in English. It is often used in formal written Japanese or in formal speeches. It is constructed by using the verb in its dictionary form followed by 'べからざる' and a noun, usually related to behavior or events." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + べからざる + Noun" :examples [{:ja "人を裏切るべからざる行為だ。" :en "Betraying others is an act that must not be done."} {:ja "子供を傷つけるべからざる行動だ。" :en "Hurting a child is a behavior that must not happen."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-169" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる べからず" :reading "〜ru bekara zu" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'must not do' or 'should not do'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'must not do' or 'should not do'." :long "The ～るべからず grammar point is used to express a strong prohibition or firmly established rule. It can be translated as 'must not do' or 'should not do'. It is common in formal or instructive settings (e.g., signs, notices). The formation is straightforward with verbs in dictionary form followed by べからず." :formation "Verb-ru + べからず" :examples [{:ja "嘘をつくべからず。" :en "One must not tell lies."} {:ja "彼らの秘密を漏らすべからず。" :en "You must not leak their secrets."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-170" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる べく" :reading "Verb-ru beku" :meaning "Expresses the meaning of 'in order to', 'for the purpose of'." :short "Expresses the meaning of 'in order to', 'for the purpose of'." :long "The 'Verbる べく' grammar point is used to indicate a strong intention or purpose of the speaker. It can be translated as 'in order to' or 'for the purpose of' in English. It is more common in written language or formal contexts." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + べく" :examples [{:ja "成功するべく、日々努力しています。" :en "In order to succeed, I am working hard every day."} {:ja "健康を維持するべく、毎日運動をしています。" :en "In order to maintain my health, I exercise every day."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-171" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる べくもない" :reading "Verb-ru beku mo nai" :meaning "Means 'it's impossible to...' or 'there's no way to...'." :short "Means 'it's impossible to...' or 'there's no way to...'." :long "This grammar point is used when you want to express that something is impossible or there is no way to do something. The verb before 'べくもない' should be in its dictionary form." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + べくもない" :examples [{:ja "その問題はとても難しく、解くべくもない。" :en "That problem is so difficult that it's impossible to solve."} {:ja "彼女はあまりに速く走って、追いつくべくもない。" :en "She runs so fast that there's no way to catch up."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-172" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる までもない" :reading "〜ru made mo nai" :meaning "It's not necessary to the extent of; no need to." :short "It's not necessary to the extent of; no need to." :long "The 〜るまでもない grammar point is used to express that something is not necessary to the extent of doing that action. It can be translated as 'no need to' in English." :formation "Verb-る + までもない" :examples [{:ja "そんなに心配するまでもない。" :en "There's no need to worry that much."} {:ja "電話するまでもないことだ。" :en "It's not something you need to call about."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-173" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる ものとする" :reading "〜ru mono to suru" :meaning "Assuming or deciding something as..." :short "Assuming or deciding something as..." :long "The ～るものとする grammar point is often used in formal or legal contexts to express a decision or assumption. It can be translated as 'it is decided/assumed that...' or 'shall be deemed that...'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ものとする" :examples [{:ja "ここでの話し合いは秘密とするものとします。" :en "The discussions here are to be kept confidential."} {:ja "この契約は明日から有効とするものとする。" :en "This contract shall become effective from tomorrow."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-174" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる や否や" :reading "Verb-ru ya ina ya" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'as soon as', 'the moment...'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'as soon as', 'the moment...'." :long "The grammar point Verbる や否や is used to express something happening immediately after another action or event. It can be translated as 'as soon as...' or 'the moment...'. The verb must be in dictionary form." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + や否や" :examples [{:ja "彼女が家に帰るや否や、すぐにテレビをつけました。" :en "As soon as she returned home, she turned on the TV."} {:ja "私が郵便箱を開けるや否や、手紙が落ちました。" :en "The moment I opened the mailbox, the letters fell out."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-175" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる 始末だ" :reading "〜ru shimatsu da" :meaning "Indicates a (often unfortunate) result or conclusion of an action." :short "Indicates a (often unfortunate) result or conclusion of an action." :long "The ～る始末だ grammar point expresses the outcome or conclusion of an action, typically a negative or unfortunate result. It can be translated as 'end up...', 'the result is...', or 'the conclusion is...'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + 始末だ" :examples [{:ja "忘れてしまって、パスポートを家に置いてくる始末だった。" :en "I ended up leaving my passport at home because I forgot."} {:ja "毎晩遅くまで働いて、倒れる始末だ。" :en "I work until late every night and end up collapsing."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-176" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "Verbる 嫌いがある" :reading "～ru kirai ga aru" :meaning "Tendency to do a negative action; have a bad habit of ~." :short "Tendency to do a negative action; have a bad habit of ~." :long "The ～る嫌いがある grammar point is used to express a negative tendency or habit. It often carries a critical or disapproving tone and points out someone's habitual action." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + 嫌いがある" :examples [{:ja "彼女は遅刻する嫌いがある。" :en "She has a tendency to be late."} {:ja "私の父はお酒を飲み過ぎる嫌いがある。" :en "My father has a habit of drinking too much alcohol."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-177" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "いつまで～のやら" :reading "itsumade ~ no yara" :meaning "Expresses uncertainty regarding duration; 'don't know when... will end'." :short "Expresses uncertainty regarding duration; 'don't know when... will end'." :long "The いつまで～のやら grammar point is used to express uncertainty about when something will end or finish. It is often used in situations where the speaker is unsure about when a particular state of affairs or event will conclude." :formation "いつまで + Verb (dictionary form) + のやら" :examples [{:ja "この試験勉強はいつまで続くのやら。" :en "I don't know when this exam study will end."} {:ja "彼の話がいつ終わるのやら、わからない。" :en "I don't know when his talk will finish."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-178" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～が Verb られる" :reading "〜ga Verb rareru" :meaning "Expresses potential or ability to do something; 'can', 'able to'." :short "Expresses potential or ability to do something; 'can', 'able to'." :long "The ～が Verb られる grammar pattern generally refers to the potential form of Japanese verbs (i.e., 'can do something'). However, the exact formation depends on the verb group. For most Group 1 (五段) verbs, potential form changes the final -u to -eru (e.g., 書く→書ける). For Group 2 (一段) verbs, you often see ～られる (e.g., 食べる→食べられる). In English, it translates as 'can' or 'able to'." :formation "❶ (Group 1) Verb:  書く → 書ける / 飲む → 飲める, etc.\n❷ (Group 2) Verb-ます stem + られる (食べる→食べられる、見る→見られる)" :examples [{:ja "彼がこの問題を解けると思いますか？" :en "Do you think he can solve this problem?"} {:ja "私がその大きな箱を運べるか？" :en "Can I carry that big box?"}]} {:id "grammar-n1-179" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～かと思いきや" :reading "〜ka to omoikiya" :meaning "Expressing unexpected outcomes; 'just when I thought...', 'but...'." :short "Expressing unexpected outcomes; 'just when I thought...', 'but...'." :long "The ～かと思いきや grammar point is used when the outcome turns out different from what was initially assumed or expected. It can be translated as 'just when I thought...' or simply 'but (actually)...'. It often highlights that the speaker’s assumption was proven wrong by subsequent events." :formation "Sentence + かと思いきや (Verb plain form, i-/na-Adjective, or Noun + だ + かと思いきや)" :examples [{:ja "明日は晴れるかと思いきや、まさかの雨が降った。" :en "I thought it would be sunny tomorrow, but unexpectedly it rained."} {:ja "彼が試験に落ちたかと思いきや、合格していた。" :en "I thought he had failed the test, but he actually passed."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-180" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～がゆえに" :reading "～ga yue ni" :meaning "Expresses the reason or cause; 'because', 'due to', 'as a result of'." :short "Expresses the reason or cause; 'because', 'due to', 'as a result of'." :long "The ～がゆえに grammar point is used to express a reason or cause, often in more formal writing or speech. It roughly corresponds to 'because (of)', 'due to', or 'as a result of' in English." :formation "Noun / Verb / Adjective + がゆえに" :examples [{:ja "愛情がゆえに、彼は彼女を許した。" :en "Because of love, he forgave her."} {:ja "突然の雨がゆえに、試合は延期された。" :en "Due to the sudden rain, the match was postponed."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-181" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～がゆえの Noun" :reading "〜ga yue no Noun" :meaning "Expresses the reason or cause for something; 'because of', 'due to'." :short "Expresses the reason or cause for something; 'because of', 'due to'." :long "The ～がゆえの Noun pattern is used to describe a noun that exists or occurs due to some cause. It is often used in more literary or formal contexts." :formation "Noun / Verb-dictionary form + がゆえの + Noun" :examples [{:ja "愛がゆえの悲しみ。" :en "Sadness due to love."} {:ja "健康がゆえの幸せ。" :en "Happiness due to good health."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-182" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～から Noun に 至る まで" :reading "〜kara 〜ni itaru made" :meaning "Expressing range from one thing to another; 'from...to', 'ranging from...to'." :short "Expressing range from one thing to another; 'from...to', 'ranging from...to'." :long "The ～から〜に至るまで grammar point emphasizes a range or scope extending from one extreme to another. It can be translated as 'from...to...' or 'ranging from...to...' in English. It can apply to people, places, times, objects, and so on." :formation "Noun (start) + から + Noun (end) + に至るまで" :examples [{:ja "このバンドの曲は、青年から老人に至るまで、人々に愛されています。" :en "The songs of this band are loved by people ranging from the young to the elderly."} {:ja "小さい物件から大きな建物に至るまで、全てのプロパティの管理を我々が行います。" :en "We handle all property management, ranging from small units to large buildings."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-183" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ごとく" :reading "〜gotoku" :meaning "To seem like; as if; as though." :short "To seem like; as if; as though." :long "The ～ごとく grammar point is used to express similarity or resemblance. It is quite formal/literary, meaning 'as if,' 'as though,' or 'like.' It can be used with verbs and nouns." :formation "Verb-て + ごとく / Noun + の + ごとく" :examples [{:ja "彼は鳥のごとく飛んでいるように見えた。" :en "He looked as though he was flying like a bird."} {:ja "彼は死んだごとく眠っていた。" :en "He was sleeping as though he were dead."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-184" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～こととて" :reading "〜koto tote" :meaning "Used to express the reason or premise for a decision or judgment; 'since', 'because'." :short "Used to express the reason or premise for a decision or judgment; 'since', 'because'." :long "The ～こととて grammar point expresses a reason or premise in a slightly formal or literary way. It can often be interpreted as 'since' or 'because'." :formation "Verb (plain form) + こととて / Noun + の + こととて / (な-Adjective + な) + こととて" :examples [{:ja "彼は裁判に勝つために全資産を使うこととて、弁護士に頼んだ。" :en "Since he's using all his assets to win the trial, he hired a lawyer."} {:ja "明日は大切な試験があることとて、今夜は早く寝ます。" :en "Since I have an important exam tomorrow, I’m going to bed early tonight."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-185" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ずにすんだ" :reading "〜zuni sunda" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'was able to do without'; 'managed to avoid'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'was able to do without'; 'managed to avoid'." :long "The ～ずにすんだ grammar point indicates that one was able to avoid doing something or managed to get by without doing it. It translates as 'managed to avoid ~' or 'got by without ~.'" :formation "Verb-ず form + に + すんだ" :examples [{:ja "彼は事故を起こさずにすんだ。" :en "He managed to avoid having an accident."} {:ja "私は手術せずにすんだ。" :en "I was able to do without surgery."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-186" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～だろうとなかろうと" :reading "〜darou to nakarou to" :meaning "Expresses 'regardless of whether it is or isn't'." :short "Expresses 'regardless of whether it is or isn't'." :long "The ～だろうとなかろうと grammar point is used to express the idea of 'regardless of whether it is or isn't.' It translates roughly as 'whether it is ~ or not' in English. This construction emphasizes that the speaker’s stance or action does not change, regardless of the two possible conditions." :formation "Noun / i-Adjective / na-Adjective / Verb + だろうとなかろうと" :examples [{:ja "試験が難しいだろうとなかろうと、全力で頑張ります。" :en "Regardless of whether the exam is difficult or not, I will do my best."} {:ja "彼が来るだろうとなかろうと、私たちは始めます。" :en "Whether he comes or not, we will start."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-187" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～つもりだ" :reading "〜tsumori da" :meaning "Expresses the speaker's intention or plan." :short "Expresses the speaker's intention or plan." :long "The ～つもりだ grammar point is used to express the speaker's intention or plan to do something. It translates as 'intend to,' 'plan to,' or 'think that (I will).' It's often used with the verb in its dictionary form." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + つもりだ" :examples [{:ja "明日は早く起きるつもりです。" :en "I plan to wake up early tomorrow."} {:ja "私は日本語を勉強するつもりです。" :en "I intend to study Japanese."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-188" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～つもりで" :reading "〜tsumori de" :meaning "Expresses the speaker's intention or plan; 'with the intention of', 'planning to'." :short "Expresses the speaker's intention or plan; 'with the intention of', 'planning to'." :long "The ～つもりで grammar point is used to express the speaker's intention or mindset when doing something. It can often be translated as 'with the intention of' or 'with the mindset that.'" :formation "Verb-dictionary form + つもりで" :examples [{:ja "私は日本に住むつもりで日本語を勉強しています。" :en "I am studying Japanese with the intention of living in Japan."} {:ja "彼は全てを失うつもりでギャンブルをしました。" :en "He gambled with the intention of risking everything."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-189" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ではすまない" :reading "〜dewa sumanai" :meaning "Expresses that just doing something is not enough or will not suffice." :short "Expresses that just doing something is not enough or will not suffice." :long "The grammar point ～ではすまない is used when the speaker strongly feels that an action or situation alone is not sufficient or acceptable, implying that more serious measures are needed or that the consequences are more severe." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + ではすまない / い-Adjective + ではすまない / な-Adjective + ではすまない / Noun + ではすまない" :examples [{:ja "ただ謝るではすまない。何か行動を取らなければならない。" :en "Just apologizing will not suffice. Some action needs to be taken."} {:ja "この問題は議論だけではすまない。具体的な計画が必要だ。" :en "This problem won't be solved with just discussions. A concrete plan is needed."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-190" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とあって" :reading "〜to atte" :meaning "Expresses a special situation or event due to certain circumstances." :short "Expresses a special situation or event due to certain circumstances." :long "The ～とあって grammar point is used to indicate that, due to a special or noteworthy circumstance, a particular situation has occurred. It often translates as 'because (it is)...' or 'given that...' in English, emphasizing the uniqueness of the cause." :formation "Noun + とあって / (Sometimes Verb + とあって, depending on context)" :examples [{:ja "春休みとあって、公園は子供たちでいっぱいだ。" :en "Because it's spring break, the park is full of children."} {:ja "試験前とあって、図書館は学生で混んでいる。" :en "With exams coming up, the library is crowded with students."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-191" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とあれば" :reading "〜to areba" :meaning "Expresses a condition; 'if', 'when', 'since'." :short "Expresses a condition; 'if', 'when', 'since'." :long "The ～とあれば grammar point is used to express a condition or premise in which a certain outcome or action naturally follows. It can be translated as 'if,' 'when,' or 'since,' depending on context. It emphasizes that under this specific condition, a certain response is expected." :formation "Verb-casual + とあれば / い-Adjective + とあれば / な-Adjective + だとあれば / Noun + だとあれば" :examples [{:ja "無料とあれば、誰でも利用したいと思います。" :en "If it's free, anyone would want to use it."} {:ja "この曲が流れるとあれば、彼は必ず踊り始めます。" :en "If this song starts playing, he will definitely begin to dance."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-192" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～といえども" :reading "〜to iedomo" :meaning "Even if it is said that; Even though; Although." :short "Even if it is said that; Even though; Although." :long "The ～といえども grammar point is used to show a contrast or contradiction. It is similar to 'even though' or 'although' in English. It can be used with nouns, verbs, adjectives, etc., to emphasize that the reality is different from the assumption or label." :formation "Noun + といえども / Verb-plain form + といえども / Adjective + といえども" :examples [{:ja "彼が天才といえども、ミスをすることはあります。" :en "Even though he’s a genius, he still makes mistakes."} {:ja "このパソコンが新しいといえども、問題があります。" :en "Even though this computer is new, it has problems."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-193" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～といったらありはしない" :reading "〜to ittara ari wa shinai" :meaning "Express extreme exclamation; 'extremely', 'unbelievable', 'unsurpassed'." :short "Express extreme exclamation; 'extremely', 'unbelievable', 'unsurpassed'." :long "The ～といったらありはしない grammar point is used to express an extreme degree of something, often surprise or admiration. It can be loosely translated as 'unbelievably...' or 'incredibly...'." :formation "Noun + といったらありはしない / な-Adjective + といったらありはしない" :examples [{:ja "彼の英語の上手さといったらありはしない。" :en "His English is unbelievably good."} {:ja "このスイーツの美味しさといったらありはしない。" :en "These sweets are unbelievably delicious."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-194" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～といったらありゃしない" :reading "〜to ittara arya shinai" :meaning "Emphasizes that something is to the extreme; 'you have no idea how...', 'it's incredibly...'." :short "Emphasizes that something is to the extreme; 'you have no idea how...', 'it's incredibly...'." :long "The ～といったらありゃしない grammar point is an idiomatic expression used to emphasize that something is extremely or incredibly a certain way—often beyond words. It can be loosely rendered as 'you wouldn't believe how...' or 'it's indescribably...'." :formation "Verb-casual + といったらありゃしない / い-Adjective + といったらありゃしない / な-Adjective + だ + といったらありゃしない / Noun + だ + といったらありゃしない" :examples [{:ja "その店のラーメンは美味しいといったらありゃしない。" :en "You have no idea how delicious the ramen at that shop is."} {:ja "彼女の料理の腕前は上手だといったらありゃしない。" :en "You have no idea how skilled she is at cooking."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-195" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～といったらない" :reading "〜to ittara nai" :meaning "Expresses the extreme of something; 'nothing can be more ... than this'." :short "Expresses the extreme of something; 'nothing can be more ... than this'." :long "The ～といったらない grammar point is used to emphasize the high degree or intensity of an action or state. It can be translated as “nothing can be more ... than this” or “it’s extremely...”. It conveys that something is so intense or extreme that it cannot be adequately described." :formation "い-Adjective + といったらない / な-Adjective + だといったらない / Noun + だといったらない" :examples [{:ja "今日は寒いといったらない。" :en "It’s extremely cold today."} {:ja "彼女の料理の美味しさといったらない。" :en "Nothing can be more delicious than her cooking."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-196" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ときている" :reading "〜to kite iru" :meaning "Highlights a situation or condition that is serious/extreme, often with a negative nuance." :short "Highlights a situation or condition that is serious/extreme, often with a negative nuance." :long "The grammar point ～ときている is used to emphasize that some circumstance is particularly notable—often negatively. It implies, “Given that things are so ~, it’s no wonder that...” or “Because the situation is (too) ~, such-and-such follows.” It is relatively informal and more common in spoken language." :formation "Verb-casual / い-Adjective / な-Adjective（＋だ） / Noun（＋だ） + ときている" :examples [{:ja "仕事が忙しいときているので、なかなか休みが取れません。" :en "Because work is so busy, I just can’t take any time off."} {:ja "経済が悪いときているだけに、会社が大量解雇を発表した。" :en "Since the economy is in such bad shape, the company announced mass layoffs."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-197" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ところを" :reading "〜tokoro wo" :meaning "Indicates a contrasting or inconvenient timing; 'just when...', 'even though...'." :short "Indicates a contrasting or inconvenient timing; 'just when...', 'even though...'." :long "The grammar point ～ところを is often used when something happens (or one acts) at a certain moment/condition that makes the timing inconvenient or contrary to what is expected. It can imply “in spite of the fact that...” or “just when...” depending on context." :formation "Verb-ている + ところを / Noun + の + ところを / い-Adjective + ところを / な-Adjective + な + ところを" :examples [{:ja "ちょうど映画を見ているところを、停電で真っ暗になった。" :en "Just when I was in the middle of watching a movie, the power went out and everything went dark."} {:ja "美味しく食べているところを、彼が全部横取りしてしまった。" :en "Right when I was enjoying my meal, he took it all away."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-198" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とされる" :reading "〜to sareru" :meaning "Indicates a general belief or widely accepted view; 'is considered', 'is seen as', 'is said to be'." :short "Indicates a general belief or widely accepted view; 'is considered', 'is seen as', 'is said to be'." :long "The ～とされる grammar point expresses what is generally believed, accepted, or reputed. It can be translated as 'is considered to be,' 'is seen as,' or 'is said to be.' It’s often used in formal or written contexts. The structure differs slightly depending on whether it’s used with a noun, adjective, or verb." :formation "Verb-casual + とされる / い-Adjective + とされる / な-Adjective + だとされる / Noun + だとされる" :examples [{:ja "サッカーは世界で最も人気のあるスポーツとされています。" :en "Soccer is considered the most popular sport in the world."} {:ja "彼は最も有能な科学者とされる。" :en "He is considered the most talented scientist."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-199" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～としたところで" :reading "〜to shita tokoro de" :meaning "Means 'even if' or 'even assuming'; emphasizes that the outcome won’t change." :short "Means 'even if' or 'even assuming'; emphasizes that the outcome won’t change." :long "The ～としたところで grammar point means 'even if' or 'even assuming that ~.' It highlights that even if the stated condition were met, the result would not significantly change. Often used to convey a sense of futility or skepticism in hypothetical statements." :formation "Verb-casual + としたところで / い-Adjective + としたところで / な-Adjective + だとしたところで / Noun + だとしたところで" :examples [{:ja "彼が来るとしたところで、何も変わらないだろう。" :en "Even if he comes, nothing will probably change."} {:ja "美味しいお菓子を作るとしたところで、それが売れる保証はない。" :en "Even if we make delicious sweets, there's no guarantee they’ll sell."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-200" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とすると" :reading "〜to suru to" :meaning "Expresses 'if', 'when', or 'assuming that' in a speculative sense." :short "Expresses 'if', 'when', or 'assuming that' in a speculative sense." :long "The ～とすると grammar point is used for hypothetical or speculative situations, often in the sense of 'if we assume...', 'when it comes to...', or 'given that...' . It sets up a condition and then states the expected or possible outcome." :formation "Verb-casual + とすると / い-Adjective + とすると / な-Adjective + だとすると / Noun + だとすると" :examples [{:ja "彼が来るとすると、もうすぐ到着するはずです。" :en "If he’s coming, he should arrive soon."} {:ja "この仕事を終えるとすると、最低でも3日はかかるでしょう。" :en "Assuming we finish this work, it will take at least three days."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-201" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とすれば" :reading "～to sureba" :meaning "Used to express 'if it were the case that...', 'if we assume that...'." :short "Used to express 'if it were the case that...', 'if we assume that...'." :long "The ～とすれば grammar point poses a hypothetical condition and then draws a conclusion or inference. It translates as 'if we suppose/assume that...' or 'if it is the case that...'." :formation "Verb-casual + とすれば / い-Adjective + とすれば / な-Adjective + だとすれば / Noun + だとすれば" :examples [{:ja "彼が嘘つきだとすれば、その話は信じられない。" :en "If he’s a liar, then his story can’t be believed."} {:ja "あなたが一緒に行くとすれば、私も行きます。" :en "If you are going, I'll go too."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-202" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～となったら" :reading "〜to nattara" :meaning "Used to express 'if it comes to...', 'when it becomes the case that...'." :short "Used to express 'if it comes to...', 'when it becomes the case that...'." :long "The ～となったら grammar point is used to describe a situation where, if matters progress to that stage, a certain action or outcome will follow. It’s often translated as 'when it comes to...', 'if it comes to...'." :formation "Verb-casual + となったら / Noun + となったら" :examples [{:ja "旅行に行くとなったら、荷造りをしなければいけません。" :en "If it comes to going on a trip, we have to pack."} {:ja "試験に落ちるとなったら、どうしよう。" :en "What should I do if I fail the exam?"}]} {:id "grammar-n1-203" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～となると" :reading "〜to naru to" :meaning "Used to highlight a certain circumstance; 'when it comes to...', 'if it becomes...', 'in the event that...'." :short "Used to highlight a certain circumstance; 'when it comes to...', 'if it becomes...', 'in the event that...'." :long "The ～となると grammar point highlights a specific situation or condition and indicates what normally or naturally happens under that circumstance. It can be translated as 'when it comes to...', 'if it becomes...', or 'in the event that...'." :formation "Verb-casual + となると / い-Adjective + となると / な-Adjective + だとなると / Noun + だとなると" :examples [{:ja "試験となると、多くの学生が緊張する。" :en "When it comes to exams, many students get nervous."} {:ja "東京へ引っ越すとなると、生活費が一気に上がる。" :en "If you move to Tokyo, the cost of living goes up drastically."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-204" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～となれば" :reading "〜to nareba" :meaning "Expresses the idea of 'if it comes to...; when we talk about...; in the case of...'." :short "Expresses the idea of 'if it comes to...; when we talk about...; in the case of...'." :long "The ～となれば grammar point is used to express a situation where a particular case or condition brings about a certain result or consequence. It translates as 'if it comes to...; when we talk about...; in the case of...' in English." :formation "Verb-casual + となれば, い-Adjective + となれば, な-Adjective + だとなれば, Noun + だとなれば" :examples [{:ja "彼がリーダーとなれば、プロジェクトは成功するでしょう。" :en "If he becomes the leader, the project will probably succeed."} {:ja "明日が雨だとなれば、ピクニックをキャンセルしましょう。" :en "If it turns out to be raining tomorrow, let's cancel the picnic."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-205" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とのことだ" :reading "〜to no koto da" :meaning "Expresses something that the speaker heard; 'I've heard that', 'I was told that'." :short "Expresses something that the speaker heard; 'I've heard that', 'I was told that'." :long "The ～とのことだ grammar point is used to express something that the speaker has heard from someone else. It can be roughly translated as 'I've heard that', 'I was told that' in English. It is a formal way to present hearsay or information the speaker did not directly witness but was informed about." :formation "Simplified phrase + とのことだ" :examples [{:ja "週末にパーティーがあるとのことだ。" :en "I've heard that there is a party this weekend."} {:ja "彼は来年ロンドンに引っ越すとのことだ。" :en "I was told that he is moving to London next year."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-206" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とはいえ" :reading "～to wa ie" :meaning "Even though; although; but" :short "Even though; although; but" :long "The ～とはいえ grammar structure is used to express the contradictory nature of a situation or event. It means 'even though' or 'although', signifying a sense of contrast or contradiction. The usage of this structure is mainly seen in a formal or written context." :formation "Noun + とはいえ, な-Adjective + だとはいえ, い-Adjective + とはいえ, Verb-casual + とはいえ" :examples [{:ja "彼は頭が良いとはいえ、少し怠け者だ。" :en "Although he is smart, he is a bit lazy."} {:ja "このレストランは美味しいとはいえ、ちょっと高い。" :en "While this restaurant is delicious, it's a bit expensive."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-207" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とみえて" :reading "〜to miete" :meaning "It seems that; apparently" :short "It seems that; apparently" :long "The ～とみえて grammar point is used to express the speaker's judgment about a situation based on visible or known information. It is often translated as 'apparently' or 'it seems that' in English." :formation "Verb-casual + とみえて, い-Adjective + とみえて, な-Adjective + だとみえて, Noun + だとみえて" :examples [{:ja "彼は運動が得意とみえて、毎日ジムに行っています。" :en "He seems to be good at exercise, as he goes to the gym every day."} {:ja "彼女は忙しいとみえて、なかなか会えない。" :en "She seems to be busy, as it's hard to meet her."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-208" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とみられる" :reading "～to mirareru" :meaning "It is considered that; It is thought that; It is believed that" :short "It is considered that; It is thought that; It is believed that" :long "The ～とみられる grammar point is used to express how an idea, fact, or circumstance is considered or understood. This phrase is often used in news or reports to show the speaker's or writer's estimation, judgment, or present belief about something." :formation "Verb-casual + とみられる, Noun + とみられる" :examples [{:ja "彼の行動は無責任とみられる。" :en "His behavior is considered irresponsible."} {:ja "この絵はピカソの作品とみられている。" :en "This painting is believed to be a work of Picasso."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-209" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～とみると" :reading "〜to miru to" :meaning "Expresses the concept of 'when one sees it as', 'viewed as', or 'considering'." :short "Expresses the concept of 'when one sees it as', 'viewed as', or 'considering'." :long "The ～とみると grammar point is used to express the concept of seeing something from a specific perspective and responding accordingly. It often brings awareness toward something or a change in perception. It is translated as 'when one sees it as', 'viewed as', or 'considering'." :formation "Verb-casual + とみると, い-Adjective + とみると, な-Adjective + だとみると, Noun + だとみると" :examples [{:ja "この問題を数学の視点でとみると、解決策が見えてきます。" :en "When one sees this problem from a mathematical perspective, a solution begins to emerge."} {:ja "彼女が優秀な学生だとみると、その賞をもらったのは当然です。" :en "Considering she is an excellent student, it's natural that she received that award."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-210" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "どんなに～うが" :reading "donna ni ～ u ga" :meaning "Expresses 'no matter how much...', 'even if...'." :short "Expresses 'no matter how much...', 'even if...'." :long "The grammar point どんなに～うが is used to express a condition that is not affected by the degree of an action or state. It can be translated as 'no matter how much...' or 'even if...'. It is generally used with verbs, い-adjectives and な-adjectives in their volitional or かろう/だろう form." :formation "どんなに + Verb-volitional + うが, どんなに + い-Adjective(～かろう) + が, どんなに + な-Adjective + だろうが" :examples [{:ja "どんなに頑張ろうが、彼の速さには追いつけない。" :en "No matter how hard I try, I can't catch up with his speed."} {:ja "どんなに食べようが、彼女は太らない。" :en "No matter how much she eats, she doesn't gain weight."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-211" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～と言わんばかりに" :reading "〜to iwan bakari ni" :meaning "Indicates an action or behavior that strongly implies a certain unspoken message or feeling." :short "Indicates an action or behavior that strongly implies a certain unspoken message or feeling." :long "The ～と言わんばかりに grammar point is used to express that someone's action or behavior is so strong that it's as if they are saying something without actually saying it. It can be translated as 'as if to say' or 'almost as though stating'. This form is often used when the speaker wants to convey speculation about someone's unvoiced thoughts or feelings based on their behavior." :formation "Verb-volitional + と言わんばかりに, い-Adjective + と言わんばかりに, な-Adjective + だと言わんばかりに, Noun + だと言わんばかりに" :examples [{:ja "彼女は優勝したと言わんばかりに笑った。" :en "She laughed as if to say she won."} {:ja "彼は倒れると言わんばかりの疲労感があった。" :en "He was so fatigued it was as if he might collapse."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-212" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～と言わんばかりの Noun" :reading "～to iwan bakari no Noun" :meaning "Expresses the concept of 'as if to say'; 'almost says'; 'suggests'." :short "Expresses the concept of 'as if to say'; 'almost says'; 'suggests'." :long "The ～と言わんばかりの Noun grammar point is used to express that someone's action or a situation suggests a certain message, even though it is not explicitly said. It can be translated as 'as if to say', 'almost says', or 'suggests'. It is typically used to convey an unspoken message through someone's demeanor or behavior." :formation "Verb-plain form + と言わんばかりの Noun" :examples [{:ja "田中さんは嬉しくて飛び上がると言わんばかりの笑顔を見せた。" :en "Tanaka showed a smile as if to say he was so happy he could jump for joy."} {:ja "彼女は僕に話しかけると言わんばかりの視線を送った。" :en "She sent me glances as if to say she wanted to speak with me."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-213" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ながらに" :reading "～nagara ni" :meaning "Expresses a state or condition continuing just as it is (e.g. 'from birth', 'while crying', 'in the old way')." :short "Expresses a state or condition continuing just as it is (e.g. 'from birth', 'while crying', 'in the old way')." :long "The ～ながらに grammar point is used to indicate a condition that remains unchanged from some starting point or in a certain manner. It appears in set phrases such as 生まれながらに (from birth), 涙ながらに (while crying), 昔ながらに (in the old/original way), 子供ながらに (even though [still] a child), etc." :formation "Common set phrases: 生まれながらに, 涙ながらに, 昔ながらに, 子供ながらに, etc." :examples [{:ja "彼女は生まれながらにして優れた音感を持っている。" :en "She has had an excellent sense of pitch from birth."} {:ja "彼は子供ながらに大人の会話を理解していた。" :en "Even though he was just a child, he understood adult conversations."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-214" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ながらの Noun" :reading "〜nagara no Noun" :meaning "Indicates one action or state happening at the same time as the noun in question (e.g. 'dinner while watching TV')." :short "Indicates one action or state happening at the same time as the noun in question (e.g. 'dinner while watching TV')." :long "The ～ながらの Noun grammar point is used to modify a noun by describing an action happening simultaneously. For example, テレビを見ながらの夕食 means 'dinner while watching TV.' It can be translated as 'while ~ing' or 'during ~.'" :formation "Verb-ますstem (or plain minus る) + ながら + の + Noun" :examples [{:ja "テレビを見ながらの夕食は、子供たちにとって楽しいだろう。" :en "Eating dinner while watching TV might be fun for the children."} {:ja "地図を読みながらの運転は危険です。" :en "Driving while reading a map is dangerous."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-215" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ながらも" :reading "〜nagara mo" :meaning "Means 'even though' or 'despite'." :short "Means 'even though' or 'despite'." :long "The ～ながらも grammar point is used to express contrast, highlighting the difference between what is usually expected from a situation and what actually happens. It can be translated as 'even though' or 'despite.'" :formation "Verb stem + ながらも, い-Adjective + ながらも, な-Adjective/Noun + ながらも" :examples [{:ja "彼は貧しいながらも、幸せな生活を送っている。" :en "Despite being poor, he leads a happy life."} {:ja "僕は忙しいながらも、毎日運動しています。" :en "Even though I am busy, I exercise every day."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-216" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～なくはない" :reading "〜naku wa nai" :meaning "Expresses the idea 'it's not that it's impossible' or 'it's not that I can't'." :short "Expresses the idea 'it's not that it's impossible' or 'it's not that I can't'." :long "The ～なくはない grammar point implies that while something may not be your first choice or not guaranteed, it's not outright impossible. Translates to 'It's not that it's impossible,' 'It's not that I don't/can't ~.'" :formation "Verb-ないform + なくはない, い-Adjective－い + くはない, な-Adjective + ではなくはない, Noun + ではなくはない" :examples [{:ja "それを終えるのは難しくなくはない。" :en "It's not that it's impossible to finish it."} {:ja "彼に話すのは怖くなくはない。" :en "It's not that I'm entirely unafraid to talk to him."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-217" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～なくもない" :reading "〜naku mo nai" :meaning "Expresses 'not impossible' or 'might as well'." :short "Expresses 'not impossible' or 'might as well'." :long "The ～なくもない grammar point is used to suggest that something is not necessarily preferred or likely, but it's also not out of the question. It can often translate to 'It’s not a bad idea' or 'It might be feasible.'" :formation "Verb-ないform + なくもない, い-Adjective－い + くもない, な-Adjective + でもない, Noun + でもない" :examples [{:ja "彼がもうすぐ戻るなら、待つなくもない。" :en "If he’s coming back soon, waiting wouldn’t be a bad idea."} {:ja "この本が役に立つなら、買うなくもない。" :en "If this book is useful, it might not be a bad idea to buy it."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-218" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～なら～なりに" :reading "" :meaning "Means 'if it is X, then it's X in its own way', emphasizing unique qualities." :short "Means 'if it is X, then it's X in its own way', emphasizing unique qualities." :long "The ～なら～なりに grammar point shows that something has its own particular way or style of being, based on that condition. It often conveys a positive acceptance or appreciation of the situation 'in its own way.'" :formation "Noun/Adjective + なら + (same Noun/Adjective) + なりに" :examples [{:ja "彼はまだ初心者なら初心者なりに頑張っている。" :en "Since he’s still a beginner, he’s doing his best in his own beginner way."} {:ja "この料理は辛いなら辛いなりに美味しい。" :en "If this dish is spicy, it’s delicious in its own spicy way."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-219" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～には及ばない" :reading "〜ni wa oyobanai" :meaning "Means 'no need to go that far' or 'not necessary'." :short "Means 'no need to go that far' or 'not necessary'." :long "The ～には及ばない grammar point is used to show that an action or degree of effort is unnecessary or excessive. It often translates to 'there’s no need to...' or 'it doesn’t warrant...' in English." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + には及ばない" :examples [{:ja "ファーストクラスに乗るには及ばない、エコノミーでも十分だ。" :en "There’s no need to go as far as flying first class; economy is enough."} {:ja "毎回会うたびにギフトを持って行くには及ばない。" :en "There’s no need to bring a gift every time you meet her."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-220" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～に堪えない" :reading "～ni taenai" :meaning "Indicates something is unbearable or not worth doing (e.g., 'unbearable to watch', 'unbearable to listen to')." :short "Indicates something is unbearable or not worth doing (e.g., 'unbearable to watch', 'unbearable to listen to')." :long "The ～に堪えない grammar pattern is used when something is so unpleasant, awful, or painful that one cannot tolerate it. Common verbs include 見るに堪えない, 聞くに堪えない, 読むに堪えない, etc., meaning 'unbearable to see/hear/read.'" :formation "Verb-dictionary form + に堪えない, Noun + に堪えない" :examples [{:ja "彼の冗談は聞くに堪えない。" :en "His jokes are unbearable to listen to."} {:ja "その映画は見るに堪えないほどひどい出来だ。" :en "That movie is so bad that it’s unbearable to watch."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-221" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～に堪える" :reading "～ni taeru" :meaning "Used to say that something is 'worth doing' or 'can stand up to' something (the positive counterpart of ～に堪えない)." :short "Used to say that something is 'worth doing' or 'can stand up to' something (the positive counterpart of ～に堪えない)." :long "The ～に堪える grammar point indicates that something is good enough or worthy enough to do (read, see, listen to, etc.). Common examples include 読むに堪える, 見るに堪える, 聞くに堪える, etc., meaning 'worth reading/watching/listening to.'" :formation "Verb-dictionary form + に堪える, Noun + に堪える" :examples [{:ja "彼の歌声は聞くに堪える。" :en "His singing voice is worth listening to."} {:ja "この小説は読むに堪える内容だ。" :en "This novel is worth reading."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-222" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～に耐える" :reading "～ni taeru" :meaning "To endure or withstand something; 'endure', 'withstand', 'tolerate'." :short "To endure or withstand something; 'endure', 'withstand', 'tolerate'." :long "The ～に耐える grammar point is used to express the concept of enduring or withstanding something, often involving a degree of difficulty or hardship. It can be translated as 'endure', 'withstand', or 'tolerate' in English. Here we focus on usage with nouns (e.g., a force or a hardship to endure)." :formation "Noun + に耐える" :examples [{:ja "彼は厳しい修行に耐えた。" :en "He endured the harsh training."} {:ja "この橋は強風に耐えられる。" :en "This bridge can withstand strong winds."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-223" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～に至った" :reading "〜ni itatta" :meaning "Expresses the outcome or final conclusion of a process; 'reached the point', 'led to'." :short "Expresses the outcome or final conclusion of a process; 'reached the point', 'led to'." :long "The ～に至った grammar point is used to express the final outcome or conclusion that has been reached as a result of a process or a series of events. It can be translated as 'reached the point', 'led to', or 'resulted in' in English. It often indicates a significant outcome." :formation "Verb-てform + に至った or Noun / Verb-dictionary + に至る (more general)." :examples [{:ja "彼は練習を重ねて、ついにプロのギタリストに至った。" :en "After repeated practice, he finally became a professional guitarist."} {:ja "調査を進めるうちに、驚くべき結論に至った。" :en "As the investigation progressed, it led to a surprising conclusion."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-224" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～に越したことはない" :reading "〜ni koshita koto wa nai" :meaning "Expresses that something is the best or most desirable course of action; 'nothing better than...'." :short "Expresses that something is the best or most desirable course of action; 'nothing better than...'." :long "The ～に越したことはない grammar point indicates that a certain state or action is the most favorable or ideal. It literally conveys the idea that nothing surpasses the stated condition." :formation "Verb (dictionary form) + に越したことはない, い-Adjective + に越したことはない, な-Adjective/Noun + である + に越したことはない" :examples [{:ja "早く準備するに越したことはない。" :en "It's best to prepare early."} {:ja "新鮮な食材を使うに越したことはない。" :en "There's nothing better than using fresh ingredients."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-225" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～に難くない" :reading "～ni katakunai" :meaning "Expresses 'not difficult to ~' in a formal/literary sense; e.g., 'not hard to imagine/understand'." :short "Expresses 'not difficult to ~' in a formal/literary sense; e.g., 'not hard to imagine/understand'." :long "The ～に難くない (～にかたくない) grammar is typically used in formal or literary contexts to indicate that something is not difficult. Common expressions include 想像に難くない ('not hard to imagine'), 理解に難くない ('not difficult to understand'), 察するに難くない, and so on." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + に難くない (commonly with 想像する, 理解する, 察する, etc.)" :examples [{:ja "彼の喜びは想像に難くない。" :en "It's not hard to imagine his delight."} {:ja "この結果が彼女を失望させたことは理解に難くない。" :en "It's not difficult to understand that this outcome disappointed her."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-226" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～のは Noun ぐらいのものだ" :reading "〜no wa Noun gurai no mono da" :meaning "Conveys that 'only (Noun) is the case' or 'nothing but (Noun)'." :short "Conveys that 'only (Noun) is the case' or 'nothing but (Noun)'." :long "The ～のは Noun ぐらいのものだ pattern indicates that something is exclusively or solely that particular Noun, conveying the sense of 'That's the only thing...' or 'There's nothing but...' about a situation." :formation "Verb-casual + のは + Noun + ぐらいのものだ, い-Adjective + のは + Noun + ぐらいのものだ, etc." :examples [{:ja "この小説を読むのは暇つぶしぐらいのものだ。" :en "Reading this novel is nothing but a way to kill time."} {:ja "今日の夕食で良かったのはピザぐらいのものだ。" :en "About the only good thing at tonight's dinner was the pizza."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-227" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ば～ものを" :reading "～ba～mono o" :meaning "Expresses regret or a missed opportunity: 'if only', 'had I but...'." :short "Expresses regret or a missed opportunity: 'if only', 'had I but...'." :long "The ～ば～ものを grammar point shows regret about what could have been, often translated as 'if only...' or 'it would have been better if...'. It can be used with verbs, adjectives, or nouns, usually in a past or conditional form." :formation "Verb-ば + Verb-past + ものを, い-Adjective + ければ + ものを, な-Adjective/Noun + であれば + ものを, etc." :examples [{:ja "早く起きれば、遅刻しなかったものを。" :en "If only I'd woken up early, I wouldn't have been late."} {:ja "もっと頑張れば、テストに合格したものを。" :en "If only I'd tried harder, I would have passed the test."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-228" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～びた" :reading "～bita" :meaning "A suffix meaning “looking or seeming like ~” (often appears with nouns/adjectives)." :short "A suffix meaning “looking or seeming like ~” (often appears with nouns/adjectives)." :long "The suffix ～びた attaches to certain words to mean “to have the look/feel of ~.” Common examples include 大人びた (adult-like), 古びた (old/worn), 田舎びた (countrified). It expresses that someone or something appears or has become like that noun/adjective." :formation "[Noun/Adjective stem] + びた (forms a 連体形 modifier, e.g., 大人びた人 “an adult-like person”)" :examples [{:ja "大人びた考え方をする子供" :en "A child who thinks in a very grown-up way"} {:ja "古びたカバンを捨てられない。" :en "I can't throw away that old, worn-looking bag."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-229" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～びる" :reading "〜biru" :meaning "A suffix meaning “to appear/to become ~,” often used with certain adjectives or nouns." :short "A suffix meaning “to appear/to become ~,” often used with certain adjectives or nouns." :long "The suffix ～びる is used to form verbs meaning 'to become like' or 'to seem like.' Typical examples include 大人びる (to become or behave like an adult), 古びる (to become old or look old), 田舎びる (to become rustic/countrified)." :formation "Adjective/Noun + びる (to form a verb)" :examples [{:ja "彼女は年齢より大人びている。" :en "She seems older than her actual age."} {:ja "この建物はすっかり古びている。" :en "This building has become completely old and worn."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-230" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ぶった" :reading "～butta" :meaning "Often appears as a past form of ～ぶる, meaning 'acted/pretended like ~' (or literally 'hit/bashed', depending on context)." :short "Often appears as a past form of ～ぶる, meaning 'acted/pretended like ~' (or literally 'hit/bashed', depending on context)." :long "The verb ～ぶる means 'to act/behave as if one is ~' (e.g., 偉そうにぶる: 'to act all high and mighty'). In its past tense, ～ぶった can describe someone who acted or pretended in a certain way. Be aware that ぶった can also simply be 'hit' (past tense of 打つ/ぶつ) depending on the kanji/spelling, but as a grammar pattern, it’s about 'putting on airs.'" :formation "Noun/Adjective + ぶる → ぶった (past tense), e.g., 偉そうぶった, 大人ぶった" :examples [{:ja "彼は何でも知っているかのようにぶった態度を取る。" :en "He takes on an attitude as though he knows everything."} {:ja "偉そうぶった物言いをされると、少し腹が立つ。" :en "It irritates me when he speaks in such a high-and-mighty way."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-231" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ぶって" :reading "〜butte" :meaning "Te-form of ～ぶる, meaning 'acting/pretending to be ~'." :short "Te-form of ～ぶる, meaning 'acting/pretending to be ~'." :long "～ぶる (in te-form: ～ぶって) indicates 'behaving as if one were ~.' This is often used negatively to criticize someone for putting on airs or pretending to know more than they do (e.g., 知ったかぶって 'to act like a know-it-all')." :formation "Noun/Adjective + ぶる → ぶって (te-form)" :examples [{:ja "彼は何でも知ったかぶって話をする。" :en "He talks as if he knows everything."} {:ja "彼女は何も知らないふりをしてぶっていたが、本当は全部わかっている。" :en "She was acting like she didn’t know anything, but in reality, she understands it all."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-232" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ぶり" :reading "〜buri" :meaning "Expresses the amount of time elapsed since doing something last." :short "Expresses the amount of time elapsed since doing something last." :long "The ～ぶり grammar point is used to express that a certain amount of time has passed since you last did something. It often translates as 'for the first time in ~' or 'after (time period)'." :formation "Time expression + ぶり" :examples [{:ja "三年ぶりに彼に会いました。" :en "I met him for the first time in three years."} {:ja "一週間ぶりにジムに行った。" :en "I went to the gym for the first time in a week."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-233" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ぶる" :reading "〜buru" :meaning "Used when someone is acting or pretending to be something they are not (often a negative connotation)." :short "Used when someone is acting or pretending to be something they are not (often a negative connotation)." :long "The ～ぶる grammar point is used when someone pretends or acts like something they aren't. It often has a negative nuance, suggesting insincerity or putting on airs. Common usage includes nouns or descriptive words before ぶる (e.g., 学者ぶる, 偉そうぶる, etc.)." :formation "Noun / (Adjective stem) + ぶる" :examples [{:ja "彼はあたかも知っているかのように学者ぶる。" :en "He acts like a scholar, as if he knows everything."} {:ja "女優ぶるのはやめて、自分自身でいろ。" :en "Stop acting like you’re an actress and just be yourself."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-234" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～までだ" :reading "～made da" :meaning "Expresses a limit or extent; 'it's only up to...', 'no more than...'." :short "Expresses a limit or extent; 'it's only up to...', 'no more than...'." :long "The ～までだ grammar point is used to emphasize that something goes only up to a certain limit or extent. It can also imply 'it's only this and nothing more.'" :formation "Verb (dictionary/ない form) + までだ, い-Adjective + までだ, Noun + までだ" :examples [{:ja "彼とは友達までだ。それ以上の関係ではありません。" :en "With him, it's only friendship—nothing more."} {:ja "飲み放題は9時までだ。それ以降は追加料金が必要だ。" :en "The all-you-can-drink deal is only until nine. After that, there's an extra charge."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-235" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～もなんでもない" :reading "〜mo nandemonai" :meaning "Used to dismiss or negate something’s importance; 'it’s nothing at all', 'it doesn’t matter'." :short "Used to dismiss or negate something’s importance; 'it’s nothing at all', 'it doesn’t matter'." :long "The ～もなんでもない pattern is used to deny or downplay the significance of something. It can mean 'it doesn’t matter at all' or 'it’s no big deal.'" :formation "Verb-dictionary form + もなんでもない, い-Adjective + もなんでもない, な-Adjective + なもなんでもない, Noun + もなんでもない" :examples [{:ja "100万円を負けるもなんでもない。" :en "Losing a million yen is no big deal at all."} {:ja "彼が社長だもなんでもない。" :en "It doesn't matter one bit that he's the president."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-236" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～ものとして" :reading "～mono to shite" :meaning "Indicates an assumption or supposition; 'assuming', 'under the assumption that'." :short "Indicates an assumption or supposition; 'assuming', 'under the assumption that'." :long "The ～ものとして grammar pattern is used to express a supposition or assumption: 'assuming that ~', 'on the premise that ~'. It often sets up a condition or rule as the basis for some action or decision." :formation "Verb-casual + ものとして, い-Adjective + ものとして, な-Adjective + なものとして, Noun + のものとして" :examples [{:ja "雨が降るものとして、傘を持って行きました。" :en "Assuming it would rain, I took an umbrella."} {:ja "彼が病気なものとして、代わりに私が出席した。" :en "On the assumption that he was sick, I attended in his place."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-237" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～んがために" :reading "〜n ga tame ni" :meaning "Emphasizes the reason or purpose for an action; 'in order to', 'for the sake of'." :short "Emphasizes the reason or purpose for an action; 'in order to', 'for the sake of'." :long "The ～んがために form is a formal/literary expression meaning 'in order to' or 'for the purpose of.' It attaches to the negative stem (ない-form minus ない) of the verb: e.g., する→せん, 行く→いかん, 保つ→たもたん, etc." :formation "Verb-negative stem + んがために" :examples [{:ja "健康を保たんがために、毎日運動をしています。" :en "I exercise daily in order to maintain my health."} {:ja "合格せんがために、一生懸命勉強します。" :en "I study hard in order to pass."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-238" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～んばかりに" :reading "〜n bakari ni" :meaning "Indicates 'as though about to ~' or 'as if to say ~'." :short "Indicates 'as though about to ~' or 'as if to say ~'." :long "The ～んばかりに form is typically built on the negative stem of a verb (ない-form minus ない: 行か→行かん, 言わ→言わん, 知ら→知らん, etc.) + ばかり. It describes someone’s action or expression as though it were about to happen, or as though conveying a certain meaning without explicitly stating it." :formation "Verb-negative stem + ん + ばかりに" :examples [{:ja "彼は何も知らんばかりに、私の質問を無視した。" :en "He ignored my question as if he didn’t know anything."} {:ja "その子は泣かんばかりに顔をしかめた。" :en "The child made a face as if he were about to cry."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-239" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～差し支えない" :reading "〜sashitsukaenai" :meaning "Indicates no problem or obstacle; 'it’s fine if...', 'there’s no issue'." :short "Indicates no problem or obstacle; 'it’s fine if...', 'there’s no issue'." :long "The ～差し支えない grammar point expresses that doing something (or something occurring) is not a problem and causes no inconvenience. It’s often used in polite or formal situations." :formation "Verb-て form + も差し支えない, Noun + が差し支えない" :examples [{:ja "多少遅くても差し支えないです。" :en "It’s no problem if you’re a bit late."} {:ja "部屋が少し汚くても差し支えない。" :en "It doesn’t matter if the room is slightly messy."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-240" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～折に" :reading "〜ori ni" :meaning "Indicates the time or occasion when something happens; 'when', 'at the time of'." :short "Indicates the time or occasion when something happens; 'when', 'at the time of'." :long "The ～折に grammar point is used in a somewhat formal register to express that one event or action takes place on the occasion of another. It essentially means 'when' or 'at the time of'." :formation "Verb-dictionary form + 折に, Verb-た形 + 折に, Noun + の折に" :examples [{:ja "父が病気の折に、私は留学していました。" :en "When my father was ill, I was studying abroad."} {:ja "日本へ行く折に、お土産を買ってきてください。" :en "When you go to Japan, please buy souvenirs."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-241" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～極まりない" :reading "〜kiwamarinai" :meaning "Expresses an extreme state, often negative; 'utterly', 'extremely', 'boundlessly'." :short "Expresses an extreme state, often negative; 'utterly', 'extremely', 'boundlessly'." :long "The ～極まりない grammar point indicates an extreme or utmost degree, most commonly with a negative nuance (e.g., 失礼極まりない, 危険極まりない). It can sometimes be used for positive contexts, but that usage is less common." :formation "な-Adjective（語幹）+ 極まりない, い-Adjective（連用形）+ こと + 極まりない, Noun + の極まりない" :examples [{:ja "彼の失礼な態度は極まりない。" :en "His rude attitude is utterly beyond words."} {:ja "その運転は危険極まりない。" :en "That driving is extremely dangerous."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-242" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～極まる" :reading "〜kiwamaru" :meaning "Also expresses an extreme degree or state, typically negative." :short "Also expresses an extreme degree or state, typically negative." :long "The ～極まる form often appears with な-adjectives: e.g., 失礼極まる, 非常識極まる ('extremely rude/unreasonable'). It's largely synonymous with ～極まりない but tends to appear in set phrases. Both forms are quite formal and emphatic." :formation "な-Adjective（語幹）+ 極まる, い-Adjective（連用形）+ こと + 極まる, etc." :examples [{:ja "彼の言動は失礼極まる。" :en "His words and actions are extremely rude."} {:ja "その態度は非常識極まる。" :en "That behavior is utterly irrational."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-243" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～足りない" :reading "～tarinai" :meaning "Expresses insufficiency or lack; 'not enough', 'insufficient'." :short "Expresses insufficiency or lack; 'not enough', 'insufficient'." :long "The ～足りない pattern simply means that something is not sufficient in quantity, time, detail, ability, etc. It’s the negative form of 足りる ('to be enough')." :formation "Noun + (が) 足りない, Verb-casual + ほど/だけ + (が) 足りない" :examples [{:ja "私の日本語の能力はまだまだ足りない。" :en "My Japanese skills are still not enough."} {:ja "時間が足りないから、今すぐ行かなきゃ。" :en "We don't have enough time, so we must go right now."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-244" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～足る Noun" :reading "〜taru Noun" :meaning "Indicates that something is worthy or sufficient to be called/used as ~; 'worthy of', 'enough to ~'." :short "Indicates that something is worthy or sufficient to be called/used as ~; 'worthy of', 'enough to ~'." :long "The classical pattern 'Noun + に足る + Noun' or 'Verb + に足る + Noun' means 'something is worthy of or sufficient for ~.' For instance, '信頼に足る人物' means 'a person worthy of trust.' It’s different from the everyday verb 足りる ('to be enough')." :formation "Noun/Verb-dictionary form + に足る + Noun" :examples [{:ja "彼は信頼に足る人物だ。" :en "He is a person worthy of trust."} {:ja "この小説は読むに足る価値がある。" :en "This novel is well worth reading."}]} {:id "grammar-n1-245" :track :grammar :level "N1" :display "～限りだ" :reading "〜kagiri da" :meaning "Expresses an extreme degree of emotion or reaction; 'I feel so ~', 'as ~ as can be'." :short "Expresses an extreme degree of emotion or reaction; 'I feel so ~', 'as ~ as can be'." :long "The ～限りだ structure is often used with adjectives describing the speaker's emotion, highlighting how strongly the speaker feels. Translations often include 'I’m as ~ as can be,' 'I feel so ~.'" :formation "い-Adjective + 限りだ, な-Adjective + な/である + 限りだ, Noun + の + 限りだ" :examples [{:ja "彼女が急に辞めてしまった。驚きの限りだ。" :en "She suddenly quit. I'm as shocked as can be."} {:ja "息子が成功した。嬉しい限りだ。" :en "My son succeeded. I'm so happy."}]}])

